.SH DESCRIPTION
.B socketley
is a high-performance Linux daemon and CLI that manages network runtimes
(servers, clients, proxies, Redis-compatible caches, S3 object storage, authoritative DNS, MQTT brokers) in a
Docker-like style \(em
with clustering, WebSocket, Lua scripting, and io_uring async I/O.
.PP
.B "TLS terminates on a server or cache listener, on both of a proxy's legs, and on a client's dial."
A
.B server
runtime encrypts its own listening port with
.BR \-\-tls " " \-\-cert " " \-\-key ,
and verifies client certificates with
.BR \-\-ca .
A
.B proxy
does the same on its own port, verifies client certificates with
.BR \-\-client\-ca ,
and reaches an
.B https://
backend over TLS \(em verifying the chain and the endpoint's name, and
presenting a client certificate with
.BR \-\-client\-cert " " \-\-client\-key .
A
.B client
runtime dials its target over TLS with
.BR \-\-tls ,
verifying the chain against
.B \-\-ca
or the system trust store and matching the host from
.B \-t
against the certificate; it presents its own certificate with
.BR \-\-cert " " \-\-key .
There is no way to switch either check off \(em an unverified TLS client buys
encryption against somebody recording the wire and nothing at all against
somebody sitting on it.
A
.B cache
terminates TLS on its own listener with the same three flags a server uses, and
RESP runs on the decrypted stream, so a Redis client needs no change. The one
thing it refuses is
.B \-\-tls
together with
.BR \-\-replicate :
replication is not encrypted, so the pair would put the whole dataset on the
wire in the clear. An
.B mqtt
broker terminates TLS on its MQTT and WebSocket ports with the same three flags,
and with
.B \-\-ca
takes the name of a verified client certificate as the client's user. A runtime
carrying a configuration that cannot be honoured
(for example one restored from a state file written by an older release)
refuses to start rather than running in cleartext. See
.B TLS
under
.BR LIMITATIONS .
.PP
The io_uring event loop automatically takes advantage of advanced kernel
features when available: multishot receive (one SQE services many reads)
and registered file descriptors (skip per-op
.BR fget / fput
overhead). Each feature is probed at startup and falls back transparently
to standard ops on older kernels, so no configuration is needed.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B daemon
Start the socketley daemon.
When installed system-wide (via .deb package or install.sh), the daemon is
managed by systemd and starts automatically \(em you do not need to run this
command manually. Use
.B systemctl start socketley
or
.B systemctl enable socketley
to control the service.
.PP
When running from the build directory (development mode), start the daemon
manually:
.B ./bin/Release/socketley daemon &
.PP
Creates IPC socket at /run/socketley/socketley.sock (system install) or
/tmp/socketley.sock (development mode).
On startup, restores persisted runtime configurations. Runtimes that were
running when the daemon last stopped are auto-started.
.PP
The daemon ignores
.BR SIGHUP ,
so a closed terminal or a stray
.B kill \-HUP
leaves it running.
.PP
Takes
.BI \-\-cpus " set"
\(em the ring setting described under
.BR "COMMON FLAGS" ,
here shaping the ring this daemon is about to create rather than stating what a
runtime requires of one. It is the command-line spelling of
.BR SOCKETLEY_CPUS ,
and the flag wins when both are given. It also takes
.B \-\-fast\-lo
(below) and the experimental Raft flags described under
.BR "CLUSTER MODE" .
Any other argument is refused: this
command used to ignore its command line entirely, so
.B socketley daemon \-\-cpus 0\-1
started an unpinned daemon and exited 0.
.IP
It cannot be combined with
.B \-\-name
or
.BR \-\-cluster ,
which address a daemon that is already running \(em a ring's CPU set is fixed
when the ring is created, so there would be nothing left for them to change.
.IP
Every runtime is its own process and every one of them inherits this set, so
the set is also how many of them share a core. Measured with fifty idle
servers beside one doing a ping-pong, the median round trip did not move \(em
30.2 against 29.4 microseconds \(em while the 99th percentile did: 156.5
microseconds on six cores against 51.7 on twelve. Idle runtimes are not free
company, because each still wakes on its own stats interval; what they cost is
paid by the tail of whichever one is working, and widening the set is the
lever. The daemon itself stayed under one per cent of a core at fifty
runtimes, and creating one took the same fourteen milliseconds at five as at
fifty, so neither of those is the thing to watch.
.TP
.BR "daemon \-\-fast\-lo " [ own | all | trust ]
Put TCP pairs whose both ends are 127.0.0.1 or ::1 on a kernel shortcut. A BPF
.B sockops
program enters both sockets in a sockhash when the connection is established,
and an
.B sk_msg
program places every send straight into the peer's receive queue, skipping
TCP/IP, the loopback device and its softirq. Off unless given.
.IP
.B all
(the default when no mode is given) takes any local client connecting to a port
one of this daemon's runtimes listens on, the runtimes among themselves, and
every connection a runtime makes to a listener on 127.0.0.1 whoever owns it: a
proxy with
.B \-\-backend 127.0.0.1:8080
in front of a local application, a client runtime or a Lua HTTP call to a local
service.
.B own
takes only pairs whose both ends are this daemon's runtimes, such as a client
runtime connected to a server runtime.
.B trust
is
.B all
without the receiver rule below: a send is redirected into a foreign receiver
too, so a foreign client of a cache and a proxy's foreign backend are
accelerated in both directions. It is for an operator who knows that every
local process reading a socket of such a pair does so with
.BR recv ,
.B read
or
.BR recvmsg .
.RS
.PP
.B Warning:
a byte redirected into a socket waits in an ingress queue that only
.B recvmsg
drains. A local process that reads with
.BR splice (2),
.BR sendfile (2),
.B TCP_ZEROCOPY_RECEIVE
or a relay built on them (Go's
.B io.Copy
between two TCP connections, HAProxy with splicing) never sees those bytes:
under
.B trust
it loses them, with no error on either side (measured: a backend reading with
.B splice
behind a proxy echoed 0 of 500 bytes). Such readers cannot be told apart from
the outside, which is why
.B all
does not redirect into a foreign socket at all. Use
.B all
unless every local client and backend is known.
.B socketley daemon stats
reports which mode is running.
.RE
.IP
Under
.B own
and
.B all
a send is redirected only when the socket that will
.I receive
it is one of this daemon's workers' \(em a runtime that connects (its socket is
marked when it does) or one that accepts on a runtime's listener. A send whose
receiver is a foreign socket goes by ordinary TCP, and each direction of a pair
is decided by its own receiver. This is because a socket that receives through
the kernel's ingress queue can only be read with
.BR recvmsg :
a foreign reader using
.BR splice (2),
.BR sendfile (2),
Go's
.B io.Copy
between two TCP connections, or HAProxy's splicing would lose the redirected
bytes, and those readers cannot be told apart from the outside. This bounds the
shortcut to what it can accelerate safely: into a runtime always, out to a
foreign peer never (unless
.B trust
is given).
.IP
What that means for the common shapes, measured on one box with alternating
passes:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
Runtime to runtime (a client runtime to a server runtime, both ends this
daemon's) is accelerated in both directions under every mode: 64 B round trips
1.3\(en1.4x one connection and eight, 16 KB about 1.2\(en1.5x; a client runtime
against a cache, off against
.BR all ,
110\(en116K against 83\(en85K round trips a second with one in flight.
.IP \(bu 2
A foreign client of a cache is accelerated only on the request it sends in;
the reply comes back by TCP. So a small request/large reply workload gains
little under
.BR all :
.B redis\-benchmark
(50 connections, 4 threads, six alternating passes, requests per second off /
.B all
/
.BR trust "):"
.B GET
.B \-P1
240\(en279K / 222\(en250K / 387\(en667K,
.B SET
.B \-P1
222\(en286K / 197\(en273K / 462\(en632K, GET
.B \-P16
2.3\(en4.0M / 2.7\(en3.4M / 5.3\(en6.9M, and a bulk reader of 64 KiB values
1377\(en1488 / 1276\(en1322 / 2464\(en3040 MB/s. A request that carries the
payload does gain under
.BR all :
64 KiB
.B SET
pipelined 8 deep, 49.9K against 59.1K, 1.18x. Only
.B trust
takes the full gain of a foreign client.
.IP \(bu 2
A proxy in front of a foreign local backend accelerates, under
.BR all ,
the two legs that end in the proxy and leaves the two that end in a foreign
socket on TCP, so a foreign backend that reads with
.B splice
keeps every byte. Under
.B trust
both are redirected: 64 B round trips through a proxy to a C echo backend, one
connection, 44\(en48K off, 41\(en45K
.BR all ,
86\(en88K
.BR trust .
.RE
.IP
Needs
.B CAP_BPF
and
.B CAP_NET_ADMIN
(root, or
.B setcap cap_bpf,cap_net_admin+ep
on the binary) and cgroup v2 at /sys/fs/cgroup. Without them the daemon logs
.B fast\-lo: unavailable, serving without it
with the reason, and serves on the ordinary path.
.B own
attaches to the daemon's cgroup and
.B all
and
.B trust
to the root cgroup; the daemon's own processes are the workers its supervisor
forked, whose pids it keeps in a map. Under
.B all
and
.B trust
the program fires for every process on the host, so it takes only sockets in
the daemon's own network namespace \(em its netns cookie is baked in at load,
and a container's identical 127.0.0.1 tuple is left alone. This needs
.B SO_NETNS_COOKIE
in the kernel; without it the shortcut refuses to attach rather than serve
across namespaces. The programs and maps are anonymous and detached
when the daemon exits.
.IP
What it costs:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
The redirected backlog is bounded, by
.BR "daemon \-\-fast\-lo\-limit " "\fIper\-pair\-MiB\fR,\fIglobal\-MiB\fR (default 4,64)."
A redirected send does not wait for the receiver, so the program counts each
pair's sends and, each time the count passes a mark, asks a reconciler thread
through a BPF ring buffer for the peer's true queue, which the thread reads
with one exact
.B sock_diag
lookup and hands back within tens of microseconds. A reader that keeps up is
snapped back and its pair stays on the shortcut however much it moves; a pair
whose peer really holds the per-pair bound, or whose count reaches twice it
unanswered, is tainted to TCP \(em which then gives the ordinary
.B EAGAIN
backpressure. A flag taints new sends once the sum across pairs reaches the
global budget; a sweep once a second recomputes that sum and re-asks any check
whose answer was lost, and an idle pair costs nothing. Without the bound a peer
that stopped reading took 731 MiB before the kernel's global TCP memory
.RB ( tcp_mem )
ran out; with the default it stays near 4 MiB per pair.
.IP \(bu 2
The taint is permanent for the pair's life. This is what keeps bytes in order,
since
.B tcp_bpf_recvmsg
drains the redirect queue before the TCP receive queue, so a byte sent by TCP
after the taint is still read after the ones already redirected.
.IP \(bu 2
Two edges the bound does not catch tightly: a pair moving gigabytes a second
whose answer is held up for more than about a millisecond (the reconciler
starved of CPU) reaches the hard ceiling and is tainted, which costs speed and
not bytes; and many pairs stalling in the same instant can each hold their
per-pair bound before the global flag is raised, which then holds the sum
there.
.IP \(bu 2
Shortcut bytes skip netfilter, conntrack and the loopback device: nftables rules
on
.BR lo ,
.B tcpdump \-i lo
and the byte counters in
.B ss
do not see them;
.B socketley daemon stats
and the metrics endpoint say what the shortcut is carrying.
.IP \(bu 2
127.0.0.1 and ::1, including 127.0.0.1 IPv4\-mapped on a dual\-stack socket;
the rest of 127/8 stays on TCP. A runtime listens and a proxy or client runtime
connects over IPv4, so a ::1 pair here is a Lua
.B socketley.http()
call to a local service on ::1, or to a name that resolves there first. With
both ends entered a ::1 pair gains about what a 127.0.0.1 pair does (64 B round
trips 1.3x against 1.4x, 16 KB 1.2x against 1.3x); a call that opens a
connection per request gains
nothing, since entering and leaving the sockhash costs about what the request
saves (\-3% at 64 B).
.IP \(bu 2
A pair enters only when both ends are in the map before its first byte, and a
socket that sent a byte by TCP stays on TCP for its lifetime, so no byte
overtakes another. A client sending TCP Fast Open data stays on TCP.
.IP \(bu 2
The proxy's
.B SOCKETLEY_PROXY_SPLICE
path reads a queue a socket on the shortcut does not fill, so a proxied pair
with a 127.0.0.1 leg stays on the copy path while it is on.
.IP \(bu 2
Under
.B own
and
.B all
a process that is not this daemon's is never redirected into \(em only its
sends into one of our workers are, and our workers read with
.BR recvmsg .
So a foreign relay copying between two TCP connections with Go's
.B io.Copy
or HAProxy with splicing on keeps every byte on the leg it receives, and needs
no
.B own
to be safe. This is the receiver rule above;
.B trust
gives it up.
.RE
.RS
.PP
.nf
sudo socketley daemon --fast-lo &        # all
sudo socketley daemon --fast-lo own &
sudo socketley daemon --fast-lo trust &  # only if no local reader splices
.fi
.RE
.TP
.BI "daemon \-\-name " "name " "[\-\-cluster " "dir" "]"
Configure the running daemon for cluster mode. These flags are sent as IPC
commands to the already-running daemon (auto-started if not running).
.B \-\-name
sets the daemon's identity in the cluster.
.B \-\-cluster
enables cluster discovery on the given shared directory (see
.BR "CLUSTER MODE" ).
The name can be changed at any time; if cluster mode is active, the cluster
file is republished under the new name.
.RS
.PP
.nf
socketley daemon --name node1 --cluster /shared/cluster
socketley daemon --name new-name   # rename while cluster is active
.fi
.RE
.TP
.BI "create " "type name " "[flags]"
Create a new runtime instance.
.I type
can be: server, client, proxy, cache, storage, dns, sync, idp, db, mqtt, mail, tunnel, or any registered type \(em see
.BR "REGISTERED TYPES" .
.I name
is a unique identifier for the runtime.
.IP
With
.B \-s
and a
.BR \-\-lua " script,"
.B on_start
runs inside this command. A raise there does not take the runtime down \(em it
is created and running, with whatever
.B on_start
established before it failed \(em but the command exits 1 and names the callback
and the reason, so a start whose set-up did not run is not reported as a clean
one. Fix the script and
.B reload\-lua
to re-run it.
.TP
.BI "attach " "type name port " "[\-\-owner " "name" "] [\-\-pid " "pid" "]"
Register an external process as a runtime visible in
.BR ls / ps .
The daemon does NOT bind the port or forward any I/O \(em the external process
manages its own network connections. The runtime appears as running and can
be inspected or removed like any other runtime.
.RS
.TP
.I type
Runtime type: server, client, proxy, or cache.
.TP
.I name
Unique name for this external runtime.
.TP
.I port
Port number the external process is listening on (informational only).
.TP
.B \-\-owner \fIname\fR
Optional owner runtime name.
.TP
.B \-\-pid \fIpid\fR
The process behind this runtime. Recorded so that
.B "socketley stop " \fIname\fR
sends it
.BR SIGTERM :
without it the daemon has nothing to stop and the runtime only leaves the
listing. An SDK-embedded process passes its own
.BR getpid() .
.IP
The pid must belong to the user issuing the command. The daemon signals it on
the caller's behalf, and it will not do that for a process the caller could not
signal themselves \(em on the packaged daemon, whose socket is shared with the
.B socketley
group, that would lend out the daemon user's privileges. A daemon running as
root is unrestricted, since root can signal anything in any case.
.RE
.TP
.BI "add " "path " "[\-\-name " "name" "] [\-s]"
Register and manage an external binary (built with the C++ SDK).
The daemon will fork+exec the binary and manage its full lifecycle:
auto-restart on crash, re-launch on daemon boot, and normal
start/stop/remove commands.
.RS
.TP
.I path
Path to the executable (must exist and be executable).
.TP
.B \-\-name \fIname\fR, \fB\-n\fR \fIname\fR
Name for the runtime. If omitted, derived from the binary's filename
(basename without extension).
.TP
.B \-s
Start immediately after adding.
.PP
The binary receives
.B SOCKETLEY_MANAGED=1
and
.B SOCKETLEY_NAME=\fIname\fR
environment variables. When using
.BR daemon_attach() ,
these cause the binary to re-attach to the existing runtime entry
and skip the atexit self-removal.
.PP
.nf
socketley add /usr/local/bin/myapp -s
socketley add /usr/local/bin/myapp --name chat
socketley stop chat
socketley start chat
socketley remove chat
.fi
.RE
.TP
.BI "start " "name|pattern" "... [\-i]"
Start one or more runtimes. Accepts multiple names, glob patterns
.RB ( * ", " ? ", " [] ),
or a mix. With
.BR * ,
starts all non-running runtimes.
.RS
.TP
.B \-i
Interactive mode. Starts the runtime and opens an interactive session.
For server: stdin is broadcast to clients, received messages appear on stdout.
For client: stdin is sent to server, received messages appear on stdout.
For cache: stdin accepts cache commands, responses appear on stdout (like redis-cli).
Refused for a proxy \(em it relays between a client and a backend and has no
stream of its own for typed input to join. The refusal comes before the start,
so the runtime is left exactly as it was; see
.BR LIMITATIONS .
Press Ctrl+C to detach (runtime continues running).
If the runtime is already running, attaches without restarting.
.IP
A runtime lives in its own process, and the session reaches it: what is typed
is delivered to the workers holding the connections, and their descriptor for
the session is handed to each of them so what a client says arrives at the
terminal with nothing of the daemon's between. Under
.BI \-\-shards " N"
that is every shard \(em a typed line reaches all of their clients, and any of
them can be heard back.
.IP
A session survives a
.B restart
of the runtime it is attached to and is re-attached to the new workers. It does
not end when the runtime is
.BR stop ped:
nothing tells it, and the operator has to press Ctrl+C.
.RE
.IP
For a runtime that lives in a worker process,
.B start
waits for the child to report before it returns \(em so the port is accepting
by the time the command exits, and a refusal the child alone can judge is the
command's own exit status rather than something
.B ls
shows two seconds later. Exit 1 names the runtime if the worker would not take
its configuration, and carries the reason if the script's
.B on_start
raised. One budget of two seconds is spent for the whole command, not one per
runtime: the children are forked together and come up concurrently, so a
.B start
of twenty costs about the same wait as a start of one. A child still starting
after that, such as a storage runtime reading a large store's files after a
crash, is waited for by the CLI rather than the daemon, which stays free: the CLI
asks the daemon whether it serves yet five times a second, for as long as its
workers keep using CPU, and answers 2 once they have used none for 15 seconds.
.BR create " \-s"
and
.B restart
wait the same way.
.TP
.BI "stop " "name|pattern" "..."
Stop one or more running runtimes. Accepts multiple names, glob patterns
.RB ( * ", " ? ", " [] ),
or a mix. With
.BR * ,
stops all running runtimes.
.IP
A stopped runtime executes no script. Its
.B on_stop
runs, and then every
.B set_timeout
and
.B set_interval
timer and every
.B socketley.subscribe
registration it holds is severed, so nothing it armed can fire afterwards \(em
and none of the three can be armed again until it is started, which is what
stops a script that called
.B socketley.stop
on itself from putting one straight back.
.TP
.BI "remove " "name|pattern" "..."
Remove one or more runtimes from the registry. Cannot be undone.
Accepts multiple names, glob patterns, or a mix. Running runtimes
are stopped first. With
.BR * ,
stops and removes all.
.TP
.BI "send " "name" " [message]"
Send a message to a running server or client runtime.
For servers, the message is broadcast to all connected clients.
For clients, the message is sent to the connected server.
Respects the runtime's mode setting (fails if mode is receive-only).
.TP
.BI "send " "mail " "\fB\-\-to \fIaddress\fR [\fB\-\-cc\fR|\fB\-\-bcc \fIaddress\fR] [\fB\-\-from \fIaddress\fR] [\fB\-\-reply\-to \fIaddress\fR] [\fB\-\-subject \fItext\fR] [\fB\-\-text \fItext\fR] [\fB\-\-html \fIfile\fR] [\fB\-\-attach \fIfile\fR[:\fItype\fR]] [\fB\-\-inline \fIimage\fR] [\fB\-\-header \fR'\fIName\fR: \fIvalue\fR'] [\fB\-\-raw\fR]"
A message written and queued by a mail runtime, which answers its queue id:
its text from
.B \-\-text
or stdin, an HTML part beside it from a file, attachments in base64, every
flag but
.B \-\-to
said as often as needed. A name or address may be
.BR "Name <a@b>" .
Without
.B \-\-from
a runtime with
.B \-\-no\-reply
sends from no\-reply@ its first domain.
.B \-\-raw
queues stdin as the whole message, its headers its own. A message past 4 MiB
goes to the runtime by a file in its outbox under the state directory, so one
up to
.B \-\-max\-size
is sent whole; a message to a server or a client that begins with \- is given
after \-\-. A
.B :type
after an attachment is taken as its type only where it is one, type/subtype,
and the file does not exist with it.
.B \-\-inline
sends an image inside the message, which its HTML shows as
.BI cid: name
by the image's file name, so a reader sees it without fetching anything. See
.BR "MAIL FLAGS" .
.RS
.PP
.nf
socketley send m \-\-to ops@example.com \-\-subject "Backup done" \-\-text ok
df \-h | socketley send m \-\-to "Ops <ops@example.com>" \-\-subject "Disks" \e
    \-\-attach report.pdf:application/pdf
.fi
.RE
.TP
.BI "send " "mail " "\fB\-\-template \fIfile\fR \fB\-\-to \fIaddress\fR|\fBidp:\fIidp\fR[\fB/\fIgroup\fR]|\fBdb:\fIdb\fB/\fIcollection\fR|\fBjson:\fIfile\fR [\fB\-\-set \fIname\fB=\fIvalue\fR] [\fB\-\-lua \fIfile\fR] [\fB\-\-ad\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-check\fR] [\fB\-\-rate\-limit \fIn\fR[\fB/m\fR|\fB/h\fR]] [\fB\-\-test\fR] [\fB\-\-test\-to \fIaddress\fR]"
A message for each recipient, of their own, from a template: an HTML file, or
.B \-\-text
for plain text alone, and the subject, with placeholders.
.B {{name}}
is the recipient's value of that name,
.B {{name|otherwise}}
says what stands where they have none, and
.B {{{name}}}
puts a value into the HTML as it is, which escapes every other. The values are
.BR \-\-set ,
given for every recipient, and what each recipient's source says of them: an
address of
.B \-\-to
has its
.BR email ;
.BI idp: idp
the idp's accounts that are not disabled, those of a group after a slash, each
with its username, name, email and every field it keeps but its password;
.BI db: db / collection
the collection's documents, each with its fields, an object's below its name
.RB ( address.city );
.BI json: file
a JSON array of objects, or one a line. Each needs an
.BR email ,
and a
.B name
is written into its To. With an HTML template the plain part beside it is the
HTML's text, its links written out. The messages go at
.B \-\-rate\-limit
a second, or a minute or an hour after a slash
.RB ( 300/m ;
two a second unless it says), so a new sender's first thousand do not land in
the spam folder for coming at once; what was skipped or failed is said by
recipient, and a line says how many were sent.
.IP
A template is Lua.
.B {{ expr }}
is the value of any Lua expression and
.B {% code %}
runs where it stands, a tag alone on its line taking the line with it:
.RS
.PP
.nf
{% if vip then %}<p>Your code: {{code}}</p>{% end %}
{% for _, p in ipairs(products) do %}
<li>{{p.title}}: {{ string.format("%.2f", p.price) }}</li>
{% end %}
.fi
.RE
.IP
A name with dots is a field below another, no value rather than an error where
one on the way is missing, and a list of words is written joined by commas.
.BR html (s),
.BR url (s)
and
.BR raw (s)
escape, percent-encode, and keep a value unescaped;
.BR include (\(dqfile\(dq)
puts in another template, from beside this one, filled for the same recipient.
A template reaches its recipient's values, what the script defines, string,
table, math, os.date and os.time, and no file and no process.
.B \-\-lua
.I file.lua
runs once before the first recipient and reaches all of Lua;
.BI on_recipient( r )
is called with each recipient's values before their message is written, to
change them, or to leave the recipient out by returning false and why, and
.BI on_link( url ", " r )
with each link of the HTML but the one to unsubscribe, its answer the link
written and nil keeping it. An error in Lua is a message that cannot be
written, said with its file and line.
.IP
A template is filled strictly: a recipient whose message has a name with no
value and no default is skipped, and one whose message is the first stops
them all before any is sent, as does a first the runtime refuses, since a
template that fails its first recipient is wrong for all of them.
.B \-\-no\-check
lets a name with no value be empty, and sends on past a first refused.
.B \-\-test
shows the first recipient's message and how many there are, and sends nothing;
.B \-\-test\-to
sends that message, its subject marked [test], to the address it names alone.
.IP
.B \-\-ad
marks an advertisement. Each goes to one recipient, never to one who
unsubscribed, with a link to unsubscribe of their own where the template says
.BR {{unsubscribe}} ,
or below what it says, and List-Unsubscribe naming it and a mailto: (RFC 2369),
with List-Unsubscribe-Post for a provider's one click (RFC 8058), and List-Id
naming its list by its sender, such as <news.example.com> (RFC 2919). The link is
.BI https:// webmail /unsubscribe
where the runtime has
.B \-\-web
and
.BR \-\-domain ,
whose GET asks and whose POST unsubscribes, so a scanner opening every link
unsubscribes no one; the mailto: is
.BI unsubscribe@ domain
with the token in its subject. A token is the address and an HMAC of it under the
runtime's key, so it is kept nowhere and forged by no one. What is no
advertisement still goes to an address that unsubscribed.
.RS
.PP
.nf
socketley send m \-\-template autumn.html \-\-inline logo.png \-\-to db:shop/customers \e
    \-\-from news@example.com \-\-subject "Hello {{name|there}}, your code {{code}}" \e
    \-\-ad \-\-rate\-limit 5
socketley send m \-\-template autumn.html \-\-to idp:auth/vip \-\-set code=VIP30 \-\-test
socketley send m \-\-template order.html \-\-lua shop.lua \-\-to db:shop/orders \-\-test\-to me@example.com
.fi
.RE
.PP
.PP
If no message is given, reads from stdin:
.nf
echo "hello" | socketley send myserver
cat data.txt | socketley send myclient
.fi
.PP
The word
.B send
may be left out when something is piped in, so
.B "echo \(dqhello\(dq | socketley myserver"
is the same command. This form is only taken when standard input is not a
terminal, which is also every script, pipeline and CI step \(em so a
mistyped command reaches it too. When nothing arrives on standard input,
socketley cannot tell a misspelled command from a runtime that was piped
zero bytes, and says so, naming both readings and leading with the typo:
.nf
$ socketley gibtsnicht < /dev/null
unknown command: gibtsnicht
  If gibtsnicht is a runtime you meant to send to, nothing arrived on standard input:
    echo 'data' | socketley gibtsnicht
.fi
.TP
.BI "stats " "name|pattern" "..."
Display runtime statistics including total connections, messages processed,
bytes in/out, and type-specific counters. Accepts multiple names, glob
patterns, or a mix. With
.BR * ,
shows stats for all runtimes.
.IP
One field describes how the runtime's I/O is running rather than what it has
done, and it is read from the ring itself rather than from what was asked for
\(em a cpuset can refuse the pinning, and a runtime whose real CPU set cannot be
read back is one nobody can debug:
.RS
.TP
.B cpus
The CPU set in force, in the same spelling
.B \-\-cpus
accepts, with
.B (not applied)
appended when the pinning was attempted and refused.
.TP
.B dial_attempts
Client only. The dials this client has made, beside
.B total_connections
which counts the ones the kernel found a peer for. A non-blocking connect
answers EINPROGRESS for every target that is not already there, so a dial is an
intention and the two figures come apart exactly when a target is flapping or
gone \(em a client reconnecting all afternoon shows one climbing and the other
standing still. Every other runtime counts
.B total_connections
at something that happened to it: the server and the cache at an
.BR accept (2),
the proxy once
.B on_auth
has admitted the client.
.TP
.B upstreams
Server only, and only when
.B \-u
was given: how many of the configured upstreams the kernel says are connected,
over how many were configured. The left number answers the same question
.B dial_attempts
separates for a client, and for the same reason \(em it used to be raised one
line after the non-blocking connect(), so a server whose upstream host was not
there at all reported
.B upstreams:1/1
for twenty-five seconds out of thirty. It is a flapping upstream that makes the
two numbers differ, and an unreachable one that makes the left stay at zero.
.RE
.IP
For a runtime running in its own process this describes that process's ring, not
the daemon's.
.TP
.B daemon stop
Stops the daemon this invocation would talk to, over its socket rather than by
pid, taking every runtime it supervises with it. It answers before it goes, and
exits 2 when nothing is serving that socket.
.TP
.B daemon stats
The daemon's own figures as
.IB key : value
lines, the shape
.B stats
gives a runtime. Today that is the loopback shortcut
.RB ( "daemon \-\-fast\-lo" ),
whose bytes
.B tcpdump \-i lo
and nftables never see:
.B fast_lo
is
.BR off ,
.BR own ,
.BR all ,
.BR trust ,
or
.B unavailable
with
.B fast_lo_reason
saying why. When attached:
.B fast_lo_sockets_shortcut
and
.B fast_lo_sockets_tcp
count the entered sockets that send by the shortcut and those pinned to TCP
for good (as of the last once\-a\-second sweep; a pair is two sockets, and each
direction is its sender's);
.B fast_lo_entered
counts sockets entered since start;
.BR fast_lo_tainted_bound ,
.BR _unanswered ,
.B _budget
and
.B _peer_off
count taints by reason \(em the peer really held the per\-pair bound, no answer
came before twice the bound, the global budget was reached, the peer was not on
the shortcut (it received by TCP before it could enter, or had closed);
.B fast_lo_held_bytes
is the unread redirected backlog summed across pairs, which the global budget
.RB ( fast_lo_budget_bytes )
is compared against, an upper bound for a pair below 64 KiB;
.B fast_lo_over_budget
is 1 while new sends are being tainted for it. The metrics endpoint carries the
same as
.BR socketley_fast_lo_info{mode} ,
.BR socketley_fast_lo_sockets{path} ,
.BR socketley_fast_lo_entered_total ,
.BR socketley_fast_lo_tainted_total{reason} ,
.BR socketley_fast_lo_held_bytes ,
.BR socketley_fast_lo_budget_bytes ,
.B socketley_fast_lo_pair_limit_bytes
and
.BR socketley_fast_lo_over_budget .
The counting is one atomic add per entered socket and per taint, never per send.
.RS
.PP
.nf
socketley daemon stats
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B idp\-user \fIidp\fR add \fIuser\fR [\fB\-\-password \fIpw\fR|\fB\-\fR] [\fB\-\-name \fIname\fR] [\fB\-\-email \fIaddr\fR] [\fB\-\-groups \fIa,b\fR] [\fB\-\-set \fIfield\fB=\fIvalue\fR]... [\fB\-\-reset\fR]
.TQ
.B idp\-user \fIidp\fR passwd \fIuser\fR \fB\-\-password \fIpw\fR|\fB\-\fR [\fB\-\-reset\fR]
.TQ
.B idp\-user \fIidp\fR set \fIuser field\fB=\fIvalue\fR...
.TQ
.B idp\-user \fIidp\fR disable\fR|\fBenable\fR|\fBlogout\fR|\fBremove\fR|\fBshow \fIuser\fR
.TQ
.B idp\-user \fIidp\fR list\fR|\fBimport \fIstorage\fR
.TQ
.B idp\-user \fIidp\fR totp \fIuser\fR on\fR|\fBoff
.TQ
.B idp\-user \fIidp\fR app\-password \fIuser\fR add \fIlabel\fR|\fBremove \fIlabel\fR|\fBlist
The accounts an
.B idp
runtime keeps, changed in the process that keeps them.
.B add
makes one under a new fixed id, which it prints and which every token names it
by, so a username changed later is still the same account;
.B \-\-password \-
reads the password from the first line of stdin, off the command line others
can read, and
.B \-\-reset
makes it set again at the next sign-in.
.B set
gives any field a value, an empty one removing it: a field the provider does
not know is kept and carried as it is, for the applications that use it.
.B logout
ends every session and refresh token the account holds;
.B passwd
does too.
.B show
prints the fields, the password as set and never its hash.
.B totp on
turns on a second step of signing in: it prints the key, and the otpauth://
link that gives it to an authenticator app, and from then on a web sign-in asks
the six digits the app shows after the password, a code taken once at every
shard and across a restart, the last step taken kept with the account (RFC 6238);
.B off
turns it off.
.B app\-password add
makes a password of four groups of four letters for one mail app, printed
once, which signs in over SMTP, IMAP, POP3 and JMAP and is taken back on its own
by
.BR remove ;
with the second step on, mail apps sign in with these alone, since they have no
code to give. A user does all of it on their account page,
.BR /account ,
the second step turned on only once a code proves the app has the key.
.B import
takes a storage runtime's users over, with the password each has there, an
account of the same name already here kept as it is. An admin of the storage
comes over as none here, since an admin here is root by SSH certificate, and
is named in the output so that
.B set \fIuser\fB groups=admins
makes it one. A refusal is exit 1.
.RS
.PP
.nf
socketley idp\-user auth add smiley \-\-password \- \-\-groups admins
socketley idp\-user auth set smiley picture=https://storage.example.com/me.png
socketley idp\-user auth import ui
.fi
.RE
.TP
.BI "login " "idp" " \fR[\fB\-\-client \fIid\fR]"
.TQ
.BI "logout " idp
A person signed in at an
.B idp
runtime, this machine's or another's, by the device grant (RFC 8628):
.B login
prints the address and the code to allow there, waits until a browser signed
in as that person allows it \(em the page names the application asking and
what allowing it gives, and for a client marked
.B ssh
under
.B \-\-ssh\-ca
says it gets SSH certificates, as root for an admin, and takes the password
again, or the code where the second step is on \(em and keeps the tokens in
.I ~/.config/socketley/tokens/
(0600), from where
.B ssh\-cert
and
.B tunnel
act as that person, refreshing the access token when it runs out. The client
is
.B socketley
unless named; the provider allows the grant only to a client marked
.B device
in its
.BR \-\-clients .
.B logout
forgets the tokens. A refused or expired code is exit 1.
.RS
.PP
.nf
socketley login auth.example.com
.fi
.RE
.TP
.BI "ssh\-cert " idp " \fR[\fB\-\-key \fIfile.pub\fR]"
An SSH certificate for the person signed in at
.I idp
by
.BR login ,
for
.I ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub
or, without one,
.I ~/.ssh/socketley_ed25519
made for it, written beside the key as
.IR \-cert.pub ,
where ssh finds it. It holds the hours the provider's
.B \-\-ssh\-ca
says and names the username, root for the admins group, and
.I name
for each group
.BI ssh: name .
A provider refusing it, as for a token given to a client not marked
.BR ssh ,
is exit 1.
.RS
.PP
.nf
socketley ssh\-cert auth.example.com
ssh root@server.example.com
.fi
.RE
.TP
.BI "idp\-ssh " idp " ca"
.TQ
.BI "idp\-ssh " idp " host\-sign " "file.pub " "\fB\-\-names \fIhost,...\fR [\fB\-\-days \fIN\fR]"
The SSH authority of an idp runtime of this daemon:
.B ca
prints its public key, for sshd's
.B TrustedUserCAKeys
and a client's
.B @cert\-authority
line;
.B host\-sign
certifies a host key for the names given, 365 days unless told, the
certificate written beside the key for sshd's
.BR HostCertificate .
.RS
.PP
.nf
socketley idp\-ssh auth ca > /etc/ssh/socketley_user_ca.pub
socketley idp\-ssh auth host\-sign /etc/ssh/ssh_host_ed25519_key.pub \-\-names server.example.com
.fi
.RE
.TP
.BI "tunnel " "edge" " invite\fR|\fBset " agent " \fR[\fB\-\-http \fIname\fR[\fB=\fIauth\fR]\fI,...\fR] [\fB\-\-tcp \fIname\fB:\fIport,...\fR]"
.TQ
.BI "tunnel " edge " list\fR|\fBrevoke \fIagent"
The agents a tunnel edge takes:
.B invite
prints an invitation, used once, for an agent to offer the services named
(an HTTP one at
.IB name . domain ,
a TCP one on its port, on every address of the machine or, as
.IB name : address : port ,
on that one alone), and invited again an agent takes a new one; an HTTP
service with
.BI = auth
asks each of its visitors for it, as a proxy's
.B \-\-auth
does;
.B set
changes what it offers and keeps its key;
.B revoke
cuts it off. See
.BR "TUNNEL FLAGS" .
.RS
.PP
.nf
socketley tunnel edge invite pi \-\-http home=idp:auth,nas \-\-tcp mc:25565
.fi
.RE
.TP
.BI "tunnel join " edge " \fR[\fB\-\-name \fIagent\fR] [\fB\-\-http \fIservice\fB=\fIhost\fB:\fIport,...\fR] [\fB\-\-tcp \fIservice\fB:\fIedge-port\fB=\fIhost\fB:\fIport,...\fR] [\fB\-\-replace\fR] [\fB\-\-idp \fIidp\fR]"
This machine made an agent of an edge, by an admin of the edge's idp: signed
in by a code allowed in a browser when no
.B login
is kept here, for this join alone and kept nowhere, the edge adds the agent, named after this machine's
host name unless
.B \-\-name
says otherwise, and the agent runtime of that name is made and joins at once.
Each HTTP service asks its visitors to sign in at the edge's idp until the
edge's owner says otherwise with
.BR "tunnel " "edge" " set" .
An agent of that name the edge holds as joined is replaced only with
.BR \-\-replace ,
as for a machine set up anew; a runtime of that name on this machine already
is exit 1, and the edge is not asked. Prints where each service is.
.RS
.PP
.nf
socketley tunnel join https://edge.example.com \-\-http ollama=127.0.0.1:11434
.fi
.RE
.TP
.BI "tunnel connect " "edge host" : port \fR|\fIagent\fB/\fIservice " \fR[\fB\-\-idp \fIidp\fR]"
.TQ
.BI "tunnel ssh " edge " \fR[\fB\-\-agent \fIagent\fR] [\fB\-\-idp \fIidp\fR]"
A stream to a
.B \-\-forward
target of a tunnel edge, between stdin and stdout, for the person signed in at
.I idp
by
.BR login ,
the edge's own idp, asked of the edge, unless given; an idp the edge names
is trusted with a sign-in kept here only when it is beside the edge
.RB ( auth.example.com
for
.BR edge.example.com ),
and any other only when given with
.BR \-\-idp ;
.B ssh
is to 127.0.0.1:22 there, for ssh's
.BR ProxyCommand .
An admin of the edge's idp may name an agent's TCP service instead,
.IB agent / service ,
and
.B \-\-agent
names that agent's
.BR ssh ,
so a machine at home is reached by ssh through 443 with no port opened for it
anywhere.
Errors are said on stderr, since stdout is the stream. A target the edge does
not name is exit 1.
.RS
.PP
.nf
ssh \-o ProxyCommand='socketley tunnel ssh edge.example.com' root@server
ssh \-o ProxyCommand='socketley tunnel ssh edge.example.com \-\-agent pi' me@pi
.fi
.RE
.TP
.BI "db\-backup " "db file"
Every document of a db runtime written into
.IR file ,
off its event loop while it serves; the command waits for it and is silent
when it is written. See
.BR "DB FLAGS" .
.TP
.BI "db\-restore " "db file"
A db runtime's documents replaced by a backup's: the file is read whole first
and one that is not a backup changes nothing; then the runtime is stopped, its
documents kept aside beside them as
.IB dir .before\-restore ,
the backup put in their place, and the runtime started again if it was running.
.TP
.BI "db\-query " "db collection " "\fR['\fIquery\fR']"
The documents of a collection a query of the db's API finds, printed as its
answer,
.BR {"docs":[...]} :
.I query
is the object
.B POST /c/\fIcollection\fB/_query
takes, its where, sort, limit (up to 1000) and skip, every document up to the
limit without one. It is asked as the operator, as a script is.
.RS
.PP
.nf
socketley db\-query shop customers '{"where":{"country":"de"},"limit":50}'
.fi
.RE
.TP
.BI "mqtt\-pub " "mqtt " "\fR[\fB\-\-qos \fR0|1|2] [\fB\-\-retain\fR] \fItopic\fR [\fIpayload\fR]"
A message published on an mqtt runtime as its own, at the QoS and retained when
asked: the payload is the words after the topic, or stdin when there are none,
its last newline dropped, carried byte for byte. Silent when the broker has it;
a topic with
.B +
or
.B #
or one starting with
.B $
is refused with exit 1.
.RS
.PP
.nf
socketley mqtt\-pub broker \-\-qos 1 \-\-retain firmware/latest 2.4.1
gzip \-c report.json | socketley mqtt\-pub broker reports/daily
.fi
.RE
.TP
.BI "mqtt\-sub " "mqtt filter " "\fR[\fB\-\-count \fIn\fR]"
Every message the broker accepts whose topic
.I filter
matches, printed as it arrives as one JSON line,
.BR {"topic":..,"payload":..,"qos":..,"retain":..,"client":..} ,
a payload that is not UTF-8 as
.BR {"base64":..} ,
until interrupted or
.I n
have been printed. It takes no session and changes nothing; a line
.B {"dropped":n}
says that many were not read in time.
.TP
.BI "mqtt\-sessions " mqtt
The broker's sessions, of every shard, as a JSON array: the client id, the
user, whether it is connected, the protocol version, the peer, its
subscriptions, the messages queued and in flight, the seconds left until it
expires while away, and its shard.
.TP
.BI "mqtt\-kick " "mqtt client " "\fR[\fB\-\-end\fR]"
Disconnects the client, telling a 5.0 client it was an administrative action,
without sending its will; the session stays for it to come back to, or with
.B \-\-end
ends too. A client id with no session is exit 1.
.TP
.BI "mqtt\-retained " mqtt " \fR[\fIfilter\fR] | \fB\-\-clear \fItopic"
The retained messages whose topics the filter matches
.RB ( # ,
every one but
.BR $SYS ,
unless given) as a JSON array, or the one on
.I topic
cleared.
.TP
.BI "mqtt\-passwd " "file user " "\fR[\fB\-\-delete\fR]"
Sets the password of
.I user
in a users file for
.BR \-\-users ,
read from stdin, or asked for twice without echo on a terminal, and kept as a
scrypt hash; the file is made with mode 0600 and replaced whole, every other
line kept. With
.B \-\-delete
the user goes. It needs no daemon; a broker, and a mail runtime's
.BR \-\-users ,
read the file again within two seconds.
.RS
.PP
.nf
socketley mqtt\-passwd /etc/socketley/mqtt.users sensor\-17
.fi
.RE
.TP
.BI "dnssec " dns
The DS record each zone of a dns runtime with
.B \-\-dnssec
is to be given at its parent, the registrar, and the DNSKEY it stands for, a
line each; the keys are made where the runtime has not made them yet, so the DS
can go to the registrar before the zone is served signed. A zone whose parent
does not have its DS is answered as if unsigned by every validating resolver;
one whose parent has a DS for another key is not answered at all, so a key is
never removed while a DS names it.
.RS
.PP
.nf
socketley dnssec ns
.fi
.RE
.TP
.BI "mail " "\fR[\fImail\fR] " "\fR[\fBqueue \fR[\fIfilter\fR] | \fBshow \fIid\fR | \fBflush \fR[\fIid\fR] | \fBdrop \fIid\fR | \fBdns \fR[\fB\-\-check\fR] | \fBtrace \fIaddress\fR | \fBusage \fR[\fIuser\fR] | \fBsuppressed\fR | \fBforgive \fIaddress\fR | \fBunsubscribed\fR | \fBresubscribe \fIaddress\fR | \fBlearn \fIuser\fB/\fIfolder \fB\-\-as spam\fR|\fBham\fR | \fBexport \fIuser \fR[\fIdir\fR] | \fBimport \fIuser file\fR|\fIdir\fR | \fBsieve \fIuser \fBlist\fR|\fBget \fIname\fR|\fBput \fIname file\fR|\fBcheck \fIfile\fR|\fBactivate \fIname\fR|\fBdeactivate\fR|\fBdelete \fIname\fR]"
The runtime's name may go unsaid where the daemon has one mail runtime alone.
Hourly, and as it starts, it asks whether the PTR of each address of its host
names that host again (forward-confirmed reverse DNS, without which Gmail and
Outlook refuse or file its mail as spam, and which is set where the address is
rented), and whether a blocklist of its
.B \-\-dnsbl
names one; either is said in its summary and the log.
Alone, a few lines of what the runtime is and how it does: its domains, users
and mailboxes, its ports, the webmail's name, what waits, what was delivered,
deferred, bounced, received and stored, and its lists. A mail runtime's spool,
of every shard:
.B queue
lists what waits (its id, size, sender, the recipients still waiting and the
tries), those whose id or sender holds
.I filter
where one is given;
.B show
the envelope of one, each recipient with what it was last answered;
.B flush
tries every message, or the one named, now;
.B drop
deletes one, told to nobody.
.B dns
prints the MX, SPF, DKIM, DMARC and SRV records its domains need, for a zone kept
elsewhere than a dns runtime of this daemon, and with
.B \-\-check
asks this machine's resolvers for each and says which are there, missing or
otherwise;
.B trace
.I address
says what became of the latest mail from or to an address, the newest last:
delivered, deferred with why, bounced, or refused and with what answer, each
also a line of the log;
.B usage
.RI [ user ]
what each user's mailboxes hold against their quota, every user of its idp
where none is named;
.B suppressed
the addresses bounces said are dead, which are not mailed again until
.B forgive
lets one go;
.B unsubscribed
the addresses that unsubscribed from its advertisements, until
.B resubscribe
takes one back.
.B learn
teaches the spam filter a folder of a user's, spam or not, such as their Junk
and their INBOX on a new machine, where the filter has learned nothing yet.
.B export
writes a user's mailbox out as mbox files, a folder each, as
.IB dir / folder .mbox
under a directory the daemon makes for itself alone and refuses where it holds
files already, under
.I /var/tmp/socketley\-mail
where none is named; the flags go as Status and X\-Status, the keywords as
X\-Keywords and the internal date in each "From " line.
.B import
reads an mbox, or every
.I .mbox
under a directory, into a user's folders, a file's into the folder its name
says, an empty file an empty folder: what
.B export
wrote, Thunderbird's, or a Google Takeout's, whose X\-Gmail\-Labels say the
folder (Inbox, Sent, Drafts, Spam as Junk, Trash, a label of the user's,
Archive for the rest) and whose read and starred say the flags. Both wait for
the end and say how many messages went. A mailbox on another IMAP server moves
over IMAP itself, with
.BR imapsync (1)
or a mail client.
.B sieve
lists a user's Sieve scripts, the active one marked, prints one, puts one from
a file
.RB ( \-
for stdin) once it compiles, checks one, makes one active or none, or deletes
one, as ManageSieve does. See
.BR "MAIL FLAGS" .
.RS
.PP
.nf
socketley mail m queue
socketley mail m show 65e5113d0e2120000000
socketley mail m dns >> /etc/bind/example.com.zone
socketley mail m sieve alice put main filter.sieve
socketley mail m sieve alice activate main
socketley mail m export alice /var/backups/alice
socketley mail m import alice ~/Takeout/Mail/All\ mail\ Including\ Spam\ and\ Trash.mbox
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B git\-sync \-\-url \fIurl\fR \-\-branch \fIname\fR|\fB*\fR \-\-work \fIdir\fR \-\-endpoint \fIhost\fB:\fIport\fR \fR[\fB\-\-tls\fR [\fB\-\-ca \fIfile\fR]] \fB\-\-bucket \fIlibrary\fR \fB\-\-name \fIfolder\fR [\fB\-\-keep \fIdays\fB,\fIweeks\fB,\fImonths\fR] [\fB\-\-move\-from \fIlibrary\fR]
One run of a repository a storage runtime keeps (see
.B Git repositories
under
.BR "STORAGE FLAGS" ),
which the runtime starts itself and which runs by hand the same: it fetches
into the bare clone under
.IR dir ,
and when a ref moved writes the branch's files and a bundle into the library at
.IR host : port
with the credentials in
.BR SOCKETLEY_GIT_ACCESS_KEY ,
.B SOCKETLEY_GIT_SECRET
and, for temporary ones,
.BR SOCKETLEY_GIT_SESSION_TOKEN ;
.B SOCKETLEY_GIT_TOKEN
is a GitHub token for a private repository, sent only to an https:// URL.
.B \-\-ca
names the certificate the runtime at
.IR host : port
must present over
.BR \-\-tls ,
which the runtime passes its own.
.B \-\-move\-from
names a library the folder was written to before: once the tree is whole in
the new one, the backups there are copied over, its files there removed, and
the library itself once nothing else is in it; one this user may not read is
left as it is. Exit status 0 when it ended well, 2
when it did not, 3 when a run of the same directory is under way;
.IR dir /state.json
says how.
.TP
.B acme issue \-\-domains \fIname\fR[\fB,\fIname\fR...] \fR[\fB\-\-cert \fIfile\fR] [\fB\-\-key \fIfile\fR] [\fB\-\-email \fIaddress\fR] [\fB\-\-directory \fIurl\fR] [\fB\-\-challenge http\-01\fR|\fBtls\-alpn\-01\fR|\fBdns\-01\fR]
A certificate from an ACME CA by hand, as certbot gives one: the account is made
once per CA and kept in
.IR state\-dir /.acme/,
the names are proven over HTTP-01 by the daemon's runtimes on port 80, or with
.B "\-\-challenge tls\-alpn\-01"
by its TLS runtime on port 443, or with
.B "\-\-challenge dns\-01"
\(em which a wildcard takes by itself \(em by a
.B dns
runtime of the daemon that serves the name's zone, as
.B \-\-acme
describes, and the chain and a new key are written to the files, the key first,
by default those
.B \-\-acme
uses for the first name, so a runtime serving them takes them up. It needs no
daemon to be running, only one of its runtimes on the port the challenge is asked on. Silent on
success; the CA's reason and exit status 2 otherwise. It is what a runtime with
.B \-\-acme
runs to renew.
.TP
.B daemons
Every daemon that has registered itself, with its pid, whether it still answers,
and the executable serving it \(em which differs per socket once a daemon built
on the SDK is running.
.IP
An asterisk marks the one this invocation would talk to;
.B SOCKETLEY_SOCKET
selects another. The list is a set of files, one per daemon, written after the
socket is served and removed on a clean exit; every entry is probed rather than
trusted, so a daemon that was killed outright is reported
.B dead
rather than listed as running, and its file is removed once its pid is gone too.
.TP
.BI "restart " "name " "[edit flags] [\-i]"
Stop and start a runtime in one step, with any
.B edit
flag given applied in between \(em which is how a setting that cannot change
while a runtime runs is changed at all:
.nf
socketley restart api \-p 9001
socketley restart ns\-proxy \-\-shards 1
.fi
.IP
One command and not a
.B stop
followed by a
.BR start ,
because the runtime being restarted may be the one the command travels
through: an entrance proxy carries the dashboard it was asked from, so the
stop arrived and the start never did, and the entrance stayed down with its
configuration unchanged. The daemon does all of it, so whatever becomes of the
caller's connection the runtime ends up running. A flag the
.B edit
refuses leaves the runtime running and unchanged.
.IP
The flags are
.BR edit 's,
read by
.B edit
and never handed to
.BR start :
handing them there had a flag's VALUE read as a runtime name and that runtime
started, which is why they were refused for a while.
.B \-i
is restart's own and attaches to the runtime once it is up. A runtime that was
stopped is started, as it was before, flags or no flags: it is the lifecycle
verb, and it means one thing. For a change that is to be stored WITHOUT
starting anything, see
.B edit \-\-restart
below.
.IP
A runtime's new worker is forked only once its previous worker has exited, since
that process holds the ports, unix paths and files until then; the
.BR start ,
.B reload
or
.B create \-s
that brought it up answers after that fork, and the daemon serves everything else
meanwhile. The previous worker is given five seconds before it is killed, or a
cache writing its dump that dump's own bound, so a command can outlast the CLI's
five-second wait for an answer while the runtime still comes up.
.TP
.BI "reload " "name|pattern" "..."
Restart one or more running runtimes (stop + start). Accepts multiple
names, glob patterns, or a mix. Non-running runtimes are skipped.
With
.BR * ,
restarts all running runtimes.
.IP
A runtime that lives in a worker process has its child taken down and forked
again, so the script on disk is re-read and a change to it takes effect. The
command waits for the new child, exactly as
.B create \-s
does, and answers for it: exit 1 naming the runtime if the worker refuses the
configuration it was handed, and exit 1 with the reason if the reloaded
script's
.B on_start
raises. Success stays silent.
.TP
.BI "reload-lua " "name|pattern" "..."
Hot-reload the Lua script attached to one or more runtimes. The Lua
context is destroyed and re-created from the original script path.
Accepts multiple names, glob patterns, or a mix. Runtimes without
Lua scripts are skipped. With
.BR * ,
reloads Lua on all runtimes that have scripts loaded.
.IP
.B on_start
runs again, on the new script, and this is the whole difference between
reloading a file and reloading a runtime. Everything a script establishes at
run time lives in its Lua context \(em every
.B socketley.subscribe
registration and every
.B set_timeout
or
.B set_interval
timer \(em and destroying the context destroys all of it. Since the ordinary
place to make those is
.BR on_start ,
a reload that did not re-run it left the runtime serving with no subscriptions
and no timers, and said nothing. So: anything
.B on_start
does must be safe to do twice.
.B socketley.create
there is the case that costs something, and it refuses a name that already
exists rather than duplicating it.
.IP
.B on_stop
does
.B not
run for the outgoing script. The runtime is not stopping \(em it keeps its
listener, its clients and its state right through the reload \(em and
.B on_stop
is where a script says goodbye to exactly those.
.IP
An
.B on_start
that raises is reported like a script that would not load: exit 1, with the
reason on the terminal. The new script is in force in that case, with whatever
its
.B on_start
managed before it failed; a script that would not
.I compile
leaves the previous one running instead. The message says which.
.IP
A runtime that runs in a worker is asked rather than reloaded here: its script
lives in that process and only that process can re-run it. The command waits
for the worker to report the declaration the new script makes \(em bounded the
way
.B create
bounds its own wait \(em so that when it returns, a gateway in front of that
runtime is already enforcing what the edited script says, including when the
edit removed the
.B routes
table altogether. A worker that does not answer in time leaves the previous
declaration in force and says so in the log; a script that does not compile
leaves the previous one running, as it does in the daemon.
.IP
A reload that succeeds prints nothing and exits 0. A script that would not load
is named on stdout with the reason \(em the parse error, or whichever
.B filters
or
.B routes
rule it broke \(em and the command exits 1. The runtimes that did reload are
still reloaded, and the ones that did not go on running the script they had, so
the message is the only sign that an edit is not live.
.TP
.BI "show " "name|pattern" "..."
Print runtime JSON configuration to stdout. Accepts multiple names,
glob patterns, or a mix. With
.BR * ,
prints all runtimes.
.TP
.BI "edit " "name " "[flags] [\-\-restart]"
Edit a runtime's configuration. When called with flags (e.g. \-p 9001),
applies the changes directly.
.B \-\-restart
stops the runtime first and starts it again afterwards, which is what a
setting refused "while running" needs \(em and
.B import
takes it the same way, before the document:
.nf
socketley edit ns\-proxy \-\-shards 1 \-\-restart
socketley show ns\-proxy | socketley import ns\-proxy \-\-restart
.fi
.IP
The daemon does the stop, the change and the start as one command, so a caller
whose connection the runtime carries cannot be cut off between them; see
.BR restart .
A change the runtime refuses leaves it running and unchanged \(em it was stopped
and started around the refusal, since whether a setting needs that is only
answered by applying it, and an edit that is refused rolls back whole.
A runtime that was already stopped stays stopped. The flags accepted here are a subset of the
ones
.B create
takes: the common settings
.RB ( \-p ,
.BR \-b / \-bp / \-bt / \-bpt ,
.BR \-\-log ,
.BR \-w ,
.BR \-\-max\-connections ,
.BR \-\-rate\-limit ,
.BR \-\-global\-rate\-limit ,
.BR \-\-idle\-timeout ,
.BR \-g ),
the server and client flags, and the proxy mesh flags
.RB ( \-\-strategy ,
.BR \-\-health\-* ,
.BR \-\-circuit\-* ,
.BR \-\-backend\-timeout ,
.BR \-\-retry ,
.BR \-\-retry\-all ),
and a tunnel's own flags, such as an agent's
.BR \-\-offer ,
which a running tunnel takes only with
.BR \-\-restart .
Anything else \(em including
.BR \-\-lua ,
.BR \-\-drain ,
.BR \-\-reconnect ,
the proxy's
.BR \-\-backend ,
.B \-\-protocol
and
.BR \-\-sidecar ,
and every cache flag \(em is refused with
.B "unknown flag"
and exit status 1; change those through the editor or
.BR import ,
which reach the full field set.
.PP
Four flags reach a
.I running
runtime of any type at once:
.BR \-\-max\-connections ,
.BR \-\-rate\-limit ,
.B \-\-global\-rate\-limit
and
.BR \-\-idle\-timeout .
They are the limits a runtime reads at the moment it uses them \(em one per
accept, one per message, one per idle sweep \(em so changing one changes the
next decision and nothing already decided. The daemon sends the edited
configuration to every shard.
.PP
On a
.B proxy
nine more do, for the same reason: they are routing and mesh
.I policy,
read when a backend is picked, a request is armed or a failure is counted, and
nothing is built from them.
.BR \-\-strategy ,
.BR \-\-health\-interval ,
.BR \-\-health\-path ,
.BR \-\-health\-threshold ,
.BR \-\-circuit\-threshold ,
.BR \-\-circuit\-timeout ,
.BR \-\-backend\-timeout ,
.B \-\-retry
and
.BR \-\-retry\-all .
.B \-\-health\-interval
re-times the sweep already armed, so at most one period of the old value
elapses before the new one applies.
.PP
.B \-\-health\-check
is not among them, and is the one proxy flag whose reason differs from the
others': it arms a sweep timer when the runtime starts, so turning it on has no
timer to arm. It is stored and applies at the next start like everything below.
.PP
Every other flag
.B edit
accepts on a running runtime is stored and takes effect at that runtime's next
start. Not as policy: a
.B \-p
is a bound socket, the TLS flags are a context the open handshakes already
used, a proxy's backends are open connections, and a server's
.B \-\-http
directory decides what a request in flight is being answered from. None of
those can change under a connection that exists. So
.B show
reports the new value while the runtime holding the port keeps the old one,
until
.RB "\(oq" "socketley restart " name "\(cq."
The flags that cannot even be stored while running say so and change nothing:
.BR \-p ,
.B \-\-listen
and
.BR \-\-shards .
.PP
The TLS flags are an exception only in what they refuse. Editing is the usual
way to put encryption on something that already exists, so on
.I every
runtime type
.BR \-\-tls ,
.BR \-\-cert ,
.B \-\-key
and
.B \-\-ca
\(em and, on a proxy,
.BR \-\-client\-ca ,
.B \-\-client\-cert
and
.B \-\-client\-key
\(em are accepted here. Certificates are read when a runtime starts, so a
running one keeps the identity it handshook with until it is restarted. An edit
that would leave the set incomplete \(em a certificate with no
.BR \-\-tls ,
.B \-\-tls
with no key pair on a listener, half a client-certificate pair, or
.B \-\-tls
on a cache that replicates \(em is refused and changes nothing, so a mistyped
flag cannot leave a runtime half-configured. See
.BR LIMITATIONS .
When called without flags (or with only \-r/\-\-reload),
opens the runtime's JSON configuration in
.BR $VISUAL ,
.BR $EDITOR ,
or
.BR vim .
After saving, changes are validated and applied.
.TP
.BI "edit " "name " "\-r"
Interactive editor mode with auto-reload. After saving changes, the
runtime's Lua script is automatically hot-reloaded. Alias:
.BR \-\-reload .
.TP
.BI "export " dir
This daemon's setup written as Lua into
.IR dir :
.B config.lua
with an
.B include
of a file for each runtime under
.BR runtimes/ ,
a dns runtime's zones under
.B zones/
and a handler script under
.BR lua/ ,
in an order that makes a runtime before those that name it. A runtime another
made, such as a dns runtime's entrance, is made again by that one and left out;
the daemon's own settings are carried from its configuration file. What the
runtimes read and the setup does not hold \(em a credentials file, a token
file, a certificate outside the state directory \(em is named at the top of
.BR config.lua ,
to be put on the next machine as well. Their data is not in it. The setup is
read back and each runtime planned from it before the command ends, so one that
it would make otherwise is named and the exit code is 1; a directory that holds
a
.B config.lua
already is refused. See
.B "A machine's setup"
under
.BR "LUA CONFIGURATION" .
.TP
.BI "export \-\-full " file " \fR[\fB\-\-compress fast\fR|\fBbest\fR|\fBnone\fR] [\fB\-\-threads\fR \fIn\fR]"
The setup and its data in one tar archive, gzip around it unless
.B \-\-compress none
says otherwise (\fBbest\fR looks longer for what repeats, as
.B gzip \-9
does; what does not get smaller, as photos and attachments, goes in as it is),
compressed a MiB at a time on as many threads as the CPUs it may run on, or
.B \-\-threads
.IR n ,
into one stream any gzip reads: the state directory \(em every runtime's file, the mailboxes' index,
the idp's accounts and keys, the Sieve scripts, the calendars' journals, the
DKIM and DNSSEC keys, the certificates \(em the daemon's configuration
directory, and what each runtime keeps elsewhere: a storage runtime's buckets,
a cache's file and its journal, a zone, a script, a file of tokens. A db
runtime that runs is written by its own backup, which holds what it holds at
one moment, rather than copied while it writes; a cache gives what its file
and its journal hold, what a crash would leave it, every write but the last
.B \-\-persistent\-interval
where it journals; the Lua setup
.B export
writes goes in beside them. Not in it: a log, what a runtime writes as its
output, a backups directory, and the files a sync runtime carries, which are
the user's own; each is named when the command ends. Run as root or as the
daemon's user, while it runs or not, the archive written 0600 and only once it
is whole.
.RS
.PP
.nf
socketley export \-\-full /var/backups/socketley\-$(date +%F).tar.gz
.fi
.RE
.TP
.BI "restore " file " \fR[\fB\-\-force\fR]"
What
.B export \-\-full
wrote put back where it was, owned by the users it was owned by where they are
on this machine, each file written beside its place and moved there whole. The
daemon is stopped first, and started again brings every runtime back from its
state as it was, running where it ran. A state directory that holds runtimes
already is refused unless
.BR \-\-force ;
a path outside those the archive's manifest names refuses the archive.
.RS
.PP
.nf
systemctl stop socketley
socketley restore /var/backups/socketley\-2026\-10\-25.tar.gz
systemctl start socketley
.fi
.RE
.TP
.BI "import " "name " "[\-\-restart] " "json"
Update a runtime's configuration from a JSON string. With no
.I json
argument the document is read from standard input instead, so
.B show
pipes straight into
.BR import :
.nf
socketley show name | socketley import name
.fi
That form has no command-line length limit, which the quoted one does.
Whitespace outside string literals is stripped before the document is sent,
so the pretty-printed output of
.B show
is accepted as-is. Accepts the same
fields as the interactive editor (see below). Fields marked
.B (read-only)
are ignored. Fields marked
.B (requires restart)
cannot be changed while the runtime is running.
Port, protocol, and UDP settings cannot be changed while running.
.PP
.B "A field the document does not mention is left alone."
Only what is named is applied, so a partial document changes only those fields.
Clearing one is written out rather than implied \(em
.BR """tls"": false" ,
.BR """cert_path"": """"" \(em because a document that simply omitted a field
used to CLEAR it, and a runtime configured for TLS could be handed
.B {"name":"x"}
and answer in cleartext afterwards with the command reporting success.
.PP
.B import
is also the fuller of the two ways to reconfigure a runtime.
.B edit
takes a subset of the flags: it has no cache\-specific ones at all, and no
.BR \-\-backend " or " \-\-lua .
Anything it refuses with "unknown flag" can still be set by dumping the
document with
.BR show ,
changing the field and importing it back.
.PP
A JSON document is held to the same rules the flags are: on every runtime type
.BR tls ,
.BR cert_path ,
.BR key_path " and " ca_path
are accepted as a complete set and refused as an incomplete one. On a
.B cache
a document setting
.B tls
alongside
.B replicate_target
is rejected outright and nothing is applied, because replication is not
encrypted (see
.BR LIMITATIONS ).
.BR mesh_client_ca ,
.B mesh_client_cert
and
.B mesh_client_key
belong to a proxy alone.
.PP
Clearing any of them is written out and never implied, the same rule the
paragraph above states for every field: a document that simply omits a field
leaves the runtime's own value in place. That is how a runtime restored from an
older state file with certificate paths it can no longer read is made startable
again \(em by naming them empty, not by deleting the lines:
.nf
socketley show name > cfg.json
# set the TLS and mesh paths to "" in an editor, do not delete the lines
socketley import name "$(cat cfg.json)"
.fi
.PP
Deleting them used to clear them, and that is what changed: a runtime
configured for TLS could be handed
.B {"name":"x"}
and answer in cleartext afterwards, with the command reporting success.
.PP
A document that leaves out
.B name
and
.B type
is about the runtime the command names, with that runtime's type, so one field
is changed with one key:
.nf
socketley import ui '{"domain":"storage"}'
.fi
.SS Edit: JSON Configuration Fields
The interactive editor opens a JSON file with the following fields.
Fields marked
.B (read-only)
are ignored on import. Fields marked
.B (requires restart)
cannot be changed while the runtime is running \(em stop the runtime first.
.PP
.B Identity fields:
.TS
l lw(56).
name	Runtime name \fB(requires restart)\fR
id	6-char hex identifier (read-only, ignored on import)
type	T{
.ad l
Runtime type: server, client, proxy, cache, storage, dns, sync, idp, db, mqtt, mail, tunnel (read-only, ignored on import)
T}
was_running	Internal daemon state (ignored on import)
.TE
.PP
.B Common fields (all types):
.TS
l l lw(42).
Field	Type	Description
_
port	number	T{
.ad l
Listener/connection port \fB(requires restart)\fR
T}
listen	string	T{
.ad l
"unix:/abs/path.sock" \(em an AF_UNIX listener instead of a port \fB(server/proxy/cache; requires restart)\fR
T}
log_file	string	State transition log file path
write_file	string	Message write file path
lua_script	string	Lua script path (absolute)
bash_output	bool	Output messages to stdout
bash_prefix	bool	Prefix messages with [name]
bash_timestamp	bool	Prefix messages with [HH:MM:SS]
max_connections	number	Max concurrent connections (0 = unlimited)
rate_limit	number	T{
.ad l
Messages/sec per connection (0 = unlimited)
T}
global_rate_limit	number	T{
.ad l
Messages/sec across all connections (0 = unlimited)
T}
idle_timeout	number	T{
.ad l
Close idle connections after N seconds (0 = disabled)
T}
drain	bool	T{
.ad l
Graceful shutdown (flush writes before close)
T}
tls	bool	T{
.ad l
Terminate TLS on the listener \fB(server, proxy)\fR; dial TLS \fB(client)\fR
T}
cert_path	string	T{
.ad l
PEM certificate \fB(server, proxy; needs tls)\fR; on a \fBclient\fR, the certificate it presents
T}
key_path	string	T{
.ad l
PEM private key \fB(server, proxy; needs tls)\fR; on a \fBclient\fR, the key for cert_path
T}
ca_path	string	T{
.ad l
Client CA for mutual TLS \fB(server, needs tls)\fR; on a \fBproxy\fR, the trust store for TLS backends; on a \fBclient\fR, the trust store for its target
T}
target	string	Client target host:port \fB(requires restart)\fR
cache_name	string	Connected cache runtime name
group	string	Group tag for dynamic proxy discovery
.TE
.PP
.B Server fields:
.TS
l l l.
Field	Type	Description
_
mode	string	"inout", "in", "out", or "master"
udp	bool	Use UDP instead of TCP \fB(requires restart)\fR
master_pw	string	Static password for master mode
send_to	string	"sender", "others", "master", comma separated
http_dir	string	Static file root, as \fB\-\-http\fR
http_cache	bool	Hold those files in memory, as \fB\-\-http\-cache\fR
upstreams	array	Upstream addresses, as \fB\-\-upstream\fR
.TE
.PP
.B Client fields:
.TS
l l l.
Field	Type	Description
_
mode	string	"inout", "in", or "out"
udp	bool	Use UDP instead of TCP \fB(requires restart)\fR
reconnect	number	Auto-reconnect max attempts (-1=off, 0=infinite)
.TE
.PP
.B Proxy fields:
.TS
l l lw(42).
Field	Type	Description
_
protocol	string	"http" or "tcp" \fB(requires restart)\fR
strategy	string	"round-robin", "random", or "lua"
backends	array	T{
.ad l
List of backend addresses ["ip:port", "@group", ...]
T}
health_check	number	T{
.ad l
0 disabled, 1 TCP connect, 2 HTTP GET \(em the number \fB\-\-health\-check\fR stored
T}
health_interval	number	T{
.ad l
Health check interval in seconds (default 5)
T}
health_path	string	HTTP health check path (default "/health")
health_threshold	number	Failures to mark unhealthy (default 3)
circuit_threshold	number	Errors to open circuit (default 5)
circuit_timeout	number	Seconds before half-open (default 30)
backend_timeout	number	T{
.ad l
Seconds for the first response byte (default 2, 0 = off)
T}
retry_count	number	Retry count (0 = disabled)
retry_all	bool	Also retry non-idempotent methods
mesh_client_ca	string	T{
.ad l
CA for certificates clients present \fB(needs tls)\fR
T}
mesh_client_cert	string	T{
.ad l
Client certificate presented to backends
T}
mesh_client_key	string	Its private key (both or neither)
.TE
.PP
These are the names in the document, which are not in every case the names of
the flags or of the Lua configuration keys that set the same things: the
document spells them
.BR retry_count ,
.BR mesh_client_ca ,
.B mesh_client_cert
and
.BR mesh_client_key ,
where
.B \-\-retry
and
.B \-\-client\-ca
are the flags and
.B retry
and
.B client_ca
are the config-file keys. There is no
.B sidecar
field: it is a flag and a config key that sets several of the fields above, and
nothing writes or reads it in a state document.
.PP
