.PP
.B Example JSON (server):
.nf
{
    "name": "api",
    "id": "a1b2c3",
    "type": "server",
    "port": 9000,
    "was_running": true,
    "lua_script": "/home/user/handler.lua",
    "max_connections": 100,
    "rate_limit": 1000,
    "mode": "inout"
}
.fi
.TP
.B settings \fR[\fB\-a\fR|\fB\-\-all\fR] [\fB\-j\fR|\fB\-\-json\fR]
Every
.B SOCKETLEY_*
setting as the daemon and the processes it starts see it: its value, and
whether that came from the daemon's environment, from the
.B settings
table of its configuration file (see
.BR "Daemon Configuration" ),
or is the default, with what it is for. The plain listing holds the knobs an
operator may turn;
.B \-\-all
adds the test suite's hooks, the variables a command reads from its own
environment, and the ones the daemon sets for what it starts, each marked in a
.B FOR
column.
.B \-\-json
prints the same rows as an array of objects with
.BR name ,
.BR env ,
.BR value ,
.BR from ,
.B for
and
.BR meaning .
Every variable under
.B ENVIRONMENT
is one row.
.TP
.B dashboard
Print the URL of the built-in web dashboard. Requires
.B daemon.metrics_port
to be set in the daemon configuration file (see
.BR "Daemon Configuration" ).
The dashboard provides a live-updating view of all runtimes, connections,
and throughput. It listens on loopback and answers a Host other than this
machine's 403, so a page whose name was rebound to 127.0.0.1 reads nothing; a
request that carries
.BR Via ,
which no page can set, is a proxy's and is served whatever its Host, so a proxy
with
.B \-\-domain
and
.B \-\-auth
can put it on the internet. Its pages are never framed, sniffed, cached or
named in a Referer. Example output:
.nf
Dashboard: http://localhost:9100/
.fi
.TP
.B ls \fR[\fB\-\-json\fR | \fB\-j\fR | \fB\-\-format json\fR] [\fB\-\-silent\fR | \fB\-s\fR] [\fIcolumns\fR]
List all runtimes (all states). Docker-style output with columns:
ID, NAME, TYPE, PORT, CONN (connections), OWNED BY, STATUS, CREATED, GROUP.
With \fB\-\-json\fR or \fB\-\-format json\fR, outputs a JSON array of runtime objects.
With \fB\-\-silent\fR or \fB\-s\fR, prints only runtime names (one per line).
.IP
The STATUS column of a running runtime is its uptime, followed by two notes
when they apply.
.BI ( N " restarts)"
means the daemon found the worker dead and forked a replacement that many
times; the uptime is the replacement's, and a runtime holding state in memory
\(em a cache above all \(em lost it at every one of them. The JSON form
reports the same number in
.BR restarts .
.IP
.B NOT RESPONDING
means the worker process is alive and has said nothing for fifteen seconds
while spending at least half a core. A worker publishes on every completion, so
one doing any work at all is heard from within a quarter second: silence means
either idle, which costs no CPU, or stuck inside a callback, which costs a
core. Only the second is reported, and it is only reported \(em nothing is
killed, because a script deliberately computing for a long time looks the same
from outside the process. A worker stuck without spending CPU, blocked rather
than spinning, is indistinguishable from an idle one and is not reported.
.IP
The JSON form carries a
.B listen
field beside
.BR port :
empty for an ordinary runtime, and
.BI unix: /path/to.sock
for a server started with
.BR \-\-listen .
The PORT column shows
.B \-
for one of those, since it has none.
.IP
When more than one daemon is running, a line under the table says how many and
which one answered \(em including when the table is empty, because a listing
from the wrong daemon looks exactly like an idle machine. It is printed only for
the default table:
.BR \-\-json ,
.B \-s
and the column selectors are what scripts read and carry nothing extra.
.B ps
does the same.
.RS
.PP
.B Column selectors
\(em pass one or more to show only those columns:
.BR \-\-id ,
.BR \-\-name ,
.BR \-\-type ,
.BR \-\-port ,
.BR \-\-status ,
.BR \-\-conn ,
.BR \-\-owner ,
.BR \-\-created ,
.BR \-\-group .
.RE
.TP
.B ps \fR[\fB\-a\fR | \fB\-\-all\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR | \fB\-j\fR | \fB\-\-format json\fR] [\fB\-\-silent\fR | \fB\-s\fR] [\fIcolumns\fR]
List only running runtimes. Same format as
.BR ls .
With \fB\-\-json\fR, outputs a JSON array (includes uptime field).
With \fB\-\-silent\fR or \fB\-s\fR, prints only runtime names (one per line).
.RS
.PP
A runtime that did not come up is in state
.BR failed ,
and
.B ps
does not list it \(em so a runtime that failed to start produces no row here,
and if nothing else is running, no output at all. That is the state you go
looking for when something did not start, so look for it with
.B ps \-a
or
.BR ls .
.PP
.B \-a\fR, \fB\-\-all
.br
List runtimes in every state, not only
.BR running .
The STATUS column then carries the state
.RB ( Created ,
.BR Stopped ,
.BR Failed )
in place of an uptime, and \fB\-\-json\fR reports it in the \fBstatus\fR field.
Bare
.B ps
is unchanged, so anything parsing it keeps seeing running runtimes only.
.PP
.B Column selectors:
.BR \-\-id ,
.BR \-\-name ,
.BR \-\-type ,
.BR \-\-port ,
.BR \-\-uptime ,
.BR \-\-conn ,
.BR \-\-owner ,
.BR \-\-created .
.PP
.B \-\-status
and
.B \-\-uptime
are two names for one column, headed
.BR UPTIME .
It holds an uptime for a running runtime and the state
.RB ( Created ,
.BR Stopped ,
.BR Failed )
for anything else, so under
.B \-a
a row can read
.B Failed
beneath a header saying
.BR UPTIME .
That is the same column
.B ls
heads
.BR STATUS ;
the header is left alone because scripts select on it.
.RE
.TP
.BI "owner " "name"
Show ownership info for a runtime: parent name, children list, and
on_parent_stop policy (stop or remove).
.TP
.BI "cluster " "[subcommand]"
Inspect the cluster. Requires a running daemon with cluster mode enabled
(via
.B daemon \-\-name
+
.BR "daemon \-\-cluster" ).
The cluster directory is read from the daemon automatically.
.RS
.TP
.B ls
List all daemons in the cluster with runtime counts and heartbeat status.
.TP
.B ps
List all runtimes across the entire cluster.
.TP
.BI "group " "name"
Show all members of a specific group across the cluster.
.TP
.BI "show " "daemon\-name"
Print a daemon's raw cluster JSON file.
.TP
.B stats
Show cluster summary: daemon counts, runtime totals, group membership.
.TP
.B watch
Continuously refresh the cluster view every 2 seconds (Ctrl+C to stop).
.RE
.TP
.B ping
Ask the daemon to identify itself. Prints
.BI "socketley\-daemon " "version pid dev" : "ino path"
for the daemon serving the socket, where the last two fields name the
executable it is running \(em its device and inode, and its
.I /proc/self/exe
link, which carries
.B (deleted)
once the file it was started from has been replaced. The path is last because a
path may contain spaces. A daemon built before those fields existed answers with
the version and pid alone, and is not treated as a different build for it.
.RS
.PP
Every command runs this first, before it sends anything: a
.BR connect (2)
that succeeds only proves something is listening on the path, not that the
thing listening is a daemon, and a command written to a stranger is a command
delivered to a stranger. A path held by something that does not answer this is
refused, with nothing sent to it.
.RE
.TP
.B help \fR| \fB\-\-help\fR | \fB\-h\fR
Print help. What it prints depends on where it is printing to.
.RS
.PP
On a
.BR terminal ,
it opens the interactive browser: a menu, not a page, reached by number and
taking no topic argument \(em everything is behind the menu.
.PP
Anywhere
.BR else \(em
a pipe, a redirect, a CI step \(em it prints a plain\-text summary of the
commands and exits
.BR 0 .
Neither standard input nor standard output being a terminal is what decides
it; there is no flag, because
.B socketley \-\-help
is what a person types either way and only the destination differs. A menu
drawn into a pipe is a menu nobody can answer, and it used to arrive with a
screen\-clear escape at the front of the captured output and an exit status
of 1 behind it \(em this project's code for bad input, reported to a caller
whose only mistake was asking for help.
.PP
Does not require, and does not start, a daemon. The browser is
.I man/socketley\-help.sh
under the source tree the binary was built in, and
.I /usr/share/socketley/socketley\-help.sh
otherwise \(em nearest first, so a binary built in a tree prints that tree's
help rather than an installed copy from another version. If neither is
readable the command says so and exits 2, the installation's fault and not
bad input. This page is the reference and that
browser is the tour \(em they cover the same ground and are kept in step with
each other.
.RE
.TP
.B version \fR| \fB\-\-version\fR | \fB\-v\fR
Print the socketley version number and exit. Does not require, and does not
start, a daemon. Standard output is the version number in every case; anything
about the daemon on the socket goes to standard error, so
.B $(socketley version)
is unaffected by all of it.
.RS
.PP
Reported there: a daemon whose version differs, and \(em since the version is
one compile-time literal that every build of a release carries \(em a daemon
still executing a file that has since been replaced at the path it was started
from. That second one is the ordinary case of rebuilding while a daemon holds
its runtimes, and the reason a new flag can be rejected by a daemon reporting
the same version as the binary that has it.
.PP
It is reported only when the daemon and this CLI name the same executable path.
Running the CLI from a copy elsewhere on disk says nothing: a copy is not
evidence about anybody's build, and comparing inodes across different paths
would report one on every command.
.RE
.TP
.BI "\-\-lua " "lua_file"
Load Lua orchestration configuration. Optionally configures daemon name
and cluster mode, then creates multiple runtimes from a single config file.
.SH DAEMON FLAGS
These flags configure the running daemon via IPC commands. The daemon is
auto-started if not already running. One daemon serves a socket: it holds a
lock on the socket's path with
.B .lock
appended for as long as it runs, and a second one started meanwhile \(em by
hand, or by a command's auto-start \(em waits until the first answers and exits
0, or exits 1 after ten seconds of a holder that never does.
.TP
.BI "\-\-name " "name" " | \-n " "name"
Set this daemon's identity. Required before enabling cluster mode.
Can be changed at any time; if cluster mode is active, the cluster file
is republished under the new name (old name is removed).
.TP
.BI "\-\-cluster " "dir"
Enable cluster mode on the given shared directory. Each daemon publishes
its runtime state there every 2 seconds and scans for peer daemons. Proxy
.B @group
backends automatically discover runtimes on remote daemons.
In Docker Compose, mount a shared volume at this path.
The advertised host address is set automatically via
.BR gethostname (3).
Requires
.B \-\-name
to be set first.
.SH COMMON FLAGS
These flags work with all runtime types:
.TP
.BI "\-p " "port"
Set listener port (server, proxy, cache, storage, dns) or target port (client).
.BI "\-p " address : port
sets
.B \-\-bind
as well.
.TP
.BI "\-\-bind " address
The IPv4 address the listener port is bound on, alone, such as
.B 127.0.0.1
for a runtime only a reverse proxy on the same host reaches; without it, or with
.BR 0.0.0.0 ,
every address. Changed while stopped, by
.BR edit ,
or by
.BR import 's
.B bind
field, and named in
.BR "ls \-\-json" .
An address that is not one is refused when it is given; one the host does not
have fails the start.
.TP
.B \-s
Start immediately after creation.
.TP
.B \-\-test
Dry run: the type, the name and every flag are checked, and then nothing is
kept. No runtime is left in
.BR ls ,
no state file is written and the name is free for the
.B create
that follows. It used to keep the runtime -- unstarted, unsaved, and holding
the name until the daemon restarted -- which made the real
.B create
answer "runtime already exists", and made
.B start
on it bind a port no state file would ever bring back.
.IP
It does not bind the port, and a
.B \-\-lua
script is loaded \(em which is what validates it, and runs its top level \(em but
.B on_start
is not called, because nothing is started. What it answers is whether this
command line is one
.B create
accepts.
.TP
.BI "\-\-log " "file"
Append one line per state transition to
.IR file :
.IP
.EX
2026\-09\-10 11:48:22 api created
2026\-09\-10 11:48:22 api running
2026\-09\-10 11:48:31 api stopped
.EE
.IP
Dated as well as clocked, because this file outlives a day, unlike the daemon's
own log. Created with mode 0600 and opened for append, so a restart adds to the
record rather than replacing it.
.IP
The first line is the state the runtime was in when the flag was given, which
is how
.B created
appears at all: it is the state a runtime is constructed in, before any flag
has been parsed. Giving
.B \-\-log
by
.B edit
to a runtime that is already serving therefore opens the file with a
.B running
line, which is where the record starts rather than a transition anybody made.
.IP
What is recorded is the state
.B ls
reports, which is the daemon's. A runtime lives in its own process and that
process keeps its own copy of the same value; both carry the path, and both
writing would double every line. One consequence is visible and worth
expecting: an out-of-process runtime whose worker refuses its configuration
records
.BR created ,
.B running
and then
.BR failed ,
because
.B running
is what the daemon had asked for and reported for the moment before the worker
answered.
.IP
The path must be absolute. A relative one would be resolved against the
.I daemon's
working directory \(em wherever it was started, and
.B /
under systemd \(em so it is refused rather than written somewhere the operator
would not think to look.
.IP
A path that cannot be opened or written is reported once, at
.BR ERROR ,
and then not again.
.TP
.BI "\-w " "file"
Write every received message to file, one line each, in the bytes they arrived
in \(em this is the file half of
.BR \-b ,
and it is written at the same point in the runtime, where the message is
counted.
.IP
The file is created with mode 0600 on the first message and not at the start,
so a runtime nothing is sent leaves nothing behind, and it is opened for append,
so a restart adds to it rather than truncating it. Under
.BI \-\-shards " N"
the N workers share the one path: an append this size is atomic on a regular
file, so the lines interleave without tearing.
.IP
Unlike
.BR \-b ,
the bytes are not scrubbed of control characters. That scrubbing exists to stop
a peer driving the operator's terminal with an escape sequence, and a file is
not a terminal.
.IP
The path must be absolute, for
.BR \-\-log 's
reason above.
.IP
.B \-w
takes effect on a runtime that is already serving, so
.BI "edit " "name" " \-w other.log"
moves the writing without a restart. A path that cannot be opened or written is
reported once, at
.BR ERROR ,
and then not again \(em a runtime under load would otherwise put one line in
the log per message through it.
.IP
The files written by
.B \-w
and
.B \-\-log
are held open once written, so a file renamed away keeps receiving the lines;
rotate them with logrotate's
.BR copytruncate .
.TP
.BI "\-\-lua " "script"
Attach Lua configuration script with callbacks. A script that does not load
refuses the command with exit 1, and the reason is printed \(em the parse error,
or whichever
.B filters
or
.B routes
rule it broke \(em rather than only the fact.
.TP
.B \-b
Output received messages to stdout (bash mode).
.TP
.B \-bp
Output messages with runtime name prefix: [name] message
.TP
.B \-bt
Output messages with timestamp: [HH:MM:SS] message
.TP
.B \-bpt ", " \-btp
Output messages with both timestamp and prefix: [HH:MM:SS] [name] message.
Both spellings are accepted and mean the same thing; only
.B \-bpt
was documented, so
.B \-btp
worked and could not be looked up.
.TP
.BI "\-\-max\-connections " "n"
Limit maximum concurrent connections. New connections beyond this limit
are immediately closed. 0 means unlimited (default). Alias:
.BR \-\-max\-conn .
.TP
.BI "\-\-read\-buffer " "size"
How much may be accumulated FROM one connection before it is refused, with
K/M/G suffixes. Default 1M. The inbound twin of
.BR \-\-write\-buffer ,
and like it, every runtime has one.
.IP
The largest single value inside that buffer is half of it, which leaves room
for the framing around it. So the default gives a cache a 512K ceiling on any
one value \(em and those were already the two numbers this was compiled with,
1 MiB of accumulation and 512 KB of value, so the halving is named here rather
than introduced. An unconfigured runtime accepts exactly what it accepted
before.
.IP
Raise it for a cache holding documents rather than fields: Redis's own
.B proto\-max\-bulk\-len
defaults to 512M, a thousand times this. Lower it for a runtime facing the
open internet, where the buffer is memory a client controls. Values under 64K
are raised to it rather than refused, because the derived value ceiling would
otherwise be under what an ordinary form post or JSON document needs.
.IP
It is per connection, and nothing bounds the sum but
.BR \-\-max\-connections ,
which defaults to 0 \(em no limit. Measured on a cache with 200 connections:
one that has sent nothing costs about 13 KB, and one holding an unfinished
command costs the whole of what it has sent, up to this buffer. So the figure
to size against is
.BR \-\-read\-buffer " \(mu " \-\-max\-connections ,
and at both defaults that product is unbounded: the ceiling is the worker's
descriptor limit, over a million here. An unfinished command is not a
malformed one and nothing refuses it \(em only its size is bounded, and only
per connection.
.IP
A value past the ceiling is answered
.B "\-ERR value is larger than this cache accepts (limit N bytes)"
and the connection is closed, which is what Redis does for a bulk length it
will not take \(em the body was never read, so there is no framed position to
continue from. It is deliberately NOT
.BR "\-ERR protocol error" :
the command was framed correctly and only its value was too big, and a client
library told its framing is broken reconnects and sends the same value again.
A length that genuinely does not parse still gets the protocol error.
.IP
One caveat, measured: the refusal is written and then the connection closed, so
a client still sending when that happens receives an RST and Linux discards the
queued reply. Overshoots up to about two megabytes read the reason; one eight
times the ceiling does not. Raising the bound is the answer for a deployment
that needs the larger values.
.IP
Editable on a running runtime, and the change takes at once: the bound is
compared on every read and the value ceiling is derived at each parse, so
nothing holds the old number.
.TP
.BI "\-\-write\-buffer " "size"
How much may be owed to ONE connection before the runtime stops waiting for it,
with K/M/G suffixes. Default 8M. Every runtime has a write queue, so this
applies to all four types \(em a client's queue is the one that fills when its
server stops reading.
.IP
This is arithmetic rather than a preference. The queue is per connection, so
the bound multiplies:
.IP
.nf
100 streams        800 MB
1,000 streams        8 GB     (a box with 11 GiB serves about this many)
10,000 streams      80 GB     not possible
.fi
.IP
8M is right for a few large transfers and wrong by about sixty for many
concurrent streams; nginx serves ten thousand with 32\(hy64 KB apiece. Nothing
could previously say which shape a runtime was, because these were two
compile\(hytime constants. Measured, 400 sends of 64 KB into a peer that is
not reading: a default runtime takes 129 of them and one at
.B "\-\-write\-buffer 256K"
takes 5.
.IP
It is a throughput knob as well, and which direction helps is the same question
read from the other side. A 1 MiB chunked response, against a C floor that puts
the same bytes on the wire with one
.BR write (2)
per chunk, in microseconds over that floor per request:
.IP
.nf
                8 streams    64 streams    RSS
128K             +75.7        +139.0        45 MB
256K             +51.6        +128.1        52 MB
1M               +17.6        +218.1        74 MB
8M (default)     +27.3        +234.0       106 MB
.fi
.IP
So a deep queue wins with few streams and loses with many: at 64 streams,
dropping the default to
.B 256K
is 28 per cent more throughput for 22 per cent less CPU per request, and at 8
streams the same move costs 24 per cent.
.IP
The reason is the cache and not the queue, and the two settings measured against
each other are the proof: same code, same bytes, the same number of copies, one
number changed. CPU per MiB served at 64 streams, with the profile taken from the
same window as the rate so that a component divides inside one run:
.IP
.nf
                total   memmove   kernel copy   page clearing
8M (default)     315      103          87            29
256K             254       40          43            30
.fi
.IP
Both copies halve. Ours is one
.B memmove
per byte either way and the kernel's
.B _copy_from_iter
is one copy per byte either way, so neither does less work \(em the bytes are
closer. L2 is 4 MiB per core here and L3 is 16 MiB shared; at 64 streams the
default owes up to about 69 MB at once, out of both, while
.B 256K
owes about 8 MB and stays in. A C floor doing the same one copy per byte, into a
buffer it reuses, spends 29 and 21 on those two components, so what is left once
the working set is accounted for is small.
.IP
This was measured the wrong way first and the wrong way is worth naming: a share
from one profile multiplied by a total from another run. Each share was sound
\(em 32.7 and 27.6 per cent of a core \(em but the core was doing different
amounts of work in the two runs, so the product was a figure with no referent.
.IP
The sweep also measured
.B 64K
and got a figure 0.6 to 0.9 per cent from
.BR 128K ,
which is right: a value under 128K is raised to it, so those two arms are one
buffer measured twice, and that spread is the harness repeating itself rather
than a difference.
.IP
One number and not two: the pacing watermark that
.B on_writable
fires on is derived from this and never set beside it. A pair could be set
inconsistently, and a watermark above the bound is a runtime that paces only
after it has already closed the connection. A script reads the derived value as
.BR self.watermark() ,
which is a call rather than a field because the answer moves.
.IP
It is half the bound while the runtime is keeping up, and when the runtime is
holding more than 8 MiB in total it becomes that budget divided by the live
connections instead \(em never above the configured half, never below 64 KB,
and back to the half once the backlog has been gone for 50 milliseconds. What
the operator sets is the bound, which is memory and decides when a connection is
closed; how deep a producer is allowed to run before it pauses is a cache
question, because these bytes are written here and then read again by the kernel
copying them into a socket.
.IP
Measured on a 1 MiB chunked response, seven interleaved passes, responses
byte\(hyidentical in every arm: 4,480 responses a second at 16 streams against
5,273, and 3,634 at 64 against 4,221 \(em and unchanged inside the spread at 8
and at 128. Dividing by the connection count without asking whether anything was
actually backed up was tried first and cost 19 per cent at 8 streams, where the
queues never fill and the only effect is stopping a producer four times as often.
.IP
This does not bound memory. The peak is still the bound times the connections
that reach it, because a bound that shrank under a live connection would close it
for a backlog it had been allowed to build. For many clients at once that is
still this flag's own job: 60 MB at
.B "\-\-write\-buffer 256K"
against 146 at the default, on the same load.
.IP
Values under 128K are raised to it rather than refused: the watermark is half
the bound and the largest single entry these queues build is 64 KB, so a
smaller bound is a runtime that cannot send one message. Editable on a running
runtime, and the change reaches the script at once \(em the bound is read on
every send rather than cached anywhere.
.TP
.BI "\-\-rate\-limit " "n"
Limit messages per second per connection using a token bucket algorithm.
Excess messages are silently dropped (server); a
.B cache
answers
.BR "error: rate limited" ,
since its protocol has a way to say so; on a
.B proxy
the connection is closed instead, because a request dropped in silence leaves
its client waiting for a response that is never coming \(em a hang rather than a
refusal. The bucket starts full, so a connection may spend a whole second's
allowance at once and then waits for it to refill. 0 means unlimited (default).
.IP
Not accepted on a
.BR client ,
together with
.B \-\-max\-connections
and
.BR \-\-idle\-timeout .
All three describe inbound traffic, and a client opens one connection outward
and accepts none, so there is nothing for any of them to act on. They were
taken and reported before \(em
.B show
answered
.B """rate_limit"": 5"
on a runtime with no rate-limiting code at all, which tells an operator a
control is in force that does not exist. Refused at
.BR create ", " edit " and " import
alike; import writes the field without passing the flag parser, so a guard in
one place is not a guard.
.IP
Refused rather than implemented, because each could be given a meaning and each
meaning is a decision. An outbound throttle has to say what becomes of a message
that may not go yet \(em queue it and report success for something that leaves
in ten seconds, fail the send, or drop it. An idle timeout has to say what
happens after the close, since a client reconnects the moment its connection is
dropped.
.IP
On a proxy a token is charged per read completion rather than per HTTP request,
which is the same event
.B \-\-global\-rate\-limit
is charged on and the same one
.B total_messages
counts; a client that pipelines four requests into one segment therefore pays
one token.
.IP
A
.B client
refuses the flag: it limits what arrives, and a client has nothing arriving
from connections of its own.
.TP
.BI "\-\-global\-rate\-limit " "n"
Limit total messages per second across all connections using a shared
token bucket. Excess messages are silently dropped (server) or responded
with an error (cache). 0 means unlimited (default).
.IP
Refused on a client for
.BR \-\-rate\-limit 's
reason: it caps the total across all inbound connections, and a client has one
outbound connection.
.TP
.BI "\-\-idle\-timeout " "duration"
Close connections that have been idle (no data received) for longer than
this. A sweep timer runs every 30 seconds to
check for idle connections. 0 means disabled (default).
A bare number is seconds; a suffix of
.BR s ", " m ", " h ", " d " or " w
spells seconds, minutes, hours, days or weeks
.RB ( 300 " and " 5m " both work)."
.IP
This closes connections that are
.IR quiet ,
which is not the same as closing ones that are
.IR dead ,
and it is not needed for the second. Every accepted TCP connection is given
TCP keepalive at 60/10/3 \(em a minute of silence, then three probes ten
seconds apart \(em so a peer that loses power, or vanishes behind a NAT that
forgot it, is reaped without any timeout being set. A websocket that says
nothing for an hour is quiet and alive, and keepalive leaves it alone; only
.B \-\-idle\-timeout
closes that one. A unix listener is not given keepalive, having no peer that
can disappear silently.
.TP
.B \-\-drain
Enable graceful shutdown. When stopping, the runtime closes its listener
but flushes all pending write queues before closing connections.
.IP
Refused on a client, which has no listener and no inbound connections: what a
client owes its peer on the way down is a separate question from this one, and
the flag did nothing there.
.TP
.BI "\-\-cpus " "set"
The CPUs this runtime's I/O may run on. Accepts
.BR max ,
a range such as
.BR 0\-3 ,
or a list such as
.B 0,1,2,3
whose elements may themselves be ranges
.RB ( 0\-1,3 ).
Default
.BR max ,
which applies no pinning at all \(em not a mask of the CPUs that are online now,
so a CPU that comes online later is still available to the loop.
.IP
A CPU this machine does not have, a descending range, or a set that comes out
empty is refused when the runtime is created. It is never quietly rounded up to
.BR max :
a config written for a sixteen-core machine and deployed on a four-core one
would otherwise run nowhere near where its author believes, and every
measurement taken afterwards would be measuring something else.
.IP
A ring's CPU set is fixed by the kernel when the ring is created, and a ring
belongs to a process, not to a runtime: every runtime inside one daemon shares
that daemon's ring. So this flag states what a runtime requires, and
.B create
refuses a requirement the ring it would run on does not meet, naming the
.B socketley daemon
flag or the
.B SOCKETLEY_CPUS
setting that would satisfy it. It cannot be changed with
.BR edit .
The value in force is reported by
.BR "socketley stats" .
.IP
It is persisted with the runtime, and the same refusal is made again when a
persisted runtime is restored: a daemon restarted without the setting that
satisfied a runtime's requirement leaves that runtime
.BR failed ,
with the reason in the daemon log, rather than starting it on a ring that does
not meet it and reporting the ring it got. A runtime that asked for nothing
records nothing and is never refused for it.
.TP
.B \-\-tls
.TQ
.BI "\-\-cert " "file"
.TQ
.BI "\-\-key " "file"
.TQ
.BI "\-\-ca " "file"
.B "All runtime types."
Encrypt this runtime's transport. What that means depends on whether the role
listens or dials.
.PP
On a
.BR server ", " proxy " or " cache ,
TLS terminates on the runtime's own listening port, and
.B \-\-tls
requires both
.BI \-\-cert " file"
(a PEM certificate, chain allowed) and
.BI \-\-key " file"
(its PEM private key) \(em a listener has to have something to terminate TLS
with.
.PP
A
.B cache
terminates TLS exactly as a server does, and everything above the record layer
is unchanged: RESP is still auto-detected from the first byte of the
.I decrypted
stream, so a Redis client speaks the protocol it always did with TLS switched
on, and the line protocol works on the same port. This is what allows a cache
to be reached across a network it does not trust.
.PP
On a
.BR client ,
TLS is dialled rather than terminated, and
.B \-\-tls
on its own is the complete configuration: there is no listening port to hold a
certificate, and the peer is verified against the system trust store by
default.
.BI \-\-cert " file"
and
.BI \-\-key " file"
are optional there and mean the certificate this client
.I presents
when the peer asks for one (mutual TLS); half a pair is refused, because it
would present nothing while the flags said otherwise.
.PP
.BI \-\-ca " file"
means one thing on each role, because only some of them verify a peer they
dialled. On a
.BR server " or " cache
it enables mutual TLS: a client must then present a certificate signed by that
CA, and one that does not is rejected during the handshake. On a
.B proxy
it is the trust store used to verify the certificate a
.B backend
presents \(em the proxy's own listener asks for client certificates with
.BI \-\-client\-ca " file"
instead. On a
.B client
it is the trust store for the peer named by
.BR \-t ;
without it the system store is used.
See
.B "mTLS Flags"
under
.BR "PROXY FLAGS" .
.PP
A dialled connection \(em a client's target, a proxy's backend \(em is verified
on two counts, and neither can be turned off. The chain must be signed by
something in the trust store, and the certificate must be valid for the host
that was dialled: the name in
.B \-t
for a client, matched against the certificate's DNS SAN, or against its IP SAN
for an address literal. The name checked is always the one configured, never
one read back out of the certificate that arrived. TLS 1.2 is the minimum
version. There is deliberately no skip-verify option: a TLS client that does
not verify buys encryption against an eavesdropper and nothing at all against
somebody in the path, while reporting itself as encrypted either way.
.PP
A listener speaks TLS 1.2 and 1.3. Over 1.2 it offers only ECDHE key exchange
with AEAD ciphers, Mozilla's intermediate list, and it resumes a session from
its own cache rather than from a ticket, whose key lives as long as the process
and would open every session it ever sealed to whoever learned it.
.PP
Trust stores are read when the runtime starts, and a listener's certificate and
key are looked at again every two seconds: replaced on disk \(em by
.BR \-\-acme ,
certbot or a copy \(em they are taken up for the handshakes from then on,
without a restart and without dropping a connection, once both load and match.
A pair that does not is kept out, said once in the log, and the old one goes on
serving. The certificate file is sent with every certificate in it, so a CA's
intermediates go with the leaf. A
certificate that cannot be loaded, a key that does not match it, and any
incomplete combination (\c
.B \-\-tls
without a key pair on a listener, a key pair or a trust store without
.BR \-\-tls ,
half a client-certificate pair) are refused: the runtime goes to the
.B failed
state and nothing binds the port or dials the target. There is no fallback to
cleartext on any path. A client whose peer fails verification drops the socket
and re-dials on its usual backoff; it never retries in the clear.
.PP
These flags are refused on a
.B client
or a
.B server
combined with
.B \-\-udp
(that would need DTLS), and on a server combined with
.B \-\-upstream
(the upstream leg would still dial in cleartext). On a
.B cache
they are refused together with
.BI \-\-replicate " host:port"
\(em replication is not encrypted, so the pair would stream the whole dataset
in the clear off a port that reports itself as encrypted. See
.B TLS
under
.BR LIMITATIONS .
.TP
.BI "\-\-acme " "name" \fR[\fB,\fIname\fR...]
.TQ
.BI "\-\-acme\-email " "address"
.B "server, proxy, cache, storage."
A certificate for the names from an ACME CA, Let's Encrypt unless
.B SOCKETLEY_ACME_DIRECTORY
names another, issued and renewed by the runtime itself:
.B \-\-acme
turns on
.B \-\-tls
and keeps the certificate and its key in
.IR state\-dir /.acme/ name /,
for the first name. Until the CA has issued one, a self-signed certificate valid
for a week stands in, so the listener starts at once; the issued one replaces it
within seconds, and is renewed when it has less than thirty days left, tried
again after a minute, then twice as long each time up to six hours, with the
CA's reason in the log.
.B \-\-acme\-email
is the account's contact, where the CA writes before a certificate expires.
.IP
A runtime on port 443 proves its names over TLS-ALPN-01, on its own listener:
the CA's handshake asks for
.B acme\-tls/1
by the name, is shown a challenge certificate for it, and no other handshake is,
so a proxy on 443 alone is enough. Any other runtime proves them over HTTP-01: the CA asks
.BI http:// name /.well\-known/acme\-challenge/ token
on port 80, and every socketley
.BR server ,
.B proxy
and
.B storage
of the daemon answers that path on any port, for a token the daemon is waiting
on, before it routes, serves or runs a script, so a runtime on port 80 \(em the
proxy that redirects to HTTPS, say \(em is all it takes. Any other token goes
where the path would have gone, so a site's own files there, as certbot's
webroot writes them, are served as before. A name is a DNS name with a dot; an address is refused.
.IP
A wildcard,
.BR *.example.com ,
is proven over DNS-01, the one challenge that can: the answer goes to the
daemon's
.B dns
runtimes as the TXT record of
.BI _acme\-challenge. name
(see
.B Names runtimes are reached by
under
.BR "DNS FLAGS" ),
the CA is told to look
.B SOCKETLEY_ACME_DNS_WAIT
seconds later, and the record is gone once it has. It needs a
.B dns
runtime of the same daemon that is authoritative for the zone, and, if the zone
has secondaries, the time for them to take the new serial.
.B SOCKETLEY_ACME_CHALLENGE=dns\-01
proves every name that way, for a host whose port 80 is closed.
.IP
.nf
socketley create proxy web \-p 443 \-\-acme example.com,www.example.com \\
    \-\-acme\-email ops@example.com \-\-backend 127.0.0.1:8080 \-s
socketley create proxy plain \-p 80 \-\-backend 127.0.0.1:8080 \-s
.fi
.TP
.BR \-\-domain " [" \fIname\fR[\fB,\fIname\fR...]]
The names the runtime is reached by; with no name after it, the runtime's own
.RB ( "create server chat \-\-domain"
is reached as
.BR chat ).
They are names a
.B dns
runtime with
.B \-\-publish
answers for with this machine's addresses: a name without a dot is taken under
each zone it serves
.RB ( chat
is
.B chat.example.com
for
.BR example.com ),
one with a dot as written, and
.BI *. name
covers every name beneath. A runtime removed takes its names with it. On a
.B storage
runtime the flag is its own and also names the virtual hosts of its buckets
(see
.BR "STORAGE FLAGS" ),
which are published with the wildcard beneath it. The names of
.B \-\-acme
are published too.
.TP
.BI "\-g " "name"
.TQ
.BI "\-\-group " "name"
Assign the runtime to a named group. Groups enable dynamic proxy backend
discovery: a proxy using
.B @groupname
as a backend will route to all running members of that group at connection
time. New group members are discovered automatically without restarting
the proxy.
.IP
A member joins its group when it is actually listening, not when it is created.
For a runtime that runs in its own process this is a few milliseconds after
.B create
returns, because the port is bound in that process and the daemon publishes the
member once the worker reports it. Until then the member is absent from the
group rather than present and refusing connections \(em being routed to an
address that is not accepting yet would trip the routing proxy's circuit breaker
against a member that is merely young.
.TP
.BI "\-\-listen unix:" "/path/to.sock"
Listen on an AF_UNIX socket instead of a TCP port. Taken by every runtime that
listens \(em server, proxy, cache and storage; a client dials out and listens on
nothing, so on one the flag is refused with a pointer at
.BR "\-t unix:" ,
the connect side of the same socket. The other half of
.BI \-\-backend " unix:" /path/to.sock
under
.BR "PROXY FLAGS" ,
so a proxy and the backend it fronts can talk on one host without a loopback
TCP stack between them \(em or serve its own front door on one.
.RS
.PP
What it is worth, measured on a cache serving the same client over both, on
Linux 7.2.2: a round trip falls from 10.64 to 6.57 microseconds, and throughput
at pipeline depth 1 rises 2.2x to 2.4x. Most of that belongs to the kernel and
not to this daemon \(em Redis over its own unix socket gains 2.0x on the same
box, so any server on one machine can have it. TCP exists to make an unreliable
link reliable, and over loopback there is no link \(em but sequence numbers,
windows, retransmit timers, an skb per direction through IP, and netfilter
walking every packet all run regardless. On a host running Docker, netfilter
alone is 0.9 microseconds of that round trip. An AF_UNIX socket has none of it.
.PP
The saving is per \fImessage\fR and not per operation, so a pipelining client
sees much less of it: at depth 100 it is 0.016 microseconds an operation. That
is still 1.25x here, because at that depth the cache's own per-message work is
only about 8 microseconds and the saving is a quarter of it; Redis, whose
per-message work is nearer 32 microseconds, gains 1.02x and may as well not
bother.
.PP
Without
.B \-\-shards
a runtime on a unix socket is one core; with it, each shard binds a node of its
own, see below. Reach for it for a local sidecar.
.PP
.B Every unix stream socket on the data path has a 1 MiB send buffer.
That is each one accepted off a
.B \-\-listen unix:
node or a companion node, the one a proxy dials to a
.BI \-\-backend " unix:"
or to a local runtime's node, and the one a client dials with
.BR "\-t unix:" .
On a unix stream socket the sender's buffer is the whole of the flow control,
an accepted socket inherits nothing from its listener, and the kernel's default
is 208 KB. socketley asks
for 512 KiB, which the kernel doubles; the request is capped at
.IR net.core.wmem_max ,
which is 212992 on a stock kernel, so there the buffer is 416 KB. Measured on
one connection of pipelined 64 KB GETs: a reader that pays for each read (a
Python client reading 4 MiB at a time) went from 2.2 to 3.0 GB/s, with the
cache spending 41% less CPU per byte; a TCP proxy in front of a local cache,
reaching it over its node, 10% more; a reader that reads cheaply was already
at about 10 GB/s, bound by the cache's core, and is unchanged, as are small
requests. It is a ceiling and not an allocation: an idle connection holds
nothing in it, and a connection whose reader has stopped holds at most 1 MiB in
the kernel, where it held 208 KB.
.PP
Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-p ,
with
.B \-\-udp
(that would be a unix datagram socket, which is a different thing and is not
this), with
.B \-\-tls
(a unix socket is not on a network; the permissions on the node are what
restrict it), and on a cache with
.B \-\-cluster
(cluster members advertise ports out of a formula, and RESP has no way to write
a filesystem path as a node address). Each combination is refused at start with
the reason in the daemon
log, rather than one of the two winning quietly.
.PP
.B \-\-shards is not among them.
A sharded runtime binds one node per shard,
.IR name #i.sock,
which is the rule the daemon's companion nodes already follow. N processes
binding
.I one
path would unlink each other's node, and only the last to bind would stay
reachable; N paths are N listeners. See
.B Sharding
under
.B SERVER FLAGS
for which node a caller should pick, which is a real question on a cache and
not one on a server.
.PP
The path must be absolute and shorter than 108 bytes, which is what the kernel's
.I sun_path
holds. Both are refused when the flag is read \(em the kernel truncates a long
path silently, and a trimmed path names a different socket, very possibly a real
one belonging to something else.
.PP
.B Under \-\-shards the rule is applied to the longest node, not to the path as typed.
.RI name #15.sock
is three bytes longer than
.IR name .sock,
so a path that fits can still be a set that does not \(em and the shard that
did not fit would fail at its bind with the earlier shards already serving. It
is refused at configuration instead, naming the longest node rather than the
one that was typed.
.PP
.B The socket node is created 0600.
Connecting to a unix socket needs write permission on the node, so leaving that
to the umask means a daemon running under
.B umask 0
publishes a listener anybody on the machine can talk to. There is deliberately
no flag to widen it: a peer running as another user cannot connect, and the way
round that today is a parent directory the operator owns and controls.
.PP
The node is removed when the runtime stops. One left behind by a daemon that was
killed, or by a machine that lost power, is taken over on the next start \(em but
only after connecting to it to check that nobody is serving it. A path that
something IS serving is refused, so a second runtime cannot take a live socket
away from the first.
.PP
A server listening this way is reachable as a proxy backend both by its path and
by its runtime name. It is deliberately NOT offered as an
.BI @ group
member: a group's members are resolved to network addresses per request, and an
entry the group path cannot dial would take a turn of the round\-robin and answer
503 with it.
.PP
.nf
socketley create server app --listen unix:/run/app.sock --http /srv/www -s
socketley create proxy  gw  -p 8080 --backend app -s
socketley create cache  kv  --listen unix:/run/kv.sock -s   # redis-cli -s works
.fi
.RE
.SS The node beside the port
A
.B server
or an unsharded
.B cache
that listens on a TCP port also listens on an AF_UNIX node, created by
the daemon in a directory named after its own socket \(em
.IR <socket> .rt/ \(em
and named after the runtime. Named after the socket and not simply placed beside
it, because the default socket is
.I /tmp/socketley.sock
and every user on the machine shares that directory. Nothing has to be
configured for it, and nothing about the port changes: a runtime created with
.B \-p 9000
is reached on 9000 from anywhere, exactly as before. The node is additional,
never a replacement \(em nothing outside this machine can reach one.
.PP
It exists for one caller: another runtime on this machine. A proxy whose backend
is written as a
.I runtime name
takes it. So does one whose backend is written as an
.I address
\(em but only while a running runtime publishes exactly that host and port. A
name asks for the runtime and may choose how to reach it; an address asks for
the address, so the substitution holds only while the two are the same thing.
Move the runtime to another port and the address means what it says again.
.PP
What it is worth, measured on this box on Linux 7.2.2 with the same proxy in
front of the same server and only the backend leg differing: a request falls
from 25.33 to 22.68 microseconds, and the whole process tree spends 4.0
microseconds less CPU on each one \(em 12 of 12 interleaved passes, median of
the per\-pass differences. The CPU saves more than the clock does, which is what
a saving under load looks like: a serial round trip waits on wakeups, and the
freed CPU has nowhere to go until there is a second request wanting it.
.PP
.B The node is 0600 in a directory the daemon creates 0700.
Only the user the daemon runs as can reach it. Measured: another local user
reaches the port and is refused on the node with
.BR EACCES ,
and cannot list the directory or create a node in it either. The port is not
narrowed by any of this \(em it is exactly as open as it was \(em but the leg
between two runtimes no longer travels over something every local user could
also connect to.
.PP
The directory is checked and not merely created. A
.BR mkdir (2)
answering
.B EEXIST
says a directory is there and nothing about whose it is, so the daemon stats it:
anything that is not a directory it owns, with no group or other access, turns
companion nodes off for the whole daemon and says so once in the log. Without
that check a stranger could create the directory first and hold a live node at a
path the daemon was about to publish \(em and a live node is one the bind
refuses to take, which would leave the path published and served by them.
.PP
Two things it takes away, both worth knowing before relying on them:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.B A firewall rule on loopback does not apply.
nftables and iptables see packets, and an AF_UNIX socket produces none. A rule
that drops traffic to a runtime's port does not stop another runtime reaching it
over the node.
.IP \(bu 2
.B tcpdump \-i lo shows nothing.
The proxy writes one line to the daemon log per backend at start, naming the
path it will use; that line is the only evidence the node was taken.
.RE
.PP
A runtime gets no node when it is neither a server nor a cache, when it has no
port \(em one already on
.B \-\-listen unix:
has nothing to add \(em when it is
.BR \-\-udp ,
or when its listener is TLS, since a cleartext node beside an encrypted port
would undo what the port was configured to give. A proxy keeps its port alone,
and so does a cache with
.BR \-\-shards :
a path is one node and N processes would unlink each other's, which is why
.B \-\-listen
gives each shard a node of its own. One rule decides both what the daemon publishes and what
a runtime binds, so a type that is not wired to a listener is never advertised
as having one \(em a path published into thin air is a backend that is simply
down. A proxy configured with backend TLS keeps TCP for the same reason, rather
than quietly dropping the encryption it was asked for.
.SS Between runtimes on one machine
Everything one runtime asks of another \(em
.BR socketley.send ,
.BR self.route ,
and a server's
.BI \-\-cache " name"
\(em used to travel through the daemon: up one control socket and down the
other. The daemon still carries the first such crossing, and then introduces
the two processes to each other with a socket pair neither had before; the ones
after it go straight across.
.PP
There is nothing to configure and nothing to see from outside. The pair has no
path in the filesystem, so it can be neither connected to nor squatted \(em only
the two processes that were handed the ends can use it. The daemon logs one line
naming the pair when it introduces them, and
.BI "socketley stats " name
reports
.BR peer_links ,
.B peer_direct
and
.B peer_relayed
so a crossing that went the long way can be told from one that went across.
.PP
What it is worth, measured with a server posting every cache command to a cache
runtime, the two arms differing only in whether they were introduced: a round
trip falls from 31.60 to 21.14 microseconds and the process tree spends 10.75
microseconds less CPU per command \(em 12 of 12 interleaved passes, median of
the per\-pass differences. A third of the round trip was the daemon.
.PP
A crossing falls back to the daemon whenever the link is not usable, so nothing
depends on it: a link that never forms, or one whose peer has gone, costs the
speed it would have saved and nothing else. Routed connections are pinned to
whichever path they opened on and keep it for their whole life, because a
message that changed route mid\-stream could arrive ahead of one sent before it.
.SS Which port a runtime is on
.BR ls ,
.BR ps ,
.B stats
and the metrics endpoint report the port a runtime
.I binds
\(em which is not always the one it was configured with. A runtime created
without
.B \-p
takes its type's default (8000 for a server, 8080 for a proxy, 9000 for a
cache), and those surfaces used to print the configured zero: the
.B PORT
column read
.BR \- ,
.B ps \-\-json
and
.B stats
read
.BR 0 ,
and no command would tell an operator which port their runtime was actually
answering on.
.PP
.B show
is deliberately not in that list. Its output is the state document, and it goes
back in through
.BR import ,
so writing the default into it would pin a runtime that had been left to take
whichever default its type has.
.PP
A runtime that binds no port at all \(em a client, a
.BI \-\-listen " unix:"
listener, a process registered with
.BR attach ,
or a server reachable only through its owner \(em reports
.BR 0 .
.SS One address, one runtime
A runtime is refused a start when another runtime on this daemon is already
serving the address it would bind \(em a TCP port, a UDP port, or a
.BI \-\-listen " unix:"
path. The refusal names both runtimes and reaches the terminal, and on the boot
restore it names both in the daemon log.
.PP
The kernel will not refuse it. Every listener is opened with
.BR SO_REUSEPORT ,
because that is the whole mechanism behind
.BR \-\-shards :
N worker processes of
.I one
runtime binding one port and letting the kernel hand each connection to one of
them. A second
.I runtime
claiming the same number is indistinguishable from a second shard, so the bind
succeeds and the connections split between the two at random. Measured before
this was refused, on two servers created on one port: both started, both
listened, nothing was logged, and twenty messages arrived nine and eleven.
.PP
What is still accepted, because it is not one address:
.BR "\-\-udp beside TCP" ,
which are separate namespaces in the kernel \(em a datagram server and a stream
server on the same number are two listeners that never meet;
.BR "a restart" ,
which passes through
.B stopped
and takes back its own port, since the rule reads state and not configuration;
.BR "\-\-shards" ,
whose workers are one runtime; and a runtime that binds nothing at all \(em a
client, a process registered with
.BR attach ,
or a server reachable only through its owner.
.PP
A clustered cache holds more than one: the configured port, and
.IR base+1 .. base+N
for the shards, which is the formula
.B CLUSTER SLOTS
publishes. A runtime that had taken one of those would be sent keys it does not
hold.
.PP
The refused runtime is left in the state it was in \(em
.B created
or
.BR stopped ,
not
.B failed
\(em because nothing was built and nothing broke. Stop the runtime named in the
refusal, or give one of the two an address of its own.
.SH SERVER FLAGS
.TP
.BI "\-\-mode " "mode"
Message flow direction:
.B inout
(bidirectional, default),
.B in
(receive only),
.B out
(send only),
.B master
(one client claims master, only master's messages are broadcast).
.IP
A direction is not an answer. In
.B inout
with no script, every message is repeated to other connections and not to the
one that sent it, so a single client talking to itself hears nothing and a
second client hears everything. See
.BR \-\-send\-to .
.IP
Relayed messages are held for a connection until the server knows what protocol
it speaks, and delivered once it does \(em through that protocol's framing, so a
WebSocket client receives them as frames rather than as raw text. A connection is
classified by its first message. This matters because the alternative is a
corrupted handshake: a client whose opening move is an HTTP request or a
WebSocket upgrade would otherwise be sent relay bytes in front of the response it
is waiting for.
.IP
A client that connects and sends nothing is served anyway, after a delay of at
most 250 ms, and is treated as a plain line\-delimited TCP client from then on.
Such a client has nothing outstanding for the relayed bytes to arrive in front
of, so there is no handshake to protect. The delay is paid once per connection,
not once per message: everything relayed after the first delivery goes out
immediately. A client that has sent part of a request and not finished it is not
covered by this and is held until its request completes, however long that takes
\(em that one is waiting for an answer, and the answer must come first.
.IP
The hold is capped at 64 KB per connection. Past that the oldest held messages
are dropped to make room, so a client that stays silent through more than 64 KB
of relay traffic in under 250 ms receives the most recent messages and not the
whole backlog. The cap is per connection and applies only before classification;
once a connection is classified, relay traffic goes straight to its write queue
and the ordinary
.B \-\-max\-connections
and write\-queue limits govern instead.
.IP
A connection that speaks HTTP is not in the relay set and receives no relayed
messages at all, from the request that identifies it as HTTP onward. HTTP has no
framing for a message the server sends on its own: before a response such a
message replaces the status line the client is parsing for, and between two
keep\-alive requests it is read as the status line of the next response. There is
no point at which it would be well formed, so it is not sent rather than delayed.
A WebSocket connection is unaffected \(em it arrives over the same HTTP, but once
upgraded it has framing for server\-initiated messages and receives everything.
That is the supported way for a browser client to receive relay traffic.
.IP
A connection that speaks RESP is excluded on the same grounds and from the
command that identifies it as RESP onward. RESP2 matches replies to commands by
their order on the stream and by nothing else, so a relayed message is read as
the reply to whatever that client asks next; and what the relay writes is the
message text, not a RESP value, so a Redis client reads a plain letter where a
type byte belongs and answers with a protocol error. Encoding it as a value
would not help, because the only channel RESP2 has for a server\-initiated value
is pub/sub, which the client enters with SUBSCRIBE and which a server runtime
does not implement. A RESP client on a relaying server still receives its own
replies, anything a script addresses to it by client id, and the answers to
.B "cache <command>"
messages; what it no longer receives is other clients' traffic.
.TP
.BI "\-\-send\-to " "who[,who...]"
Who the built\-in path answers when no script does, as a comma separated set of
recipients:
.B sender
(the connection the message came from),
.B others
(every other connection, the master excepted),
.B master
(the master connection, which only
.B "\-\-mode master"
ever has).
.IP
The recipients are disjoint, so they compose:
.B "\-\-send\-to sender,others"
reaches everyone including the sender, and
.B "\-\-send\-to sender,master"
answers whoever spoke and sends the master a copy. There is no
.B all
for that reason \(em
.B sender,others
already says it, and one behaviour under two spellings is the mistake this
flag was built so as not to repeat.
.IP
Unset keeps what the mode has always done:
.B inout
answers
.BR others ,
and
.B master
drops what a non\-master says.
.IP
In
.B "\-\-mode master"
the flag decides what happens to a NON\-master's message; the master's own
still reaches everyone else, which is what the mode is.
.B "\-\-send\-to master"
forwards prefixed \(em the sender's descriptor stays in front of the line so
the master can tell two clients apart.
.B "\-\-send\-to others,master"
is a moderated room the master reads along with.
.IP
Refused rather than accepted and ignored, per recipient:
.B in
and
.B out
answer nobody at all, and
.B master
names a connection no other mode has.
.IP
In
.BR inout ,
and for the master's own message in
.BR master ,
a script's
.B on_message
takes precedence over every recipient: when it is defined, nothing is sent
automatically and
.B self.send
or
.B self.broadcast
decides. What a non\-master says in
.B master
mode has never reached
.B on_message
at all \(em only
.BR on_client_message ,
which does not suppress anything \(em so there the recipients apply whether or
not a script is loaded.
.IP
Before this flag existed, UDP and TCP disagreed: a UDP sender received its own
datagram back and a TCP sender did not.
.B others
is now what both do unset, and
.B "\-\-send\-to sender,others"
is the old UDP behaviour under a name.
.TP
.B \-\-udp
Use UDP instead of TCP. The server binds a single SOCK_DGRAM socket.
Peers are auto-registered on first datagram. Each datagram is one message
(no line-parsing). Broadcast sends to all known peers via sendto().
Not available for cache or proxy.
.IP
.B "An exposed UDP server can reflect traffic."
With no Lua handler every datagram is relayed to every other peer that has sent
one, and a peer is learned from a source address, which a sender can forge; keep
such a server on a trusted network or give it a Lua handler that decides.
.IP
Refused with
.BR "\-\-mode master" ,
which this page called incompatible for a long time while nothing enforced it.
Master mode claims a connection as the master and a datagram is not a
connection, so the pair is not merely useless: a datagram beginning
.B "master "
reached that code with no sender and took the worker down with it.
.TP
.BI "\-\-master\-pw " "password"
Static password for master mode authentication. A client sends "master <password>"
to claim master role. Only one master at a time; master is released on disconnect.
Alternatively, use the
.B on_master_auth(client_id, password)
Lua callback for dynamic authentication.
.IP
Failed attempts are counted per source address. A connection is refused at
accept, before a byte is read, once that address has failed ten attempts within
sixty seconds; the count decays after sixty seconds of quiet. This is a property
of master mode and not of this flag \(em it applies whichever of the two
authenticators is in use.
.IP
Requires
.BR "\-\-mode master" ,
and is refused without it. The
.B "master "
prefix is read in exactly one place, so in any other mode the password is not a
credential: it is an ordinary message, and a client that sends it has its
password relayed to every other connection verbatim.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-master\-pw
.RB ( edit
only.) Remove the password from a server that has one, which is what makes
leaving master mode possible \(em the pair above is refused as a set, so a mode
change alone would be refused while the password remained. It has a flag of its
own because passing an empty string to
.B \-\-master\-pw
cannot express it: the daemon receives one line and splits it on whitespace, so
an empty argument does not survive the wire and arrives as the flag with
nothing after it.
.TP
.BI "\-\-http " "directory"
Serve static files from the given directory over HTTP. When a browser makes a
plain HTTP GET request (without a WebSocket upgrade header), the server responds
with the requested file. GET / serves index.html. HTML files (.html, .htm)
automatically get a WebSocket connection script injected before </head>.
.IP
.B "Note that this modifies the file it serves."
Every .html and .htm response is rewritten on its way out, so a directory
holding documents this server did not author \(em uploads, a generated report,
a third\-party page \(em is delivered altered, with a script that opens a
WebSocket back to this runtime. There is no flag to turn it off; if that is not
wanted, serve those files from something else.
Content-Type is detected from the file extension. WebSocket upgrades continue
to work as before on the same port. Only GET requests are served; other HTTP
methods fall through to raw TCP handling.
.IP
.B "Range requests."
Every whole\(hyfile answer carries
.BR "Accept-Ranges: bytes" ,
and a request with a
.B Range
field is answered
.B "206 Partial Content"
with
.B Content-Range
stating the file's whole length and
.B Content-Length
stating the slice. That is what lets a media player seek, and what lets an
interrupted download resume \(em without it a player scrubs by downloading
from the beginning, and Safari will not play
.B <video>
at all. All three forms are served:
.IP
.nf
Range: bytes=0\-499      the first 500 bytes
Range: bytes=500\-       from 500 to the end
Range: bytes=\-500       the last 500 bytes
.fi
.IP
An end past the file is clamped to the last byte. A range starting at or past
the end is
.BR "416 Range Not Satisfiable" ,
carrying
.B "Content-Range: bytes */<length>"
\(em which is how a client that was not told the length learns it.
.IP
Two requests are answered with the whole file instead, both deliberately. A
multi\(hyrange request
.RB ( "bytes=0\-9,20\-29" )
needs a
.B multipart/byteranges
body, which this does not produce; RFC 9110 14.2 permits ignoring the field,
and the whole file is more useful than a wrong slice. And a range on
.B .html
or
.B .htm
is ignored because the injected script above changes the body's length, so a
byte offset into the file is not an offset into what is served \(em those
responses do not claim
.B Accept-Ranges
either, rather than inviting a seek that cannot be honoured.
.IP
.B "Concurrency of the zero-copy path."
A file is sent with
.BR splice (2)
through a pipe, which keeps the bytes out of user space entirely. That pipe used
to be one per runtime, so exactly one connection at a time could take the path
and everything else fell back to a read into memory followed by a write.
Measured on 4 MiB files, seven interleaved passes of each build:
.IP
.nf
concurrent   before      after     factor
1            2.9 ms      2.9 ms     0.98
2           11.9 ms      4.2 ms     2.80
4           26.1 ms      5.3 ms     4.97
8           55.8 ms     10.5 ms     5.33
.fi
.IP
Each connection now makes its own pipe. How many exist at once is a budget in
bytes and not a count \(em 64 MB of kernel pipe, divided by the pipe size, which
follows
.B \-\-write\-buffer
capped at 1 MB. So the default allows 64, and a runtime configured small because
it serves many streams gets MORE pipes out of the same budget rather than fewer.
Past the count the read path serves the remainder exactly as it did before.
.IP
More requests reach for a pipe than used to: the splice arm now starts at
256 KiB rather than at 1 MiB, because that is where a kept response stops being
the faster answer. A server of 300 KiB images that never touched this budget
before may now sit against it, and what it gets when it does is the read path,
which is what it had for those files all along.
.IP
That boundary is worth knowing, because the fallback is not merely slower. Worker
throughput and RSS on 4 MiB files:
.IP
.nf
                     at/below the count      past it
default (64)         12.7-14.2 GB/s          1.20 GB/s
                     RSS 35 MB               RSS 234 MB
\-\-write\-buffer 512K  12.3-14.0 GB/s at 128 concurrent, RSS 36 MB
.fi
.IP
The read path allocates the WHOLE file per request, which is why memory there
follows concurrency times file size while the pipes do not: the kernel fills a
pipe's buffer as it is used. That is also why the count is 64 and not the 8 it
was first set to \(em the pipes cost less than the fallback they avoid, and 8
put the boundary at about ten concurrent requests.
.IP
.B "On a TLS listener there is no zero-copy path at all,"
and this is the bound to know before serving large files over one. Splicing
would hand the peer plaintext in the middle of an encrypted stream, so every
request takes the read path whatever the pipe budget says. Measured, worker RSS
on a 256 MB file:
.IP
.nf
plaintext, one request        +2 MB          (splice)
TLS, one request            505 MB          about twice the file
TLS, three concurrent     1,250 MB
.fi
.IP
Twice, because the file is read into memory and the response is then assembled
around it \(em the same doubling recorded for a 400 MiB .html, where it came to
1219 MiB. It is held for the duration of the request, and it is the client that
decides how many such requests there are: ten concurrent 256 MB requests would
be about 5 GB.
.IP
.B \-\-http\-cache
bounds the multiplication but not the size. The file is held once and shared, so
concurrency stops mattering, at the cost of holding it permanently from startup
\(em measured at 154 MB for a 64 MB file, which is the file plus the two
prebuilt responses. Otherwise keep the files a TLS listener serves small, or put
a plaintext listener behind something else that terminates TLS.
.IP
The repair is a read path that works in bounded pieces, as the request side now
does with
.BR on_http_body ,
rather than one allocation the size of whatever was asked for. That is a change
to the most intricate function in this path and is not made here.
.IP
A slice is never stored in the revalidation map and never answered from it.
The map is keyed by path and holds the whole file's response, so a stored 206
would answer every later request for that path with one reader's slice and
that reader's
.BR Content-Range .
.IP
The client's
.B Connection
request header decides whether the connection is reused, with the HTTP version
supplying only the default (keep\-alive for 1.1, close for 1.0). A request
carrying
.B Connection: close
is answered with
.B Connection: close
and the server then shuts down its own write side first, so the 60\-second
TIME\-WAIT that follows is held on the server's fixed listening port instead of
on one of the client's ephemeral ports. The header is parsed token\-wise and
case\-insensitively, so
.B "Connection: close, TE"
counts as close.
.IP
Without
.B \-\-http\-cache
this is the development mode, and a file edited on disk is picked up by the
next request. It reaches that without re\-reading the file every time: the
response is kept in memory and one
.BR statx (2)
per request decides whether it is still the file's response. The check is on
the device, the inode number, the size and the modification time together
\(em the inode number is part of it because writing a new file and renaming it
over the old one, which is what
.BR mv ,
.BR cp ,
.B "git checkout"
and most editors actually do, leaves the size and can be given back the old
timestamp exactly.
.IP
The memory this uses is bounded, unlike
.BR \-\-http\-cache 's,
because it fills from whatever is requested rather than from a directory the
operator can see the size of before starting: once the kept responses reach
64 MiB in total no further file is added, and the per\-file cap depends on
whether the connection asking has a faster path available at all.
.IP
.nf
plaintext, not .html   256 KiB   anything larger is spliced instead
TLS, or an .html         1 MiB   neither has a splice arm
.fi
.IP
That split is measured rather than tidy. A kept response is sent by copying it
into the socket; splicing sends the page cache by reference and copies nothing.
At 64 concurrent requests for a 1 MiB file that is 5,931 responses a second kept
against 14,049 spliced, and 11,977 against 26,599 at 512 KiB \(em so keeping a
large file makes serving it slower, not faster. Small files reverse it, because
splice's own setup is then the whole cost: at 4 KiB it is 146,987 a second kept
against 66,905 spliced.
.IP
The cap sits at 256 KiB rather than lower because splice buys that throughput
partly with CPU, and the price falls with size: per MiB served it is 340 us at
256 KiB, 163 us at 1 MiB and 143 us at 2 MiB, against a flat ~170 us for the
copy. Above the cap the trade is free or better; at 256 KiB it would be 59 per
cent more CPU for 115 per cent more throughput, which is a trade and not
something a default should decide for an operator.
.IP
A TLS connection cannot splice at all, since that would hand the peer plaintext
in the middle of an encrypted stream, and an .html has a script injected into it
\(em so for those two the larger cap stands, and there the map is what keeps a
file from being read, and allocated, once per request.
.IP
A file that is not kept is not read either, outside those two cases: it goes
from the page cache to the socket without passing through this process. See
.B SOCKETLEY_HTTP_REVALIDATE
to switch the whole mechanism off, which also puts every size back on the
copying path.
.TP
.B \-\-http\-cache
When used with \-\-http, reads all files into memory at startup instead of
consulting the filesystem on each request. Faster for production, and the
difference from plain
.B \-\-http
is one
.BR statx (2)
per request. The cache is rebuilt on
.BR reload\-lua ,
and only then: a file edited on disk is
.B not
picked up, which is the reason to use this flag and the reason not to.
Each file is held twice, once with a
.B Connection: keep\-alive
response header and once with
.BR "Connection: close" ,
so that a cache hit stays a refcount bump and still answers with the header the
client asked for.
.TP
.TP
.BI "\-u " "host:port"
.TQ
.BI "\-\-upstream " "host:port"
Add an outbound upstream connection. The server connects to the specified
host:port on startup and auto-reconnects with exponential backoff (1s\(en16s)
if the connection drops. Multiple \-u flags may be specified, or multiple
addresses separated by semicolons (e.g. \-u "feed:9000;cache:6379").
Each upstream gets a stable 1-based conn_id for use in Lua callbacks.
.PP
.RS
Upstream Lua callbacks:
.TP
.B on_upstream(conn_id, data)
Called for each newline-delimited message received from an upstream.
.TP
.B on_upstream_connect(conn_id)
Called when an upstream connects (including reconnects).
.TP
.B on_upstream_disconnect(conn_id)
Called when an upstream disconnects.
.RE
.RS
Upstream Lua actions:
.TP
.B self.upstream_send(conn_id, msg)
Send a message to a specific upstream.
.TP
.B self.upstream_broadcast(msg)
Send a message to all connected upstreams.
.TP
.B self.upstreams()
Returns a table of currently connected upstream conn_ids.
.RE
.TP
.BI "\-\-cache " "name"
Link this server to an existing cache runtime. Clients can then send
"cache <command>" messages (e.g. "cache set foo bar", "cache get foo")
which are executed against the linked cache and the response returned to
the sender only. Normal messages are still processed according to the
server's mode.
.IP
.B "The link also archives: every ordinary message is written to that cache."
A message that is not a
.B cache
command is stored under an auto\-incrementing integer key \(em
.BR 1 ,
.BR 2 ,
.B 3
and on \(em as well as being handled by the server's mode. So two clients
saying
.B hello
and
.B world
to a linked server leave
.B "get 1"
answering
.B hello
and
.B "get 2"
answering
.BR world .
The counter is per server and starts again when the server restarts, so a
.B "\-\-persistent"
cache keeps growing across restarts while keys are overwritten from
.B 1
upward. Link a cache to a server only when that archive is wanted; a server
that merely wants its clients to be able to reach a cache pays for a write per
message.
.IP
The command is not executed on the connection that asked for it. The
server hands it to the daemon and answers when the reply comes back, one
turn of the event loop later, so a
.B cache
command costs a round trip that the same command typed at the cache's own
port does not. See
.B Cache Access via Server
below for what that guarantees and what it costs.
.SS Sharding
.TP
.BI "\-\-shards " "N"
Serve this server from
.I N
worker processes instead of one, sharing the listening socket through
.BR SO_REUSEPORT .
The kernel gives each incoming connection to one of them. 1 to 64; the default
is 1, which is what every server was before this flag was offered here.
.PP
A runtime is one process and therefore one event loop on one core. This is the
only way to give a single server more than that.
.PP
.B "With or without \-\-lua."
A scripted server used to refuse this flag, because the three things a script
can ask about its clients were answered per PROCESS: on four shards
.B self.connections()
reported a quarter of them,
.B self.broadcast
reached a quarter of them, and
.B self.clients()
listed a quarter of them \(em as descriptors, so an id from one shard named a
different socket on another. None of it was reported.
.PP
Each of those now crosses a shard boundary.
.B self.connections()
sums every shard's own published figure out of a shared mapping, with no round
trip.
.B self.broadcast
and
.B socketley.send
addressed to this runtime reach every shard's clients, once each.
.B self.clients()
lists the whole runtime \(em this shard's descriptors, plus a stable handle for
each client a sibling holds \(em and
.B self.send
on one of those handles reaches the shard that owns it.
.PP
.B "Per-connection state is per shard, and that is what sharding means."
A connection belongs to exactly one shard, so every callback about it runs
there and nowhere else.
.B on_connect
for a client a sibling accepted fired in that sibling, and
.B on_message
for it will too. A script therefore holds no state for the ids
.B self.clients()
hands it from another shard: it can name them, count them and send to them, and
it has never seen them arrive. Design the script for that \(em keep
cross-connection state in a
.B cache
runtime, which every shard reaches, rather than in a Lua table at file scope,
which each shard has its own copy of.
.PP
.B "self.route works from a shard."
Routing hands a live client to another runtime, which answers it while this
shard keeps the socket and relays the bytes; the target is reached across the
process boundary as from any worker, so
.B \-\-shards
changes nothing about it. A routed client belongs to the shard that accepted it,
like every other connection.
.B unroute,
.B get_route
and
.B owner_send
read this shard's own tables.
.PP
.B "What it bought, measured."
One server runtime in
.B \-\-http
mode with
.BR \-\-http\-cache ,
driven by
.B "socketley_bench proxy http-keepalive"
over 64 keep-alive connections; seven interleaved passes, median of the per-pass
ratios. The cores are the SERVER processes' own \(em daemon plus workers, from
.I /proc/<pid>/stat
\(em and not the box's, because a box-wide figure includes the benchmark client and
at this concurrency the client is the larger half of it.
.PP
.RS
.TS
l r r r.
shards	throughput	cores	per core
_
1	126,497 req/s	0.99	127,775 req/s
8	451,097 req/s	4.78	94,372 req/s
.TE
.RE
.PP
3.57x at eight shards, with the per-pass ratios spread from 3.18x to 4.26x.
.PP
.B "That figure is a server's, and does not transfer to a cache."
A server's shards are interchangeable, so every one of them answers every
request. A cache's are not: the shard a connection lands on relays every key it
does not own, and behind one address that is
.RI ( N \-1)/ N
of them. Measured at four shards that is 1.75x rather than a server's 3.57x,
and at two it is 1.05x for 1.91 cores. See
.B CACHE FLAGS
for the table.
.PP
.B "One shard is exactly one core."
0.99 of one, at every concurrency tried, while the machine had fifteen more
sitting idle. That is the whole case for this flag stated as a measurement: a
server does not run out of machine, it runs out of the single event loop it is
allowed.
.PP
.B "Per-core efficiency falls by about a quarter, and that is the price."
127,775 req/s per core at one shard against 94,372 at eight. Sharding buys
throughput with cores, not with efficiency; if the machine has cores that are
otherwise idle that is a good trade, and if it does not, this flag makes things
worse rather than better. Do not set more shards than the machine has cores to
give them.
.PP
.B "Measure with enough connections to feed the shards, or the flag looks worse than it is."
The same eight shards driven by only
.I 16
connections read 294,917 req/s and 2.54x \(em not because they were slower but
because they were starved: the arm used 4.12 cores where the 64-connection run
used 4.78, and
.B SO_REUSEPORT
hands out whole connections, so sixteen over eight shards is two apiece with an
uneven tail and the slowest shard gates the pass. A shard count above the
client's concurrency measures the client.
.B "The load is balanced per connection, not per request."
.B SO_REUSEPORT
picks a shard when the connection is accepted and never revisits it, so one
heavy long-lived connection makes one shard hot while the others idle, and the
spread over a small number of connections is uneven \(em eight connections over
four shards were measured landing 3/2/2/1. Sharding helps a server carrying many
comparable connections; it does nothing for a server carrying one big one.
.PP
.B "The surface still answers about the runtime, not the shard."
.B ls
shows one entry,
.B stats
reports the connection count summed across the shards and labels each shard's
detail text,
.B stop
and
.B remove
take all of them down, and the count is persisted so a daemon restart brings
back the number that was asked for. The count may be changed with
.B edit
only while the runtime is stopped, for the same reason
.B \-p
may.
.PP
.B "\-\-shards gives each shard a node of its own."
.B SO_REUSEPORT
is not what a unix socket does \(em
.I N
processes binding one path would unlink each other's node and only the last
would be reachable \(em so the path is not shared. A base of
.I /run/app.sock
becomes
.IR /run/app#0.sock ,
.IR /run/app#1.sock ,
and so on, one per shard, and a caller picks any of them. A base with no
.B .sock
on the end gets the index appended instead.
.PP
Refused only when the longest of those names would not fit: the kernel's limit
is on the node, and a shard whose name is truncated is a shard nothing can
reach. The refusal names that longest member.
.PP
See
.B Sharding
under
.B PROXY FLAGS
for the fuller treatment of the flag.
.SS Multi-Protocol Auto-Detection
Server connections automatically detect the protocol from the first bytes
received. No flags are needed \(em all four protocols coexist on the same
port:
.TP
.B TCP (newline-delimited text)
Default. Lines terminated by
.B \\en
are delivered as messages.
.TP
.B HTTP
Detected when the first bytes are a valid HTTP method (GET, POST, etc.).
Requests are handled by the
.B on_http_request
Lua callback if defined, or by the static file server if
.B \-\-http
is set, or responded with 404 otherwise. HTTP/1.1 keep-alive is supported, and
an explicit
.B Connection
request header overrides the version's default \(em see
.B \-\-http
for what the server does with it.
.TP
.B WebSocket
Detected when an HTTP request contains
.BR "Upgrade: websocket" .
The server completes the RFC 6455 handshake and transparently frames/deframes
messages. The
.B on_websocket
callback fires after the upgrade. A text message that is not valid UTF\-8
fails its connection with close code 1007 and never reaches a script; a
fragmented message is checked once it is whole.
A message the server relays without a script \(em to other clients, back to
its sender, or to the master \(em goes out as the kind of frame it arrived in,
text or binary. A message a script sends goes out as text when it is valid
UTF\-8 and as binary otherwise, as does a line relayed from a TCP client.
.TP
.B RESP (Redis protocol)
Detected when the first byte is
.BR * .
Useful for cache-linked servers or custom RESP-based protocols. Like an HTTP
connection and for the same reason, a RESP connection is not in the relay set:
it is answered, but it is not sent other clients' messages. See
.B \-\-mode
under
.BR "SERVER FLAGS" .
.PP
.B "How much it waits for before deciding."
The tests above read four bytes \(em every HTTP method is matched on four
characters, the RESP markers on two \(em so a connection that has sent fewer
than that is normally left to send more.
.PP
A completed line is the exception, and it has to be: a client that writes
.B "hi\\en"
and waits to be answered will not send a fourth byte until it is, so waiting for
one would be waiting for something that only arrives after the answer. A line
that ended inside four bytes cannot be an HTTP method \(em each is four
characters and none contains a newline \(em so the protocol is decided there and
then, as RESP if the first two bytes say so and as TCP otherwise. Short lines
are ordinary input on a line protocol and are delivered like any other.
.PP
This is about the
.I first
line only. Once a connection's protocol is known, lines of any length are
delivered as they arrive.
.PP
Browser example:
.nf
const ws = new WebSocket("ws://localhost:9000");
ws.onmessage = e => console.log(e.data);
ws.send("hello");
.fi
.SS Request Framing
The server frames an HTTP request by exactly the rules the proxy uses, and
they are the same rules because they are the same code. A request whose
header block gives no answer, or two answers, to "how long is the body" is
answered
.B 400 Bad Request
and its connection is closed. That happens when the block contains any of:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
a bare LF or a bare CR \(em every line must end with CRLF
.IP \(bu 2
whitespace between a field name and its colon
.RB ( "Content\-Length : 6" )
.IP \(bu 2
a continuation (obs\-fold) line, i.e. one starting with a space or tab
.IP \(bu 2
a field line with no colon, or with an empty field name
.IP \(bu 2
more than one
.B Content\-Length
field, or a
.B Content\-Length
together with a
.B Transfer\-Encoding
.IP \(bu 2
a
.B Content\-Length
that is not a whole decimal number
.RB ( "Content\-Length: 6x" )
.RE
.PP
A tab is legal optional whitespace around a field value \(em a
.B Content\-Length
separated from its colon by a tab rather than a space is an ordinary
request and is served, as is a value with trailing whitespace. Values reach
.B on_http_request
with that whitespace removed from both ends.
.PP
.B Transfer\-Encoding
depends on what the script asked for, and on which coding it is.
.IP \(bu 2
.B chunked
alone, with a script that defines
.BR on_http_body :
decoded, and the body delivered in pieces like any other. There is no length
to bound it by, which is what chunked is for.
.IP \(bu 2
.B chunked
alone, without that callback: still
.BR "501 Not Implemented" ,
because nothing is going to read the body incrementally and a body whose end
this end cannot find is refused rather than treated as though it had none.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR "gzip, chunked" :
.B 501
even with the callback. Chunked is the final coding, so the chunk sizes do
delimit it, and the bytes inside are still compressed \(em taking the framing
off and handing them on as the body would hand on gzip and call it the body.
.IP \(bu 2
A coding that is not chunked at all, such as
.B gzip
by itself:
.BR "400 Bad Request" ,
because nothing can say where the body ends.
.PP
So a chunked request through a socketley proxy to a socketley server is served
when the server's script takes bodies and answered 501 when it does not; the
proxy forwards the framing verbatim either way and relays whatever comes back.
.B Content\-Length
remains the simpler form and is what a client that knows its own length should
send.
.PP
Why a server bothers with any of this, when it is the last recipient and
has nobody to disagree with: it usually is not. The moment anything sits
in front of it \(em this proxy, or any other \(em there are two parsers
reading one byte stream, and a header a strict parser ignores and a
lenient one honours moves the boundary between one request and the next.
Since the server serves pipelined requests without waiting for another
read, a body it wrongly measured as empty is read as the next request:
one message in, two answered, and the client chooses what the second one
says. Compliant clients emit none of these shapes.
.PP
A request whose header block is still arriving is held until the
terminating blank line, subject to a 16 KiB header limit
.RB ( "431 Request Header Fields Too Large" )
and a 10 second header deadline
.RB ( "408 Request Timeout" ).
The deadline is answered rather than dropped in silence, the same status the
proxy sends for the same case: a client that gets a bare close cannot tell a
deadline from a crash, a firewall or the wrong port.
.PP
The deadline is the same one the proxy applies, and for the reason given
there: it is fixed, is not configurable, and is deliberately independent of
.BR \-\-idle\-timeout ,
which defaults to 0 and so cannot end a half\-sent request \(em a client
that sends a request line and then stops sends no further bytes for the
16 KiB limit to count. It starts at the first read that left the block
incomplete and later bytes do not renew it. Neither an idle keep\-alive
connection between requests nor a slow request body is measured against it.
.PP
The server notices it on the sweep that runs every 30 seconds, so a
connection is closed between 10 and 40 seconds after it stops speaking; a
proxy has a chain of its own and closes between 10 and 20. The deadline is
the same in both.
.SS Cache Access via Server
When a server is linked to a cache with
.BR \-\-cache ,
clients can send "cache <command>" messages to execute commands directly against
the linked cache. The response is sent back to the sender only (never broadcast).
Normal messages are still processed according to the server's mode.
.PP
.nf
socketley create cache store -p 9001 -s
socketley create server api -p 9000 --cache store -s
# Client sends: "cache set foo bar" \(-> receives "ok"
# Client sends: "cache get foo" \(-> receives "bar"
# Client sends: "hello"            \(-> handled by the server's mode, AND
#                                     stored in `store` under the key "1"
.fi
.PP
The last line is the part that surprises people. An ordinary message is
archived into the linked cache under an auto\-incrementing integer key as well
as being handled normally; see
.B \-\-cache
under
.BR "SERVER FLAGS" .
.PP
The request is asynchronous. The server does not run the cache's command
interpreter itself \(em it posts the command and answers the client when
the reply arrives, which is the next turn of the daemon's event loop.
That is a round trip the synchronous version did not pay, on the order of
tens of microseconds on loopback; it is what allows the cache to be a
different process later without anything on the client's side changing.
Nothing else waits on it: the server keeps serving every other connection
while the reply is outstanding.
.PP
What holds:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
Replies on one connection come back in request order, so a client may
pipeline
.B cache
commands and match answers to questions positionally.
.IP \(bu 2
A command addressed to a cache that is not running is answered with an
.B error:
line rather than with silence.
.IP \(bu 2
A client that shuts down its write side after asking still receives its
answer: the connection is held open until every reply it is owed has been
written.
.RE
.PP
What does not: nothing is promised about the order of a
.B cache
reply against other traffic on the same connection. A broadcast goes to
every client except the sender and a cache reply goes to the sender
alone, so there is no interleaving to define.
.PP
A connection may have at most 4096 unanswered
.B cache
commands outstanding, and the daemon at most 32768 across all of them. A
client that pipelines past its share is disconnected rather than refused
in place: a refusal written now would arrive ahead of the answers already
queued in front of it, and a client that cannot tell which answer belongs
to which question has no protocol left.
.PP
Default port: 8000
.SH CLIENT FLAGS
.TP
.BI "\-t " "host:port | unix:/path/to.sock"
Target server to connect to. Default: 127.0.0.1:8000.
.IP
.BI unix: /path/to.sock
dials the socket node a runtime with
.B \-\-listen unix:
serves \(em the connect side of the same address, judged by the same rules
(absolute, under the kernel's
.I sun_path
limit). Refused with
.B \-\-udp
and with
.BR \-\-tls ,
for the listener's reasons under
.BR "COMMON FLAGS" .
.TP
.BI "\-\-mode " "mode"
Message flow direction:
.B inout
(bidirectional, default),
.B in
(receive only),
.B out
(send only).
.TP
.B \-\-udp
Use UDP instead of TCP. Creates a connected SOCK_DGRAM socket.
Each datagram is one message (no line-parsing).
Not available for cache or proxy.
.TP
.BI "\-\-reconnect " "[max\-attempts]"
Enable automatic reconnection with exponential backoff when the connection
is lost. Backoff starts at 1 second and doubles each attempt to a ceiling of
16 seconds, with up to half a second of jitter \(em the dials of a client whose
target is gone fall at 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 16, 16 seconds. Set to 0 for infinite
attempts (default when flag is given without a value). Set to a positive number
to limit reconnection attempts. A negative count is refused: \-1 is what the
stored field holds for "off", so the flag whose only purpose is to turn
reconnection on used to turn it off, silently and at exit 0. Reconnection is off
until the flag is given, which is how it stays off.
.IP
A client flag, and refused on a server, a proxy or a cache: it re-dials a
connection this runtime made, and those three take connections rather than make
them. It was accepted and reported by
.B show
on all four, and read by the client alone.
.IP
"When the connection is lost" includes a peer that never says so. A close sends
a FIN or an RST and is acted on at once, but a machine that loses power, a link
that partitions or a firewall that forgets an idle flow send nothing at all, and
an idle client that never writes would never find out. Every client socket
therefore carries TCP keepalive: 60 seconds idle, then three probes ten seconds
apart, so a peer that has silently gone is noticed in about 90 seconds and the
backoff above starts from there. There is no flag for it \(em the kernel's own
default is two hours, which for this purpose is the same as never.
.PP
A client also takes the TLS flags \(em see
.B \-\-tls
under
.BR "COMMON FLAGS" .
.B \-\-tls
alone dials the target over TLS and verifies it against the system trust
store;
.BI \-\-ca " file"
supplies a trust store instead, and
.BI \-\-cert " file"
with
.BI \-\-key " file"
present a certificate to a peer that asks for one. The host in
.B \-t
is the name the certificate has to be valid for. A verification failure costs
the socket and the client re-dials on the backoff above; it is never retried
in cleartext, and there is no way to skip the check.
.B \-\-tls
with
.B \-\-udp
is refused \(em that would need DTLS.
.SH PROXY FLAGS
.TP
.BI "\-\-listen unix:" "/path/to.sock"
Serve the proxy's front door on an AF_UNIX socket instead of
.BR \-p .
The full treatment is
.B \-\-listen
under
.BR "COMMON FLAGS" .
With
.B \-\-shards
each shard binds a node of its own,
.IR base#N.sock ,
because SO_REUSEPORT is not a path.
.TP
.BI "\-\-backend " "addr"
.B A hostname is resolved off the request path.
A backend named by hostname is looked up when the proxy starts and, if that
fails, again on the proxy's periodic sweep, on a thread of its own \(em never while a request is being
served. A request that arrives before the name resolves is refused rather than
waiting for it. That is deliberate: the daemon has one thread for every runtime
it holds, so a resolver that hangs on the request path stops not only this
proxy's clients but every server, cache and client in the same daemon. The cost
is that a name which starts resolving is picked up within one sweep instead of
immediately.
.IP
.B IPv4 only.
Every address this daemon resolves is AF_INET: a backend, an upstream, a client
target and every listener. A hostname that has only a AAAA record resolves to
nothing and the backend is reported unreachable, with no line anywhere saying
why. This is a property of the whole daemon and not of this flag; it is written
here because this is where an operator meets it first. The one address that is
not an internet address at all is
.BI unix: /path/to.sock
below, which resolves nothing because there is nothing to resolve.
.IP
Add backend server. Can be ip:port,
.BI unix: /path/to.sock ,
a runtime name, or
.BI @ groupname
to dynamically target all running members of that group. Repeatable or comma-separated.
Group backends are resolved at connection time, so new group members are picked up
automatically without restarting the proxy.
.IP
An address may carry a scheme:
.BI https:// host : port
is reached over TLS,
.BI http:// host : port
in cleartext, and a bare
.IB host : port
follows whatever the backend-TLS options below say. The port may be omitted
after a scheme, in which case it is 443 or 80. See
.B "mTLS Flags"
for what verifies such a connection.
.IP
.BI unix: /path/to.sock
reaches a backend over an AF_UNIX socket instead of loopback TCP. A sidecar
proxy and the process it fronts are on one host, and loopback TCP charges that
pair for a whole stack \(em sequence numbers, checksums, windows \(em on a
connection that never sees a wire. Measured on a quiet machine, 64\-byte round
trips through this proxy: one connection unpinned, 41.4 us over TCP against
37.0 us over AF_UNIX; eight connections unpinned, 66,620 round trips a second
against 85,217 \(em 16 to 28% depending on where the scheduler puts the three
processes.
.IP
The throughput figure is the one to plan with, and why is worth stating. What
the swap removes is CPU: 17.5 us of it per round trip across proxy and backend
over TCP against 14.5 over AF_UNIX, which is to the tenth what a two\-process
ping\-pong predicts for that leg. On one core with a single connection almost
none of it shows up as latency \(em 21.5 us against 20.7 \(em because a serial
round trip there is waiting on wakeups rather than on work, so freed CPU has
nothing to do. A proxy under load is short of exactly that CPU, which is why
the same change is worth under a microsecond in the first measurement and a
fifth of the throughput in the second.
.IP
The path must be absolute, and shorter than 108 bytes, which is what the
kernel's
.I sun_path
holds. Both are refused at
.BR create / start
rather than at traffic time: the kernel truncates a long path silently, and a
trimmed path names a different socket \(em quite possibly a real one belonging
to something else. A relative path is refused because it would be resolved
against the daemon's working directory, which is not something the operator
writing the flag can see.
.IP
A unix backend is never reached over TLS.
.BR \-\-ca ,
.B \-\-client\-cert
and
.B \-\-client\-key
encrypt every backend leg at once, so a proxy given both them and a
.B unix:
backend is refused at start rather than quietly connecting in the clear.
Filesystem permissions on the socket node are what restrict access to it.
.IP
A socket path that is not there, and a stale socket node left behind by a
process that has gone, both answer the client
.B 502
\(em the same answer an unreachable TCP backend gets, and for the same reason:
that is the far end failing, not this one.
.TP
.BI "\-\-strategy " "strategy"
Load balancing strategy:
.B round-robin
(default),
.B random
,
.B lua
(custom via on_route callback).
.TP
.BI "\-\-protocol " "protocol"
.B http
(default, path-based routing) or
.B tcp
(raw byte forwarding).
.IP
In
.B tcp
mode a client that shuts its write side with
.BR shutdown (2)
has that half\-close passed on to the backend, and the return direction is held
open until the backend itself closes. That is the only way a byte relay can tell
a backend where a request ended \(em there is no framing to say so \(em and it
is what makes
.BR "nc \-N" ,
a one\-shot
.BR redis\-cli ,
and any shell pipeline into the proxy work: each of those sends, half\-closes,
and then reads the answer. The half\-close is issued only once everything the
client sent has actually reached the backend socket, so it cannot overtake the
request it terminates.
.IP
What ends the hold is the backend's own close, a client that resets, or
.B \-\-idle\-timeout
where one is set. A half\-closed client is held no longer than one that never
half\-closed: a TCP relay has no request and no response, so no deadline sweep
applies to either.
.PP
Default port: 8080
.PP
HTTP mode: Requests to /<proxy-name>/* are forwarded with prefix stripped.
.PP
In
.B http
mode a request carrying an upgrade offer \(em an
.B Upgrade
header together with a
.B Connection
header listing the
.B upgrade
token \(em is forwarded with both of those headers intact, rather than having
them stripped as hop-by-hop. If the backend answers
.BR "101 Switching Protocols" ,
that response is relayed verbatim and the connection becomes a bidirectional
byte relay until one end closes. This is what makes
.B ws://
work through a proxy in http mode. See
.B WebSocket and other protocol upgrades
under
.BR LIMITATIONS
for what the proxy does and does not do with such a connection.
.TP
.BI \-\-domain " name" [, name ...]
On a proxy, the names it is reached by through the entrance (see
.B \-\-proxy
under
.BR "DNS FLAGS" ).
Reached by name, it serves under no
.BI / name /
prefix: the path and the Host go to the backend as they came, as a site behind
it expects them. From a hop on this machine \(em the entrance in front of it \(em
.B X\-Forwarded\-Proto
and
.B X\-Forwarded\-Host
are kept rather than written over, so the backend is told the scheme the client
used, not the cleartext hop between them.
.IP
.nf
socketley create proxy hub \-p 8089 \-\-bind 127.0.0.1 \\
    \-\-backend 127.0.0.1:8088 \-\-domain hub \-\-backend\-timeout 5m \-s
.fi
.TP
.BI \-\-auth " bearer:file\fR|\fBbasic:\fIfile\fR|\fBidp:\fIruntime\fR|\fBnone"
Who may send a request through, asked before any of it reaches a backend.
.B bearer:
admits
.BI "Authorization: Bearer " token
with a token that is a line of
.I file
(blank lines and lines starting with # aside).
.B basic:
admits HTTP Basic with an access key and its secret key from
.IR file ,
a credentials file as a storage runtime's
.B \-\-credentials
reads it, of a user who is an admin there; a key outside any
.B [user]
is the default owner's, an admin. A request without them is answered
.B 401
with the
.B WWW\-Authenticate
to answer in, and the backend is never sent the
.B Authorization
field. The file is read again when it changes, at most once a second; one that
is gone or does not parse refuses everybody, and says so in the log. An address
that fails five times within fifteen minutes is refused even the right answer,
.B 429
with
.BR Retry\-After ,
until fifteen minutes after its first failure, an IPv6 address by its /64;
behind the entrance, a hop on this machine, the address is the last in
.BR X\-Forwarded\-For ,
the one the entrance heard from. Each failure is logged with that address.
Set at
.BR create ;
.B edit
changes it on a stopped proxy.
.IP
.nf
socketley create proxy metrics \-p 9191 \-\-bind 127.0.0.1 \\
    \-\-backend 127.0.0.1:9190 \-\-domain metrics \\
    \-\-auth basic:/etc/socketley/storage\-ui.creds \-s
.fi
.IP
.BI idp: runtime
puts a sign-in in front of every route: the sign-in cookie of the
.B idp
runtime of that name in this daemon, verified here against the key it
publishes beside its state, each session once and then remembered until it
expires. A browser without one, a GET or HEAD that accepts HTML, is sent with
.B 302
to sign in and back to the address it asked for; anything else is answered
.BR 401 .
The backend is told the account's fixed id in
.BR X\-Forwarded\-User ,
its username in
.B X\-Forwarded\-Preferred\-Username
and its address in
.BR X\-Forwarded\-Email ,
which a client's own of those names never reaches; the provider's own cookies
are taken out of the
.B Cookie
field it is sent, every other cookie kept. The cookie reaches a proxy
on another name only when the provider's
.B \-\-cookie\-domain
covers both.
.IP
.nf
socketley create proxy wiki \-p 8100 \-\-backend 127.0.0.1:3000 \\
    \-\-domain wiki.example.com \-\-auth idp:auth \-s
.fi
.TP
.BR \-\-redirect\-https " [\fIport\fR|" none ]
Answer every request with
.B 308
to the name it asked for and its path, over HTTPS on
.I port
(443 when not given, and then not written into the URL). An ACME HTTP\-01
challenge this daemon holds is still answered first. A
.B dns
runtime whose entrance is on 443 makes one on port 80 as its child.
.SS Sharding
.TP
.BI "\-\-shards " "N"
Serve this proxy from
.I N
worker processes instead of one, sharing the listening socket through
.BR SO_REUSEPORT .
The kernel gives each incoming connection to one of them. 1 to 64; the default
is 1, which is what every proxy was before this flag existed.
.PP
A runtime is one process and therefore one event loop on one core. This is the
only way to give a single runtime more than that. It is worth reaching for only
when a proxy is saturated \(em below saturation a single connection is bound by
the cost of a cross-core wakeup, and another process cannot help with that.
.PP
A shard is an implementation detail of a runtime, not a runtime.
.B ls
shows one entry,
.B stats
reports the connection count summed across the shards and labels each shard's
detail text,
.B stop
and
.B remove
take all of them down, and the count is persisted so a restart brings back the
number that was asked for.
.PP
.B "It is a declaration, and not every type may make it."
Sharding is correct exactly when nothing is shared between connections, and
nothing can detect that \(em not the flags, and not the script, because a table
at file scope is indistinguishable from one that happens never to be read
twice. So the operator declares it, and the flag is offered only where they are
in a position to. A
.B cache
shards by key ownership: each shard owns the slice of the keyspace that hashes
to it, and a command naming a key some other shard owns is forwarded to that
shard, executed there, and answered back through the connection that asked. A
.B SET
on one connection and a later
.B GET
on another therefore agree, whichever shards they happened to land on \(em see
.B Sharding
under
.B CACHE FLAGS
for what that costs. A
.B client
refuses it because it has no listening socket to share. A
.B server
may shard with or without
.BR \-\-lua ;
each shard loads the script into its own interpreter, so per\-shard state is
per\-shard and is not shared between them \(em see
.B Sharding
under
.B SERVER FLAGS
for what that means for a script.
.PP
.B "One combination is refused on every type."
.B \-\-shards
with
.B "\-\-listen unix:"
is refused because
.B SO_REUSEPORT
is not what a unix socket does \(em
.I N
processes would unlink each other's socket node and only the last to bind would
be reachable.
.PP
.B "What it bought, measured."
An
.B http
proxy in front of four
.B \-\-http
backends, driven by
.BR wrk :
.PP
.RS
.TS
l r r.
shards	throughput	CPU per shard
_
1	40,285 req/s	1.00 cores
2	94,401 req/s	0.99 cores
4	150,175 req/s	0.91 cores
8	142,775 req/s	0.47 cores
.TE
.RE
.PP
3.73x at four shards, and close to linear: each shard stays at a full core until
the machine runs out of them. Eight shards on a ten-core box is past that point
and buys nothing \(em the per-shard figure halving is what running out looks
like. Do not set more shards than the machine has cores to give them.
.PP
.B "The backend tier saturates first."
That is the first thing anyone will hit, and it is not a property of this flag.
The same proxy in front of a
.I single
backend stops at 2.14x however many shards it is given, with the backend pinned
at exactly 1.00 cores \(em the shards were idle and waiting on it. A sharded
proxy needs something behind it that is not one process, whether that is several
backends, a
.B \-\-strategy
across a group, or a backend that is itself sharded. Measure what saturated
before reading a number as this flag's ceiling.
.PP
.B "It does nothing for \-\-protocol tcp, for a different reason."
A
.B tcp
proxy moves bytes with
.BR IORING_OP_SPLICE ,
which io_uring preps
.B REQ_F_FORCE_ASYNC
\(em so that data path never runs on the shard's event loop at all. It runs on
io-wq kernel threads, and it was therefore never bounded by the number of
processes: at
.I one
shard, a tcp proxy under 100 connections was measured holding 11 threads, 10 of
them
.BR iou\-* ,
and burning 1.85 cores. Sharding such a proxy adds processes to a path that is
already parallel, and throughput does not move. If you shard a tcp proxy and see
no change, this is why. The ceiling
.B \-\-shards
exists to lift is the
.B http
one above, where the runtime really is one event loop on one core.
.PP
.B "The load is balanced per connection, not per request."
.B SO_REUSEPORT
picks a shard when the connection is accepted and never revisits it, so one
heavy long-lived connection makes one shard hot while the others idle, and the
spread over a small number of connections is uneven \(em eight connections over
four shards were measured landing 3/2/2/1. Sharding helps a proxy carrying many
comparable connections; it does nothing for a proxy carrying one big one.
.PP
The shard count may be changed with
.B edit
only while the runtime is stopped, for the same reason
.B \-p
may: applying it to a running proxy would mean retiring workers out from under
live connections.
.SS Health Check Flags
.TP
.BI "\-\-health\-check " "[tcp|http]"
Enable active health checks for backends. In
.B tcp
mode, the proxy opens a TCP connection to each backend at the configured
interval \(em success means the port is listening. In
.B http
mode, the proxy sends an HTTP GET to
.B \-\-health\-path
and considers the backend healthy on a 2xx response.
Unhealthy backends are removed from the load balancer pool until they
recover.
.TP
.BI "\-\-health\-interval " "duration"
Interval between health checks. Default: 5 seconds. A bare number is
seconds; the suffixes
.BR s ", " m ", " h ", " d " and " w
are accepted
.RB ( 30 " and " 5m " both work)."
.TP
.BI "\-\-health\-path " "path"
HTTP health check path. Default:
.BR /health .
Only used when
.B \-\-health\-check
is set to
.BR http .
.TP
.BI "\-\-health\-threshold " "n"
Number of consecutive health check failures before a backend is marked
unhealthy. Default: 3. A single successful check restores the backend.
.SS Circuit Breaker Flags
.TP
.BI "\-\-circuit\-threshold " "n"
Number of consecutive backend errors (connection refused, timeout, 5xx)
that trips the circuit breaker to the
.B open
state. While open, all requests to that backend fail immediately without
attempting a connection. Default: 5.
.TP
.BI "\-\-circuit\-timeout " "duration"
How long an open circuit stays open before transitioning to
.BR half-open .
In half-open state, a single probe request is allowed through \(em if it
succeeds, the circuit closes (fully healthy); if it fails, the circuit
re-opens. Default: 30 seconds. A bare number is seconds; the suffixes
.BR s ", " m ", " h ", " d " and " w
are accepted
.RB ( 30 " and " 5m " both work)."
.TP
.BI "\-\-backend\-timeout " "duration"
How long a backend may take to produce the
.I first
byte of a response before the request is given up on. The request is
then retried on another backend if
.B \-\-retry
allows it, and answered
.B 504 Gateway Timeout
otherwise; either way the backend is charged with an error, so the
circuit breaker above opens and takes it out of rotation. Default: 2
seconds. Set to 0 to wait indefinitely. A bare number is seconds; the
suffixes
.BR s ", " m ", " h ", " d " and " w
are accepted
.RB ( 30 " and " 5m " both work)."
.IP
This is the only thing that notices a backend which accepts connections
and then stops answering. Such a backend passes a TCP health check, and
a request that never completes is recorded as neither success nor
failure, so without a deadline it keeps its full share of the rotation
and every request routed to it is held until the client gives up. HTTP
mode only \(em TCP mode has no request/response framing to measure. Only
the wait for the first byte is bounded, so a slow or streaming response
body is never cut short once the backend has begun answering \(em unless
the client has closed its side. Such a client cannot end the answer
itself, so from then on the backend may go this long without sending a
byte before both connections are closed, with no error charged; a
backend that keeps sending is never cut short.
.IP
The 2-second default applies whether or not the flag is given. If any
endpoint behind this proxy normally needs longer than that to
.I begin
answering \(em a report that is computed on demand, a cold cache, a long
query \(em raise
.B \-\-backend\-timeout
to cover it or set it to
.B 0
to disable the deadline. Left at the default, such an endpoint has its
requests cut off at 2 seconds and is charged an error each time, which
opens the circuit breaker on a backend that is working correctly.
.SS Retry Flags
.TP
.BI "\-\-retry " "n"
Retry failed requests up to
.I n
times on the next backend in the pool (0 = disabled, default). Only
idempotent HTTP methods (GET, HEAD, PUT, DELETE, OPTIONS) are retried
unless
.B \-\-retry\-all
is set. For TCP mode, any failed connection attempt is retried.
.TP
.B \-\-retry\-all
Also retry non-idempotent methods (POST, PATCH). Use with caution \(em
the request body is replayed to the next backend, which may cause
duplicate side effects.
.SS What a Request That Never Reaches a Backend Is Answered
In
.B \-\-protocol http
a client that has sent a whole request always gets a status line back, and
which status it is names the end that failed. A retry, where
.B \-\-retry
allows one, is tried first in every case below; these are what is sent when
no retry is available or every retry also failed.
.TP
.B 502 Bad Gateway
The backend's answer. It refused the connection, was unreachable, could not be
resolved, closed before a byte of the response arrived, or rejected the TLS
handshake. The backend is charged to its circuit breaker. A client that
retries a 502 immediately is asking a backend that has just said no.
.TP
.B 503 Service Unavailable
This proxy's own shortage, reached before the backend was asked anything: the
local ephemeral port range exhausted
.RB ( EADDRNOTAVAIL ),
descriptors exhausted
.RB ( EMFILE ", " ENFILE ),
memory or buffers exhausted
.RB ( ENOMEM ", " ENOBUFS ),
or a submission that did not reach the io_uring ring. Nothing is charged to
any circuit breaker \(em the backend is healthy and the same request will very
likely succeed on the next attempt. 503 is also the answer when there is no
backend to send to at all: every member of an
.B @group
is out, or the single backend's breaker is open.
.TP
.B 504 Gateway Timeout
The backend accepted (or never finished accepting) and produced no first byte
inside
.BR \-\-backend\-timeout .
Charged to the breaker.
.PP
Two cases deliberately get no status line.
.B \-\-protocol tcp
has no request and no response, so a byte pipe whose backend never came up is
closed and nothing is invented for it. And a request whose backend has already
put bytes on the client's wire is past the point where a status line could be
written: it would land at an offset that is not a message boundary, and the
client would read it as the middle of the response it is already receiving.
Such a connection is closed, and the backend that produced an unfinished
response is charged to its breaker so the next client is routed elsewhere.
.PP
.B "A script runs when its runtime starts, not when it is created."
.B create
compiles the file \(em so one that does not parse is refused on the spot, by
the command that asked \(em and the worker runs it. Without
.B \-s
there is no worker yet and nothing has run: no top level, no
.BR socketley.subscribe ,
and no check of a global like
.BR tick_ms .
Those happen at the
.B start
that forks the worker, and a refusal there is reported by that command.
.PP
It used to be otherwise for a runtime kept in the daemon, where
.B create \-\-lua
loaded and ran the file at once. There is no such runtime now.
.SS Process Isolation
Every runtime runs in its own supervised worker process. There is no flag for
this and nothing to configure: it is where a server, a client, a proxy and a
cache each run.
.PP
A configuration file with
.B daemon_process
in it is refused rather than ignored, for either value, and the refusal names
the line to delete.
.PP
What used to argue for keeping a runtime inside the daemon was the Lua API:
thirteen calls were refused in a worker, and the count is now zero.
.RS
.TP
.B Reads of the map
.BR socketley.send ,
.BR .list ,
.B .get
and all four of
.BR socketley.cluster .
The daemon pushes the worker a replica of the map and a snapshot of the cluster
view, and these are answered out of them.
.TP
.B Changes to the map
.BR socketley.create ,
.BR .start ,
.BR .stop ,
.BR .remove .
Not out of the replica \(em being told what the map holds is not permission to
alter it. They are asked of the daemon over a socket of the worker's own and
they wait for the answer, so
.B "if socketley.create(...) then"
branches on what happened rather than on what was accepted. Measured: 9.95,
10.20 and 10.52 microseconds over three runs of 20000, on a path a script
reaches deliberately and never per message.
.TP
.B Moving a connection
.B socketley.pipe
across two runtimes, and
.BR self.route() .
Despite the name, neither hands a descriptor over: the origin keeps the socket
and relays the bytes, and the target answers as though the connection were its
own. Names travel; the connection does not.
.RE
.PP
One refusal survives, for one case: a worker forked by a daemon that could not
give it the control plane these need \(em an older build, or one that could not
create the socket. It says so, and says to restart the daemon.
.PP
A cross\-runtime request costs about 50 microseconds more than the same call in
one address space, measured, which a server doing
.B \-\-cache
pays on every command.
.PP
A script's
.B routes
declaration is not given up. It is produced by running the script, which happens
in the worker, and travels back to the daemon on the control socket the worker
already holds; the placeholder publishes it to the proxies in front of the
runtime. See
.BR "Declared Routes" .
.PP
The daemon forks and execs itself, hands the worker its configuration and its
routing table over a control socket, and keeps a placeholder for it in the
runtime map. What the CLI shows is unchanged:
.BR ps ,
.BR ls ,
.BR show ,
.B stats
and the Prometheus endpoint report the same figures they would for an
in\-process runtime, because the worker publishes them and the daemon stores
them where an in\-process runtime would have written them.
.PP
The port is bound in the worker; no descriptor is passed across the boundary. If
the worker dies the daemon restarts it and the runtime stays
.BR running ;
if it dies more than five times in ten seconds the runtime is left
.B failed
rather than being forked in a loop. Either way every other runtime is told to
let go of connections routed to or from it, because a killed worker runs no
teardown of its own.
.PP
Lua callbacks run inside the worker, against the worker's own state, and a
cache's
.B PUBLISH
reaches the worker's
.B subscribe
callbacks. The figures the daemon reports were read by the worker when they were
printed. See
.B "Out-of-process runtimes"
under
.BR LIMITATIONS .
.SS mTLS Flags
A proxy has two legs and each has its own half of mutual TLS. The names say
which: \fBclient\fR here always means "a TLS client", and the proxy is one of
those towards its backends while its own callers are ones towards it.
.TP
.BI "\-\-client\-ca " "path"
The CA that a certificate presented
.I by a client
must be signed by. This is mutual TLS on the proxy's own listening port, so it
requires
.BR \-\-tls " " \-\-cert " " \-\-key ;
without them it is refused, because on a cleartext port it would authenticate
nobody while
.B show
reported mutual TLS.
.TP
.BI "\-\-client\-cert " "path"
.TQ
.BI "\-\-client\-key " "path"
The certificate and key this proxy presents
.I to a backend
that asks for one. Both or neither: half a pair loads nothing, so it is
refused rather than leaving a leg that presents no certificate after the flag
said it would.
.PP
Any of
.BR \-\-ca ,
.B \-\-client\-cert
and
.B \-\-client\-key
also turns the backend leg itself to TLS, for every backend \(em none of the
three means anything against a cleartext peer, and honouring them without
encrypting would be the fail-open reading of all three. Use an
.B https://
backend address instead to encrypt one backend and leave its neighbours alone.
.PP
A TLS backend connection is
.B verified,
always: the chain against
.BI \-\-ca " file"
when one is given and against the system CA store otherwise, and the
certificate against the endpoint the request was routed to \(em a host name
matched against the certificate's DNS names with partial wildcards refused, an
address literal against its IP SAN. TLS 1.2 is the minimum version. There is
no option to disable any of that; a backend presenting a private-CA or
self-signed certificate needs that CA in
.BI \-\-ca " file"
or in the store OpenSSL reads. A backend whose certificate fails either check
is answered 502 (or retried, where
.B \-\-retry
is set) and is charged to its circuit breaker \(em the request is never sent
in the clear as a fallback.
.PP
Connections to a TLS backend are pooled and reused, like cleartext ones \(em a
run of forty requests to one
.B https://
backend costs one handshake rather than forty. The session is pooled with the
descriptor, so the reused connection is the same record layer part way through
the same stream, not a new one over an old socket. A pooled socket goes back
only to the endpoint it was opened to, which is also the name its certificate
was verified against and the name sent as SNI, and never survives a restart:
the trust store and client certificate the leg verifies against are rebuilt
when the runtime starts, and the pool is emptied when it stops. Before a socket
is reused, whatever is waiting on it is decrypted and inspected \(em a session
ticket or a key update is consumed and the connection kept, application data is
a response nobody asked for and the connection is dropped rather than handed to
the next client.
.B \-\-health\-check http
works alongside it and probes over TLS, verifying the backend's certificate to
the same standard the data path does \(em a backend it cannot verify is marked
unhealthy rather than reported healthy.
.SS Sidecar Mode
.TP
.B \-\-sidecar
Convenience shorthand that enables a service-mesh-ready configuration
in a single flag. Equivalent to:
.nf
\-\-health\-check tcp \-\-circuit\-threshold 5 \-\-circuit\-timeout 30 \-\-retry 2 \-\-drain
.fi
.IP
Individual flags can still be specified alongside
.B \-\-sidecar
to override the defaults (e.g.
.BR "\-\-sidecar \-\-health\-check http \-\-health\-path /ready" ).
.IP
.B \-\-sidecar
does not set
.BR \-\-backend\-timeout ,
but it runs into it: the deadline is 2 seconds by default, and the
circuit breaker
.B \-\-sidecar
turns on is what the expiry is charged to. Five slow requests in a row
open the circuit for 30 seconds. Add
.BI "\-\-backend\-timeout " seconds
alongside
.B \-\-sidecar
whenever the application behind it can take longer than 2 seconds to
send its first response byte, or
.B \-\-backend\-timeout 0
to switch the deadline off.
.PP
Example:
.nf
socketley create proxy myapp \-\-sidecar \-\-backend 127.0.0.1:3000 \\
    -p 8080 \-\-health\-check tcp -s

socketley create proxy slowapp \-\-sidecar \-\-backend 127.0.0.1:3000 \\
    -p 8080 \-\-backend\-timeout 30 -s
.fi
.SH CACHE FLAGS
.TP
.BI "\-\-listen unix:" "/path/to.sock"
Serve the cache on an AF_UNIX socket instead of
.B \-p
\(em
.B "redis\-cli \-s"
connects to it, and both the plaintext and RESP protocols are detected as on a
port. The full treatment is
.B \-\-listen
under
.BR "COMMON FLAGS" ;
refused with
.BR \-\-cluster ,
because members advertise ports out of a formula and RESP has no way to write a
filesystem path as a node address.
.IP
.B It is worth measuring before it is worth arguing about.
On this box, one cache and one redis\-server both loaded with the same keys,
eight client processes, GET:
.IP
.nf
socketley over TCP      2,969,848/s        redis over TCP    1,411,960/s
socketley over AF_UNIX  4,517,387/s        redis over AF_UNIX 2,319,573/s
.fi
.IP
Both sides gain about half again, which is the TCP and netfilter path coming
out of the measurement rather than anything either program does differently.
On a box with conntrack loaded and nftables rules on the OUTPUT hook \(em any
box running Docker \(em that path is not free, and a peer on the same machine
does not need it.
.TP
.B \-\-listen with \-\-shards binds one node per shard.
Shard
.I i
gets
.IR name #i.sock ,
inserted before the suffix, by the rule the daemon's own companion nodes
already follow. The pair used to be refused, and the refusal's reason was
sound for a design nobody was proposing: N processes binding
.I one
path unlink each other's node and only the last stays reachable. N paths are N
listeners.
.IP
.B The formula is the interface.
On a server every shard answers every request, so a caller takes any node. On a
.I cache
they are not interchangeable, and that is where choosing pays: a caller that
hashes the key the way
.B CLUSTER KEYSLOT
does and connects to that shard's node crosses nothing, which is the same
saving
.B \-\-cluster
buys between ports. A caller that picks a node at random still gets correct
answers \(em the shard crosses to the owner \(em at the crossing's cost.
.IP
.B "What that crossing costs, measured."
One daemon pinned to four cores, eight
.B redis-benchmark
processes of twenty-four connections each on the other twelve, GET at depth 1;
six interleaved passes with the order rotated, the first discarded, a clock
probe on both ends of the run. Client CPU is recorded beside every row because
a capped client inverts the sign of a comparison before it changes its size; it
never passed 5.8 of its twelve cores.
.IP
.RS
.TS
l r r r r.
arrangement	GET/s	x1	cores	us/op
_
1 shard	240,671	1.00	1.00	4.16
2 shards, one address	251,959	1.05	1.91	7.60
4 shards, one address	421,093	1.75	3.95	9.38
4 shards, slot-routed	673,017	2.80	3.65	5.43
.TE
.RE
.IP
.B "Behind one address the shards buy far less than the cores they take."
Two of them take 1.91 cores and return 1.05x; four take 3.95 and return 1.75x.
Half the keys, then three quarters, belong to a sibling, and the per-operation
cost of serving them roughly doubles \(em 4.16 us at one shard against 7.60 at
two and 9.38 at four. Point a client that picks the owner at the same four
shards and they return 2.80x at 5.43 us, which is most of what the crossings
were costing.
.IP
.B "How well the crossings batch decides the size of this, and the CLIENT decides that."
The mesh queues a crossing and flushes once per loop turn, so requests that
arrive together cross together. At the same 192 connections, twelve client
processes of sixteen connections give the two-shard arm 0.90x where eight of
twenty-four give it 1.05x \(em while the one-shard arm reads 235,421 and
240,671, unmoved. Measured on a floor of two processes and two rings, one
crossing at a time costs 8.13 us where thirty-two in a batch cost 282 ns
apiece. So a figure for this flag is a figure for an arrival pattern, and the
one above is the friendlier of the two shapes tried.
.IP
.B "The ceiling above that is the machine, not this flag."
Four shards routed by key reach 2.80x. Four independent
.B redis\-server
processes on the same four cores, driven the same way, reach 2.88x \(em and
socketley is ahead of them in absolute throughput at every count tried, 1.12x
at one and 1.09x at four. Neither scales linearly on loopback here, so a cache
that must scale wants
.B \-\-cluster
and a client that routes \(em and the 3.57x quoted for a server under
.B SERVER FLAGS
does not carry over.
.IP
Measured against the same cache on TCP with
.BR \-\-cluster ,
both clients routing by key, four shards: at least
.B 1.22x
in AF_UNIX's favour. Two later passes read higher and are not quoted, because
the TCP arm fell across them for a reason that was not the port pool
(TIME\-WAIT peaked at 1,235 of 28,231) and is not understood.
.IP
.B A remote client gets none of this.
AF_UNIX is local, so the benefit lands on hops between processes on one
machine. The hop that cannot take it today is a proxy in front of a
.I sharded
cache: a proxy does not read keys, so it would land on the wrong shard
.RI ( N \-1)/ N
of the time. Teaching it
.B cache_shard_of
would close that, and is not done.
.TP
.BI "\-\-persistent " "file"
Persistent storage file. Cache is saved on stop and loaded on start.
.IP
Written
.BR 0600 .
The dump holds whatever was cached, and because it is
.I loaded
on start it is not a record of what the cache held but what the cache will
hold. Earlier versions wrote it
.BR 0644 ,
readable by every account on the machine; a dump left by one of those is
narrowed when the cache next loads it, with a line in the daemon log.
.IP
The mode is all this can control. Replacing a file needs write permission on
the
.I directory
rather than on the file, so put
.I file
somewhere only this daemon's user can write \(em not a world\-writable
directory shared with other accounts.
.IP
.B With \-\-shards each shard writes its own slice, and the set survives a changed shard count.
One shard writes
.I file
itself; above one they are
.IR file .shard0 ,
.IR file .shard1
and so on. Each records the shard count it was written under and a fingerprint
of the function that decides which shard owns a key, so a cache dumped at four
shards and started at eight is redistributed rather than loaded into the wrong
slices. Every key survives; measured across 1\->4, 4\->1, 4\->8, 8\->4 and 4\->4.
.IP
A set that cannot be trusted refuses the start rather than serving part of
itself: a missing slice is named, and files that disagree about the shard count
\(em two runs' dumps in one place \(em are refused together. Slices left by a
run with more shards are removed by the dump that supersedes them.
.TP
.BI "\-\-persistent\-interval " "duration"
Journal every accepted write beside the dump, flushed on this cadence: an
acknowledged write reaches the kernel at most
.I duration
after it was accepted, so a killed or OOM\-killed worker loses at most the
last interval of writes instead of everything since the last stop or FLUSH.
Requires
.BR \-\-persistent ;
refused without it. Durations read as everywhere \(em
.BR 30s ", " 5m ", " 1h
\(em and a bare number is seconds.
.IP
The journal lives at
.IB file .journal
(per shard,
.IB file .shardN.journal ),
written
.BR 0600 .
A clean stop and the admin
.B FLUSH
command write the dump and cut the journal behind it; an unclean stop leaves a
tail, which the next start replays on top of the dump before serving. A tail
torn mid\-record by the crash is dropped at the last complete record, with the
dropped byte count in the daemon log. TTLs are journaled as absolute
deadlines, so a key that had five seconds left when it was written has
whatever remains of those five seconds after a replay \(em possibly nothing.
.IP
The flush is a write into the kernel's page cache and deliberately not an
fsync: the promise covers a dead process, not a dead machine. A power cut can
additionally lose what the kernel had not yet written back.
.IP
A journal with records written under a different shard count (or a different
ownership function) refuses the start rather than guessing at an order for
one key's history across two files: recover under the split that wrote it and
stop cleanly, or delete the
.B .journal
files to discard the tail.
.TP
.BI "\-\-mode " "mode"
Access control mode:
.B readonly
(GET, SIZE only),
.B readwrite
(GET, SET, DEL, SIZE \- default),
.B admin
(all commands including FLUSH, LOAD).
.IP
.B readonly
applies to this cache's own Lua script as well, and there it is quieter than on
the wire. A client that sends a write is told
.RB \(dq "denied: readonly mode" \(dq;
the twelve mutating
.B self.*
calls refuse by answering exactly what they answer when there was nothing to do
\(em
.B false
for nine of them and an empty string for
.B self.lpop
and
.BR self.rpop .
.B self.cache_mode()
is how a script tells the two apart, and the return values are listed with the
calls under
.BR "LUA API" .
.TP
.BI "\-\-maxmemory " "size"
Maximum memory for cache data. Supports K, M, G suffixes (e.g., 100M, 1G).
When exceeded, behavior depends on eviction policy. 0 means unlimited (default).
.IP
Suffixes are
.BR K ", " M " and " G
and nothing else: a value the parser cannot read whole is refused rather than
half\-read. That matters because the obvious near\-misses used to be accepted
silently \(em
.B 100MB
was taken as a hundred BYTES, and a value so large that the multiplication
wrapped came out as 0, which is the spelling for "unlimited". Both are errors
now.
.TP
.BI "\-\-eviction " "policy"
Eviction policy when maxmemory is reached:
.B noeviction
(reject writes, default),
.B allkeys\-lru
(evict least recently used),
.B allkeys\-random
(evict random keys).
.TP
.B \-\-resp
Force RESP2 protocol mode for all connections (Redis wire protocol).
By default, the cache auto-detects RESP mode when a connection's first
byte is '*'. With this flag, all connections use RESP regardless.
.TP
.BI "\-\-replicate " "host:port"
Connect as a follower to the specified leader cache. The follower receives
a full data dump on connect, then receives ongoing write command replication.
The follower operates in read-only mode.
.IP
A value containing a newline replicates whole. The link carries one command
per line, and a cache value is arbitrary bytes, so such a value used to end at
its first line break on the follower and the remainder was interpreted as a
command of its own \(em on the initial dump and on live writes alike, with both
sides reporting success. A command that cannot be written as a line is now
length-framed instead; one that can is sent unchanged, so a leader and a
follower of different versions agree on every dataset that previously worked.
A follower too old to understand a frame stores nothing for that command rather
than storing a truncated value.
.IP
Replication is
.I not
encrypted, in either direction, so it cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-tls :
that pair is refused at
.BR create ,
by
.BR edit " and " import ,
and again at every
.BR start .
A
.B \-\-tls
cache also refuses the
.B replicate
command, so it never becomes a leader either. See
.B TLS
under
.BR LIMITATIONS .
.IP
.B A sharded cache cannot be a replication leader, and says so.
A follower arrives as one connection and
.B SO_REUSEPORT
gives it to one shard; that shard must be the only writer its socket ever has,
and the other shards have no socket to it at all. Promoting it anyway would
send the follower one shard's writes and report success, so
.B replicate
is refused on a cache with more than one shard, naming the reason. The follower
protocol has no per\-shard sequence and no end\-of\-dump marker, which is what
fanning the stream out would need first.
.IP
A sharded cache may be a
.IR follower .
Every shard dials the leader and keeps the part of the stream its slice owns,
so it holds the leader's keys once rather than once per shard \(em at the cost
of each shard reading the whole stream.
.TP
.B \-\-tls
.TQ
.BI "\-\-cert " "file"
.TQ
.BI "\-\-key " "file"
.TQ
.BI "\-\-ca " "file"
Terminate TLS on this cache's listening port, exactly as a server runtime does
on its own.
.B \-\-tls
requires
.BI \-\-cert " file"
and
.BI \-\-key " file";
.BI \-\-ca " file"
additionally demands a certificate from every connecting client (mutual TLS).
RESP and the line protocol both run on the decrypted stream, so an existing
Redis client needs no change beyond speaking TLS. Described in full under
.BR "COMMON FLAGS" .
.PP
Default port: 9000
.TP
.BI "\-\-shards " "N"
Run the cache as
.I N
worker processes over one keyspace. Which shard owns a key is decided by
CRC16 of the key modulo 16384 \(em Redis's own hash slot function, hash tags
included \(em and each shard owns a contiguous range of those slots.
.IP
The
.I N
shards share one port under
.BR SO_REUSEPORT :
a client connects, the kernel gives it to whichever shard the four\-tuple
hashes to, and a key another shard owns is fetched from that shard over the
mesh. The cache is one store as far as any client can tell.
.IP
That crossing costs CPU, and with
.I N
shards a random key is foreign
.RI ( N \-1)/ N
of the time. Measured on this build, 8 client processes \(mu 50 connections,
three interleaved passes per arm, medians, requests per second per
.I server
core:
.IP
.nf
four shards, clients on the shared port     63,849   15.66 us/req
the same runtime with \-\-cluster, clients
  routed to the per\-shard ports             87,401   11.44 us/req
four independent single\-shard caches        87,663   11.40 us/req
.fi
.IP
Absolute levels are not comparable between machines or between sessions; the
ratio within one interleaved sweep is what is claimed. The same pair measured
again hours later on the same binary read 84,828 and 116,894 \(em every level
a third higher, the ratio 1.387 against 1.369.
.IP
.B "\-\-lua under \-\-shards: the script runs in every shard, and each one keeps its own slice."
The keyspace calls of the
.B self.*
table \(em
.BR self.set ,
.BR self.get ,
.B self.del
and the seventeen beside them \(em act on the keys
.I this
shard owns and answer a key it does not exactly as they answer a refusal:
.B false
for the mutating ones, an empty string for
.B self.get
and the two pops,
.B nil
for
.BR self.sadd .
A crossing is not available to them: the value has to be returned on the same
turn and the mesh answers on a later one.
.IP
For a callback that runs in all
.I N
processes \(em
.BR on_start ,
a timer \(em that is exactly right: the shards partition the work between
them and the keyspace is written once. For one driven by a connection \(em
.BR on_miss ,
.BR on_write ,
.B on_delete
\(em the key the callback was handed is always its own, and any
.I other
key it touches is somebody else's.
.B self.owns(key)
is how a script asks before it acts, and it is true for every key when the
cache is one process.
.IP
.B self.publish
is the exception that is not refused.  The message goes to every shard, over
the same mesh the wire's
.B PUBLISH
uses, because a subscriber is not a key and no shard owns it. What is this
shard's alone is the
.IR count :
the siblings report back on a later turn and this call answers on the turn it
was made.
.TP
.B \-\-public
Serve peers on other machines.  A cache has no password and answers every
client with everything it holds, so by default it serves only this machine:
a TCP peer whose address is not loopback is answered
.B \-DENIED
and closed before a byte of its is read.  This is Redis's protected mode, for
the reason Redis has it.
.B \-\-public
lifts it, and so does mutual TLS
.RB ( \-\-tls " " \-\-ca ),
where the client certificate is the credential.  A follower on another host
needs its leader to be public for the same reason.  Set at
.BR create ;
a running cache takes it through
.BR import .
.TP
.B \-\-cluster
Publish a routable slot map, so that a cluster\-aware client sends each key
straight to the shard that owns it and no crossing happens at all. Off by
default.
.IP
.B "Read this before you type it: a cache with \-\-cluster cannot sit behind a proxy used for access control."
Client\-side routing means the client connects to every shard
.IR directly .
This cache tells it where they are, and the address it gives is the one the
.I asking
connection arrived on. Put the cache behind a proxy and the asking connection
is the proxy's, so the map names the address the
.I proxy
reaches the cache on. Either the client can reach that address too \(em and the
proxy's rules have just been bypassed by a map the cache handed out \(em or it
cannot, and the client spends its retry budget on a connection refused.
Neither outcome produces a log line.
.IP
This is not a limitation of this implementation. It is what client\-side
routing is, and Redis Cluster has exactly the same property. There is no
setting that makes a routing client and a proxy enforcement point work
together; the choice is which of the two you want. Without
.B \-\-cluster
the cache is reached only through its one port, which is what a proxy in front
of it depends on.
.IP
.B Each shard binds its own port.
Shard
.I i
binds
.IR port + 1 + i ,
in addition to the shared port, which keeps working exactly as it does without
the flag. A client that connects to a per\-shard port has chosen that shard on
purpose, and a key it does not own is answered with
.BI \-MOVED " slot host:port"
rather than fetched. That is what lets
.BR "redis\-cli \-c" ,
.BR "redis\-benchmark \-\-cluster" ,
redis\-py, go\-redis, lettuce and node\-redis route for themselves.
.IP
.B A cache with \-\-cluster therefore needs port+1 .. port+N free, and refuses to start otherwise.
The address a client is sent to is a formula, computed by whichever shard
answers
.BR "CLUSTER SLOTS" ,
which has no way to learn whether its siblings bound their ports. A shard that
ran without one would be advertised anyway and every client would spend its
retries on a connection refused, so the start fails instead, naming the port
and naming this flag. Without
.B \-\-cluster
nothing but the shared port is bound and two four\-shard caches one port apart
work as they always did.
.IP
.B \-\-cluster is legal at any shard count.
At
.B "\-\-shards 1"
it publishes a cluster of one node on the base port and binds nothing extra,
so a client can be written once and deployed at any
.IR N .
.IP
.B CLUSTER SLOTS, SHARDS, NODES, INFO, MYID, KEYSLOT and COUNTKEYSINSLOT
are answered on every port and at every shard count, with or without this
flag \(em a client that asks deserves a straight answer.
.B CLUSTER INFO
reports
.B cluster_enabled:1
only when
.B \-\-cluster
was given; without it the reply is
.BR cluster_enabled:0 ,
and
.B CLUSTER SLOTS
publishes a single node holding every slot on the shared port, which is both
true and safe. There is no cluster bus, no failover, no slot migration and no
.BR ASK :
the map is a formula and there is one owner per slot for the life of the run.
.IP
.B On a per-shard port, a command that asks about the keyspace answers for that shard.
.BR DBSIZE ", " KEYS ", " SCAN ", " FLUSH " and " LOAD
report or act on the slice that shard holds, which is what a cluster client
means when it asks a node, and what
.B redis\-cli \-\-cluster call
adds up itself. On the shared port they still fan out across every shard and
merge, as they always have.
.B PUBLISH
fans out on both, because a subscriber on another shard is a subscriber.
.IP
A multi\-key command on a per\-shard port whose keys are all owned by one other
shard is answered with
.BR \-MOVED ;
one whose keys span shards is answered with
.BR \-CROSSSLOT .
The second is not pedantry: redirecting it would send the client to a shard
that would redirect it back.
.SS Cache Protocol
.PP
.B A pipelined batch is answered in full or the connection is closed,
and either way what it costs is bounded. One connection may owe 8 MiB; a batch's
replies are built before the loop returns to the ring, so nothing drains while it
runs and a batch has to fit inside that bound. Past it the client is closed, with
a line saying which one and how much it was owed.
.IP
So the depth worth using is the one whose replies fit: sixteen 256 KiB values is
4 MiB and is answered whole, sixty\(hyfour is 16 MiB and is cut. For small values
the bound is nowhere near \(em a 64 byte value at depth 16 is a kilobyte.
.IP
That bound used to be a number the runtime failed to keep. The batch loop did not
stop when the connection had been marked closing, and
.B flush_responses()
answers true for a closing connection \(em a skip its callers read as success \(em
so the 1 MiB cap on the reply buffer called a flush that did nothing and cleared
nothing, and the rest of the batch was still parsed and still answered into it.
Measured, one connection asking for a 256 KiB value and reading none of it:
.IP
.nf
                        before        after
    64 pipelined GET    +17 MB        +11 MB
   512 pipelined GET   +141 MB        +10 MB
  4,096 pipelined GET  +1,063 MB      +11 MB
 16,384 pipelined GET    +941 MB      +11 MB
.fi
.IP
A GET is about thirty bytes against a 256 KiB answer, so that was roughly 8,700 to
one, bounded by nothing but how many commands fit in one read. It is flat now.
.PP
.B A cache serving large values spends most of its time copying them,
and that is a property of the design rather than a defect to tune around.
Profiled at 256 KiB values, 16 clients, one command in flight, sampling the
worker process:
.IP
.nf
              userspace memcpy   kernel copy   page clearing
  GET               28%             18%            13%
  SET               42%             28%             \(em
.fi
.IP
A GET copies the stored value once, into the reply buffer, and the kernel copies
it again into the socket. A SET copies twice: once out of the receive ring into
the connection's parse buffer, and once out of that into the store. Removing the
GET copy means writing straight out of the store while a send is in flight, which
needs the store to promise that a value outlives it \(em a promise every mutating
path would have to keep, including the LRU eviction and TTL expiry that an
unrelated key's write can trigger. Removing a SET copy means accumulating a bulk
body outside the parse buffer, which the replication, sharding and cluster paths
would all have to carry. Neither is a local change and neither is made here.
.IP
.B Sharding is not the answer to it, which is worth knowing before reaching for
.BR \-\-shards .
Measured at 256 KiB values, 32 clients, against one shard:
.IP
.nf
                                   SET       GET    cpu/op
  1 shard                       14,903    14,503     66 us
  4 shards, client not routing  23,175     8,306    151 us
  4 shards, \-\-cluster, routed   19,920    19,960    158 us
.fi
.IP
A sharded cache without
.B \-\-cluster
relays a command that arrived on the wrong shard rather than answering \-MOVED,
which is what lets an ordinary client work against one at all \(em and relaying a
256 KiB value puts it through a second process, so GET comes out at 0.57 of the
single shard. With
.B \-\-cluster
and a client that routes, four shards buy 1.38x on GET and 1.34x on SET, against
the 3.2\(en3.6x at eight shards that small messages see. Either way the arm burns
about 2.4 times the CPU per operation.
.IP
So what is left for an operator is the value size itself. In one run at sixteen
clients: 64 bytes costs 4.4 us of CPU per operation, 4 KiB costs 4.8, 64 KiB
costs 11.4 and 256 KiB costs 40.8. No setting closes that gap.
.IP
.B The receive ring is sixty\(hyfour buffers of 64 KiB, and its footprint is what counts.
A provided\(hybuffer ring is touched in rotation, so what it costs is count times
size against the core's cache rather than the size of one buffer. The ring was
512 buffers of 4 KiB, and raising only the size \(em to a 32 MiB ring \(em cost
4.5% at 64 byte values and 24.5% at 256 KiB. Holding the footprint near the
core's cache instead, measured on one binary with only the two variables
differing, seven interleaved passes, 4 KiB x 512 against 64 KiB x 64:
.IP
.nf
  SET, 16 deep, 1 KiB values, 50 clients     +22.4%
  SET, 16 deep, 1 KiB values, 1000 clients   +24.5%
  SET, 16 deep, 4 KiB values                  +9.8%
  small values, 64 KiB and 256 KiB values     within 1.5%
.fi
.IP
The same size in 128 buffers, an 8 MiB ring, lost 12% at 256 KiB values, and
32 of them ran dry under 1000 pipelining clients. The knob exists to be
measured, and it has been.
.PP
.B A read of a key held as another type answers \-WRONGTYPE over RESP.
.B get
on a list, 
.B llen
on a string,
.B hgetall
on a sorted set: all of them used to answer as though the key were absent \(em
nil, 0, an empty array \(em which is worse than an error because a caller
believes it and goes to its database for something the cache already holds.
The probe runs only where a read has already missed its own container, so a hit
costs nothing and a store holding one type costs four
.B empty()
tests.
.B mget
is the exception, and Redis's: it answers nil per wrong\-typed key.
.PP
The text protocol keeps its own answers \(em "nil", "end", 0 \(em because
they are its documented contract and a text client is not a Redis client.

Connect via TCP and send commands (newline-terminated, all lowercase).
.PP
Nine of the commands listed below are RESP only, and the line protocol answers
"failed: unknown command" to them:
.BR client ,
.BR config ,
.BR eval ,
.BR evalsha ,
.BR hello ,
.BR psubscribe ,
.BR punsubscribe ,
.B script
and
.BR smismember .
.PP
Six of those are protocol and administration. The other three have one reason
between them: the line protocol has no spelling for a list of arguments, since
everything after the key is the value so that a value may contain spaces. That
leaves no way to write smismember's members, or the keys and arguments eval and
evalsha take after their script.
.PP
.B Strings:
.TP
.BI "set " "key value"
Store a key-value pair. Returns "ok", "error: type conflict", or
"error: out of memory".
.TP
.BI "get " "key"
Retrieve value. Returns value or "nil".
.TP
.BI "del " "key"
Delete key (any type). Returns "ok" or "nil".
.TP
.BI "exists " "key"
Check if key exists. Returns "1" or "0".
.TP
.BI "incr " "key"
Increment integer value by 1. Returns new integer.
.TP
.BI "decr " "key"
Decrement integer value by 1. Returns new integer.
.TP
.BI "incrby " "key delta"
Increment integer value by delta. Returns new integer.
.TP
.BI "decrby " "key delta"
Decrement integer value by delta. Returns new integer.
.TP
.BI "append " "key value"
Append to string value. Returns new length.
.TP
.BI "strlen " "key"
Return string length. Returns 0 if missing.
.TP
.BI "getrange " "key start end"
.TQ
.BI "substr " "key start end"
Return the substring between two inclusive offsets, negative counting from the
end, empty rather than an error when the range is inverted or the key absent.
.TP
.BI "setrange " "key offset value"
Overwrite from
.I offset
onward, zero-padding a shorter value up to it, and return the new length.
.TP
.BI "incrbyfloat " "key delta"
Add a floating-point delta and return the new value. Replicated as its
.I result
rather than as itself, because two builds may round the same addition
differently and a follower would then drift from its leader.
.TP
.BI "getdel " "key"
Return the value and remove the key in one step.
.TP
.BI "getset " "key newvalue"
Set new value and return old value. Returns old value or "nil".
.TP
.BI "mget " "key [key ...]"
Get multiple keys. Returns one value per line (nil if missing), then "end".
.TP
.BI "mset " "key value [key value ...]"
Set multiple key-value pairs atomically. Returns "ok".
.TP
.BI "type " "key"
Return type of key: "string", "list", "set", "hash", or "none".
.TP
.BI "keys " "pattern"
List all matching keys (glob patterns, "*" for all). Returns keys then "end".
.TP
.BI "setnx " "key value"
Set key only if it does not exist. Returns "1" (set) or "0" (not set).
.TP
.BI "setex " "key seconds value"
Set key with expiry. Equivalent to SET + EXPIRE. Returns "ok".
.TP
.B "set " "key value [nx|xx] [ex " sec "|px " ms "] [keepttl] [get]"
Over RESP,
.B set
takes Redis's options.
.B nx
writes only if the key is absent and
.B xx
only if it is there;
.BR ex " and " px
set a deadline in seconds or milliseconds. Either must be positive: zero or
less is refused and NOTHING is written, because a key stored with a deadline
that came out at zero and no deadline on it is immortal and its owner has been
told the write worked. An argument that is not a number is refused as one,
rather than as a syntax error about the word after it.
.IP
.B keepttl
leaves the deadline alone, which a plain
.B set
clears \(em it is what a session refresh needs, where the value changes and
the hour it has left does not. Asking for
.B keepttl
together with
.BR ex " or " px
is a contradiction and is refused rather than resolved in one of the two
directions.
.IP
.B get
answers the value that was replaced instead of "ok", nil when there was none
\(em and answers it even when
.B nx
refused the write, which is what makes it a read-and-write in one round trip.
A key holding something other than a string answers
.B \-WRONGTYPE
rather than nil, because a caller asking for the old value wants to hear that.
.TP
.BI "psetex " "key milliseconds value"
Set key with millisecond-precision expiry. Returns "ok".
.TP
.BI "scan " "cursor [match pattern] [count n] [valuematch glob] [withvalues]"
.TQ
.BI "     " "[type t] [field f]... [valuerange " min " " max "]"
Cursor-based key iteration. Returns next cursor (0 = done), then matching keys, then "end".
Use cursor=0 to start; repeat with returned cursor until 0 is returned.
.IP
.B A numeric range, which a glob cannot express.
.I valuerange
takes the bounds a zset range already speaks --
.BR \-inf ", " +inf ", and a leading " (
for an exclusive one -- and keeps the values that fall between them.  With
.I field
it is that field that has to be in range.
.IP
A value that is not a number matches NO range, not even
.BR "\-inf +inf" .
The whole of the value has to be the number: "30kg" is not thirty, and a reader
that stopped at the digits would be answering a question nobody asked.
.IP
.B More than one condition at once.
.I field
may be given more than once, up to four times, and the rule is one sentence: a
.I field
applies to the value predicates that follow it, until the next
.IR field .
So
.RS
.IP
.B "scan 0 field city valuematch berlin field age valuerange 25 40"
.RE
.IP
returns the hashes that satisfy BOTH -- a conjunction, not a choice, and a hash
missing either field satisfies neither.  The order does not change the answer.
.IP
Asking the same thing with two scans and an intersection in the client costs two
walks and the transfer of both result sets: measured over 50,000 records where
each condition keeps about a quarter and the pair keeps a fifteenth, 96.5 ms and
25,841 keys against 37.8 ms and 3,335.  It is also the only way to get an answer
that was true at one moment, because two scans are two walks of a keyspace that
need not have held still between them.
.IP
A value predicate given before any
.I field
asks about the key's own value, as it always has -- and mixing the two in one
scan matches nothing, because no key is both a string and a hash.
.IP
.B Asking what a FIELD of a hash holds.
.I field
walks the hashes and matches
.I valuematch
against what the named field of each one holds, rather than against the key's own
value -- which is the question a database is asked, for the layout records are
usually kept in.  A hash WITHOUT the field does not match, not even a predicate
that matches everything: that is a different answer from a hash whose field is
empty, and asking for the empty one is
.IR "valuematch \(dq\(dq" .
With
.I withvalues
it is the field's value that comes back beside the key.  From outside this is an
.B hget
per hash key: measured at 15.8 ms against 1.5 ms over 5,000 hashes, and that
against a client already pipelining and already using
.IR type .
.IP
.B Returning the value beside the key.
.I withvalues
returns key and value interleaved, the way
.B hscan
returns field and value, so a client that wanted the records does not send a
second
.B mget
for them: the walk had the value in hand when it matched the key.  It restricts
to strings for the same reason
.I valuematch
does.  Measured as one page used as a query it is worth about a sixth of the
operation, two round trips becoming one; over a whole keyspace it is worth less,
because the bytes move either way and only the second lookup and the extra
commands go.  Note that the reply is then sized by VALUES rather than by keys,
which is why
.I count
keeps its small default here and only a value predicate raises it.
.IP
.B Restricting to one type.
.I type
takes one of
.BR string ", " list ", " set ", " hash " or " zset
in any case, and returns only keys of that type.  It is close to free: the type
of a key is the map it is stored in, and the walk visits the five maps in a
fixed order anyway, so a restriction is a walk of one map instead of five.  A
name no type has matches nothing rather than everything.
.IP
.B Keep the options constant across an iteration.
.IR match ", " count ", " valuematch " and " type
are properties of the whole walk, not of one call: the cursor names the map the
walk has reached, so handing a cursor from a restricted walk back without the
restriction resumes partway through the keyspace and never returns what came
before it.  The reverse direction is caught -- a value or type walk given a
cursor from elsewhere starts its map again rather than trusting a bucket index
belonging to a different table.
.IP
.B Filtering on the value.
.I valuematch
returns only the keys whose VALUE matches the glob, which a client would
otherwise do by fetching the whole keyspace and discarding nearly all of it.
Only a string key holds a single value, so a list, set, hash or zset never
matches one, not even when it contains the pattern as a member.
With
.I valuematch
present,
.I count
bounds the keys EXAMINED rather than the keys returned and defaults to 1000:
the predicate is selective, so a page bounded by results would walk the whole
keyspace in a single call.
This is a scan and not an index. It costs about 8 per cent over a plain scan
per key examined, and nothing is maintained on the write path.
.IP
.B What an iteration guarantees.
A key that is present for the whole iteration is returned at least once,
provided the store did not grow enough to rehash its table during it. A key may
be returned more than once, and a key added or removed while the iteration is
running may or may not appear \(em so a caller that is counting should count
distinct keys rather than replies.
.IP
The exception is worth being precise about, because it is the one Redis does not
have. A rehash moves keys between buckets, and a key moved into a bucket this
iteration has already passed will not be returned by it. Redis avoids that with
reverse\-binary iteration, which needs a power\-of\-two bucket count; the tables
here use prime counts and cannot offer one. The cursor carries the bucket count
so a rehash is at least noticed: the map being walked restarts, which trades
duplicates \(em allowed above \(em for the skip, which is not.
.IP
.B count
is a hint, as it is in Redis. Whole buckets are returned, so a reply may carry
somewhat more or fewer keys than asked for.
.TP
.BI "size " "[type t]"
Return the number of stored keys.  Without
.I type
that is every type together, which is what
.B DBSIZE
answers over RESP; with it, the count of that one type and nothing else.
.IP
Both are O(1): the count of a type is the size of the map it is stored in, so
this is a lookup and not a walk.  Counting the same thing with
.B "scan ... type"
is the whole map walked and every key name sent back -- measured at 50.6 ms
against 0.107 ms over 50,000 hashes, and the gap widens with the keyspace
because only one side of it grows.  A name no type has counts nothing rather
than everything.
.PP
.B Lists (ordered, deque-backed):
.TP
.BI "lpush " "key value"
Insert at front. Returns "ok" or "error: type conflict".
.TP
.BI "rpush " "key value"
Insert at back.
.TP
.BI "lpop " "key"
Remove and return front element. Returns value or "nil".
.TP
.BI "rpop " "key"
Remove and return back element.
.TP
.BI "llen " "key"
Return list length.
.TP
.BI "lindex " "key index"
Return element at index (negative = from end).
.TP
.BI "lrange " "key start stop"
Return range of elements (multi-line, "end" terminator). Negative indices supported.
.TP
.BI "lset " "key index value"
Replace the element at
.IR index .
Answers "nil" when the key is absent and refuses an index past the end.
.TP
.BI "lrem " "key count value"
Remove up to
.I count
occurrences from the head, from the tail when negative, all of them when zero,
and return how many went.
.TP
.BI "ltrim " "key start stop"
Keep only the inclusive range and drop the rest; a range that selects nothing
removes the key, because an empty list is not a key here.
.TP
.BI "linsert " "key before|after pivot value"
Insert beside the first occurrence of
.IR pivot ,
returning the new length, 0 when the key is absent and \-1 when the pivot is.
.TP
.BI "blpop " "key [key ...] timeout"
.TQ
.BI "brpop " "key [key ...] timeout"
.B lpop
and
.B rpop
that wait. The keys are tried in the order given and the first with anything in
it answers at once; when none has, the caller waits until something is pushed
or
.I timeout
seconds pass.
.B 0
waits for ever. The reply names the key as well as the element, because with
several keys the caller cannot otherwise tell which one it came from.
.IP
.B The longest-waiting client is served first.
A worker pool that served its newest member would starve the one that has been
idle longest, which is the member most likely to be healthy.
.IP
.B Everything pipelined behind a blocking pop waits for it.
This is the only command here that answers on a later turn than the one it
arrived on, so the rest of that connection's input stays unparsed until the
wait ends \(em otherwise the second reply would overtake the first.
.IP
.B Not available on a sharded cache, and refused by name.
Every shard is a process of its own: the client waits in one and the key it
waits on can be owned by another, and the crossing protocol carries answers to
questions rather than a push arriving somewhere nobody asked. Refused the way
.B replicate
is, rather than blocking on a key this process will never see written.
.IP
What replicates is the pop and not the wait \(em a follower running
.B blpop
would be waiting for its own push and would never see the leader's.
.TP
.BI "rpoplpush " "source destination"
.TQ
.BI "lmove " "source destination left|right left|right"
Move one element from one end of
.I source
to one end of
.IR destination ,
returning it.
.B rpoplpush
is
.B "lmove src dst right left"
under its older name. The two keys may be the same, which rotates the list.
.IP
.B Every refusal happens before the element is taken.
This is the reliable-queue move: a job leaves the pending list and arrives in
the processing one, and a worker that dies between the two is why the pattern
exists. A wrong-typed destination, a memory limit or a destination on another
shard are therefore all decided first \(em the element is never in neither
list.
.IP
On a sharded cache the pair must hash to one shard.
.B \-CROSSSLOT
otherwise, which is Redis's answer and not a crossing: moving it would be a
write in one process that a refusal in another cannot undo.
.PP
.B Sets (unique unordered members):
.TP
.BI "sadd " "key member"
Add member. Returns "ok", "exists", or "error: type conflict".
.TP
.BI "srem " "key member"
Remove member. Returns "ok" or "nil".
.TP
.BI "sismember " "key member"
Check membership. Returns "1" or "0".
.TP
.BI "scard " "key"
Return set cardinality.
.TP
.BI "spop " "key"
Remove and return one member. The member is the one this process's iteration
order reaches first, so it replicates as the
.B srem
it turned out to be rather than as itself \(em a follower running
.B spop
would pick a different one.
.TP
.BI "srandmember " "key"
The same member, left in place.
.TP
.BI "smismember " "key member [member ...]"
One 1 or 0 per member, in the order asked.
.TP
.BI "smembers " "key"
Return all members (multi-line, "end" terminator).
.PP
.B Hashes (nested key-value maps):
.TP
.BI "hset " "key field value"
Set hash field. Returns "ok", "error: type conflict", or
"error: out of memory".
.TP
.BI "hget " "key field"
Get hash field value. Returns value or "nil".
.TP
.BI "hdel " "key field"
Delete hash field. Returns "ok" or "nil".
.TP
.BI "hlen " "key"
Return number of hash fields.
.TP
.BI "hgetall " "key"
Return all fields and values (multi-line "field value\\n", "end" terminator).
.TP
.BI "hkeys " "key"
.TQ
.BI "hvals " "key"
The fields alone, or the values alone.
.TP
.BI "hmget " "key field [field ...]"
One value per field, "nil" for a field that is not there.
.TP
.BI "hmset " "key field value [field value ...]"
Several fields at once.
.TP
.BI "hexists " "key field"
1 or 0.
.TP
.BI "hstrlen " "key field"
The length of a field's value, 0 when it is not there.
.TP
.BI "hincrby " "key field delta"
Add to a field read as an integer, creating it at
.I delta
if it is absent, and return the new value.
.TP
.BI "hsetnx " "key field value"
Set the field only if it does not exist. Returns 1 or 0.
.TP
.BI "hscan " "key cursor" " [match " pattern "] [count " n "] [valuematch " glob "]"
.TQ
.BI "sscan " "key cursor" " [match " pattern "] [count " n "]"
.TQ
.BI "zscan " "key cursor" " [match " pattern "] [count " n "]"
.B scan
over one key's collection instead of over the keyspace: start at 0, pass back
the cursor each call, and 0 again means the walk is done.
.I valuematch
is
.B hscan\'s
alone among the three: it asks what a field HOLDS, where
.I match
asks what it is called.  A set's members and a zset's scores are not values in
that sense, and both answer a syntax error rather than pretending -- one option
meaning three things is worse than one option fewer.
.I count
is a hint and defaults to 10, as it does on Redis; a collection smaller than
one bucket-load comes back whole with cursor 0.
.IP
The cursor carries the collection's bucket count as well as its position, so a
resume can tell whether the table it is going back into is the one it left. A
rehash between two calls restarts the walk rather than silently skipping the
elements that moved.
.IP
.B The order of fields is not the order they were set in.
A hash is backed by an unordered map here, so
.BR hgetall ", " hkeys " and " hvals
return the fields in an order this process decides. Redis keeps insertion order
for a small hash because a small hash is a listpack there, and stops keeping it
once the hash grows into a hashtable \(em so a client that depends on the order
is depending on Redis's encoding, not on its contract.
.PP
.B Sorted sets:
.PP
A score per member, read back in score order. The type is exclusive like the
other four: a key that is a string refuses
.BR zadd .
.TP
.BI "zadd " "key score member"
Add or re-score a member. Returns "1" when the member is new and "0" when it
was already there and only its score moved. A score may be fractional and is
parsed whole \(em
.B 1x
is refused, not read as 1.
.TP
.BI "zrem " "key member"
Remove a member. Returns "ok" or "nil". The key goes when its last member does.
.TP
.BI "zscore " "key member"
Return the score, or "nil".
.TP
.BI "zcard " "key"
Return the number of members.
.TP
.BI "zrank " "key member"
Rank counting from the lowest score, zero-based, or "nil".
.TP
.BI "zrevrank " "key member"
The same counting from the highest.
.TP
.BI "zrange " "key start stop" " [withscores]"
Members by rank, ends inclusive, negative counting from the last \(em the rules
.B lrange
has. With
.B withscores
each line is "member score". Terminated by "end".
.TP
.BI "zrevrange " "key start stop" " [withscores]"
The same walked from the highest score.
.TP
.BI "zincrby " "key delta member"
Add to a member's score, creating it at
.I delta
if it is not there, and return the new score.
.TP
.BI "zrangebyscore " "key min max" " [withscores]"
.TQ
.BI "zrevrangebyscore " "key max min" " [withscores]"
Members whose score falls in the range, which is what a sorted set is kept for.
Both ends are inclusive; a leading
.B (
makes one exclusive, and
.BR \-inf " and " +inf
are the open ends. The reverse spelling takes its bounds the other way round,
as Redis does.
.TP
.BI "zcount " "key min max"
How many fall in that range, with the same bound spelling.
.TP
.BI "zpopmin " "key"
.TQ
.BI "zpopmax " "key"
Remove and return the lowest-scoring member, or the highest, with its score.
Replicated as the
.B zrem
it turned out to be, for the reason
.B spop
gives above.
.TP
.BI "zremrangebyrank " "key start stop"
.TQ
.BI "zremrangebyscore " "key min max"
Remove a range by position or by score and return how many went. Emptying the
set removes the key.
.PP
.B Streams:
.PP
The stream commands are Redis 7.0's, with its arguments, its answers and its
errors; the line protocol splits their words on spaces, so a field or value
there cannot hold one, and answers an array one element a line followed by
"end". An empty stream is still a key, as in Redis.
.TP
.BI "xadd " "key [nomkstream] [maxlen|minid [=|~] n [limit n]] *|id field value ..."
Append an entry and return its id.
.B *
takes the clock in milliseconds, or the last id's with its sequence counted up
when the clock has not moved past it;
.I ms\-*
asks for the next sequence in that millisecond. An id at or below the last one
is refused. A replica and the journal receive the id it got, never
.BR * .
.B maxlen ~
removes whole blocks of up to a hundred entries only, as Redis removes whole
nodes, so it can leave more than asked for.
.TP
.BI "xlen " "key"
The number of entries.
.TP
.BI "xrange " "key start end [count n]"
.TQ
.BI "xrevrange " "key end start [count n]"
Entries in id order, or the reverse, one a line as "id field value ...".
.B \-
and
.B +
are the ends, a lone millisecond takes every sequence in it, and a leading
.B (
makes a bound exclusive.
.TP
.BI "xdel " "key id [id ...]"
Remove entries and return how many there were.
.TP
.BI "xtrim " "key maxlen|minid [=|~] n [limit n]"
Remove from the oldest end and return how many went.
.TP
.BI "xread " "[count n] [block ms] streams key [key ...] id [id ...]"
Entries past each id, one a line as "key id field value ...".
.B $
is the last id when the read was asked.
.B block
waits for the next
.B xadd
to any of the keys when there is nothing yet, 0 waiting for ever; a wait
inside
.B multi
answers "nil" at once, and a script may not wait at all. On a sharded cache
every key must belong to one shard, and a blocking read is served only by the
shard that owns its streams \(em through that shard's
.B \-\-cluster
port.
.TP
.BI "xgroup " "create key group id|$ [mkstream] [entriesread n]"
.TQ
.BI "xgroup " "setid|destroy|createconsumer|delconsumer key group ..."
Make, move or remove a consumer group, or one of its consumers.
.TP
.BI "xreadgroup " "group g consumer [count n] [block ms] [noack] streams key ... id ..."
.B >
delivers what the group has not delivered and records it as pending on the
consumer, unless
.BR noack ;
any other id re-reads that consumer's own pending entries.
.TP
.BI "xack " "key group id [id ...]"
Take entries off the pending list and return how many were on it.
.TP
.BI "xpending " "key group [[idle ms] start end count [consumer]]"
The pending list: a summary, or entries with their consumer, idle time and
delivery count.
.TP
.BI "xclaim " "key group consumer min-idle id ... [idle ms] [time ms] [retrycount n] [force] [justid] [lastid id]"
.TQ
.BI "xautoclaim " "key group consumer min-idle start [count n] [justid]"
Take over entries another consumer left pending, the second walking the list
from
.I start
and also answering the ids that
.B xdel
removed from under the group.
.TP
.BI "xinfo " "stream key [full [count n]] | groups key | consumers key group"
What a stream, its groups or a group's consumers hold.
.TP
.BI "xsetid " "key id [entriesadded n] [maxdeletedid id]"
Set the stream's last id and counters, which is how a replica is told them.
.PP
.B Pattern subscriptions:
.TP
.BI "psubscribe " "pattern [pattern ...]"
.TQ
.BI "punsubscribe " "pattern [pattern ...]"
Receive from every channel a glob pattern matches \(em
.BR * ,
.B ?
and character classes, the same matching
.B keys
uses. The delivery is
.B pmessage
and carries the pattern as well as the channel, because a client subscribed to
two patterns cannot otherwise tell which one matched.
.IP
.B Every subscribe verb confirms with a count.
.BR subscribe ", " unsubscribe ", " psubscribe " and " punsubscribe
each answer a three\-element reply naming the verb, the channel or pattern, and
how many subscriptions the connection now holds. A client library counts down
from that number to know its own batch is complete \(em these used to answer
"+OK", which gives it nothing to count.
.IP
.B A pattern costs a sharded cache its publish filter, and only then.
A publish normally wakes just the shards whose filter says they may hold a
subscriber of that channel. A pattern matches channels that do not exist yet,
so it has no bit to set; a shard holding one is woken by every publish instead.
Nothing changes for a cache that has no pattern subscriber.
.PP
.B Transactions with a check:
.TP
.BI "watch " "key [key ...]"
.TQ
.B unwatch
.B exec
does nothing if a watched key was written between the
.B watch
and it \(em over RESP the reply is a nil
.IR array ,
whose type is how a client tells "did not run" from "ran and returned nothing",
and over the text protocol the word
.BR aborted .
Either way the watch ends, so a retry starts from a fresh one.
.IP
Without this,
.B multi
is a batch: the commands run together and nothing checks that the world still
looks the way the client read it. With it, the compare\-and\-set loop that a
distributed lock or a counter needs becomes possible.
.IP
.B A write to a different key does not abort.
The mark is set in one place, before the switch that runs the command, and it
is driven by a list of read\-only commands rather than a list of writes: a
write missing from a list of writes would be a transaction committing over a
change it never saw, silently, while a read missing from the read list is one
retry too many. Commands that can touch anything \(em
.BR mset ", " del
over several keys,
.BR flush ", " eval
\(em mark every watcher rather than a list.
.IP
.B watch
inside
.B multi
is refused, as it is on Redis: a watch taken after the queue started could not
be checked against the reads that decided what to queue.
.PP
.B Scripting:
.PP
Lua, over RESP, with the script coming from the client.
.TP
.BI "eval " "script numkeys key... arg..."
.TQ
.BI "evalsha " "sha1 numkeys key... arg..."
Run
.IR script ,
with the declared keys in
.B KEYS
and the rest in
.BR ARGV ,
both 1-based.
.B redis.call
runs a command and raises what it answers if that is an error;
.B redis.pcall
hands the error back as a table with
.BR err .
.BR redis.status_reply ", " redis.error_reply " and " redis.sha1hex
are there;
.B redis.log
is accepted and writes nothing.
.IP
A command that would outlive the script on the caller's connection is refused
with
.BR "\-ERR This Redis command is not allowed from script" ,
as Redis refuses it:
.BR eval ", " evalsha ", " script ", " multi ", " exec ", " discard ,
.BR watch ", " unwatch ,
the four subscribe commands,
.BR quit ", " reset ", " hello ", " auth " and " client .
A blocking pop answers at once, as its non-blocking twin would.
.IP
The return value is converted as Redis converts it: a number becomes an
integer truncated towards zero, a string a bulk, a table an array
.I up to its first hole
\(em Lua's own rule for where an array ends \(em
.B false
a nil and
.B true
the integer 1. A table carrying
.B err
or
.B ok
is an error or a status rather than a list.
.IP
.B The interpreter is not the one \-\-lua uses, and that is the point.
.B COMMON FLAGS
says of that one that it is not a sandbox: it has
.BR io ", " os ", " package
and the whole of
.B base
open, which is the right trade for a script the operator wrote and the wrong
one for a script that arrived over the network. This state is opened the other
way round \(em
.BR base ", " string ", " table " and " math ,
with every loader removed by name. Under LuaJIT the one that decides it is
.BR ffi :
.B require("ffi")
is arbitrary memory access, which is a shell on the machine, so
.B require
is not there to reach it with. A client's script also cannot see the
operator's globals, because the two states never meet.
.IP
.B A script holds the event loop of its shard while it runs.
So it runs under the same count hook a Lua callback does, and a runaway one is
stopped with
.B "a script exceeded the 10000ms deadline"
rather than holding every other client on that shard for ever. The bound is
.B callback_timeout_ms
or
.BR SOCKETLEY_LUA_TIMEOUT_MS ,
as it is for callbacks.
.IP
.B On a sharded cache a script is routed by the keys it declares.
Not a convention here but the routing itself: the shard that owns
.B KEYS[1]
runs it, keys belonging to two shards answer
.BR \-CROSSSLOT ,
and a script declaring no keys runs wherever it was asked. A script that
reaches a key it did not declare is reaching into whatever store the shard it
landed on happens to have.
.TP
.BI "script load " "script"
.TQ
.BI "script exists " "sha1 [sha1 ...]"
.TQ
.B "script flush"
The script cache.
.B eval
loads as well as runs, so a client may try
.B evalsha
first and fall back to
.B eval
on
.BR \-NOSCRIPT ,
which is the usual shape.
.IP
.B The cache is per shard, as it is per node on Redis Cluster.
A sharded cache's shared port hands each connection to whichever shard the
kernel picks, so a
.B script load
sticks to one of them.
.B eval
is what makes this self-healing; a client that wants
.B evalsha
to work everywhere sends
.B script load
to each per-shard port, which is what a cluster client does with each master.
.PP
.B Connection and introspection:
.PP
The set a client library sends before it sends anything of its own. Answering
them is what lets a connection finish being made; refusing them by name is what
lets a client fall back rather than fail.
.TP
.BI "hello " "[protover]"
The handshake. RESP2 only:
.B hello
with no argument or with
.B 2
answers a map naming the server, the version, the protocol, this connection's
id, whether the cache is clustered and whether it is a replica.
.B hello 3
is refused with
.BR \-NOPROTO ,
which is what a Redis before 6 answers and what every client falls back from
\(em an unknown-command error is not a protocol negotiation failure and need
not be handled as one.
.TP
.B info
The fields a monitor reads back: version, connected clients, memory, commands
processed, role, whether clustering is on, and the key count. On a sharded
cache the key count is
.I this shard's
slice, for the reason
.B dbsize
answers that way on a per-shard port.
.IP
The replication section is the one that differs by role, because half of its
fields have no meaning on the other side.
.B role
answers
.BR master " or " slave .
A follower adds
.BR master_host ,
.BR master_port ,
.B master_link_status
and
.BR master_sync_in_progress ;
a leader adds
.B connected_slaves
\(em the number of followers whose connection is live, not the number that ever
attached.
.IP
.B master_link_status
reads
.B up
once a full sync has finished and not when the socket opens, and
.B master_sync_in_progress
separates the two reasons it can read
.BR down :
no leader was reached at all, or a dump is still arriving. A follower whose
target was never usable leaves
.B master_host
empty and
.B master_port
at zero, which is the honest answer to "where".
.TP
.BI "config get " "parameter"
.B maxmemory
answers with the configured limit;
.BR save " and " appendonly
answer empty, which is true here; anything else answers an empty array rather
than an invented value.
.TP
.B command
An empty array. A client asks
.B command
for a table of arity and flags, this cache keeps no such table, and saying so
is more useful than a table that is wrong.
.TP
.BI "echo " "message"
.TQ
.BI "select " "index"
.B select 0
succeeds because there is one keyspace here; any other index is refused rather
than ignored, so a client addressing database 3 learns it rather than quietly
sharing database 0 with everybody else.
.TP
.BI "client " "id|getname|setname|setinfo"
.B setname
and
.B setinfo
are what redis\-py and node\-redis send on connect and are accepted;
.B id
answers this connection's descriptor.
.TP
.B auth
Refused by name. There is no password on a cache, and a silent success would
tell a client it had authenticated.
.PP
.B Transactions:
.PP
Grouping, not rollback. Between
.B multi
and
.B exec
a command is queued and answered "queued"; nothing has run, and a second
connection reading the same key still sees the old value. EXEC then runs the
queue back to back on an event loop that is single\-threaded, so no other
connection's command lands in the middle of it. A command that fails inside
.B exec
fails alone \(em the ones before it stay.
.TP
.B multi
Begin queueing. Nested
.B multi
is refused.
.TP
.B exec
Run the queue in order, then "end". Refused without a
.BR multi .
.TP
.B discard
Throw the queue away.
.PP
.B TTL / Expiry:
.TP
.BI "expire " "key seconds"
Set time-to-live. Returns "ok" or "nil" (key not found). A
.I seconds
of zero or less is a deletion, not an error: the key goes and the answer is
"ok", which is what Redis does and what a caller whose own arithmetic came out
at zero means.
.TP
.BI "ttl " "key"
Return remaining seconds, -1 (no ttl), or -2 (not found).
.TP
.BI "persist " "key"
Remove expiry. Returns "ok" or "nil".
.TP
.BI "pexpire " "key milliseconds"
Set millisecond-precision TTL. Returns "1" (set) or "0" (key not found). Zero
or less deletes, as with
.BR expire .
.TP
.BI "pttl " "key"
Return remaining TTL in milliseconds, -1 (no ttl), or -2 (not found).
.TP
.BI "expireat " "key unix_seconds"
Set expiry to an absolute Unix timestamp (seconds). Returns "1", or "0" if
there is no such key. A timestamp already past deletes the key and answers
"1".
.TP
.BI "pexpireat " "key unix_ms"
Set expiry to an absolute Unix timestamp in milliseconds. Same answers as
.BR expireat .
.PP
All four saturate rather than refusing: a deadline past the range of the
clock means "never", and one past its other end means "already gone". Redis
refuses these with an error instead, which is the one place this family
answers differently on purpose \(em the number a client sent is honoured as
far as the clock goes rather than rejected.
.PP
.B Pub/Sub:
.TP
.BI "subscribe " "channel"
Subscribe to a channel. Returns "ok". Messages are delivered as
"message <channel> <payload>\\n".
.TP
.BI "unsubscribe " "channel"
Unsubscribe from a channel. Returns "ok".
.TP
.BI "publish " "channel message"
Publish a message to all subscribers. Returns number of subscribers reached.
.PP
.B Memory Management:
.TP
.BI "maxmemory " ""
Query current maxmemory setting. Returns bytes or "0" (unlimited).
.TP
.BI "memory " ""
Query current memory usage in bytes.
.PP
.B Admin (requires --mode admin):
.TP
.BI "flush " "[path]"
Save cache to file (uses --persistent path if omitted).
.TP
.BI "load " "[path]"
Load cache from file.
.PP
Type enforcement: a key can only hold one data type. Writing to a key of a different type returns "error: type conflict".
Under \fB--maxmemory\fR a write that does not fit is refused with "error: out of memory" (RESP: "OOM command not allowed when used memory > 'maxmemory'"), and the key it would have written is left exactly as it was.
That includes a write over a key that already exists: overwriting a value, appending to it or replacing a hash field is checked against the limit like any other write.
Multi-line responses (lrange, smembers, hgetall) terminate with "end\\n".
.SS RESP Protocol (Redis Compatibility)
The cache supports the RESP2 wire protocol for compatibility with Redis clients
(redis-cli, Redis client libraries). RESP mode is auto-detected when a
connection's first byte is '*', or can be forced with
.BR \-\-resp .
.PP
Supported Redis commands, case-insensitive. A command not listed here is
answered with an error naming it, never silently ignored.
.PP
.B Strings
.RS 2
SET (EX, PX, NX, XX, KEEPTTL, GET), GET, GETSET, GETDEL, GETRANGE, SUBSTR,
SETRANGE, SETNX, SETEX, PSETEX, MGET, MSET, APPEND, STRLEN, INCR, DECR,
INCRBY, DECRBY, INCRBYFLOAT
.RE
.PP
.B Keys
.RS 2
DEL, UNLINK, EXISTS, TOUCH, TYPE, KEYS, SCAN, DBSIZE, RANDOMKEY, RENAME,
RENAMENX, COPY, FLUSHALL, FLUSHDB, EXPIRE, PEXPIRE, EXPIREAT, PEXPIREAT, TTL,
PTTL, EXPIRETIME, PEXPIRETIME, PERSIST
.PP
RENAME carries the key's expiry with it, and so does COPY \(em renaming or
copying is not a way to make an expiring key permanent. RENAME and COPY name
two keys, so on a sharded cache both must belong to one shard and keys that do
not are refused with
.BR \-CROSSSLOT ;
a key cannot be half-moved. FLUSHALL and FLUSHDB are the same command here,
because this cache has one keyspace and no SELECT-able databases to leave
behind.
.RE
.PP
.B Lists
.RS 2
LPUSH, RPUSH, LPOP, RPOP, LLEN, LRANGE, LINDEX, LSET, LREM, LTRIM, LINSERT,
LMOVE, RPOPLPUSH, BLPOP, BRPOP
.RE
.PP
.B Sets
.RS 2
SADD, SREM, SISMEMBER, SMISMEMBER, SCARD, SMEMBERS, SPOP, SRANDMEMBER, SSCAN,
SINTER, SUNION, SDIFF, SINTERSTORE, SUNIONSTORE, SDIFFSTORE, SMOVE
.PP
Set algebra reads whole sets and answers one thing, so on a sharded cache it
cannot be split across shards the way MGET or DEL can \(em an intersection
computed on two shards separately is not an intersection. Every key must
therefore belong to one shard, and keys that do not are refused with
.BR \-CROSSSLOT ,
which is what Redis Cluster answers and for the same reason. Use a hash tag
.RB ( "{user}:seen" )
to place them together.
.RE
.PP
.B Hashes
.RS 2
HSET, HSETNX, HGET, HMSET, HMGET, HDEL, HLEN, HEXISTS, HKEYS, HVALS, HGETALL,
HSTRLEN, HINCRBY, HSCAN
.RE
.PP
.B Sorted sets
.RS 2
ZADD, ZREM, ZSCORE, ZCARD, ZCOUNT, ZINCRBY, ZRANK, ZREVRANK, ZRANGE,
ZREVRANGE, ZRANGEBYSCORE, ZREVRANGEBYSCORE, ZPOPMIN, ZPOPMAX,
ZREMRANGEBYRANK, ZREMRANGEBYSCORE, ZSCAN
.RE
.PP
.B Streams
.RS 2
XADD, XLEN, XRANGE, XREVRANGE, XREAD (BLOCK), XDEL, XTRIM, XSETID,
XGROUP (CREATE, SETID, DESTROY, CREATECONSUMER, DELCONSUMER), XREADGROUP (BLOCK,
NOACK), XACK, XPENDING, XCLAIM, XAUTOCLAIM, XINFO (STREAM [FULL], GROUPS,
CONSUMERS)
.PP
Replies and errors are Redis 7.0's, compared against a redis-server by
.BR test_cache_streams_vs_redis.sh ;
XINFO STREAM's radix-tree-nodes counts no tree, since the entries sit in blocks
indexed by a sorted array. XADD, XGROUP CREATE and CREATECONSUMER are
refused with OOM at the memory limit, as Redis refuses its denyoom commands;
XREADGROUP and XCLAIM are not, which is also Redis's rule. An XREAD naming
streams of several shards is answered
.BR \-CROSSSLOT .
.RE
.PP
.B Pub/Sub
.RS 2
SUBSCRIBE, UNSUBSCRIBE, PSUBSCRIBE, PUNSUBSCRIBE, PUBLISH
.RE
.PP
.B Transactions
.RS 2
MULTI, EXEC, DISCARD, WATCH, UNWATCH
.RE
.PP
.B Scripting
.RS 2
EVAL, EVALSHA, SCRIPT LOAD, SCRIPT EXISTS, SCRIPT FLUSH
.RE
.PP
.B Connection and server
.RS 2
PING, ECHO, AUTH, HELLO, SELECT, QUIT, RESET, CLIENT (GETNAME, SETNAME, ID,
REPLY, SETINFO), CONFIG GET/SET, COMMAND, INFO, CLUSTER
.RE
.PP
.B Where the answers differ from Redis on purpose
.RS 2
The RESP array limit is 1024 arguments, not 1024*1024: args[] is a stack array
and a million views would be sixteen megabytes of it. A command over the limit
is refused with an error that names the limit, and the connection is closed --
the stream is framed, but the arguments were never stored, so it cannot
continue from a message it did not take.
.PP
Lua scripts share one interpreter state, so its globals are sealed: writing a
global raises an error naming the variable, and the globals table itself is
unnamed, so rawset and table.insert cannot reach it either. Use
.B local
for everything, including functions \(em
.B "local function f() end"
rather than
.BR "function f() end" ,
which is what Redis requires too.
.PP
One difference from Redis remains, deliberately: reading an undefined global
returns nil here, as ordinary Lua does, where Redis raises. Blocking the WRITE
is what closes the channel between two clients; erroring on the read only
catches typos, and it would break a script that tests a global to see whether
it is there.
.RE
.PP
Example with redis-cli:
.nf
redis-cli -p 9000 SET mykey myval
redis-cli -p 9000 GET mykey
redis-cli -p 9000 EVAL "return redis.call('GET', KEYS[1])" 1 mykey
redis-cli -p 9000 SUBSCRIBE news
.fi
.SS Database Backend
Cache runtimes support four Lua callbacks for DB-backed operation.
Attach a Lua script with
.BR \-\-lua
and implement whichever callbacks you need using any Lua DB library
(luasql-mysql, luasql-postgres, lsqlite3, etc.).
.PP
.TS
tab(|);
l l lw(30).
Callback|Signature|When called
_
on_miss(key)|\(-> value [, ttl_seconds]|GET returns nil \(em fetch from DB
on_write(key, value, ttl)|\(-> nothing|T{
.ad l
After successful SET/SETEX/SETNX/MSET
T}
on_delete(key)|\(-> nothing|After DEL \(em delete from DB
on_expire(key)|\(-> nothing|After TTL sweep removes key
.TE
.PP
\fBon_miss\fR may return two values (Lua multi-return): the fetched string and an
optional TTL in seconds.
If the value is nil or empty, the GET returns nil to the client normally.
If a value is returned, it is inserted into the cache and returned to
the client transparently (read-through / lazy population).
.PP
\fBon_write\fR is called synchronously on the event loop thread.
For write-heavy loads, use the write-behind pattern: buffer writes in Lua
inside \fBon_write\fR and flush to the DB in \fBon_tick\fR every few seconds.
This decouples SET latency from DB latency entirely.
.PP
Example (SQLite write-behind):
.nf
tick_ms = 2000
local db, pending = nil, {}

function on_start()
    local sq = require("lsqlite3")
    db = sq.open("/tmp/cache.db")
    db:exec("CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS cache_kv (key TEXT PRIMARY KEY, value TEXT)")
end

function on_miss(key)
    for row in db:nrows("SELECT value FROM cache_kv WHERE key=" .. db:quote(key)) do
        return row.value, 300  -- value + 300 s TTL
    end
end

function on_write(key, value, ttl)
    pending[key] = value  -- buffer
end

function on_tick(dt)
    for k, v in pairs(pending) do
        db:exec("INSERT OR REPLACE INTO cache_kv(key,value) VALUES("..db:quote(k)..","..db:quote(v)..")")
    end
    pending = {}
end

function on_delete(key)
    db:exec("DELETE FROM cache_kv WHERE key="..db:quote(key))
end
.fi
.PP
See
.B examples/cache/db-backend.lua
for a full example with MySQL, Postgres, and SQLite backends.
.SS Replication
Cache runtimes support leader-follower replication.
.PP
The follower connects to the leader and receives a full data dump, then
ongoing write commands are forwarded in real-time. Followers are read-only.
.PP
.nf
# Start leader cache
socketley create cache leader -p 9000 -s

# Start follower
socketley create cache follower -p 9001 --replicate 127.0.0.1:9000 -s

# Writes on leader are replicated to follower
echo "set key val" | nc localhost 9000
echo "get key" | nc localhost 9001    # Returns "val"
.fi
.PP
Example with netcat:
.nf
echo "set user:1 alice" | nc localhost 9000
echo "get user:1" | nc localhost 9000
echo "lpush queue task1" | nc localhost 9000
echo "hset user:1 name alice" | nc localhost 9000
echo "expire user:1 300" | nc localhost 9000
echo "subscribe news" | nc localhost 9000
.fi
.SH STORAGE FLAGS
A
.B storage
runtime serves the S3 API over one directory, so that unmodified S3 clients
(boto3, the AWS CLI, rclone, SDKs) can put, get, list and delete objects on this
machine's disk:
.PP
.nf
socketley create storage media \-p 9000 \-\-dir /srv/objects \\
    \-\-access\-key AKIAEXAMPLE \-\-secret\-key wJalrXUtnFEMI \-s
.fi
.PP
It runs in a worker process of its own like every other runtime, speaks
HTTP/1.1 with path\-style addressing (\fB/bucket/key\fR) and, given
.BR \-\-domain ,
virtual\-hosted style (\fBbucket.domain/key\fR) as well, answers errors as S3 XML
(\fB<Error><Code>\fR\|.\|.\|.) and stamps every reply with an
.BR x\-amz\-request\-id .
The flags below are the runtime's own;
.BR \-p ,
.BR \-\-listen ,
.BR \-\-tls ,
.BR \-\-cert ,
.BR \-\-key ,
.BR \-\-ca ,
.BR \-\-cpus ,
.BR \-\-max\-connections ,
.B \-\-idle\-timeout
and the common flags apply as documented under
.BR "COMMON FLAGS" .
.TP
.BI \-\-dir " path"
Where the buckets live. Required, created if it does not exist, and made
absolute against the caller's directory. A runtime is one process on one
directory, or with
.B \-\-shards
several that share it: two runtimes on one path would append to each other's journals.
.TP
.BI \-\-access\-key " key"
.TQ
.BI \-\-secret\-key " secret"
The one pair of credentials requests are signed with (AWS Signature Version 4). They go
together: one without the other is refused. The pair is kept in the state file
and printed by
.BR show ;
prefer
.B \-\-credentials
where that matters.
.TP
.BI \-\-credentials " file"
A file of several pairs, one
.IB access : secret
per line. Blank lines and lines starting with
.B #
are skipped, and the secret is everything after the first colon, so it may hold
one. A line
.BI [ id ]
begins a user, whose settings and keys follow it until the next; a file without
one is a single owner, as described under
.BR Users .
The path is made absolute against the caller's directory. The file is
read at start, refused at
.B create
and
.B start
if it does not parse or holds nothing, and read again when it changes
(checked at most once a second, on the next request): a key added to it works at
once, a key taken out stops, and a file that has gone, is empty or no longer
parses leaves the keys in force until it is repaired, with a warning in the log,
so a half\-written edit locks nobody out. A user's policy file is watched the same
way. Connections are not dropped by a reload. A key given with
.B \-\-access\-key
is one of the set too.
.TP
.BI \-\-sse\-key\-file " file"
The master key every object's data key is wrapped with under server\-side
encryption with S3\-managed keys (SSE\-S3): a file of exactly 32 bytes, or of 64 hex
digits and an optional newline, that only its owner may read. A file the group or
others may read, or of any other length, is refused at
.B create
and
.BR start ,
so the key is never on a command line or in the state file \(em only its path,
made absolute against the caller's directory. It is read at every start. Without
it a request for
.B AES256
and a bucket default that would need one are refused with
.BR "501 NotImplemented" ;
SSE\-C needs no master key. Keep the file: an object sealed under one key is not
opened by another, and is answered
.B "500 InternalError"
saying so. See
.BR "Server\-side encryption" .
.TP
.BI \-\-domain " name" [, name ...]
The domains the endpoint is reached under, such as
.BR s3.example.com :
a request whose
.B Host
is
.IB bucket . domain
(with or without a port) addresses that bucket, and its whole path is the key.
A Host equal to one of the domains, an address, or any other name is path style
as without the flag, so both styles are served at once. Names are DNS names,
compared without regard to case; anything else is refused at
.BR create .
See
.BR "Virtual\-hosted\-style addressing" .
.TP
.BI \-\-website\-domain " name" [, name ...]
The domains buckets' websites are reached under, such as
.BR web.example.com :
a request whose
.B Host
is
.IB bucket . domain
is a browser's request to that bucket's website rather than to the S3 API, and is
answered from the bucket's WebsiteConfiguration. Names are checked as for
.BR \-\-domain ,
and a Host under both is the website's. See
.BR "Website hosting" .
.TP
.BI \-\-fsync " always" | interval | never
When a write a client was answered for is on the disk.
.B always
(the default) syncs the object's file and its directory before the answer, so an
answered write survives a crash of the machine.
.B interval
answers without those syncs and syncs the whole filesystem every
.BR \-\-fsync\-interval ;
.B never
leaves it to the kernel's own writeback. Either way a crash of the process loses
nothing, and a stop syncs everything before it says it was clean. See
.BR Durability .
.TP
.BI \-\-sync " never" | active | per\-user
Which
.B sync
runtimes the storage makes to pull its users' git repositories:
.B never
makes none (the default),
.B active
one for the whole storage,
.B per\-user
one for each user. Each sync runtime is a process of its own, which is why none
is made unless asked for. Applied by
.BR import ;
a change takes the runtimes a previous setting made away at the storage's next
start. See
.B Git repositories
under the storage runtime.
.TP
.BI \-\-fsync\-interval " duration"
How often
.B \-\-fsync interval
syncs the filesystem, 1 second to an hour; default
.BR 1s .
.TP
.BI \-\-notify\-targets " file"
The webhooks a bucket's event notifications may be sent to, one
.I name url
per line, with an optional third field
.BI ca= file
naming the CA an https webhook's certificate is checked against (the system's
when absent); blank lines are skipped, and so is a field that begins with
.B #
and the rest of its line:
.RS
.PP
.nf
# name   url
audit    http://10.0.0.5:8080/s3\-events
thumbs   https://thumbs.internal/hook ca=/etc/ssl/internal\-ca.pem
feed     cache://events/stream/s3
live     cache://events/channel/s3
panel    server://dashboard
bus      mqtt://broker/storage/events
oncall   mail://m/ops@example.com,dev@example.com
.fi
.RE
.IP
A url may also name another runtime of the daemon:
.BI cache:// runtime /stream/ key
appends each event to that cache's stream as an entry of one field
.BR event ,
.BI cache:// runtime /channel/ channel
publishes it on that channel, and
.BI server:// runtime
broadcasts it to the server's clients, and
.BI mqtt:// runtime / topic
publishes it on that broker's topic at QoS 1, answered once the broker holds
it, and
.BI mail:// runtime / address\fR[,\fIaddress\fR...]
mails it to those addresses through that mail runtime, its subject what the
event is, answered once the message is in the spool; such a line takes no
.BR ca= .
An event a cache refuses or a runtime that is not running is retried like a
webhook that does not answer.
A configuration names one as
.BI arn:socketley:webhook::: name\fR.
The file is refused at
.B create
and
.B start
if a line does not parse and is read at every start; its host names are resolved
off the loop and again when a connection is refused (a host that does not resolve
is logged and its events wait until it does). Without it every destination is
refused. See
.BR "Event notifications" .
.TP
.BI \-\-replication\-targets " file"
The S3 endpoints a bucket's replication may write to, one
.I name url region access_key secret
per line, with an optional sixth field
.BI ca= file
for an https endpoint, comments and blank lines as in
.BR \-\-notify\-targets :
.RS
.PP
.nf
# name   url                          region     access key            secret
backup   https://s3.backup.internal   eu\-west\-1  AKIAREPLICATOR000001  wJal.../replicator
.fi
.RE
.IP
A configuration's
.B Role
names one by its last path segment, as
.BI arn:aws:iam:: account :role/ name\fR;
every copy to that endpoint is signed with the line's key and secret for the
line's region. The file is refused at
.B create
and
.B start
if a line does not parse and is read at every start;
.B show
and the state file keep its path, and its secrets are never logged. Without it
every replication configuration is refused. See
.BR Replication .
.TP
.B \-\-acls
Buckets given no ownership controls take ACLs, as S3 did before it disabled them
by default and as RGW does; without it they are BucketOwnerEnforced. See the
ACLs under
.BR "Supported S3 operations" .
.TP
.BI \-\-tls\-offloaded " addresses"
The proxies that terminate TLS in front of the runtime, as addresses or blocks
.RB ( 10.0.0.5 ,
.BR 10.0.0.0/24 ,
IPv6 alike), comma\-separated: a connection from one of them counts as one made
over TLS, so it may carry SSE\-C headers,
.B aws:SecureTransport
is true for it, and the URLs the runtime answers with are https. Its request's
client is the address it put last in
.BR X\-Forwarded\-For ,
for
.BR aws:SourceIp ,
the access log and the count of failed sign\-ins, which would otherwise hold
every client behind the proxy as one. Refused at
.B create
when an entry is no address. A proxy that speaks HTTP/2 to clients holds an upload to its flow\-control
window per round trip, 64 KiB unless raised: behind Apache,
.B "H2WindowSize 524288"
in the virtual host took a browser's upload from 1.2 to 5 MB/s over a line
that carries 6. Far more costs the player: the browser sends its requests on the
one connection behind as much of the upload as the window lets it queue, and at
16 MiB a request waited 0.5 to 1.1 s where it waits 0.05 to 0.1 s at 512 KiB.
.TP
.BI \-\-federation " file"
The OIDC provider and the LDAP directory whose identities STS gives temporary
credentials to, and the users they sign as, read at every start. See
.BR "Federated identities" .
.TP
.BI \-\-tiers " file"
The S3 endpoints a bucket's lifecycle may move versions to, each a storage class
named by its first field, in the format of
.BR \-\-replication\-targets ;
the URL's path is the bucket there and an optional prefix in it:
.RS
.PP
.nf
# name   url                                region     access key            secret
COLD     https://s3.archive.internal/cold/a  eu\-west\-1  AKIATIER000000000001  wJal.../tier
.fi
.RE
.IP
The file is refused at
.B create
and
.B start
if a line does not parse and is read at every start. A tier's path is this
runtime's own: what lies under it that no version here is is removed. See
.BR Tiers .
.TP
.BI \-\-nodes " file"
Makes the runtime a node of a cluster, whose nodes each keep every bucket and
take writes. The file names this node, every node by the S3 endpoint the others
reach it at (this one included, with an optional
.BI ca= file
for an https endpoint), and the key the nodes sign for each other with, the same
in every node's file; comments and blank lines are as in
.BR \-\-notify\-targets :
.RS
.PP
.nf
self  a
node  a  https://s3\-a.internal:9000
node  b  https://s3\-b.internal:9000
node  c  https://s3\-c.internal:9000  ca=/etc/socketley/cluster\-ca.pem
key   AKIACLUSTERNODES0001  wJal.../nodes
sync  1  5000
.fi
.RE
.IP
.BI sync " n " "[ms]"
is optional: a write is then answered only once
.I n
other nodes have it, or once
.I ms
milliseconds (5000) have passed whatever they say, after which its copies go on
as they would without it; without it a write is answered once it is in this
node's outbox. A file whose sync names more nodes than there are is refused.
.IP
The file is refused at
.B create
and
.B start
if a line does not parse, if
.B self
is none of its nodes, or if it names no key, and is read at every start, so a
node added to the file is taken by each node once it starts again;
.B show
and the state file keep its path, and the key is never logged. The cluster's key
is accepted beside the runtime's own credentials and is not one of them: a request
signed with it writes what another node made, is judged by no bucket policy, and
cannot be given to a user. See
.BR Cluster .
.TP
.B \-\-anonymous
Accept requests that carry no credentials at all. Required unless
credentials are given: a runtime made with neither is refused, so an endpoint is
never open by omission. With credentials as well, both kinds are served, and a
request that does carry a signature is always verified \(em a wrong one is
refused and never taken for an anonymous request. An anonymous request may send a
body in unsigned aws\-chunked framing
(\fBSTREAMING\-UNSIGNED\-PAYLOAD\-TRAILER\fR), which is decoded and its trailing
checksum checked; one signed in chunks
(\fBSTREAMING\-AWS4\-HMAC\-SHA256\-PAYLOAD\fR) is refused with
.B NotImplemented
because nobody is there to verify the signatures. Without
.B \-\-anonymous
a request with no credentials is
.B "403 AccessDenied"
unless its bucket's policy allows it, as described under
.BR "Bucket policy" .
.TP
.BI \-\-idp " runtime"
The web interface and WebDAV in a browser take the sign-in of the
.B idp
runtime of that name in this daemon: an account signed in there is the user
of the same name here, with the rights that user has here and no others, and
an account that is no user here is answered
.BR 403 .
The page offers the provider's sign-in beside its own form, and signing out
there signs out at the provider. The cookie reaches the storage only when the
provider's
.B \-\-cookie\-domain
covers its name. Accounts of the storage's own users can be taken over by the
provider with
.BR "socketley idp\-user \fIidp\fP import \fIstorage\fP" .
.TP
.BI \-\-mpu\-expire " duration"
Abort a multipart upload that has been open this long (\fB24h\fR, \fB7d\fR; a bare
number is seconds, as for every duration flag). Without it an upload is kept
until it is completed or aborted, however old. Expiry is looked for while the
runtime runs and once at every start, so an upload that outlived the setting
while the runtime was stopped is gone when it comes back.
.TP
.BI \-\-scrub " duration"
Read every object and version back once in this long (\fB30d\fR, \fB7d\fR) and
check its bytes against the MD5 its header names, so a file the disk changed is
found before a client reads it. See
.BR Scrub .
Without it nothing is read in the background.
.TP
.BI \-\-compress " duration"
Compress each version once it has gone unwritten this long (\fB1h\fR, \fB7d\fR),
where it lies, so it takes less of the disk and reads back as it was. See
.BR Compression .
Without it every body is stored as it was sent.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-bytes " size"
A quota on the bytes the runtime holds, with the
.BR K / M / G
suffixes of
.BR \-\-maxmemory .
A put that would pass it is answered
.B "507 StorageFull"
and stores nothing, and the quota can be changed on a serving runtime with
.BR import .
0, the default, is no quota.
.PP
.B edit
does not change these; \fBimport\fR does, as it does for a cache, and
refuses to move \fBdir\fR, \fBanonymous\fR, \fBdomain\fR, \fBfsync\fR, \fBfsync_interval\fR or the access key, secret key,
credentials file, SSE key file or notification targets file while the runtime is running (the contents of a credentials
file are re\-read by themselves). The quota and the expiry move under a serving
runtime.
.B \-\-shards
splits the runtime over several processes on one directory, as described under
.BR Sharding .
.SS What it shares with the other runtimes
.TP
.B TLS
.B \-\-tls \-\-cert \-\-key
terminate TLS on the listener and
.B \-\-ca
asks every client for a certificate that CA signed, as for a server or a cache.
The certificates are read when the runtime starts, so
.B reload
installs a replaced one. Every accepted connection is given a session before its
first byte is read, so a plaintext client meets the handshake and is refused,
never served in the clear, and a peer that sits in the handshake for thirty
seconds is dropped. A download over TLS is read from the file and encrypted; only
plain TCP is sent by splice, so
.B zero_copy_bytes
stays 0 for it. TLS and
.B \-\-listen unix:
cannot be combined.
.TP
.B The node beside the port
A runtime with a port also listens on
.IB <daemon\ socket>.rt/ name .sock
for callers on this machine, and a proxy given
.B \-\-backend
.I name
or a group the runtime belongs to dials that node and not the port. A proxy in
front carries GETs and PUTs of any size: with
.B \-\-protocol tcp
the request reaches the runtime exactly as signed, which is the form to use once
requests are signed, and with the default
.B http
mode the client's path carries the proxy's own
.BI / name /
prefix, which is stripped before it is forwarded.
.TP
.B Limits and timeouts
.B \-\-max\-connections
closes a connection past the limit and
.B \-\-idle\-timeout
closes a keep\-alive connection that has sat idle between requests, counted in
.BR timeouts .
.TP
.B \-\-cpus
is checked against the daemon's ring and shown by
.B stats
as it is for every type.
.TP
.B Persistence
The configuration is saved with the runtime, and a daemon restart starts a
runtime that was running with its objects, TLS settings and limits.
.TP
.B daemon \-\-fast\-lo
A storage runtime reads every body with a receive and only splices from a file to
a socket, never from a socket, so the loopback shortcut needs no exception for
it: clients on the shortcut, directly or through a proxy, put and get byte for
byte.
