.TE
.PP
A bucket's ownership controls (PutBucketOwnershipControls, GetBucketOwnershipControls,
DeleteBucketOwnershipControls, or
.B x\-amz\-object\-ownership
on CreateBucket) decide whether it takes ACLs. BucketOwnerEnforced, every bucket's
default as S3 makes it now, disables them: every bucket and object has one grant,
FULL_CONTROL to the bucket's owner, which GetBucketAcl and GetObjectAcl answer, and a
write may ask only for that ACL (\fBbucket\-owner\-full\-control\fR, or
\fBx\-amz\-acl: private\fR from the bucket's owner, as rclone and s3fs send by
default); any other canned ACL or grant is
.B "400 AccessControlListNotSupported"
(\fBInvalidBucketAclWithObjectOwnership\fR on CreateBucket) and stores nothing.
ObjectWriter and BucketOwnerPreferred take them, and with
.B \-\-acls
so does every bucket given no ownership controls (GetBucketOwnershipControls is then
.B "404 OwnershipControlsNotFoundError"
for it, as for an RGW bucket). There the canned ACLs (private, public\-read,
public\-read\-write, authenticated\-read, bucket\-owner\-read,
bucket\-owner\-full\-control, log\-delivery\-write), the
.B x\-amz\-grant\-*
headers (id=, uri= of AllUsers, AuthenticatedUsers or LogDelivery, emailAddress= of a
user's
.B email
setting) and an AccessControlPolicy document set a bucket's ACL, kept as its
document beside its policy, and an object's, kept in its header with its owner: the
writer under ObjectWriter, the bucket's owner under BucketOwnerPreferred when the
write gave that owner full control. A bucket's grants admit listing (READ), writes
and deletes (WRITE) and its ACL (READ_ACP, WRITE_ACP); an object's admit its reads
(READ) and its ACL, and are read when the object is, after the bucket's said
nothing. An explicit Deny of a policy still refuses, the bucket's owner and an admin
are admitted whatever the grants say, a public grant is
.B "403 AccessDenied"
where the public access block blocks public ACLs and counts for nothing where it
ignores them, a grantee that is no user is
.B "400 InvalidArgument"
.RB ( UnresolvableGrantByEmailAddress
for an email), and BucketOwnerEnforced is refused on a bucket whose ACL grants
anything more than its owner's full control.
A bucket's own tags (PutBucketTagging, GetBucketTagging, DeleteBucketTagging)
are at most 50 under the rules of an object's, NoSuchTagSet (404) when there are
none, and are kept and carried between shards like its CORS rules, in
.IB dir / bucket /tagging .
Every storage class S3 has is taken, and the bytes are kept here as STANDARD's
are. A class whose objects S3 reads at once (REDUCED_REDUNDANCY, STANDARD_IA,
ONEZONE_IA, INTELLIGENT_TIERING, GLACIER_IR, EXPRESS_ONEZONE) is kept and named by
HEAD, GET, the listings, GetObjectAttributes and an upload's ListParts and
ListMultipartUploads; an archive class is named STANDARD, since its name would
tell a client to restore what it can read now; a copy takes the class it names,
STANDARD when it names none, and may name one to change an object's class in
place; a version a lifecycle moved to a tier is listed and read with the tier's
name as its class. A class S3 does not have is
.BR "400 InvalidStorageClass" ;
.B x\-amz\-website\-redirect\-location
is kept and returned like the other stored headers.
Anything else \(em analytics, inventory \(em is answered
.B "501 NotImplemented"
and never mistaken for the plain operation. A single put is at most 5 GiB and a
key at most 1024 bytes.
.SS Uploads by form
A POST to a bucket whose body is
.B multipart/form\-data
is S3's POST Object, the upload a web page makes with an HTML form (posted to a
key instead, as to any key without a subresource, it is
.BR "405 MethodNotAllowed" )
and the fields
.B generate_presigned_post
and the SDKs' presigned POST write. The fields in front of the file (at most
20 KiB of them, else
.BR MaxPostPreDataLengthExceeded )
say what the upload is:
.B key
(with
.B ${filename}
given the file's name), the headers a PutObject would carry (Content\-Type,
Cache\-Control, Content\-Disposition, Content\-Encoding, Expires,
x\-amz\-meta\-*, x\-amz\-checksum\-*, x\-amz\-server\-side\-encryption*),
.B acl
and
.B tagging
(a Tagging document), and
.B success_action_status
(200, 201 with a PostResponse document, or 204, the default) or
.BR success_action_redirect ,
a 303 to that address with the bucket, key and ETag in its query. The file is
streamed into the object like a put's body, and fields after it are ignored, as
S3 does.
.PP
A form with a
.B policy
is signed in its fields: as SigV4 (\fBx\-amz\-algorithm\fR,
\fBx\-amz\-credential\fR, \fBx\-amz\-date\fR and \fBx\-amz\-signature\fR,
an HMAC\-SHA256 of the policy) or in the older form boto3 still writes by default
(\fBAWSAccessKeyId\fR and \fBsignature\fR, a base64 HMAC\-SHA1 of the policy),
the one place a signature other than SigV4 is taken, since it covers nothing but
the policy. The policy is a base64 JSON document with an
.B expiration
(an expired one is AccessDenied) and
.BR conditions :
{"field": "value"}, ["eq", "$field", "value"], ["starts\-with", "$field",
"prefix"] and ["content\-length\-range", min, max], field names compared without
case, "$bucket" being the bucket posted to and a field the form leaves out being
the empty string. A condition a field does not meet, or a field no condition
names (but the signature and its key, the policy, the file and x\-ignore\-*), is
.B "403 AccessDenied"
("Invalid according to Policy: ..."), and a file outside the range is
.B EntityTooLarge
or
.BR EntityTooSmall ,
with nothing stored. A form without a policy is an anonymous PutObject: taken
by a runtime with
.BR \-\-anonymous ,
or where the bucket policy lets anyone put the key. Either way the bucket policy and
a script's on_request see it as PutObject.
.SS Virtual\-hosted\-style addressing
S3 clients address a bucket by host name by default against AWS
(\fBhttps://\fIbucket\fB.s3.amazonaws.com/\fIkey\fR), and most can be told to
against a custom endpoint. Given
.BR "\-\-domain s3.example.com" ,
a request with
.B "Host: photos.s3.example.com"
and path
.B /2024/a.jpg
is the object
.B 2024/a.jpg
in bucket
.BR photos ,
exactly as
.B /photos/2024/a.jpg
is with
.B "Host: s3.example.com"
or with any Host that is not a subdomain of a domain given, an address
included. Everything before the domain is the bucket, dots and all; with
several domains the longest that fits decides, and a Host equal to one is that
endpoint, so
.B "\-\-domain example.com,s3.example.com"
reads
.B s3.example.com
as the endpoint and not as bucket
.BR s3 .
.PP
The signature needs nothing of its own: SigV4 signs the path as sent and the
Host header, so a virtual\-hosted request is verified over
.BI / key
and its own host, and a presigned URL in either style verifies as it was
written. What the runtime writes back follows the style of the request: the
.B Location
of CreateBucket
.RB ( / bucket
path style, the bucket's URL virtual\-hosted), of a form upload and in
.BR CompleteMultipartUploadResult .
.B x\-amz\-copy\-source
names
.IB bucket / key
in both styles, as in S3. Clients need the bucket names to resolve to the
runtime: a wildcard DNS record
.RB ( *.s3.example.com ),
or on one machine
.B \-\-domain localhost
since glibc resolves every
.IB name .localhost
to the loopback. The usual settings are boto3's
.B "addressing_style: virtual"
(in the AWS CLI's config,
.BR "s3 = addressing_style = virtual" ),
rclone's
.B "force_path_style = false"
and an mc alias set with path lookup
.BR off .
.PP
A bucket whose name is not a single DNS label \(em one with dots \(em is still
served path style, and the clients fall back to it for such names over TLS. Over
.B \-\-tls
the certificate must cover every bucket's host name, which takes a wildcard
certificate for
.BI *. domain
next to the domain itself; a wildcard matches one label only, so a bucket with
dots in its name fails verification virtual\-hosted and is reached path style.
.SS Authentication
Requests are verified as AWS Signature Version 4, in both forms: the
.B Authorization
header and a presigned URL (\fBX\-Amz\-Algorithm\fR and the rest in the
query, for GET, HEAD, PUT and DELETE). Signature Version 2, which awscli 1.x presigns with and older tools
sign with, is verified as well, in both forms:
.B "Authorization: AWS"
.IR key : signature ,
dated by
.B Date
or
.B x\-amz\-date
within fifteen minutes, and a URL's
.BR AWSAccessKeyId ,
.B Expires
and
.BR Signature ,
good until
.BR Expires ,
which may lie at most a week (604800 seconds) ahead;
its resource is the bucket and key as sent with the subresources S3 signs, and
nothing of the body is signed. Whatever region the credential scope names, none
included, is accepted. Every
.B x\-amz\-*
header a request carries must be among the signed ones, as S3 requires, but for an
.B x\-amz\-content\-sha256
header beside a presigned URL; one that is not is
.B "403 AccessDenied"
and the request is refused. Every
.B x\-amz\-content\-sha256
mode a client sends is honoured: a hex hash is checked against the body as it
streams (a body that is not the one signed is
.B XAmzContentSHA256Mismatch
and is not stored, and a presigned URL, which signs no payload, has its body checked
against a hash its sender states in the header all the same, one that is no hash
being
.BR "400 InvalidArgument" ),
.B UNSIGNED\-PAYLOAD
has nothing to check, and aws\-chunked bodies \(em
.BR STREAMING\-AWS4\-HMAC\-SHA256\-PAYLOAD ,
its
.B \-TRAILER
variant and
.B STREAMING\-UNSIGNED\-PAYLOAD\-TRAILER
\(em are decoded, with every chunk signature and a signed trailer verified, even
when the aws\-chunked body is itself carried in
.BR "Transfer\-Encoding: chunked" .
Nothing is held whole for any of them: the body goes through the same two
staging buffers as an unsigned put.
.PP
The answers are S3's:
.B SignatureDoesNotMatch
(403) for a wrong secret or a request changed after signing,
.B InvalidAccessKeyId
(403),
.B RequestTimeTooSkewed
(403) for a request dated more than fifteen minutes from the runtime's clock,
.B AccessDenied
(403) for an expired presigned URL, for none at all where no bucket policy allows
the request, and for a request a bucket policy denies, and
.B AuthorizationHeaderMalformed
(400). A denied request is answered before its body is read and the connection is
closed.
.SS Users
A credentials file may name users. Each has an id, a display name, its own keys,
optionally an identity policy, and optionally the admin flag:
.PP
.nf
# a key outside any user is the default owner, socketley, an admin
AKIAOPS000000000001:ops\-secret

[alice]
name = Alice Example
AKIAALICE0000000001:alice\-secret\-1
AKIAALICE0000000002:alice\-secret\-2

[bob]
policy = bob.json
AKIABOB000000000001:bob\-secret

[root]
admin = true
AKIAROOT00000000001:root\-secret
.fi
.PP
.BI [ id ]
begins a user: 1 to 128 printable characters without spaces or any of
.BR "/:*?,=[]\(dq<>&" .
The lines after it, up to the next, are its keys
.RI ( access : secret )
and its settings
.RI ( name " = " value ):
.B name
is the display name (the id when it is not given),
.B admin
is
.B true
or
.BR false ,
.B policy
is the user's identity policy, the JSON itself on that one line when it begins with
.BR { ,
and otherwise a file, relative to the credentials file's directory, and
.B max_bytes
is the most the buckets the user owns may hold together, a size such as
.B 10G
(see
.BR Quotas ). A setting has its
.B =
before any colon, which an access key cannot hold. A key outside any user, and the
pair given with
.BR \-\-access\-key ,
belong to the default owner,
.BR socketley ,
an admin. A file without users is that one owner, exactly as before users existed:
every key may do everything to every bucket that no bucket policy denies. A user
declared twice, a key given to two users, an unknown setting, or a policy that does
not compile is refused at
.B create
and
.B start
naming the line, and on a reload leaves the users in force.
.PP
An identity policy is a bucket policy's grammar (see
.BR "Bucket policy" ),
with the same actions and conditions, without
.B Principal
(it is the user's own), and with resources in any bucket:
.BR * ,
or
.BI arn:aws:s3::: pattern
matched as IAM matches an ARN, against the bucket name for a bucket action and
.IB bucket / key
for an object action, where
.B *
spans
.BR / :
.B arn:aws:s3:::logs\-*
names those buckets and every object in them, and
.B arn:aws:s3:::logs\-*/*
the objects alone.
.PP
A bucket is owned by the user that made it, recorded on the second line of its
.IB dir / bucket /created
(none for the default owner, as for every bucket made before users) and taken by
every shard, at its making and at every start. ListBuckets shows a user the
buckets it owns and an admin every bucket, unless one of the user's policies
denies it
.B s3:ListAllMyBuckets
(on \fB*\fR or \fBarn:aws:s3:::*\fR), which makes it 403; every Owner element \(em ListBuckets,
ListObjects, ListObjectVersions, the uploads and parts listings, GetBucketAcl and
GetObjectAcl \(em is the bucket's owner with its display name, since an object of a
bucket with ACLs disabled is its bucket owner's.
.PP
A signed request is decided in this order:
.IP 1. 4
An applying
.B Deny
in the user's identity policy or in the bucket's policy refuses it with
.BR "403 AccessDenied" .
.IP 2. 4
An admin, the bucket's owner, and anybody on a bucket that is not there (which the
operation answers
.BR NoSuchBucket )
are let in.
.IP 3. 4
An applying
.B Allow
in the identity policy or in the bucket's policy (naming the user by id, one of its
keys, or
.BR * )
lets it in.
.IP 4. 4
Anything else is
.BR "403 AccessDenied" .
.PP
A request without credentials to a bucket that is not there is answered
.BR "404 NoSuchBucket" ,
as a signed one is. A request whose
.B x\-amz\-expected\-bucket\-owner
names another user than the bucket's owner, or whose
.B x\-amz\-source\-expected\-bucket\-owner
does so for a copy's source bucket, is
.B "403 AccessDenied"
before anything is done.
A copy is also judged as a GetObject of its source in the source bucket.
CreateBucket is a credentials\-file user's whose identity policy does not deny
.B s3:CreateBucket
on the name, and a role's or a user's the IAM API made only where one of its
policies allows it, as S3 judges them. The bucket policy's own operations stay open to the owner and admins
whatever the policy says, and so do what no action names (ownership controls and the
operations answered NotImplemented); another user needs an Allow for the former and
is refused the latter.
.SS Temporary credentials
A user may ask the endpoint itself for temporary credentials, as of STS, with
any STS client pointed at the runtime's URL:
.RS
.PP
.nf
POST / HTTP/1.1
Content\-Type: application/x\-www\-form\-urlencoded

Action=AssumeRole&Version=2011\-06\-15&DurationSeconds=3600
    &RoleArn=arn:aws:iam::000000000000:role/reader&RoleSessionName=ci
    &Policy=<an identity policy, URL\-encoded>
.fi
.RE
.PP
The answer is an access key starting
.BR ASIA ,
a secret and a session token, which sign as the user whose key asked for them,
with the token in
.B x\-amz\-security\-token
or a presigned URL's
.BR X\-Amz\-Security\-Token .
The token carries the user's key, the expiry and the session policy, sealed with
that key's secret, from which the session's secret is derived too: nothing is kept,
so every shard and every restart takes it, and it ends at its expiry
.RB ( "400 ExpiredToken" ),
or as soon as the key is gone from the credentials file
.RB ( "400 InvalidToken" ).
The request is a form posted to
.BR / ,
signed for the service
.B sts
or
.BR s3 ;
temporary credentials cannot ask for more, and a runtime without keys gives none.
AssumeRole of a role the IAM API made (see
.BR IAM )
is refused unless the role's trust policy names the caller, for longer than the
role's MaxSessionDuration, and its session holds the role's policies and not the
caller's. Any other RoleArn is
.B "404 NoSuchEntity"
and gives the caller nothing. The session is bounded by its
.B Policy
(an identity policy's grammar, at most 2048 bytes), which only narrows it:
what the policy does not allow is refused even to an admin or a bucket's owner,
the bucket policy's operations included. GetCallerIdentity names the user or the
assumed role that signed, a user the IAM API made by the UserId and the path it was given. A browser's form upload is not signed with temporary
credentials.
.SS Federated identities
With
.BI \-\-federation " file"
the endpoint gives temporary credentials, unsigned, to an identity an OIDC provider
or an LDAP directory vouches for. The file holds one setting a line, comments and
blank lines as in
.BR \-\-notify\-targets ,
relative paths under its own directory:
.RS
.PP
.nf
oidc issuer https://id.example.org/realms/main
oidc client storage\-console
oidc jwks   jwks.json
oidc claim  policy
role arn:aws:iam:::role/ci   builder
ldap url    ldaps://ldap.example.org ca=/etc/ssl/ldap\-ca.pem
ldap dn     uid=%s,ou=People,dc=example,dc=org
ldap user   alice  =
ldap user   *      reader
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity
takes a JWT signed RS256, RS384, RS512, PS256, ES256 or ES384 by a key of the JWK
Set file (RSA of 2048 bits and up, EC P\-256 and P\-384), read again whenever it
changes and kept while a new one does not parse; its
.B iss
must be the issuer, its
.B aud
name a client, and
.BR exp ,
.B nbf
and
.B iat
hold within a minute, else
.B "400 InvalidIdentityToken"
.RB ( ExpiredTokenException
once it ran out). A
.B role
line for the RoleArn names the user the session signs as; without one the token's
claim (by default
.BR policy )
must name exactly one user.
.B AssumeRoleWithLDAPIdentity
binds
.B LDAPUsername
with
.B LDAPPassword
as the
.B dn
template makes it (the name escaped as a DN value), over ldap:// or ldaps://
checked against
.B ca=
or the system's CAs, within five seconds on a thread of its own
.RB ( "503 ServiceUnavailable"
when the directory does not answer,
.B "403 AccessDenied"
when it refuses); the user's own
.B ldap user
line, else the
.B *
one, names the user the session signs as
.RB ( =
for the same name). An empty password is refused before anything is sent.
.PP
Either session is the mapped user's, signed under its first key, with its rights,
narrowed by a
.B Policy
as AssumeRole's is; a RoleArn naming a role the IAM API made is assumed when its
trust policy names the mapped user, as AssumeRole's would be. A mapping to a user
that is not there, or has no key, is
.BR "403 AccessDenied" .
The file is refused at
.B create
and
.B start
when a line does not parse, and read at every start.
.SS IAM
The endpoint answers IAM's API too, to an admin, to a user whose own policies
allow the action, and never to a session: CreateUser,
GetUser, ListUsers, UpdateUser and DeleteUser; CreateAccessKey (at most two per
user), ListAccessKeys, UpdateAccessKey and DeleteAccessKey; CreateGroup,
GetGroup, ListGroups, DeleteGroup, AddUserToGroup, RemoveUserFromGroup and
ListGroupsForUser; CreateRole, GetRole, ListRoles, UpdateRole and DeleteRole;
CreateLoginProfile, GetLoginProfile, UpdateLoginProfile, DeleteLoginProfile and
ChangePassword; TagUser, UntagUser and ListUserTags;
and for users, groups and roles alike Put, Get, List and Delete of an inline
policy and Attach, Detach and ListAttached of the AWS managed policies
AmazonS3FullAccess, AmazonS3ReadOnlyAccess and AdministratorAccess. A request is a
form posted to
.BR / ,
as
.B aws iam
and the SDKs send it, signed for
.B iam
with or without a region.
.PP
What it makes is kept in
.IB dir /.iam.json,
written under a lock and renamed over, and read by every shard beside the
credentials file: a change is in force on the shard that made it at once and on the
others within 50 milliseconds, and a restart keeps it. A user's identity policy is
the statements of its inline and attached policies and its groups' together; a user
with none owns and reaches the buckets it makes, as a credentials-file user without
a policy does. A user of the credentials file cannot be made again, and policies
put on one stand beside the file's own: either's Deny refuses and either's Allow
admits; such a user is not listed. A user is deleted once it has no keys, policies or
groups or login profile left, a group once it has no members or policies, and a role once it has no
policies. Policies are checked as identity policies are; a trust policy's
statements with a condition let nobody in. ARNs name no account,
.BR arn:aws:iam:::user/ name ,
as RGW writes them. OIDC providers, customer managed policies and permission
boundaries are not served.
.PP
A login profile is a user's password for the web interface (and a WebDAV
client's Basic credentials): at least 8 characters, kept as scrypt (N=2^14, r=8,
p=1) and never as itself, for a user of the API or of the credentials file
alike. A password that matched is not hashed again for five minutes, so a drive
sending it with every request costs one hash and not one each.
.B PasswordResetRequired
makes the page ask for a new one before anything else; ChangePassword takes the
old one and changes the caller's own. A password's sessions are sealed under a
secret drawn with the profile, and a session made before the password was last
set is no longer let in.
.PP
Four tags of a user the runtime reads itself:
.B socketley:display\-name
(the name shown),
.B socketley:email ,
.B socketley:max\-bytes
(the quota on the bytes of the buckets it owns, as the credentials file's
.BR max_bytes )
and
.B socketley:disabled
.RB ( true :
neither its password nor its keys sign in, while its buckets keep their owner
and a share can still name it); other keys of that prefix are refused. A member
of the group
.B admins
is an admin, as the credentials file's
.B admin = true
makes one. An IAM user makes a bucket only where a policy allows it, as S3
judges; the web interface gives the users it makes the inline policy
.B socketley\-libraries
(Bs3:CreateBucketR on B*R), and a user owns what it makes.
.PP
A user who is no admin may call an IAM action its identity policies allow, as
.BI iam: Action
with
.B *
and
.B ?
(\fBiam:List*\fR, \fBiam:*\fR; \fB"*"\fR names S3's actions alone, so a policy written
before IAM could be delegated gives none of it, and AdministratorAccess names
\fBiam:*\fR beside it), on resources
.BI arn:aws:iam:: account :user/ "path name" ,
.BR group/ ...
or
.BR role/ ...
in any account, where
.B ${aws:username}
is the caller's user id; ListUsers, ListGroups and ListRoles act on no resource,
so only
.B "Resource": "*"
covers them. A Deny wins and no Allow is
.BR AccessDenied .
A key named by its id alone is judged as its owner's, a rename or a move needs
both ARNs, and GetUser, CreateAccessKey, ListAccessKeys, GetLoginProfile,
ListUserTags and ListGroupsForUser without a UserName act on the caller. Without
any policy a user may do to itself what IAM's console lets every user: read
itself, its keys, tags and groups, make, change and delete its own keys, set a
first password, and change its password. An admin keeps every right whatever its
policies say, and only an admin changes an admin or gives an identity IAM
rights: a policy put or attached whose Allow names an
.B iam:
action, AdministratorAccess included, a group that has one, the group
.BR admins ,
and the
.B socketley:
tags are refused to anybody else. IAM actions this runtime does not answer are accepted in a policy
and match nothing.
.SS CORS
A bucket's CORS rules let a page served from another origin use it from a
browser: read objects, and upload with a presigned URL. They are set with
PutBucketCors as S3's
.B CORSConfiguration
document, up to 100
.B CORSRule
elements each holding one or more
.B AllowedOrigin
(with at most one
.B *
standing for any run of characters, and
.B *
alone for every origin) and
.B AllowedMethod
(GET, PUT, HEAD, POST or DELETE), and any number of
.B AllowedHeader
(at most one
.B *
each, compared without case),
.BR ExposeHeader ,
an optional
.B MaxAgeSeconds
and an optional
.B ID
of up to 255 characters. A document that is not one is
.BR "400 MalformedXML" ;
an unknown method, a second wildcard or a 101st rule is
.BR "400 InvalidRequest" ,
and a refused document leaves the rules that were there.
.PP
The rules are kept in
.IB dir / bucket /cors ,
written beside it, synced and renamed over it, so they survive a restart and a
crash leaves the old rules or the new ones; they go with the bucket when it is
deleted, and a bucket made again under the name has none. With
.B \-\-shards
shard 0 writes them, whichever shard took the request, as described for the
policy under
.BR "Bucket policy" :
the request is answered once no shard can go on with the old rules, and a shard
that was not running reads them from the file when it starts.
.PP
A preflight,
.B OPTIONS
on the bucket or on a key with
.B Origin
and
.B Access\-Control\-Request\-Method
(and
.B Access\-Control\-Request\-Headers
when the request will send others), is answered without authentication from the
first rule whose origin, method and every requested header match: 200 with
.B Access\-Control\-Allow\-Origin
(the origin, or
.B *
when the rule allows every origin),
.BR Access\-Control\-Allow\-Methods ,
.B Access\-Control\-Allow\-Headers
(the headers asked for),
.B Access\-Control\-Allow\-Credentials: true
for a named origin,
.B Access\-Control\-Expose\-Headers
and
.B Access\-Control\-Max\-Age
when the rule has them, and
.BR Vary .
A bucket without rules is
.B "403 AccessForbidden"
("CORSResponse: CORS is not enabled for this bucket."), a request no rule
allows is the same with "This CORS request is not allowed", and a preflight
without
.B Origin
or with a method S3 does not have is
.BR "400 BadRequest" .
.PP
Every other request that carries an
.B Origin
some rule allows for its method gets the same headers but
.BR Access\-Control\-Allow\-Headers ,
and one that also carries
.B Access\-Control\-Request\-Method
is judged by that method and the headers it asks for and answered as that preflight,
as S3 answers it, on every answer: errors and authentication refusals included, so a page can
read why it was refused. A request from an origin no rule allows is served as
before, with no CORS headers. A runtime none of whose buckets has rules looks
nothing up for a request.
.SS Checksums
.BR x\-amz\-checksum\-crc32 ,
.BR \-crc32c ,
.BR \-crc64nvme ,
.B \-sha1
and
.B \-sha256
are taken over the body of a PutObject or an UploadPart when the request names
one \(em in that header, in a trailer of an aws\-chunked body (\fBx\-amz\-trailer\fR),
or by
.B x\-amz\-sdk\-checksum\-algorithm
\(em and compared with the value the client gave: a mismatch is
.B "400 BadDigest"
and nothing is stored. The checksum is kept in the object, echoed in the
answer, and returned by GetObject and HeadObject when the request has
.B x\-amz\-checksum\-mode: ENABLED
(except on a ranged read, which is not of the whole object), with
.B x\-amz\-checksum\-type
saying what it is of. A multipart object
has the checksum of its parts' checksums, and \fB\-N\fR after it for the number
of parts (\fBCOMPOSITE\fR), as S3 does; a part with no checksum of its own gets the upload's
algorithm anyway. An upload created with
.B "x\-amz\-checksum\-type: FULL_OBJECT"
and a CRC (and every CRC64NVME upload, as in S3) has instead the CRC of its whole
bytes, combined from its parts' CRCs and lengths without reading them again; FULL_OBJECT
with SHA\-1 or SHA\-256, or COMPOSITE with CRC64NVME, is
.BR "400 InvalidRequest" .
A checksum a CompleteMultipartUpload carries is compared with the one the parts
make, and a mismatch is
.B "400 BadDigest"
with nothing stored. Each part's own checksum is kept with the completed object,
beside its size (see
.BR "Reading by part number" ),
so a GetObject or HeadObject by part number with
.B x\-amz\-checksum\-mode: ENABLED
answers the part's checksum, with the object's
.BR x\-amz\-checksum\-type ,
and GetObjectAttributes lists it beside each part. A single\-part object answers
part 1 with its own checksum, and a copy, which keeps none of its source's part
checksums, answers part 1 with its own whole one. An object completed before part
checksums were kept answers its parts without one.
.PP
An object that arrives with no checksum is given S3's default, the
.B CRC64NVME
of its whole bytes, FULL_OBJECT: a PutObject, a POST form upload and an object a
script puts take it on the pass that takes the ETag's MD5, and answer it as they
would one the client named. An upload created with no algorithm keeps a CRC64NVME
of each part beside it, shown to no client (UploadPart, ListParts and
GetObjectAttributes answer none, and its parts by number are read without one),
and its CompleteMultipartUpload combines them into the object's default without
reading the parts again. An object stored before this, and an upload whose parts
were written before it, has none, and is not given one until it is copied.
.PP
A CopyObject's checksum is of the copy's own bytes, FULL_OBJECT, as S3 makes it: in
the algorithm
.B x\-amz\-checksum\-algorithm
names (one the runtime does not take is
.BR "400 InvalidRequest" ),
or else in its source's, under either metadata directive. A source whose checksum
is already of its whole bytes gives the copy that value; a COMPOSITE one, or one in
another algorithm, has the copy's taken from the bytes on the copy's one pass
through them, as is every copy that is sealed or opened on its way. The
CopyObjectResult carries it with its
.BR ChecksumType .
A source with no checksum gives a copy the default, CRC64NVME, unless the request
names another. A copy onto itself that names
.B x\-amz\-checksum\-algorithm
is allowed under either directive, as S3 documents it for adding or changing an
object's checksum; one that names nothing new is still
.BR "400 InvalidRequest" .
A copy made before this answers with its source's COMPOSITE
value as it was stored. UploadPartCopy takes the upload's algorithm over the bytes
it copies, whatever its source's checksum, and answers it in its CopyPartResult.
.SS Object tags
An object has at most 10 tags, set with PutObjectTagging or with
.B x\-amz\-tagging
(a URL\-encoded query string,
.IR k1 = v1 & k2 = v2 )
on a PutObject, a CreateMultipartUpload (they land on the completed object) or a
CopyObject under
.BR "x\-amz\-tagging\-directive: REPLACE" .
A key is 1 to 128 characters and a value at most 256, counted as characters and
not bytes, of letters, digits, spaces and
.B "+ \- = . _ : / @"
(outside ASCII every character but a control, a format or private\-use
character is taken, which is wider than S3's letters, digits and spaces of every
script); keys are unique and none begins
.BR aws: .
Eleven tags are
.BR "400 BadRequest" ,
any other breach
.BR "400 InvalidTag" ,
a Tagging document that is not one
.BR MalformedXML ,
and a repeated key or a broken escape in
.B x\-amz\-tagging
.BR InvalidArgument ;
a refused put stores nothing and a refused PutObjectTagging leaves the tags
there were. GetObject and HeadObject say how many tags an object has in
.BR x\-amz\-tagging\-count .
.PP
Tags live in the object's own header, beside its other metadata, and the journal
record mirrors them like the rest, so they survive a stop, a crash and a lost
journal. A tag change keeps the object's bytes, ETag and Last\-Modified, as S3
does, by not rewriting the object: the padding of the header's first 4 KiB holds
two tag slots of whole 512\-byte sectors, and a change is written into the one
the last change did not use, with a sequence number and a CRC32C, then synced
(one write and one
.BR fdatasync (2)).
A crash in the middle leaves a slot whose checksum fails, so the tags are those
before the change, never a torn header, and the key's file is the truth a start
after a crash reads them from. A set too big for the slots that are left (above
about 1.5 KiB of tags with little other metadata, or any change to a header that
fills its first 4 KiB) is written instead as a new file with the tags in its
header and the bytes copied behind it, renamed into place like a put. On ext4 a
change in place took 2.4 to 2.7 ms whatever the object's size, and a rewrite
12.6 ms for 4 KiB, 102 ms for 64 MiB and 1.07 s for 1 GiB.
.B stats
counts the two as
.B tag_writes
and
.BR tag_rewrites .
A change goes to the shard that owns the key, waits behind its other writes in
the key's lane, and is answered once the file says it and every shard has
dropped what it holds of the key, so a GET or a GetObjectTagging on any shard
sees it at once.
.SS Multipart uploads
An upload is a directory
.IB dir / bucket /mpu/ id
holding a
.B meta
file (the object it will become, in an object header) and one file per part, each
an object header and its bytes, so the runtime finds them again after a stop or a
crash. A part is written like a put \(em streamed, hashed, synced \(em and renamed
to its number, so it is whole or not there; an upload is created, and its
directory synced, before its id is given out. A part still arriving when the
worker is killed leaves nothing behind. The upload id is 32 hex digits.
.PP
Most clients cut an object into parts of one size, and a part whose place in the
object is known when it starts is written there: the upload also holds a
.B data
file with room for the object's header at the front, the first part of at least
5 MiB fixes the part size, and part
.I n
of that size (or a smaller last part) goes into
.B data
at its offset, its numbered file then holding only a header that names the place.
The shard that owns the upload hands each place out once, so a part sent again, a
part of another size and a part sent by UploadPartCopy get files of their own as
above.
.PP
CompleteMultipartUpload checks the list against what was uploaded. When the parts
listed are exactly 1 to N, all in place and of the fixed size but the last, it
writes the object's header in front of them, syncs
.B data
and commits it as the object itself, so the bytes are written to the disk once.
Otherwise it writes the header and copies the parts into a temporary file a
megabyte at a time on the ring, the placed ones read from
.BR data ,
syncs it and commits it; an upload that was open across a restart, or whose
copy\-free completion failed, always copies. Either way the commit is a put's
(rename, directory sync; the journal record behind the answer), so a crash during
completion leaves the upload intact and no object; only then are the parts removed.
The ETag is the md5 of the parts' md5s with \fB\-N\fR, as S3's. A copy passes the
bytes through the process because io_uring has no copy between two files; the loop
never blocks on the disk, and the worker holds two one\-megabyte buffers for it
whatever the object's size. warp, the AWS CLI and boto3 upload anything above 8 to
16 MiB this way, so this is the path of every large upload: on a 4\-vCPU VM,
64 MiB puts at four connections went from 3.2 to 6.5 per second when the copy
went, and 256 MiB puts at two from 0.90 to 1.81.
An upload is kept until it is completed or aborted unless
.B \-\-mpu\-expire
is set; its parts count against
.B \-\-max\-bytes
while they are held, and deleting a bucket that holds nothing but open uploads
aborts them with it, as S3 does.
A part sent again needs room for both copies until the new one lands.
.SS Reading by part number
.B "GET /bucket/key?partNumber=N"
(and HEAD) answers part
.I N
of the object as S3 does, which is how the AWS CRT, the Java transfer manager and
several Go clients download a multipart object in parallel: a
.B 206
of the part's bytes, with
.BI "Content\-Range: bytes " first \- last / size ,
the part's Content\-Length, the whole object's ETag and
.BR x\-amz\-mp\-parts\-count .
Parts are numbered 1 to the part count in the order the completion listed them.
A single\-part object (a put, or any copy) is its own part 1, answered the same
way without the parts count; an empty one is a 200 of nothing. A number past the
part count is
.BR "416 InvalidPartNumber" ,
one outside 1 to 10000
.BR "400 InvalidArgument" ,
and a Range header beside it
.BR "400 InvalidRequest" .
The conditional headers apply as on a plain GET, before the part is looked at.
.PP
The part sizes are recorded in the object's header at CompleteMultipartUpload
(as
.BR x\-socketley\-part\-sizes ,
never sent to a client): one size when every part but the last has it, which
is every in\-place completion, and otherwise the list, up to 4 KiB of it. An
upload with a checksum algorithm adds its parts' checksums, in base64, up to 4 KiB
of them
.RB ( x\-socketley\-part\-checksums ).
The journal carries them as it carries the rest of the header. A longer list, which
any upload S3 takes may need, follows the object's body in the file instead, 20
bytes and 12 more per part (24, and 12 and the checksum's width per part, when it
holds checksums), with the header naming where it begins and its SHA\-256
.RB ( x\-socketley\-part\-layout );
a part number of such an object reads the list from the file first, and a list
that does not match its digest is
.BR "500 InternalError" .
An in\-place completion whose checksums do not fit the room its header was given
writes them after the last part in the same way, and still copies nothing.
A multipart object completed before part sizes were recorded answers any part
number within its count with
.B "501 NotImplemented"
rather than a guess; a plain GET or a Range reads it as before.
.PP
CopyObject makes a single\-part object, as S3 does: a multipart source's bytes
are hashed on the way through and the copy's ETag is their md5, with no
.BR \-N ,
and its checksum, when it needs one taken, is taken on that same pass.
.SS Versioning
.B PutBucketVersioning
with
.B Enabled
makes every PUT, CopyObject and CompleteMultipartUpload of the bucket a new version
of its key with an id of its own (32 hex digits), answered in
.BR x\-amz\-version\-id ;
the versions before it stay, and GET, HEAD, DeleteObject and the tagging operations
take
.BI ?versionId= ID
to name one. A GET without one is the latest. A DELETE without one puts a delete
marker in front of the key (204 with
.B "x\-amz\-delete\-marker: true"
and the marker's id), after which a GET or HEAD of the key is
.B "404 NoSuchKey"
with
.B "x\-amz\-delete\-marker: true"
and a GET of the marker by its id
.BR "405 MethodNotAllowed" ;
a listing leaves the key out. A DELETE with a version id removes that version for
good: the marker, which undeletes the key, or the latest, after which the version
behind it is the latest. An object written before versioning was enabled is the
.B null
version, which a GET, HEAD or GetObjectTagging of it names with
.BR "x\-amz\-version\-id: null" .
.B Suspended
keeps the versions there are and writes the
.B null
version, which the next write or delete marker replaces. A bucket is never
unversioned again, as in S3, and
.B GetBucketVersioning
of one never versioned is an empty configuration. MFA delete is refused with
.BR "501 NotImplemented" .
A version id that could not be one is
.BR "400 InvalidArgument" ,
and one the key has no version of
.BR "404 NoSuchVersion" .
.PP
The latest version of a key is its file at the usual path, so a read without a
version id, on any shard, is the read of an unversioned bucket; a version that a
new latest replaces is linked to
.IB dir / bucket /versions/ ab / sha256(key) . id
and that directory synced before the new file is renamed over it, and the 256
directories of
.B versions/
are made with the configuration, which shard 0 changes like the CORS and lifecycle
documents (see
.BR "Bucket policy" );
the owner of a key writes nothing to it while unsure of the configuration, and a
write through another shard is then answered 503. Each version's header carries
its id and its order among the key's versions. The shard a body lands on writes
them into its header before the body is synced, and the key's owner keeps them
unless a newer version of the key or a change of the bucket's versioning came
first \(em including one it learns of only by reading the configuration again
before the write \(em when it writes the header again with its own.
A versioned put takes a file of the pool as an unversioned one does: it is answered
at its rename, once the link of the version it replaces and that link's directory
are synced, and the syncs of its own directory and of
.B pool/
follow the answer. A crash that takes the rename leaves the put whole in
.BR pool/ ,
and the next start links the version it would have replaced before moving it into
place \(em also when the bucket was versioned only after the put was answered. A
versioned put therefore waits for one sync more than an unversioned one when it
replaces a version, and for none more when its key is new. A version removed for
good is unlinked, or for the latest the version behind it is renamed over it.
.PP
The journal records each key's other versions and delete markers beside its
latest object, so a start after a clean stop reads a versioned bucket from its
journal as it does any other; a journal written before it did is read from the
files once and written again. Any other start reads
.B versions/
in the same parallel walk as
.BR data/ :
the headers give each key's versions in order and its delete markers, a version
left under both its names by a crash between the link and the rename is kept once
(as the latest), and a key whose latest file is gone is given its newest version
back. 100,000 keys of three versions each start in 0.19 s after a clean stop
(1.3 s when every start read the files) and in 0.86 s after a kill (1.3 s), with a
warm cache on four CPUs. With
.BR \-\-shards ,
all versions of a key are its owner's; a shard that does not own the key reads a
version by its id from the file where it is, looking at the other place once if a
rename moved it meanwhile, and a held copy of a latest is dropped on every shard
before a new version, a marker or a removal that changes the latest is answered.
.B ListObjectVersions
asks every shard and merges their pages.
.SS Object lock
A bucket made with
.B "x\-amz\-bucket\-object\-lock\-enabled: true"
is versioned and locked from the start, and
.B PutObjectLockConfiguration
locks a bucket whose versioning is
.BR Enabled ,
as S3 now allows (409
.B InvalidBucketState
otherwise). A locked bucket's versioning cannot be suspended (409
.BR InvalidBucketState )
and its lock cannot be removed; a configuration without a
.B Rule
only drops the default retention. The default,
.B GOVERNANCE
or
.B COMPLIANCE
for a number of
.B Days
or calendar
.BR Years ,
is given to every new version that names no retention itself, counted from the
moment it is written.
.PP
A version's retention is set by
.B x\-amz\-object\-lock\-mode
and
.B x\-amz\-object\-lock\-retain\-until\-date
on PutObject, CopyObject and CreateMultipartUpload (whose completed object has it),
or by PutObjectRetention; its legal hold by
.B x\-amz\-object\-lock\-legal\-hold
or PutObjectLegalHold. GET and HEAD return all three headers. A copy never takes
its source's lock, only what its own request or bucket gives it. A lock asked of a
bucket without object lock is
.BR "400 InvalidRequest" ,
a date that is not in the future
.BR "400 InvalidArgument" .
PutObjectLockConfiguration, PutObjectRetention, PutObjectLegalHold, and a PutObject
that names a retention or a legal hold, need
.B Content\-MD5
or an
.B x\-amz\-checksum\-*
of the body (checked), as S3 requires, or a SigV4 signature over the body's SHA\-256
(an
.B x\-amz\-content\-sha256
that is a hash, checked as well), which is all an SDK from before checksums sends; a
PutObject that only receives the default does not.
.PP
A version under a legal hold that is ON, or under a retention whose date the
server's clock (UTC) has not reached, cannot be removed: a DELETE or DeleteObjects
entry that names it is
.BR "403 AccessDenied" ,
and a DELETE without a version id still puts a delete marker in front of it.
.B COMPLIANCE
yields to nobody and is only lengthened.
.B GOVERNANCE
is lengthened by anyone and shortened, removed, made COMPLIANCE or deleted through
only by a request with
.B "x\-amz\-bypass\-governance\-retention: true"
whose caller has
.B s3:BypassGovernanceRetention
\(em every key the runtime has, unless the bucket's policy denies it, and an
anonymous caller only by an Allow of it. A legal hold yields to no bypass. A
lifecycle rule never expires a locked version (a current one's expiry puts a
delete marker, as it may), and DeleteBucket refuses a bucket that still holds
versions.
.PP
The retention and the hold are entries of the version's header, so the journal
and the index carry them too. A change is made by the key's owner under its lane
and written in place into the header's tag slots, crash\-safe as a tag change is:
once a version has a lock its slot holds its tags, retention and hold together, so
a torn write leaves the previous slot, and a header with no room is written again
whole. A version that never had a lock keeps the slot an older binary reads. With
.BR \-\-shards ,
a change or a removal through a shard that does not own the key is decided by its
owner, the bypass travelling with it, and the bucket's configuration, like
versioning, is decided by shard 0 in the order of the bucket's changes.
.SS Event notifications
A bucket's
.B NotificationConfiguration
(PutBucketNotificationConfiguration, GetBucketNotificationConfiguration) sends
S3's event records to webhooks. Its
.BR QueueConfiguration ,
.B TopicConfiguration
and
.B CloudFunctionConfiguration
entries are taken alike: an
.BR Id ,
a destination
.BI arn:socketley:webhook::: name
that names a line of
.BR \-\-notify\-targets ,
one or more
.B Event
and an optional
.B Filter
of an
.B S3Key
prefix rule and suffix rule. The events emitted are
.BR s3:ObjectCreated:Put ,
.B :Post
(a form upload),
.B :Copy
and
.BR :CompleteMultipartUpload ;
.B s3:ObjectRemoved:Delete
(a key deleted, or a version removed by its id) and
.B :DeleteMarkerCreated
(a delete in a versioned bucket);
.B s3:LifecycleExpiration:Delete
and
.B :DeleteMarkerCreated
(a lifecycle rule's expiry, which sends no ObjectRemoved); and
.B s3:ObjectTagging:Put
and
.BR :Delete ,
each of them by its name or by
.BR * .
The other names S3 has (ObjectRestore, Replication, .\|.\|.) are taken and send
nothing. A destination that is not a webhook's ARN, or names one the targets file
does not give, is
.B "400 InvalidArgument"
("Unable to validate the following destination configurations", the ARN as
.BR ArgumentName )
unless the request sends
.BR "x\-amz\-skip\-destination\-validation: true" ;
so are an unknown event, a filter rule other than one prefix and one suffix, two
configurations with one Id, two that share an event on overlapping keys, and
.BR EventBridgeConfiguration ,
which this endpoint cannot reach; a document that is not a configuration is
.BR "400 MalformedXML" .
An empty
.B NotificationConfiguration
removes it. It is kept in
.IB dir / bucket /notification
and changed by shard 0 like a bucket's other configuration documents.
.PP
Each event is POSTed to its webhook as S3's message, one record in a
.B Records
array, with
.BR "Content\-Type: application/json" ,
.B "Connection: keep\-alive"
and
.BR X\-Socketley\-Event\-Id ,
a number a receiver can deduplicate by:
.RS
.PP
.nf
{"Records":[{"eventVersion":"2.1","eventSource":"aws:s3",
  "awsRegion":"us\-east\-1","eventTime":"2026\-10\-02T12:00:00.123Z",
  "eventName":"ObjectCreated:Put",
  "userIdentity":{"principalId":"socketley"}, .\|.\|.,
  "s3":{"s3SchemaVersion":"1.0","configurationId":"thumbs",
    "bucket":{"name":"photos","ownerIdentity":{.\|.\|.},
              "arn":"arn:aws:s3:::photos"},
    "object":{"key":"2026/a+b.jpg","size":5821,
      "eTag":"9b2cf535f27731c974343645a3985328",
      "versionId":".\|.\|.","sequencer":"00063F7A2B1C0D9E"}}}]}
.fi
.RE
.PP
The key is form\-encoded as S3 encodes it (a space as
.BR + ,
.B /
kept, other bytes as
.BI % XX\fR),
a removal's record has no size or eTag, versionId is given in a versioned bucket,
and the sequencer is sixteen hex digits that grow with every event of the shard
that owns the key, so a key's events sort by it.
.B userIdentity.principalId
is the requester: the id of the user a signed request's key belongs to
(\fBsocketley\fR for a key outside any user), or
.B anonymous
for a request without credentials, let in by
.B \-\-anonymous
or by a bucket policy;
.B requestParameters.sourceIPAddress
is the client's address (an IPv4 client on a dual\-stack socket by its IPv4
address, a client on a unix socket by none). A lifecycle expiry names
.B s3.amazonaws.com
and no address, and a change a script makes through
.B socketley.storage
names neither. With
.B \-\-shards
both travel to the shard that owns the key with the change.
.PP
A webhook named by a host name is resolved on a thread of its own, whose answer
the loop reads from an eventfd through its ring, so no delivery and no request
waits on the resolver: once at start, again after every address the host had
refused a connection (at most once a second), and every minute while it is
delivered to. A host with several addresses is tried address by address before it
is resolved again. A stop waits for a resolve in progress, which the system
resolver's own timeout bounds.
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_NOTIFY_HOSTS
names a file of
.I name address
lines consulted before the system resolver, a test aid.
.PP
The shard that owns a key emits its events, whichever shard took the request, so a
change is one event. Its record is appended to
.IB dir /.notify/outbox. shard
when the change starts and synced beside the change's own syncs, together with
every other record waiting at that moment, and the change is answered once both
are durable, so an answered change's event survives a crash at no second sync on
the request's path. The event is sent only once the change is durable, after a
record saying it was made, which is synced before the next change of the key may
start. A start keeps an event whose change has no such record only when the object
files show the change (its ETag, size, time and version, or the key or version gone
for a removal), so a crash never sends an event for a change that was not made.
A webhook is sent one event at a time, in order: a 2xx answer
delivers it, and anything else, a refused or failed connection, or no answer in 10
seconds, is a failure tried again after 0.25 s, doubling to 8 s. A delivered
event's record is followed by one saying so, written at once and synced with the
next event's record rather than on its own, and a start sends again every event
without one, so an event is delivered at least once, and more than once only when
a crash or a 2xx lost on the way leaves its delivery unrecorded. The outbox is
written again with only the undelivered events at every start, and empty once it
has passed a megabyte with nothing left to send. At most 10000 events wait per
shard: past that a new one is dropped, counted in
.BR notify_dropped ,
and its change answered all the same.
.B stats
shows
.BR notify_sent ,
.B notify_failed
(attempts),
.B notify_queued
(waiting now),
.BR notify_dropped ,
.B notify_resolves
(host names resolved),
.B notify_connects
and
.B notify_reuses
(deliveries that went on a connection kept from the delivery before), per shard.
.PP
A webhook's connection is kept for its next event, as a proxy keeps its backends',
when the answer is HTTP/1.1, says nothing of
.BR "Connection: close" ,
and ends where its framing says; it is closed after 4 seconds with nothing on it,
after a minute, so a moved host name is followed, and whenever the webhook has
closed it, which is looked at before it is used. Over https this spares a
handshake per event: 3,000 events to a local https webhook took 4.5 seconds on a
connection each and 0.6 on one kept.
.SS Replication
A bucket's
.B ReplicationConfiguration
(PutBucketReplication, GetBucketReplication, DeleteBucketReplication) copies its
new versions, and the delete markers its clients make, to buckets of another S3
endpoint \(em another storage runtime, or any S3. It is S3's document: a
.B Role
and one or more
.BR Rule s,
each with an
.BR ID ,
a
.BR Priority ,
a
.B Status
of Enabled or Disabled, the keys it names (a rule\-level
.B Prefix
in the first schema, or a
.B Filter
of a
.BR Prefix ,
a
.B Tag
or an
.B And
of both, in the second), a
.B Destination
with the
.B Bucket
as
.BI arn:aws:s3::: bucket
and optionally an
.BR Account ,
a
.B StorageClass
(sent as the replica's
.BR x\-amz\-storage\-class )
and an
.B AccessControlTranslation
to Destination, and
.BR DeleteMarkerReplication .
The Role's last path segment names a target of
.BR \-\-replication\-targets ,
whose endpoint and credentials every rule of the configuration writes with:
.RS
.PP
.nf
<ReplicationConfiguration>
  <Role>arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/backup</Role>
  <Rule><ID>docs</ID><Priority>1</Priority><Status>Enabled</Status>
    <Filter><Prefix>docs/</Prefix></Filter>
    <Destination><Bucket>arn:aws:s3:::docs\-copy</Bucket></Destination>
    <DeleteMarkerReplication><Status>Enabled</Status></DeleteMarkerReplication>
  </Rule>
</ReplicationConfiguration>
.fi
.RE
.PP
As in S3, a bucket whose versioning is not Enabled is refused one
.RB ( "400 InvalidRequest" ),
and versioning cannot be suspended while it has one
.RB ( "409 InvalidBucketState" );
so are a second\-schema rule without DeleteMarkerReplication, delete marker
replication with a tag filter, rules of both schemas, two rules with one ID or one
priority, and overlapping first\-schema prefixes (all
.B 400
InvalidRequest or InvalidArgument); a Role that names no target is
.BR "400 InvalidArgument" ,
and a document that is not one
.BR "400 MalformedXML" .
.B ExistingObjectReplication
Enabled, ReplicaModifications Enabled, a
.B ReplicaKmsKeyID
and replication time control or metrics are
.B "501 NotImplemented"
rather than taken and not honoured; the destination bucket is not checked when
the configuration is put. The document is kept in
.IB dir / bucket /replication
and changed by shard 0 like a bucket's other configuration documents.
.PP
For every destination bucket the matching enabled rule of the highest priority
decides; a version is copied when one does, and a delete marker when that rule
replicates delete markers (a rule with a tag filter never names a marker; the
first schema always replicates them). A version is written with
.B "x\-amz\-replication\-status: PENDING"
in its header, which GET and HEAD answer, and becomes
.B COMPLETED
once every destination has it, or
.B FAILED
once one refused it, which stays FAILED; the status is written in place into the
header's slot, which then carries it with the tags, the retention and the hold.
What a version is copied by is decided when its metadata is made: a PutObject or
POST, a CopyObject (whose source's status is never copied), a
CreateMultipartUpload for the object its completion makes, and a script's put.
A later tag change or tag removal of a version that is COMPLETED or PENDING is
copied too, to the same version id there, as in S3; it has no status of its own,
and one the destination refuses is logged and given up. Not copied, as in S3: a
version removed by its id, a delete marker a lifecycle rule made, a version written
before the configuration, and an SSE\-C version, which has no status.
.PP
A version is copied by a signed PutObject streamed from its file \(em with its
Content\-Type, Cache\-Control, Content\-Disposition, Content\-Encoding,
Content\-Language, Expires, website redirect, user metadata and tags, its MD5 as
Content\-MD5 and its full\-object checksum, which the destination checks the bytes
against \(em and a multipart version by a multipart upload in the same parts and
part numbers, so the replica has the same ETag and composite checksum. An SSE\-S3
version is read in the clear, over the target's https when its URL is one, and
written with
.BR "x\-amz\-server\-side\-encryption: AES256" ,
which a destination without a master key refuses; a delete marker is a signed
DeleteObject of the key, and a tag change a signed PutObjectTagging or
DeleteObjectTagging of the version. Every request carries
.BR "x\-amz\-replication\-status: REPLICA" .
A storage runtime that is the destination judges such a write as
.BR s3:ReplicateObject ,
such a delete as
.B s3:ReplicateDelete
and such a tag change as
.B s3:ReplicateTags
(see
.BR "Bucket policy" ),
for a user the policies allow that and do not deny the write it stands for, and
takes the header from nobody else, whose request is the plain write it is;
it marks the version REPLICA, and never copies a replica or a replica's delete
marker on, so two buckets that replicate to each other copy each change once.
The request also names the source's version id in
.BR x\-socketley\-replica\-version\-id ,
which a storage runtime that is the destination gives the replica or its delete
marker, as S3 does, as long as that id is still newer than every version the key
has there and dated no more than fifteen minutes past the destination's clock; otherwise the replica gets an id of its own. Another S3 stores the
replication header as it does and ignores the other, so there a replica's version
id is its own. A replica's time and owner are the destination's own.
.PP
The shard that owns a key queues its copies, one entry per destination bucket,
in
.IB dir /.replicate/outbox. shard\fR,
synced beside the change itself and before it is answered, as its events are
(see
.BR "Event notifications" ),
so a crash never copies a change that was not made. Each target has up to 16
copies in flight, each on a connection of its own, a key's copies one at a time
and in the order they were made; a body of 64 KiB or more that is not encrypted
goes to an http target straight from its file by splice, without passing through
the runtime. A refused connection, a stall of 10 seconds,
no answer within 120 seconds of the whole request, and an answer of 408, 409,
429 or a 5xx other than 501 are tried again after 0.25 s, doubling to 8 s, and
logged at the first of a run; any other answer, or a version whose bytes cannot be
read, is FAILED and logged with the destination's error code. An entry is retired
after the version's status is written, so a crash between the two copies the
version again, which is harmless. A start queues the outbox again, and every
version its index still calls PENDING with no entry, so an answered version is
copied even if its entry was lost. A target's host name is resolved off the loop
as a webhook's is;
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_REPLICATION_HOSTS
is the test aid that points one elsewhere.
.B stats
shows
.B replication_pending
(entries waiting now),
.B replication_completed
and
.B replication_failed
(versions and delete markers),
.BR replication_retries ,
.B replication_bytes
(body bytes a destination took),
.B replication_connects
and
.B replication_reuses
(requests that went on a connection kept from the request before, as a webhook's
is), per shard.
.SS Cluster
The nodes of a cluster (\fB\-\-nodes\fR) each keep every bucket, and every
node takes reads and writes, so a client or a load balancer may use any of them.
Each change a node makes is sent to every other node, from its replication
outbox in the way a bucket's replication sends a version (see
.BR Replication ),
and is in that outbox, synced, before the client hears it was made: a version or
an object (as a PutObject, or as a multipart upload in the same parts, so its ETag
is the same everywhere), a delete marker, a tag change, a removal, a version
removed by its id, a version's retention or legal hold, a bucket made with its
owner, removed, or given or relieved of a configuration document (versioning,
object lock, policy, lifecycle, CORS, encryption, tags, website, logging, quota,
public access block, notification, replication), the IAM API's users, groups and
roles, and share links with their downloads. What a node was sent it does not
send on. A lifecycle rule's expiries are each node's own, since every node runs
the bucket's rules; so are a bucket's event notifications, which only the node a
change was made on sends, and its replication to another endpoint, which only
that node copies.
.PP
Every node ends with the same state whatever order the changes reach it in.
A version keeps the id, the time and the lock its node gave it, and one older than
a key's latest goes behind it rather than in front; a node that has not yet heard
of its bucket's versioning answers it
.B "503 SlowDown"
until it has. An object of a bucket that keeps no versions is the write made
last, by the time it was written on its node and then by the larger ETag; a removal
is stamped with its time as well, and a node keeps a record of what it removed for
24 hours, across a restart, in
.IB dir /.removed. shard\fR,
so that an older write that crosses the removal on its way is not made. A removal
is sent even by a node that did not have the key yet. The IAM document is
the one changed last on any node, so two admins who change IAM on two nodes in the
same moment keep only the later change. A share link's downloads are counted by
each node and added up, so its limit holds across the nodes once their counts have
met. A node answers a write it already has, or one older than what it has,
.B "412 PreconditionFailed"
to the node that sent it, which takes that as delivered.
.PP
A node that was stopped is sent what it missed once it is back, as a replication
target is; a change for a bucket another lane is still making or emptying waits for
it up to 15 minutes, and is then given up and logged. A node more than 100000
changes behind a shard has that shard's queue for it let go, which bounds the
memory a long absence takes, and is sent everything instead, as below, once it
answers again. A node whose data is new to
another \(em one that joined the cluster, or began again with an empty
.B \-\-dir
\(em is sent everything that node holds: every node keeps a random id of its data in
.IB dir /.cluster\-id\fR,
asks every other node for its id at start and every 30 seconds, and when an id is
new sends the IAM document, the share links, every bucket with its documents, and
every version, delete marker and object it holds, 500 keys at a time and asking
first whether the node already has each, and last what it removed in the last 24
hours, noting how far it got in
.IB dir /.replicate/seed. shard . node
so a restart goes on from there.
.PP
The nodes need the same credentials and the same
.B \-\-sse\-key\-file
(an SSE\-S3 object is sent in the clear over the node's endpoint and encrypted
again there; use https between nodes that are not on one host), and their clocks
within a second of one another, since the time a write was made decides between
two writes of one key. An SSE\-C object is not sent, as its key is its client's.
.B stats
shows, per shard,
.B cluster_pending
(changes waiting for another node),
.B cluster_sent
and
.B cluster_failed
(changes the other nodes took, and gave up),
.B cluster_in
(bucket, IAM and share records another node sent here),
.B cluster_seeds
(nodes this shard began to send everything to),
.B cluster_seeded
(versions and objects it queued for them) and
.B cluster_seeding
(nodes it has not yet queued everything for, or not yet asked) and
.B cluster_overflows
(queues let go),
.B cluster_synced
and
.B cluster_sync_timeouts
(writes answered once the nodes sync names had them, and once its wait was over).
.PP
Three nodes on one host, 4 KiB PutObject from 16 clients for 15 seconds: the two
other nodes keep up as the writes come (4 changes waiting when the clients stop,
none 0.4 seconds later). On a 4\-vCPU VPS with the two other nodes on tmpfs, a node
takes 1800 PutObject a second against 2660 alone, its own work per write growing
from 450 to 557 microseconds of CPU, which is the write read back and sent twice;
1 MiB PutObject from 8 clients, 200 a second against 415 alone, at 5.7 against 4.4
milliseconds of CPU each, the two other nodes taking every megabyte on the same
four CPUs.
.SS Scrub
With
.BR \-\-scrub ,
every shard reads each version it holds back from its file, a key at a time in
order, at a pace that makes one pass of the shard's bytes take the period, at
least a megabyte a second; a pass begins a period after the last one began, and
where a pass got to is noted in
.IB dir /.scrub. shard
every 30 seconds, so a restart goes on from there. The bytes are checked against
the MD5 the object's header names: a whole object's against its MD5, a multipart
object's part by part against the ETag its parts make, and an SSE\-S3 or SSE\-KMS
object's in its plaintext, which a chunk whose tag fails makes corrupt as well.
An SSE\-C object, whose key only its client has, and an object whose parts are not
recorded, are passed over and counted as skipped.
.PP
A copy whose bytes differ is logged once as an error, however many passes find it.
On a runtime that is no node of a cluster it stays as it is, to be restored from a
copy. On a node of one (\fB\-\-nodes\fR), a version named by its id, or an object of a
bucket that keeps no versions, is asked for of the other nodes in turn by a HEAD
that must answer its ETag; the first that holds it whole is asked to send it again,
and the corrupt copy is taken away first, with no event, no copy sent to the other
nodes and no record of a removal, and only while it is still the copy the scrub
read. The node's copy comes back under its own id, behind the latest when it was
behind it. Until it is back, a read of it on this node finds nothing.
.PP
.B stats
shows, per shard,
.B scrub_objects
and
.B scrub_bytes
(read),
.B scrub_corrupt
(found, each once),
.BR scrub_skipped ,
.B scrub_passes
and
.B scrub_last_pass_ms
(how long the last whole pass took),
.B scrub_heals
(corrupt copies another node was asked to send again) and
.B scrub_unhealed
(those no other node held whole).
.SS Compression
With
.BR \-\-compress ,
every shard looks, a key at a time, at the versions written since its last pass
that have now gone unwritten for the duration, and compresses each that is stored
in the clear, is of one part and holds at least 4 KiB: its bytes are cut into
chunks of 64 KiB, each compressed as an LZ4 block, or kept as it is when that is
no shorter, and written beside the version with a table of where each chunk lies,
then put in its place under its key's lane while it is still the version it was.
A version whose first megabyte, or whole body, does not get an eighth shorter is
left as it was. A pass begins once a quarter of the duration, and at least a
second, has passed since the last one's window; what it took is noted in
.IB dir /.compact. shard ,
so each version is looked at once. The reads go at most 64 MiB a second.
.PP
Nothing a client sees changes: the ETag, the checksums, the size and the time are
the version's, a range opens only the chunks it touches, a copy is made of the
bytes and not of the chunks, and replication, the scrub, media and WebDAV read it
as they read any other. A version under SSE, a multipart object and an object
of under 4 KiB are never compressed.
.PP
.B stats
shows, per shard,
.B compressed_objects
(versions compressed),
.B compressed_skipped
(read and left, as no shorter or changed meanwhile),
.BR compressed_failed ,
.B compressed_bytes_in
and
.B compressed_bytes_out
(their bytes, and what they take on disk now),
.B compressed_passes
and
.B compressed_gets
(GETs whose chunks were opened from the file rather than from memory).
.SS S3 Select
SelectObjectContent runs one SQL expression over an object stored as CSV
(FileHeaderInfo USE, IGNORE or NONE, any field and record delimiter, quotes,
comments) or as JSON (DOCUMENT or LINES), with an optional ScanRange, and answers
in AWS's event stream, sent as HTTP chunks while the object is read: Records
events as they come, a Progress event when asked for, then Stats (the bytes
scanned, processed and returned) and End, or an error event when the object
cannot be read past the 200. The expression is
.B SELECT
.B *
or a list of expressions with
.BR AS ,
.B FROM S3Object
with an alias and a JSON path
.RB ( S3Object[*].items[*] ),
.BR WHERE ,
.BR LIMIT ;
columns by position
.RB ( _1 ),
by header name or by JSON path
.RB ( s.a.b[0] );
the comparisons,
.BR AND ,
.BR OR ,
.BR NOT ,
.BR LIKE ,
.BR BETWEEN ,
.BR IN ,
.BR "IS NULL" ,
arithmetic and
.BR || ;
.BR CAST ,
.BR LOWER ,
.BR UPPER ,
.BR TRIM ,
.BR CHAR_LENGTH ,
.BR SUBSTRING ,
.B COALESCE
and
.BR NULLIF ;
and the aggregates
.BR COUNT ,
.BR SUM ,
.BR AVG ,
.B MIN
and
.BR MAX .
A record over 1 MiB is
.BR OverMaxRecordSize ,
and GZIP, BZIP2 and Parquet input are
.BR "501 NotImplemented" .
The object is read as a GET reads it, sealed, compressed or at a tier, and the
request needs what a GetObject of it needs, SSE\-C headers included.
.B stats
counts
.B selects
and
.B select_scanned
(the bytes their scans read).
.SS Tiers
A lifecycle rule's
.B Transition
or
.B NoncurrentVersionTransition
names a tier of
.B \-\-tiers
as its
.BR StorageClass .
When a version comes due, the shard that owns its key reads it and writes it to
the tier with one signed PUT (its Content\-MD5 the version's own when it is of
one part), under
.IB prefix bucket / mtime \- md5 \- version / key
in the tier's bucket, so the same version of every node of a cluster is one
object there; once the tier has it, the version's file here becomes its header
alone, put in place under its key's lane while it is still the version that was
read. A version under SSE, one larger than 5 GiB and a multipart object whose part
list follows its bytes stay here.
.PP
A moved version is listed, and answered on GET and HEAD, with the tier's name as
its
.BR x\-amz\-storage\-class ;
a GET, a range or a part of it is read from the tier half a megabyte at a time,
two windows ahead, with the size, ETag, time and checksums it always had.
CopyObject and UploadPartCopy from it are
.BR "403 InvalidObjectState" ,
as S3 answers for an archived object; the scrub, replication and a cluster's
seeding pass it over. A quarter day after the last, each shard walks every
tier's listing and removes there what no version of a key it owns is any more,
an hour after the tier took it at the earliest, so a version deleted, expired or
written over here is gone from the tier within six hours.
.PP
.B stats
shows
.B tier_moved
and
.B tier_moved_bytes
(versions moved and their bytes),
.BR tier_failed ,
.B tier_moving
(waiting and in flight),
.B tier_reads
and
.B tier_read_failures
(windows read back),
.B tier_swept
(objects removed at a tier) and
.B tier_gets
(GETs read from a tier).
.SS Server\-side encryption
.B "x\-amz\-server\-side\-encryption: AES256"
on PutObject, CreateMultipartUpload or CopyObject (SSE\-S3) stores the object
encrypted under a key of its own, which is wrapped by the runtime's master key
(\fB\-\-sse\-key\-file\fR), and every answer about it \(em the PUT, the upload's
steps, GET, HEAD, the copy \(em says so with the same header. A GET needs nothing:
the plaintext comes back, its ETag is the MD5 of the plaintext as for any object,
and ranges, partNumber and conditions work as they do in the clear.
.B aws:kms
(SSE\-KMS) is the same, with the object's key wrapped by a key derived from the
master key and the KMS key id the request names in
.BR x\-amz\-server\-side\-encryption\-aws\-kms\-key\-id ,
or the bucket default's, or
.B arn:aws:kms:us\-east\-1:000000000000:alias/aws/s3
when neither names one: any key id is taken, no key is kept but the master key,
and every answer names the id as the write gave it. An encryption context is taken
and not kept.
.B aws:kms:dsse
is
.BR "501 NotImplemented" ,
another value
.BR "400 InvalidArgument" ,
a KMS key or context without
.B aws:kms
.BR "400 InvalidArgument" ,
and the header on a GET or HEAD, which only what writes an object chooses,
.BR "400 InvalidArgument" .
.PP
.B PutBucketEncryption
with an
.B AES256
rule makes every new object of the bucket SSE\-S3 when its request asks nothing else,
and an
.B aws:kms
rule SSE\-KMS under its KMSMasterKeyID:
puts, copies into it, uploads begun in it and a script's
.BR socketley.storage.put .
A bucket's rule is a document like its CORS rules, changed by shard 0 with every
shard told first (see
.BR "Bucket policy" ),
kept in
.IB dir / bucket /encryption
and read at every start; one that no longer parses still encrypts, with an error in the
log.
.B DeleteBucketEncryption
stops it for new objects and leaves the ones it encrypted as they are.
.PP
SSE\-C is the client's own key, sent with every request that touches the object as
.BR "x\-amz\-server\-side\-encryption\-customer\-algorithm: AES256" ,
.B \-customer\-key
(the 32 bytes in base64) and
.BR \-customer\-key\-MD5 ,
as boto3 and the AWS CLI send them. It is taken over TLS only, the runtime's own or
a proxy's that
.B \-\-tls\-offloaded
names: the headers over plain HTTP from anywhere else or a unix node are
.BR "400 InvalidRequest" ,
and nothing is written. The key is never stored: the object's data key is wrapped
under it, which only that key opens, so a GET or HEAD without the headers is
.BR "400 InvalidRequest" ,
with another key
.BR "403 AccessDenied" ,
and with a key\-MD5 that is not the key's
.BR "400 InvalidArgument" .
CopyObject and UploadPartCopy open an SSE\-C source with
.BR x\-amz\-copy\-source\-server\-side\-encryption\-customer\-* ,
and the parts of an SSE\-C upload carry its key. As in S3, the ETag of an SSE\-C object
or part is not the MD5 of its bytes (it is derived from that MD5 under the data key),
nothing of such an object is kept in memory between requests, and a script's
.B socketley.storage.get
of one is refused, since it has no key to give.
.PP
An SSE\-C upload's part list is bound to its key when it completes, so a header
rewritten to drop trailing parts does not read. S3 does not ask CompleteMultipartUpload
for the customer key, but boto3 and the AWS CLI can send the three headers with it:
then the key must be the upload's
.RB ( "403 AccessDenied"
otherwise) and the data key is wrapped again with the digest of the part list, as
SSE\-S3 does. A completion without them is bound instead by an HMAC\-SHA256 of the
object's encryption record and its part list, under a key derived from the
.B \-\-sse\-key\-file
master key (HKDF), recorded in the header as
.B x\-socketley\-sse\-parts
and checked before every read, which then needs that master key too. With neither, the
completion is
.B "400 InvalidRequest"
naming both ways out and the upload stays open to be completed again. An upload begun
before this binding existed completes only with the headers, since its object could
not be told from an older one with its HMAC removed; without them it is
.B "400 InvalidRequest"
saying so. An object of an upload begun since is never read with its list unbound, a
missing or wrong HMAC being
.BR "500 InternalError" ;
one completed without the key before this binding existed is read as it was, unless
it carries an HMAC that does not hold.
.PP
On disk an encrypted object is its header, which records the scheme
.RB ( x\-socketley\-sse :
format, SSE\-S3 or SSE\-C, the chunk size, whether the body is one stream or one per
part, the master key's id and the wrapped data key), and then its plaintext in chunks
of 64 KiB, each sealed with AES\-256\-GCM on its own as a random 12\-byte nonce, the
ciphertext and a 16\-byte tag: 28 bytes per 64 KiB. In format 2, which every new
object and upload is written in, a chunk is sealed with the part number its stream
was uploaded as (0 for an object that is one stream), its index there and whether it
ends the stream, and every stream ends in a chunk shorter than 64 KiB, empty when its
length is whole chunks; a completed upload's data key is wrapped again with the
digest of its part list, sizes and numbers. So a header cut back to a chunk
boundary, chunks moved to another place or swapped between two parts of one upload,
and a header that names fewer parts or other ones all fail to open rather than read
as a shorter or mixed object. A range or a part reads and opens only the chunks it
touches, and the empty chunk that ends a stream when the range reaches that end; a
chunk that does not open ends the response there with a line in the log, and an
object small enough to be read whole with its header, or whose data key does not
unwrap, is answered
.B "500 InternalError"
before anything else. Objects written in
format 1, which bound a chunk to its index alone, are read as they were, and so are
parts of an upload begun under it. The parts of a multipart upload are each a stream
of their own, written where the object needs them as unencrypted ones are, and found
again by the part sizes and numbers the object records (see
.BR "Reading by part number" ).
A copy of an encrypted object, or into an encrypted one, is opened and sealed again
on its way through, under a key of its own; a tag change, a new version and the
assembly of an upload move the sealed bytes as they are. The chunks are sealed and
opened on the hashing threads (\fBSOCKETLEY_STORAGE_HASH_THREADS\fR) for bodies and
windows of more than 64 KiB, and in line below. A GET of an encrypted object cannot be
spliced from the file, so it is read a megabyte at a time, opened and written from
memory, with the next megabyte read while one is sent; an SSE\-S3 object of up to
60000 bytes is kept opened in memory like any other.
.PP
Not supported: SSE\-KMS and bucket keys, and changing the master key \(em an object
opens only under the key that wrapped its data key.
.SS Bucket lifecycle
A bucket's lifecycle configuration is S3's
.B LifecycleConfiguration
document, of at most 1000 rules, each with an
.B ID
(at most 255 bytes, unique, generated when left out),
.B Status
.RB ( Enabled
or
.BR Disabled ),
the objects it covers as a
.B Filter
\(em empty for the whole bucket, or one of a
.BR Prefix ,
a
.B Tag
(a
.B Key
and a
.B Value
the object's tags must have),
.B ObjectSizeGreaterThan
and
.B ObjectSizeLessThan
(each in bytes and exclusive), or an
.B And
of a prefix, any number of tags and the two sizes, all of which must hold (or
the older form, a
.B Prefix
of the rule's own) \(em and at least one action:
.B Expiration
by
.B Days
or by
.B Date
(midnight UTC), and
.B AbortIncompleteMultipartUpload
by
.BR DaysAfterInitiation .
.B ExpiredObjectDeleteMarker
.RB ( true ,
in an
.B Expiration
of its own), and
.B NoncurrentVersionExpiration
by
.BR NoncurrentDays ,
and with
.B NewerNoncurrentVersions
(1 to 100) only a version that many noncurrent versions of its key are newer than.
.B Transition
by
.B Days
(zero included) or by
.BR Date ,
and
.B NoncurrentVersionTransition
by
.BR NoncurrentDays ,
each to a
.B StorageClass
that names a tier of
.B \-\-tiers
(any other is
.BR "400 InvalidStorageClass" ;
see
.BR Tiers ).
.B NoncurrentVersionTransition
takes
.B NewerNoncurrentVersions
as well. A second transition of either kind in one rule is refused with
.B "501 NotImplemented"
and the configuration that was there stays; a document that is not one is
.BR MalformedXML ,
a value S3 would not take (Days of zero, a Date that is not midnight, a
repeated ID, 1001 rules, an ObjectSizeLessThan of 0, a NewerNoncurrentVersions of 0 or over 100) is
.BR InvalidArgument ,
a tag key or value an object could not have is
.BR InvalidTag ,
and what S3 refuses as
.B InvalidRequest
is: one tag key twice in an
.BR And ,
sizes with nothing between them, and a tag or size filter on a rule that
aborts uploads (an upload has neither yet) or one with
.BR ExpiredObjectDeleteMarker .
The rules are synced into
.IB dir / bucket /lifecycle
before the answer, so they survive a stop or a crash, and go with the bucket
when it is deleted. With
.B \-\-shards
a change is answered once no shard can go on with the old rules, and a shard
unsure of them expires nothing in the bucket until it has read them (see
.BR "Bucket policy" ).
.PP
The rules are carried out by a sweep in each shard over the keys and uploads it
owns. An object expires
.B Days
days after its last write, rounded up to the next midnight UTC as S3 does (one
written on the 1st at 10:30 with Days 1 goes at the midnight that begins the 3rd); with a
.B Date
every object under the rule goes from that midnight on, those written later
too. It is removed exactly as a DeleteObject removes it \(em the unlink synced,
then the journal record \(em and a script's
.B on_delete
is told. An object written again or given other tags after the sweep found it
is judged as it is then, and a configuration changed or removed meanwhile is
obeyed. The tags and size a filter asks about are the index's, in memory, so a
filter on them costs the walk no reads. An upload is
aborted
.B DaysAfterInitiation
days after it began, rounded the same way. In a versioned bucket an expiring
latest version is covered by a delete marker, as S3 does, rather than removed; a
noncurrent version is removed for good
.B NoncurrentDays
days after the version that replaced it was written, rounded the same way and
filtered by the version's own tags and size; and with
.B ExpiredObjectDeleteMarker
a delete marker that is its key's only version left is removed. A pass runs at each start, within a
second of a change to the rules and then hourly; it walks the index a page at a
time with at most eight removals in flight and a pause between steps, so a
bucket with a great many expiring keys is worked through without holding up
other requests.
.B stats
counts what it did in
.B lifecycle_expired
and
.BR lifecycle_aborted ,
and each pass that removed anything says so in the log.
.PP
A PutObject's answer, and a GetObject's or HeadObject's of the latest version,
carry
.B x\-amz\-expiration
(\fBexpiry\-date="\fIdate\fB", rule\-id="\fIid\fB"\fR) when a rule will expire the
object, naming the rule that does so first, as S3 does.
.PP
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_LIFECYCLE_DAY_SECS
is a test aid: read by the sweep alone, it makes a lifecycle day that many
seconds (at most 86400) so a rule can be watched taking effect, with passes as
often as every second. It is never meant for a runtime holding data anyone wants.
.SS Bucket policy
A bucket's policy is S3's JSON document, which can make a bucket or a part of it
readable by anyone \(em static assets, downloads \(em while every other request to
the runtime still has to be signed, and can refuse a signed key what it would
otherwise be let do. For example, anyone may read the objects under
.BR public/ :
.PP
.nf
{
  "Version": "2012\-10\-17",
  "Statement": [{
    "Sid": "PublicRead",
    "Effect": "Allow",
    "Principal": "*",
    "Action": "s3:GetObject",
    "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::media/public/*"
  }]
}
.fi
.PP
What it takes is the part of S3's grammar this runtime can honour exactly, and a
document asking for anything else is refused as
.B "400 MalformedPolicy"
naming what it could not take, never accepted to do less than it says:
.TP
.B Version
2012\-10\-17 or 2008\-10\-17, or none;
.B Id
any string.
.TP
.B Statement
one statement or a list of them, each with an optional
.B Sid
(unique),
.B Effect
.RB ( Allow
or
.BR Deny ),
.BR Principal ,
.BR Action ,
.B Resource
and an optional
.BR Condition .
.BR NotPrincipal ,
.B NotAction
and
.B NotResource
are refused.
.TP
.B Principal
.B *
or
.B {"AWS": "*"}
for everyone, signed or not; or
.B {"AWS": [...]}
naming this runtime's users and access keys, each as the key itself, as
.BI arn:aws:iam:: account :user/ name
(the name is a user's id or an access key), or as
.BI arn:aws:iam:: account :root
for every key. A
.B {"Service": ...}
principal is taken and names no requester here, so a statement naming services
alone matches nothing whatever its conditions, which are not checked: the policy
S3 documents for a log target, letting
.B logging.s3.amazonaws.com
write, is taken as it is written. Federated and canonical\-user principals and
roles are refused.
.TP
.B Action
one or a list of
.BI s3: Name
with
.B *
and
.B ?
wildcards, compared without case, or
.B *
alone. A name must be one of S3's, and those this runtime does not implement
(s3:RestoreObject, s3:GetBucketAnalyticsConfiguration, .\|.\|.) are taken and match nothing.
GetObject covers GetObject and HeadObject, by part too; PutObject covers PutObject,
CopyObject, CreateMultipartUpload, UploadPart, UploadPartCopy and
CompleteMultipartUpload; DeleteObject covers DeleteObject and DeleteObjects; ListBucket
covers ListObjects, ListObjectsV2 and HeadBucket; PutBucketCORS and
PutLifecycleConfiguration and PutEncryptionConfiguration cover the deletes of those
configurations, and GetEncryptionConfiguration covers GetBucketEncryption; the rest are
named as S3 names them (GetObjectTagging, AbortMultipartUpload,
ListMultipartUploadParts, ListBucketMultipartUploads, GetBucketLocation, CreateBucket,
DeleteBucket, GetBucketVersioning, PutBucketVersioning, .\|.\|.). A request that names a
version with ?versionId= is the version's action, as in S3: GetObjectVersion for GET
and HEAD (and for a copy's source named key?versionId=), DeleteObjectVersion for DELETE
and for each DeleteObjects entry with a VersionId (judged per entry, a refused one
answered AccessDenied in the result), and GetObjectVersionTagging,
PutObjectVersionTagging and DeleteObjectVersionTagging; ListBucketVersions covers
ListObjectVersions. Object lock has GetBucketObjectLockConfiguration and
PutBucketObjectLockConfiguration, GetObjectRetention and PutObjectRetention (also a
write that names a retention), GetObjectLegalHold and PutObjectLegalHold (also a
write that names a hold), and BypassGovernanceRetention, asked beside the action of a
request that sends the bypass header. GetBucketNotification and PutBucketNotification
cover the two notification configuration operations, and GetReplicationConfiguration
and PutReplicationConfiguration the three replication ones. A write that sends
.B "x\-amz\-replication\-status: REPLICA"
(PutObject and the steps of a multipart upload) is ReplicateObject instead of
PutObject, such a DeleteObject is ReplicateDelete, and such a PutObjectTagging or
DeleteObjectTagging is ReplicateTags: a user allowed those three and nothing else can
be the destination's replication user. The header counts only for a user allowed the
replication action, and a Deny of the write it stands for still refuses it; anyone
else's request is the plain write, its header ignored.
.TP
.B Resource
one or a list of
.BI arn:aws:s3::: bucket
for the bucket itself and
.BI arn:aws:s3::: bucket / pattern
for its objects, with
.B *
for any run of characters and
.B ?
for one; the bucket must be this one, or a pattern that covers it, as
.B arn:aws:s3:::*
does (that one, ending in
.BR * ,
covering the bucket's objects too, since IAM's
.B *
spans a slash), and a statement must have an action that applies
to one of its resources (an object action to a key pattern, a bucket action to the
bucket). Policy variables are refused.
.TP
.B Condition
.B IpAddress
and
.B NotIpAddress
on
.B aws:SourceIp
(addresses and CIDR ranges, IPv4 and IPv6, an IPv4 address mapped into IPv6 being
the IPv4 address), and
.B Bool
on
.B aws:SecureTransport
(whether the connection is TLS), and
.BR StringEquals ,
.B StringNotEquals
and
.B Null
on
.B s3:x\-amz\-server\-side\-encryption
(the request's
.B x\-amz\-server\-side\-encryption
header, compared as given: a request that does not send it equals no value, so
.B StringNotEquals
holds for it, and
.B Null
\fB"true"\fR holds for it alone). PutObject, CopyObject and CreateMultipartUpload
carry the header; UploadPart, UploadPartCopy and CompleteMultipartUpload take the
encryption their upload's creation chose and was judged on, so no statement with a
condition on it applies to them, Allow or Deny. A bucket's default encryption is
not the header, so S3's two statements that deny a PutObject without
.B AES256
refuse a put that leaves it to the default. Every condition of a statement must
hold. A client on a unix socket has no address, so it is in no range: an
.B IpAddress
never holds for it and a
.B NotIpAddress
always does.
.IP
Beyond those, IAM's operator families on the keys a request here carries:
.BR String (Not) Equals (IgnoreCase),
.BR String (Not) Like
with
.B *
and
.BR ? ,
.BR Arn (Not) Equals
and
.BR Arn (Not) Like
as their string forms,
.BR Numeric \&.\|.\|.
and
.BR Date \&.\|.\|.
(an epoch second or an ISO 8601 time),
.BR Bool ,
.B Null
and the IP pair, each with
.B IfExists
and the
.B ForAnyValue:
and
.B ForAllValues:
qualifiers. The keys:
.BR aws:Referer ,
.BR aws:UserAgent ,
.BR aws:SourceIp ,
.BR aws:SecureTransport ,
.BR aws:CurrentTime ,
.BR aws:EpochTime ,
.BR aws:username
and
.B aws:userid
(the user's id, absent for a request without credentials),
.BR aws:PrincipalType ,
.BR s3:prefix ,
.B s3:delimiter
and
.B s3:max\-keys
(a listing's query),
.B s3:versionid
and the
.BR s3:x\-amz\- .\|.\|.
and
.BR s3:object\-lock\- .\|.\|.
keys (the request's headers of those names),
.B s3:RequestObjectTag/\fIkey\fR
and
.B s3:RequestObjectTagKeys
(the request's
.B x\-amz\-tagging
header),
.BR s3:signatureversion ,
.B s3:authType
and
.BR s3:TlsVersion .
A key the request does not carry fails its condition, except under
.BR IfExists ,
a negated operator or
.BR ForAllValues ,
as IAM has it.
.B s3:ExistingObjectTag/\fIkey\fR
is refused, since the object's tags are not read before the request is judged, and
so is any other key or operator.
.PP
A request to a bucket with a policy is authenticated as it always is, and then
judged: a statement applies when its action, principal and resource match the
request and its conditions hold, an applying
.B Deny
refuses the request with
.B "403 AccessDenied"
whatever else applies, and an applying
.B Allow
lets in a request that carries no credentials, on a runtime without
.BR \-\-anonymous ,
and a signed request of a user that does not own the bucket (see
.BR Users ).
A request whose signature is wrong is refused as before and
never taken for an anonymous request; a presigned URL is a signed request. On a
runtime with
.B \-\-anonymous
every request without credentials is already let in, and a
.B Deny
for
.B *
still refuses it. A copy is also judged as a GetObject of its source, by the
source bucket's policy, so an anonymous copy needs both. DeleteObjects names its
keys in the body, so it is judged over every key of the bucket: a
.B Deny
over any key refuses it, and only an
.B Allow
over
.BI arn:aws:s3::: bucket /*
lets an anonymous one in. A request a policy refuses is answered before its body
is read. The policy's own four operations answer to ownership alone for the bucket's owner and
an admin, so a policy can neither let an anonymous request read or change it nor lock
the owner out of removing it. A request let in by a policy is still the Lua
.B on_request
hook's to refuse (see
.BR Lua ),
which never sees one the policy refused.
.PP
The document is compiled when it is set, and again when a shard starts, so a
request is judged against compiled statements: a runtime none of whose buckets has
a policy looks nothing up, a signed request to a bucket whose policy has no
.B Deny
looks up the bucket and nothing more, and only a statement that compares
.B aws:SourceIp
asks the kernel for the client's address, once per connection; the other keys are
read from the request only when a statement that names them applies.
.PP
As in S3, a request a policy lets in is told a key is not there only if it may
list the bucket the key would be in: a GET, HEAD or other object request for a
missing key is
.B "404 NoSuchKey"
when
.B s3:ListBucket
on the bucket applies to the requester (an
.B Allow
for an anonymous request, no
.B Deny
for a signed one or on a runtime with
.BR \-\-anonymous ),
and
.B "403 AccessDenied"
when it does not. For a copy the bucket is the source's.
.PP
The policy is kept in
.IB dir / bucket /policy ,
written beside it, synced and renamed over it, so it survives a restart and a
crash leaves the old one or the new; it goes with the bucket. A stored policy that
does not compile when a shard starts, because the file was edited or this version
refuses what an older one took, is not skipped: the bucket refuses every request
with
.B "403 AccessDenied"
naming why, except Put, Get and Delete BucketPolicy and GetBucketPolicyStatus by
the runtime's keys, so it can be read back and replaced or removed, and the log has
an error saying so.
.PP
With
.B \-\-shards
shard 0 makes every change of a bucket's policy and CORS, lifecycle and versioning configuration,
one at a time per bucket, whichever shard took the request, and answers it only once
no running shard can go on with the old document. It writes the new one beside the
file as
.IB kind .new
(empty for a removal), asks every other shard to stop serving the bucket until it
learns how the change ended, and only when all of them have promised renames it over
the file (or removes the file), tells them the new document and answers; a shard it
cannot reach, because it is stopped, starting, wedged or cut off, fails the change
with
.B "503 ServiceUnavailable"
and nothing changes anywhere, since the file is removed and the shards that promised
are released. A shard that promised and is never told the outcome serves nothing of
that bucket (503 ServiceUnavailable, which clients retry) until it reads the outcome
from the files: while
.IB kind .new
exists the change is undecided, and once it is gone the file is the document. A
shard re-reads them at its next request to the bucket and at its periodic sweep, so a
lost message delays a shard and never leaves it with the old document; a request on
another shard during the moment between a shard's promise and the rename can be
answered 503 the same way. A shard that starts while a change is undecided, or takes a
bucket up later than the others, reads the bucket's documents the same way before
serving it. This costs a runtime with no change in flight one counter load per
request.
.SS Server access logging
A logged bucket's every answer is a line of S3's server access log format in
objects delivered to its target bucket:
.RS
.PP
.nf
PUT /photos?logging
<BucketLoggingStatus>
  <LoggingEnabled>
    <TargetBucket>logs</TargetBucket>
    <TargetPrefix>photos/</TargetPrefix>
  </LoggingEnabled>
</BucketLoggingStatus>
.fi
.RE
.PP
A line names the bucket's owner and the bucket, the time, the client's address,
the requester's access key (\- for none), the request id, the operation
.RB ( REST.GET.OBJECT ,
.BR REST.PUT.PART ,
.BR REST.PUT.VERSIONING ,
.B WEBSITE.GET.OBJECT
and so on; a DeleteObjects is a
.B REST.POST.MULTI_OBJECT_DELETE
line and a
.B BATCH.DELETE.OBJECT
line for each key), the key, the request line, the status, the error code, the bytes of
body sent, a put's size, the milliseconds from the request's start to its answer,
the referer and user agent, the signature version and how it was given, the TLS
cipher, the Host header and the TLS version; a field this runtime does not know
is \-, as S3 writes one it has nothing for. A line is written when the answer
starts, so a download that is cut off is logged as sent.
.PP
Each shard holds its lines for each target and delivers them as one object once
a minute's worth or 1 MiB are held, named
.IB prefix YYYY-MM-DD-hh-mm-ss- unique,
or with a PartitionedPrefix
.IB prefix owner /us\-east\-1/ bucket / YYYY / MM / DD / YYYY-MM-DD-hh-mm-ss- unique
by the delivery's date. The object is put as a client's would be, encrypted when
the target encrypts and replicated and notified when it is; one that cannot be
put, the target having been deleted or gone past its quota, is dropped with a
warning in the log. Lines still held when the runtime stops are lost, as at most
a minute of them. The target must be a bucket with the same owner as the logged
one; it may be the logged bucket itself, and the deliveries are not logged. The
configuration is kept in
.IB dir / bucket /logging
and changed by shard 0 like the bucket's other documents.
.SS Public access block
A bucket's public access block keeps it from being opened to everyone by mistake:
.RS
.PP
.nf
PUT /photos?publicAccessBlock
<PublicAccessBlockConfiguration>
  <BlockPublicPolicy>true</BlockPublicPolicy>
  <RestrictPublicBuckets>true</RestrictPublicBuckets>
</PublicAccessBlockConfiguration>
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B BlockPublicPolicy
refuses a PutBucketPolicy whose policy is public, as GetBucketPolicyStatus judges
it, with
.BR "403 AccessDenied" ;
a public policy already there stays.
.B RestrictPublicBuckets
keeps a public policy from letting in any request without credentials, the
bucket's website's included, while the runtime's users keep what it and their
credentials give them, since they are all of one account.
.B BlockPublicAcls
and
.B IgnorePublicAcls
are kept and read back and change nothing, since a public ACL is refused here
whatever they say. The block is kept in
.IB dir / bucket /public\-access\-block
and changed by shard 0 like the bucket's other documents. A runtime started with
.B \-\-anonymous
lets everyone in whatever a bucket's block says, as it does whatever its policy says.
.SS How objects are stored
An object is one file at
.IB dir / bucket /data/ ab / sha256(key)
whose first bytes are a checksummed header carrying the key, size, ETag, content
type, user metadata, tags and time, so the file says what it is and any byte sequence is
a valid key; the 256 directories \fBab\fR (the first two hex digits of the hash) are made
and synced with the bucket, so a put never makes one. Each bucket also keeps an append\-only journal of puts and deletes
(\fBindex.0\fR, or \fBindex.\fIshard\fR for a sharded runtime; CRC32C\-framed, rewritten when garbage passes the live objects),
from which listings are answered out of memory.
.PP
A put writes a temporary file, syncs it, renames it into place and syncs the
directory, and only then answers 200; a delete removes the file and syncs the
directory. A put of up to 1 MiB whose length is known and that carries no
condition (no \fBIf\-None\-Match\fR, not a part) is written instead into one of a
few empty files the bucket keeps ready in \fBpool/\fR, whose names were synced
before any put took one; once that file is synced and renamed into place the put
is answered, and the syncs of \fBpool/\fR and of the object's directory follow the
answer, with the key's next put or delete waiting for them. The answered object is
on disk either way: a crash that takes the rename leaves it whole in \fBpool/\fR,
and the next start moves it into place (a file there counts as whole only when its
length and MD5 are those its header names, which for an encrypted body is the MD5 of
its sealed bytes, kept in its header as
.BR x\-socketley\-stored\-md5 ,
since a start holds no key that opens it). The journal record of either is
appended behind the answer and synced once a megabyte of records has gathered,
before a compaction and at a stop, and never merely because the queue drained,
since that sync would sit in front of the next put's own: the object files are the
truth and the journal is only the index of them, so an answer never waits for it.
A crash at any point leaves the old object or the new one and never a torn one,
and nothing that was answered is undone. A start after a clean stop (which writes
and syncs the journals last) trusts them; any other start removes temporary files
and reads every object header to reconcile the journals with the files, so a
journal that is lost, damaged or short of its last records is rebuilt from the
objects, and a delete whose record was lost does not bring the object back,
because its file is gone.
That reading happens before the listener opens and takes as long as the object
count makes it, and is the only place the loop blocks on the disk: every request
is served on the runtime's io_uring.
A stop closes the listener and the connections first and then lets the journal
writes, directory syncs and compaction already begun finish, for at most a
second; one that cannot finish them in that time is read by the next start as a
crash. Each shard holds a lock on
.BI .lock. n
in the directory until its stop is done, and a start waits up to a second and a
half, in all, for the process it replaces on its shard and for every process still
holding a shard under another
.BR \-\-shards ,
whose files it takes over, and fails if they do not let go \(em a safety net, since
the daemon forks a runtime's workers only once its previous ones have exited
(see
.BR restart ).
A request a shard has
sent a sibling and not had answered when the stop comes is answered as failed, so
it does not hold the stop.
.PP
The start after a crash reads every header even of a file the journal names with
the same size, since an overwrite whose record was lost can leave exactly that: the
header is the only thing that tells the two versions apart. What makes it fast is
that the reads are many at once. The 256 directories of every bucket are shared out
among threads, one per CPU the runtime may run on (at most 16,
\fBSOCKETLEY_STORAGE_RECOVERY_THREADS\fR), which have joined before the listener
opens; each reads its directories with 128 KiB
.BR getdents64 (2)
calls and keeps 32 opens and header reads in flight on an io_uring of its own
(\fBSOCKETLEY_STORAGE_RECOVERY_DEPTH\fR, 0 for one file at a time without a ring),
all within half of what
.B RLIMIT_NOFILE
leaves. Only a file at the one path its key gives, \fIab\fB/\fIsha256(key)\fR, is
read, which is what lets a bucket whose files match its journal one for one skip
the search for records whose file is gone. A directory that cannot be read whole,
or a descriptor table that runs out on the way, fails the start with the reason
rather than dropping the objects it did not see. With 1 KiB objects on an ext4
virtio disk and four CPUs, 100,000 objects take 0.24 s with a warm cache (0.8 s
before this was parallel) and about 1 s from a cold one (11 to 33 s before), and
1,000,000 take 2.3 s warm (9.8 s) and 19 s cold (344 s); a start after a clean
stop reads only the journals, 0.07 s for 100,000 and 0.66 s for 1,000,000.
.PP
A body is never held whole: it streams from the socket into a megabyte staging
buffer, is hashed as it passes (on helper threads from 256 KiB up, so that the
loop is not the one core that hashes every upload) and written while the next
bytes arrive, so a gigabyte upload costs a few megabytes of memory. A GET of 64 KiB or more is sent
by
.BR splice (2)
from the file through a pipe to the socket without entering the process, and the
bytes that took that path are counted as
.B zero_copy_bytes
in
.BR stats ;
smaller objects are read once, with their header, and sent from memory with the
response headers in one write.
.PP
An object of up to 60000 bytes is kept in memory after it is first read, by every
shard that reads it, up to 32 MiB per shard
(\fBSOCKETLEY_STORAGE_HOT_BYTES\fR) with the least recently read going first, and
later GETs on that shard are answered from the copy without opening the file. On the
shard that owns the key a copy is answered only while it is the version the index
names. With
.B \-\-shards
a shard that does not own the key has no index entry to compare with. The first copy
it keeps of a key in one of the 16384 slots is one the key's owner has said is the
version indexed, and from then on the owner tells it to drop its copy of any key of
that slot after it has committed or deleted the key and before it answers the client
(one round trip to those siblings, in parallel, against the milliseconds of the syncs;
none for a key whose slot no sibling has read from), and a read that a drop of its key
arrived during is not kept. A PUT, copy, multipart completion or DELETE that was answered is therefore never
followed by a GET on any shard that returns the bytes it replaced. A sibling that is down
is not waited for and holds nothing when it starts again, and a shard that starts tells
the others to drop every copy of its keys, since it may have died between renaming a new
version into place and telling them. A GET of a 4 KiB object at 32
connections on four shards took 45 us of worker CPU (summed over the shards) before the
copy was kept on every shard and takes 30 us, and about 50 thousand requests per second
becomes about 56 thousand; at one connection 30 to 20 us and 7100 to 8100 requests per
second; a PUT of 4 KiB into memory-backed storage takes about 14 us longer for the round
trip. Every copy keeps the response headers of a plain GET once it has answered one, so
a shard spends the same on a GET whether or not it owns the key; measured later on four
CPUs with another client, four shards take about 26 us per 4 KiB GET at 32 connections
against about 17 us for one shard, the difference being four half-idle loops that each
sleep and wake for every few requests rather than work that only a shard does.
.PP
.SS Performance
Measured with warp and boto3 over plain HTTP with SigV4 on one 16\-core VM with an
ext4 virtio disk, against MinIO as a single node with a single drive on the same
filesystem, three alternating passes with the box quiet before each. Reads are
faster than MinIO's: a 4 KiB GET at one connection is about 4x (8200 against 1900
per second) and at 32 connections 1.7x with one shard and 3.6x with four, a 1 MiB
GET 2x and 4x, a 64 MiB GET 1.4x and 3.8x, each at a tenth or less of the
CPU. A 64 MiB put is 1.3x at one connection and 1.1x at 32 (about 600 MiB/s, which
is what this disk takes), and 64 MiB mixed loads are level with MinIO.
.PP
On a second machine, a 4\-vCPU VM (AMD EPYC, Debian 12, kernel 7.2, warp 1.3.1 on
the same four CPUs, one shard, three alternating passes), against the same MinIO:
4 KiB GET 3.0x at one connection and 3.9x at 32, HEAD 3.9x, 1 MiB GET 2.5x,
64 MiB GET 1.8x, listing 3.1x, multi\-object delete 2.7x, 4 KiB put 1.7x at one
connection and 2.5x at 32 (about 900 and 3000 per second, this disk's sync being
fast), 1 MiB put 1.2x, 64 MiB put level (6.5 against 6.6 per second) and mixed
1 MiB loads 1.6x, with less CPU per request in every row: about nine tenths of
MinIO's for large puts, a third to a half for small ones, a seventeenth for reads. Four
shards are slower than one there, since the client shares the four CPUs; shards are
for a machine with cores to spare. From a client 31 ms away over the internet
(k6, 32 connections), 4 KiB GETs and puts run at what the round trip allows for
both servers (about 800 to 880 per second), with socketley's p99 at 45 to 47 ms
against MinIO's 48 to 147 and about 2.6x less CPU per put.
.PP
Small puts wait for the disk and for little else, so they move with it. A put of
up to 1 MiB waits for one sync, of its pooled file, where it waited for two, the
file and then its directory, and no put waits behind a sync of the journal, whose
record used to be synced behind each answer, where the next put's own sync then
queued behind it. With warp 1.3.1 (three alternating passes each), dropping that
journal sync took 4 KiB puts at one connection from 77 to 104 per second; the
pooled file took them from 107 to 118 in a fast phase of the disk and left them
level (35 and 34) in a slow one, took 4 KiB puts at 32 connections from about 430
to 600 per second and 1 MiB puts at one connection from 22 to 28 in the slow
phase. boto3's p50 for a 4 KiB put went from 13.0 ms to 11.3 and then 7.6 ms,
against MinIO's 8.5 to 17 ms on the same disk minutes apart. Against MinIO, a 4 KiB
put at one connection is level in the disk's fast phases (about 120 per second
each) and 0.6x in its slow ones (33 against 54), and at 32 connections 1.2x or
more. MinIO syncs its file once and never its directory, so a power cut can take a
put it has answered; here an answered put survives one. The disk's sync latency
drifts by two to three times within an hour, which is why these rows name their
phase.
.PP
The journal is not waited for: a stop syncs
it, and a start after any other stop reads every object header, which takes about a
quarter of a second per 100,000 objects with a warm cache and about a second from a
cold one on four CPUs (see How objects are stored). Uploads of 256 KiB or more are
hashed on helper threads (\fBSOCKETLEY_STORAGE_HASH_THREADS\fR), which is what lets
a 64 MiB put at 32 connections use the disk rather than one core.
.PP
A body of known length that arrives slower than 4 MB/s, judged over 10 ms, is
left to gather in the socket and read once per 10 ms tick shared by every such
connection, except for its last 64 KiB; each tick takes all that gathered, even
over TLS, where one receive brings 16 KiB, so a body that began slowly and
speeds up is read at the speed it comes. A slow upload is so not paid for with a
receive and a wakeup per TCP segment; its digests are handed to the hashing
threads 64 KiB at a time. A 1 MiB put sent in 1448\-byte segments at 0.8 MB/s,
eight at once, took about 35 to 60 ms of worker CPU per MiB before and about 6
to 9 after, against about 3 to 5 for the same put sent at loopback speed.
.
.SS HTTP/2
A storage runtime speaks HTTP/2 (RFC 9113) besides HTTP/1.1: over
.B \-\-tls
to a client that offers \fBh2\fR by ALPN, as every browser does, and in the clear
to one that opens with HTTP/2's preface (prior knowledge, as curl speaks it when
told to and a proxy's h2c backend does). Every request is a stream of the one
connection and runs exactly as over HTTP/1.1, S3, WebDAV, the web interface,
share links and media alike, so a browser's uploads, its player's segments and
its pictures no longer wait for one of the six connections it opens to a server
over HTTP/1.1. A stream's window is given back only as its body is taken, so a
disk that cannot keep up holds the client as a full socket does; going out, a
frame of each stream is sent in turn as the client's windows allow, a stream
told its bytes left when they reach the connection's queue, which is kept
under 1 MiB, so the next piece of a file is read while the last is written. This side reads by a window of 4 MiB a stream and 32 MiB a
connection, 128 streams open at once (the next is refused with REFUSED_STREAM),
header lists of up to 64 KiB, and frames of 16 KiB; a peer that resets more
than 400 streams, sends more than 200 PINGs or SETTINGS or 1000 empty DATA
frames in ten seconds, or a header block past 80 KiB or 64 CONTINUATIONs, is
sent GOAWAY with ENHANCE_YOUR_CALM, as RFC 9113 and the rapid\-reset advisory
(CVE\-2023\-44487) ask. A stream's file is read through the process rather than
spliced, as over TLS. A connection with no stream open is let go after the idle
timeout with GOAWAY, as is every one at a stop.
.B stats
counts
.BR h2_conns ,
.BR h2_streams ,
.BR h2_refused ,
.BR h2_resets_in ,
.B h2_resets_out
and
.BR h2_goaways .
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_HTTP2=0
turns it off, so every client speaks HTTP/1.1.
.
.SS HTTP/3
With
.B \-\-tls
a storage runtime also speaks HTTP/3 (RFC 9114) over QUIC (RFC 9000) on the
same port number over UDP, when it was built with HTTP/3 (\fBstats\fR shows
\fBh3:1\fR; \fBbin/build\-thirdparty.sh http3\fR builds the static OpenSSL 3.5,
ngtcp2 and nghttp3 it needs, and the install script runs it). Every answer over
TCP carries
.B Alt\-Svc: h3=":PORT"
so a browser tries QUIC on its next request and goes on over TCP if UDP does not
reach; each request is a stream and runs exactly as over HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2.
A connection survives a client's change of address or network, and a lost
packet holds up only its own stream, which is what HTTP/3 is for on mobile and
lossy links; on a clean wire HTTP/2 costs less CPU per byte. With
.B \-\-shards
the shards share the UDP port and the kernel picks one by the client's
address, so a client that moved may reach another: every connection ID names
its shard, and a packet for another shard's connection is handed to it, which
answers from the same port. A stream's window and a connection's start at 1 and
4 MiB and grow to 16 and 64 MiB, 128 streams are open at once, a handshake not
done in 10 seconds is dropped, and a connection is let go after 30 seconds
without a packet. What a client is sent is paced by BBR, which follows the
path's measured rate and round trip rather than backing off at each lost packet:
over a 50 ms path that loses one packet in a hundred, as Wi\-Fi and mobile
links do, a download ran ten to twenty times faster than with CUBIC, and as fast
on a clean path.
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_HTTP3_CC=cubic
(or
.BR reno )
chooses another. AES\-128\-GCM is offered first, since every packet is sealed on
its own and it costs a quarter less than AES\-256. While more than
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_HTTP3_RETRY_AFTER
handshakes (64; 0 for always) are under way, a new client is sent a Retry and
must prove its address before anything is kept for it (RFC 9000 8.1.2), so
datagrams with forged sources cannot fill the table. QUIC's TLS is TCP's: a
certificate renewed on disk is taken up the same way, and
.B \-\-ca
asks for a client certificate here too.
Behind a reverse proxy, which forwards TCP only, the proxy passes the header
on with the runtime's port: forward UDP from the port browsers reach (usually
443, which the proxy does not take for UDP) to the runtime's, give it the
certificate browsers expect, and set
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_HTTP3_ADVERTISE
to that port, or to 0 to advertise none.
.B stats
counts
.BR h3_conns ,
.BR h3_streams ,
.BR h3_retries
and
.B h3_forwarded
(packets handed to another shard), and shows the connections open now as
.BR h3_open .
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_HTTP3=0
turns it off.
.
.SS Quotas
A bucket's quota bounds the bytes its objects and versions hold and how many there
are; it is socketley's own document, which S3 has no counterpart of:
.RS
.PP
.nf
PUT /photos?quota
<BucketQuota>
  <MaxBytes>10737418240</MaxBytes>
  <MaxObjects>100000</MaxObjects>
</BucketQuota>
.fi
.RE
.PP
Either element may be left out, and 0 is no bound. Only an admin, or the runtime's
own key, or anyone on an
.B \-\-anonymous
runtime, may set, read or delete one, since a quota bounds what the bucket's owner
may store; it is kept in
.IB dir / bucket /quota
and changed by shard 0 like a bucket's other configuration documents, and a restart
keeps it. A user's
.B max_bytes
(see
.BR Users )
bounds what every bucket that user owns holds together.
.PP
A write that would take a bucket or its owner past a quota is answered
.B "403 QuotaExceeded"
and stores nothing: a PUT, a POST, a copy and the completion of a multipart upload
alike. An overwrite counts the bytes it adds over the object it replaces and adds no
object; in a versioned bucket every version counts, delete markers among them. A
quota is judged by the shard that owns the key, at the commit, one write at a time,
so writes racing each other cannot pass it together; a shard that owns the key also
refuses a body of known length before reading it. Under
.B \-\-shards
each shard holds its share of a quota, as it does of
.BR \-\-max\-bytes ,
so a bucket whose keys fall unevenly is refused somewhat before the whole is
reached. The parts of a multipart upload are not counted until it is completed.
.
.SS Website hosting
A bucket with a website configuration is served to browsers under
.BR \-\-website\-domain ,
as S3's website endpoints serve it:
.RS
.PP
.nf
socketley create storage media \-p 9000 \-\-dir /srv/objects \\
    \-\-credentials /etc/socketley/s3.keys \-\-website\-domain web.example.com \-s
.fi
.RE
.PP
with the site's configuration put on its bucket and its files uploaded by any S3
client:
.RS
.PP
.nf
PUT /docs?website
<WebsiteConfiguration>
  <IndexDocument><Suffix>index.html</Suffix></IndexDocument>
  <ErrorDocument><Key>404.html</Key></ErrorDocument>
</WebsiteConfiguration>
.fi
.RE
.PP
after which
.B http://docs.web.example.com:9000/
is the bucket's
.BR index.html .
A request to a website signs nothing and is judged as one with no credentials,
whatever it carries, so a page is read only when the bucket's policy lets anyone
read it (\fBPrincipal "*"\fR, \fBs3:GetObject\fR); on an
.B \-\-anonymous
runtime every page is. Only GET and HEAD are served, the query is ignored, and the
latest version of a key is the page.
.PP
A key that is empty or ends in
.B /
is given the index document's suffix, so
.B /guide/
is
.BR guide/index.html ,
and a key without the slash whose folder has an index document is answered
.B "302"
to the folder, where the page's relative links resolve. An object stored with
.B x\-amz\-website\-redirect\-location
is answered
.B "301"
to that location. A routing rule whose condition is a KeyPrefixEquals alone
redirects before the object is looked for, and one with an
HttpErrorCodeReturnedEquals redirects a failure with that status; its Redirect may
replace the key or its prefix and name a host, a protocol and a 3xx code, the
request's own host and protocol and 301 otherwise. A RedirectAllRequestsTo sends
every request to its host with the key kept.
.PP
A failure no rule redirects is answered with the ErrorDocument and the failure's
status, when the policy lets anyone read it, and otherwise with an HTML page
naming the error's code, message and request id, and an
.B x\-amz\-error\-code
header. A missing key is
.B "403 AccessDenied"
rather than
.B "404 NoSuchKey"
when the policy lets anyone read the bucket's keys but not list them, as on S3.
The S3 API is served on the same port as before, so a site is uploaded and read
over both.
.
.SS Drive, links, media and the web interface
Paths that begin with
.BR ~ ,
which no bucket name can, are the runtime's own applications, on the S3 port and
under the S3 runtime's users, policies and versioning:
.TP
.B /~dav/
A WebDAV drive (classes 1 and 2) for Windows, macOS, Linux file managers, rclone
and phones. It signs in with HTTP Basic, an access key as the user name and its
secret as the password; a runtime without credentials takes none. Its root is
every bucket the user owns or a bucket policy lets it list, every bucket for an
admin, and a bucket's keys are
files whose slashes are folders. A file's GET, PUT, DELETE, COPY and MOVE are the
S3 operations, judged by the same policies; MKCOL at the root makes a bucket and
within one an empty
.I folder/
marker object, as S3 browsers do. A folder's COPY, MOVE and DELETE go over every
key under it, and a copy reads each object whole, so one past 256 MiB or under a
client's own key is not copied and the answer is
.BR 500 .
A COPY or MOVE with
.B Overwrite: F
is
.B 412
when its Destination holds a file, or for a folder any object under it, and
replaces it otherwise.
Locks are granted and not enforced, and properties are not kept.
.IP
An address that fails ten sign-ins with Basic, here, at
.B /~ui/
and
.BR /~media/ ,
or ten passwords of share links within a quarter hour is answered
.B 429
with a
.B Retry\-After
until that quarter hour ends, the right secret too, while its signed-in sessions
go on; an IPv6 address counts by its /64. Each shard counts on its own, so an
address has ten tries a shard, and behind a proxy every client is the proxy's
address, so one guessing there keeps the others from signing in as well.
.IP
A file may also be sent in parts, side by side, as S3's multipart upload under
the drive's sign-in:
.B POST ?uploads
on the file begins one and answers its UploadId,
.B PUT ?partNumber=\fIn\fB&uploadId=\fIid\fR
streams a part and answers its ETag,
.B POST ?uploadId=\fIid\fR
with S3's CompleteMultipartUpload body makes the file of them, and
.B DELETE ?uploadId=\fIid\fR
abandons it; each is judged as the S3 operation it is.
.B GET ?uploadId=\fIid\fR
lists the parts that arrived (with
.B max\-parts
and
.B part\-number\-marker
for the next page), so an upload broken off is taken up again where it stopped.
A file's other PUTs ignore their query.
.IP
.nf
[drive]                      # rclone.conf, then: rclone copy ~/photos drive:holiday
type = webdav
url = http://host:9000/~dav/
user = AKIA...
pass = <the secret, as rclone obscure prints it>
.fi
.IP
.B GET /~dav/\fIbucket\fB/\fIfolder\fB/?zip
is the folder as one ZIP, saved as
.IR folder .zip,
and
.B ?zip&n=\fIname\fB&n=\fIname\fR
only the files and folders of it named; a bucket's root takes it too. It is
streamed as it is read, each file stored as it is, so its Content-Length is known
before its first byte and a browser shows how far it has come; each entry's CRC-32
is taken as its bytes pass and follows them in a data descriptor, and a file or
an offset past 4 GiB takes ZIP64. An entry's name is its key under the folder
without empty, \(lq.\(rq and \(lq..\(rq segments, with \(lq\e\(rq and control
characters made \(lq_\(rq, and numbered \(lq\ (2)\(rq, \(lq\ (3)\(rq before its
extension where another entry has it, so no key unpacks outside the folder or
over another file. Only what the user may read is in it, and what
only its client's key opens is left out. An object that changes while the ZIP
is being sent ends the connection, since its length was promised.
.IP
In a versioned bucket a deleted file is a delete marker, and its versions stay:
.B GET /~dav/\fIbucket\fB/\fIfolder\fB/?trash
lists the files under the folder whose latest version is a marker, as JSON with
the key, the time of the deletion and the size of the version behind it, and
.B POST /~dav/\fIbucket\fB/\fIkey\fB?restore
removes the marker and brings that version back. A lifecycle rule's
NoncurrentVersionExpiration empties the trash.
.B GET /~dav/\fIbucket\fB/\fIfolder\fB/?list
is a folder's listing as the web interface reads it, JSON of its subfolders'
names and its files' names, sizes, times in milliseconds and types
(\fB{"d":[...],"f":[[name,size,mtime,type],...]}\fR), about a tenth of a
PROPFIND's bytes, with an ETag of its own, so a look again with
.B If\-None\-Match
that finds nothing changed is
.B 304
and no body. A folder of 20,000 files was 9.9 MB and 44 ms as a PROPFIND and is
1.0 MB and 9 ms, and a browser read it in 40 ms rather than 400.
.B POST /~dav/\fIbucket\fB/\fIkey\fB?unzip
unpacks a ZIP object on the server into a folder beside it named as it is
without its extension, numbered (\fIname\fR (2)) when that name is taken, so
nothing is written over and nothing of it crosses the client's line. Each file
is read from the archive, opened when it is deflated, checked against its CRC-32
and size, and written as an object of its own, four at a time, as the drive's
other writes are (encrypted when the bucket encrypts, typed by its extension);
an empty folder in it becomes the folder's marker. ZIP64 archives are read;
names in UTF-8 are kept and older ones are read as code page 437. A file that is
encrypted, packed other than stored or deflated, or named outside the folder
(\fB..\fR, a control character) is skipped, as macOS's \fB__MACOSX\fR tree,
\fB.DS_Store\fR and \fBThumbs.db\fR are without a word. It needs GET of the
archive and PUT under the folder; the answer, once every file is written, is
JSON naming the folder and counting the files, folders and bytes written and
the files skipped and failed, with up to 20 names of each, 201 when none failed
and 500 when any did. An archive whose directory is over 64 MiB or holds more
than 100,000 entries is refused. The job goes on to its end when its request is
abandoned. 549 MiB unpacked from a 312 MiB archive of 502 files in 2.1 s on four
CPUs with two shards, other requests answered meanwhile in under 35 ms.
.TP
.B /~share/
Links to an object or a folder that anyone holding one reads without credentials.
.B POST /~share
with a JSON body makes one, signed with SigV4 or Basic as above:
.IP
.nf
{"bucket": "holiday", "key": "2026/", "expires_in": 604800,
 "password": "...", "max_downloads": 0, "upload": false, "note": "..."}
.fi
.IP
A key ending in
.B /
or none is a folder, read as a page, as JSON with
.BR ?format=json ,
and file by file under it;
.B upload
lets a visitor PUT files into it. The maker must be able to read what the link
names, and to write there for an upload link. A link acts as its maker, judged at
each request as the maker's own would be: a file it reads is the maker's
GetObject of that key, a folder's page, list and ZIP its ListBucket (and the ZIP
leaves out each file the maker may not read), an upload its PutObject. One the
maker may no longer use is
.BR 403 ;
one whose maker is no user of the runtime any more or is disabled, or whose
bucket was removed and made again under its name, is
.BR 410 . A password is asked for with Basic,
any user name, and kept salted and hashed;
.B max_downloads
counts each read from the start of a file and not a player's later ranges, and
.B ?download
asks a browser to save, and a folder's link read with
.B ?zip
is the folder, or the part of it the path names, as one ZIP as the drive gives
it, counted as one download; its page offers it. An expired link is
.BR 410 ,
and is removed at the next listing.
.B GET /~share
lists the caller's links, every link for an admin, and
.B DELETE /~share/\fIid\fR
revokes one.
.IP
.B "\(dqburn\(dq: true"
makes a link that removes what it names when it ends, as a file sent once:
at its expiry, ten minutes after the last download its limit allows (so a
browser resuming that download still finds it), or at its revocation. It needs
.B expires_in
or
.BR max_downloads ,
is no upload link, and is made only by one who may also delete there. A
file's link that burns opens on a page saying what it is, which counts no
download, so a chat's preview of the link costs nothing, and the file is read
with
.BR ?download .
The runtime's sweep looks the links over every five seconds and renames one
that has ended to
.IR id .burning,
which no reader opens, stamped with the time, before it removes; the shard that
renamed it removes, and one stamped more than a minute ago, left by a stop, is
taken up by the first shard to see it.
.IP
.B "\(dqnotify\(dq: \(dqhttps://...\(dq"
gives a link a webhook, POSTed a JSON object at each download it counts and when
what a link that burns names is removed:
.BR content " and " text
(a sentence, as Discord and Slack show it),
.BR event " (" downloaded " or " deleted ),
.BR bucket ", " key ", " downloads ", " max_downloads " and " expires .
One the hook does not take is posted again as a bucket's webhook events are,
and kept across a restart in
.B .share\-notify
under the runtime's directory.
Since the runtime makes the request for the link's maker, the URL must begin
with one of the prefixes
.B SOCKETLEY_SHARE_NOTIFY_ALLOW
names, comma\-separated, by default the webhooks of Discord
.RB ( https://discord.com/api/webhooks/ ,
.BR https://discordapp.com/api/webhooks/ ),
Slack
.RB ( https://hooks.slack.com/ )
and
.BR https://ntfy.sh/ ;
any other is 400. A webhook's requests go one at a time, at most 16 waiting. In a versioned bucket the removal is a delete marker and the
versions stay. A cluster does not make them. Links are files in
.IR dir /.shares/,
read by every shard.
.TP
.B /~media/\fIvariant\fB/\fIbucket\fB/\fIkey\fR
Media made by ffmpeg from an object, and kept.
.BI thumb\- width
(64, 128, 256, 320, 480, 640, 960, 1280 or 1920) is a JPEG still of a picture or
of a film a few seconds in.
.B info
is what the object is, as JSON: its length, picture size, frame rate, codecs,
whether it is HDR, the MIME type with codecs a browser is asked whether it plays
the file itself, the codecs of its copied stream, its renditions, its text
subtitles, every sound track with its codec, channels, language, title and
whether it is the default, its chapters with their start, end and title, and a
song's tags (title, artist, album, album artist, track, disc, date, genre), the
container's or, for a file without a picture, its sound's as Ogg keeps them.
.B trick
is a film's stills for a seek bar, one JPEG of 160x90 tiles, ten a row, one
every two seconds or every 1/200 of the film if that is longer; info's
.B trick
says the seconds between them, their count and their layout. They are taken
from the keyframes alone, and from every frame when the keyframes give none, in
a place of their own beside the thumbnails, one at a time a shard.
.BI sub\- stream /subs.vtt
is a text subtitle stream (SubRip, ASS, mov_text, WebVTT) as WebVTT, on the film's
clock, and
.BI sub\- stream /index.m3u8
its HLS playlist; subtitles that are pictures are not offered.
.B hls/\fIbucket\fB/\fIkey\fB/master.m3u8
is an HLS master playlist naming those renditions, with their size, peak bitrate
and codecs, for a player to switch between as its bandwidth allows: one for every
rung of 2160, 1440, 1080, 720, 480, 360 and 240 the picture fills in at least one
side, each fitted into that rung's 16:9 box and never enlarged, 720 listed first,
the source's own picture copied when it is H.264 or HEVC (HEVC tagged hvc1), and
the text subtitles as a SUBTITLES group. BANDWIDTH is the most a segment can
carry, a quarter over the rung's cap, and the levels are 4.2 up to 1080 and 5.2
above. The URLs a master names carry the object's version, so what they answer
is cached for good, and a medium's answers carry an ETag a client revalidates
with, answered 304, and take a Range.
.IP
.BI hls\- height /index.m3u8
is one rendition: H.264 held to its rung's bitrate and AAC, in CMAF segments of
four seconds. Its playlist is whole from the start, written from the film's length,
so a player seeks anywhere at once, and its segments are made as they are asked
for: a segment at or a little past where a job is making them is waited for, and
one elsewhere starts a job there, on the film's own clock, so the segments of
every job join up, and every job's init segment is the same bytes. A length past
99,999 segments (about 111 hours), or one that is no number, is taken for none: the
info gives 0 and a rendition is answered 415, as for a film of no length. An init
segment asked for with no job running begins one at the start at once, which a
seek that follows lets go, and which is held after two seconds if no segment of
it is asked for. Several jobs run over one film for viewers at different places;
one that reaches segments made before ends, one fifteen segments ahead of every
player that asks of it, or two ahead when nobody has asked of it for ten seconds,
is held still until one comes within eight, and one nobody asks of for a minute
while held, or for ten minutes running, ends; a job held still and unasked gives
its place to a new one when a shard has eight, or where renditions are made on a GPU
24 shared among the shards and never fewer than eight. What they made
is kept: a film watched once streams from the cache afterwards. HDR (PQ or HLG) is
tone-mapped to SDR for these renditions. Where a GPU with VAAPI is there, a
rendition is decoded, scaled, tone-mapped and encoded on it (an HDR film without the
mastering display metadata the GPU tone-maps from is made with x264), as
.B SOCKETLEY_MEDIA_HWACCEL
describes, and the interface plays a place the copied stream has not been
remuxed to yet from a rendition until the copy catches up.
A variant with
.BI \-a n
after it, as
.BR hls\-a1 ,
.B hls\-720\-a1
and
.BR hls\-copy\-a1 ,
carries the film's sound track
.I n
(counted from 0 among its sound tracks, as info lists them) instead of the first,
made or copied by that track's own codec and channels; its master names its
streams with it, and each is made and kept as a stream of its own, so the other
track's picture is made again.
.B hls\-copy
streams the source's own picture without transcoding, and its sound copied when
it is AAC in two channels or fewer, and made AAC otherwise; renditions always make
it AAC, as a copy begun at a seek would start at the keyframe before it.
.IP
The object is read as a GET of it would be, by its user or, under
.BI /~media/ variant /~share/ id\fR[\fB/\fIpath\fR],
through a link. ffmpeg, and ffprobe beside it, read an unencrypted object's file
directly, kept open by the shard for a minute, and any other over the runtime's
own listener with an address signed by a key in
.IR dir /.media/key
for that version of it, and what they made lies in
.IR dir /.media/
under the variant, the object's key and its ETag, so a new version is made anew
and every shard serves what one made; one thumbnail is made once however many
ask for it at once. A shard runs as many thumbnails and probes at once as it has
cores of the host's share, at least two, each on one thread of ffmpeg's (a
1600x1200 JPEG's thumbnail took 166 ms with ffmpeg's threads and 72 without),
and eight streams; a job's end is heard from a pidfd the ring polls, so what it
made is served as soon as ffmpeg exits rather than at the next look. A
request waits a minute for its medium, an object ffmpeg cannot read is
.B 415
for two minutes, and an ffmpeg a dying worker left behind is found by its pid and
ended. See
.B SOCKETLEY_FFMPEG
and
.BR SOCKETLEY_MEDIA_CACHE_MB .
.TP
.B /~ui/
A web interface, one page served from the binary. A browser asked for the
runtime's root instead, with no query, no signature and
.B Accept: text/html
among what it takes, is sent here, so the host's name alone reaches the
interface; an S3 client, which asks for none of that, still reads
.B GET /
as ListBuckets. The interface signs in with an access
key and its secret, which it sends once for the session cookie below and keeps
nowhere, and browses libraries (buckets) as a media library: films, pictures,
music and documents in their own rows, with thumbnails, lengths and what the
user left half watched on any device. Films named S01E02 or 1x02 are grouped by
season in their order and play on into the next episode; a folder's or a
library's card shows a picture called folder, cover or poster in it, else its
first picture or film; more than twelve pictures are grouped by the month of
their date (the camera's, see
.BR /~ui/taken ,
else the object's); and pictures play as a slideshow. A ZIP's menu unpacks it beside itself on the server
(\fB?unzip\fR above). It uploads several files at once,
and a file of 48 MiB or more in parts of at least 16 MiB, four requests side by
side shared among up to three files, each going to the file with the fewest on
the way, through the drive's multipart above; over HTTP/1.1, while a film or a
song plays in any of the browser's tabs (they tell each other on a
BroadcastChannel), uploads keep one request, since a browser opens six
connections at most to such a server, and take four again once it is closed,
while over HTTP/2 (see above) they keep all four beside the player, with each
file's percentage, rate,
time left and start; folders too, by dropping them or choosing one. A file in
parts whose line is lost is paused and goes on when the line is back, and one a
closed or reloaded page left is shown again, with how far it came, and goes on
from the parts the server lists once its file is chosen anew (the same name and
size, and the same modification time or a confirmation). Each part, and a
file sent whole, carries its CRC-32 in
.BR x\-amz\-checksum\-crc32 ,
which the server checks against what arrived; one refused as
.B BadDigest
is read and sent again. It selects
several items to download or delete, renames, shares, restores from the trash,
shows pictures one after another, and opens text in an editor that highlights
code, finds and replaces, previews Markdown and saves with Ctrl+S, and makes new
text files. Its search box filters the folder open and searches every library;
items dragged onto a folder, a library or a breadcrumb are moved there. The folder open is asked again every
five seconds while the page is seen (every thirty past 2000 entries), so what
another device changes shows within seconds. A download is the object byte for byte, the drive's GET with
.BR ?download ,
which a browser resuming an interrupted one sends again with the cookie.
The owner of a library shares it from the page with other users, to view or to
edit, through
.B /~ui/access
below.
.IP
Send a file sends files as wormhole does: they are uploaded into the user's
library for temporary files,
.BI tmp\- user
(the user's id in lowercase, made when it is first needed and shown as
Temporary files), each sending in a folder of its own, behind a link that burns (see
.B /~share/
above), deleted after a chosen time or number of downloads (for several files,
that number of each), with an optional password and a webhook told of each
download. Once a picture or a film is uploaded, the page asks for its thumbnails
and facts, and for a film this browser would not play itself the start of its
stream, so the server makes them before anyone waits for them. Five colour themes are chosen
from the sidebar and kept in the browser.
.IP
It signs in with
.B POST /~ui/session
and Basic credentials \(em a user's name and the password of its login profile
(see IAM above), or an access key and its secret \(em which sets the cookie
.B skl_ui
(HttpOnly, SameSite=Strict, Secure over TLS): the access key, or
.BI ~ user
for a password, the time it was made, an expiry and an HMAC-SHA256 of them under
the key's own secret, so every shard and node checks it alike and a key whose
secret changes or that is removed ends its sessions.
It lasts twelve hours and the browser's session, or thirty days with
.BR ?remember .
.B GET /~ui/session
says who is signed in and
.B DELETE /~ui/session
signs out.
.B POST /~ui/session?everywhere
ends every session of the user made before it, in each browser, by keeping its
time in
.IR dir /.ui/sessions/;
the other shards follow within a second, and a sign-in with Basic credentials is
not a session and goes on. /~dav/, /~media/ and the management of /~share/ take the cookie as
they take Basic credentials, so the page's script never holds the secret; a
change made under the cookie alone (anything but GET, HEAD, OPTIONS and
PROPFIND) must carry the header
.BR "X-Socketley-UI" ,
which no other site's page can add, and is 403 otherwise. A session never makes
a new one. A file's
.B GET /~dav/\fIbucket\fB/\fIkey\fB?download
is saved under its name.
.IP
The page's Account sets and changes the user's password and makes, deactivates and
deletes its access keys, the secret shown once. An admin's Users lists every
user with how it signs in, makes one with a password (to be changed at the first
sign\-in, if chosen), sets a password, the name shown, the email, the quota,
admin\-ness and whether it makes libraries of its own, disables, deletes, and
invites: an invitation is a link,
.BI /~ui/#invite= token ,
that lets one person choose a user name and password of their own, once, for one
to thirty days, as an admin and with a quota if it says so; only the SHA\-256 of
its token is kept, in
.IR dir /.iam.json
beside the users. Sharing a library offers the users by name.
.B POST /~ui/iam
with
.B {"Action":
\fIaction\fB,\fR \fIits fields\fB}\fR
is an IAM action as the signed\-in user, judged and answered as IAM's own;
.B GET /~ui/users
lists the users, each one's id and name to any user and all of it, with the open
invitations, to an admin;
.B GET /~ui/invite?token=
says whether an invitation holds, and
.B POST /~ui/invite
with
.B {"token", "user", "password", "name", "email"}
takes it, without a session, a token that is none counted as a failed sign\-in.
.IP
.B GET /~ui/progress
lists the films the signed\-in user has part watched, the most recent first,
with the position, the length, the size and the type; only those the user may
still read, in the version watched, so one replaced or removed since, or whose
access was taken away, is left out.
.B POST /~ui/progress
with
.B {"bucket", "key", "t", "d"}
keeps where one was left (t below 0 forgets it). Each user's positions are a
file of their own,
.IR dir /.ui/progress/,
changed entry by entry under a lock, so two devices watching different films
keep both; the latest 200 are kept. Nodes of a cluster keep their own.
.IP
.B GET /~ui/search?q=\fItext\fR[\fB&bucket=\fIname\fR]
lists the files and folders whose own name holds the text, ASCII letters in any
case, in every library the user lists (or the one named), folders first, at most
200, with
.B "more"
true when there were more or a library was too large to list whole; a file is
listed only where the user may read it. Each library is listed whole for it.
.IP
.B GET /~ui/taken?bucket=\fIname\fB&prefix=\fIfolder/\fR
says when and where the photos directly in a folder were taken, as their EXIF
says: for each JPEG, TIFF or HEIF (HEIC) the user may read, its key, its
.B taken
time as the camera's clock wrote it ("2021-07-14T18:03:22", with "+02:00" where
the camera wrote its offset) and its
.B lat
and
.BR lon .
Its first 64 KiB are read, and a HEIF's Exif item where it lies further in; what
was read is kept by the object's ETag in the shard's memory. An object stored
encrypted, compressed or in a tier is left out, and so is all past the folder's
first thousand keys
.RB ( "more" ).
The page groups a folder's pictures by the month they were taken, shows the time
in the viewer and links to the place on openstreetmap.org, which the page never
loads itself; it shows a HEIF as its thumbnail, which ffmpeg makes from 7.1 on.
.IP
.B GET /~ui/usage
lists every library the user lists with its owner, whether it is shared with
the user, its bytes and objects, summed from its listing (which holds every
shard's keys) and kept a minute, with
.B partial
when it was too large to list whole, and its quota; besides, the user's own quota
over the libraries it owns, and whether the user is an admin.
.B POST /~ui/usage
with
.B {"bucket", "max_bytes", "max_objects"}
sets a library's quota as
.B ?quota
does, 0 for none, and like it is an admin's alone. The page's Storage shows them
as bars.
.IP
.B GET /~ui/access?bucket=\fIname\fR
says who else a library is shared with, and
.B POST /~ui/access
with
.B {"bucket", "user", "access"}
shares it with a user of the credentials or the IAM API, by id:
.B read
(list and read),
.B write
(also write, delete, and restore from the trash) or
.B none
to take it back. Each is a statement of the bucket's policy, named by its Sid
.BI socketley\-ui\- access : user\fR,
and only those are read or changed: the rest of the policy is kept, and a Deny
in it still holds. Both are for whoever may read or write the bucket's policy,
its owner and an admin. The drive's root lists, besides the buckets a user owns,
every bucket whose policy lets the user list it.
.IP
A film's link is a page that plays it, which a chat embeds as a player once the
film's web copy,
.BI /~media/web/ bucket / key\fR,
is made: H.264 in an MP4 of at most 46 MiB and 1440 lines. It is made when a
link asks for it, or as the film is put into a library tagged
.BR socketley:web\-copies=on .
.B GET /~ui/library?bucket=\fIname\fR
says whether a library is, and
.B POST /~ui/library
with
.B "{\(dqbucket\(dq, \(dqweb_copies\(dq}"
tags it or takes the tag away, for whoever may change the bucket's tags.
.IP
It plays films and music, with their subtitles: the file itself where the
browser plays it, which costs the server nothing, and otherwise the master
playlist's streams through Media Source Extensions, the copied one where the
browser takes its codec and the line carries it, renditions by measured
bandwidth otherwise, or through the browser's own HLS where it has no Media
Source at all. Its quality menu, in the player, offers Original (the file, or the
copied stream where the browser does not play the file), Auto and each
rendition; a change is made on the same media source, keeping the next six
seconds already buffered while the new stream's first segment is made.
.
.SS Git repositories
The page's Sync keeps repositories in a library: each is pulled when it is added,
whenever its user asks, and by itself every day, week or month from 3:00 local
time, or never (the default), as its Pull every says; when any ref moved since
the last run, the branch's files are written under
.IB name /
of the library \(em what changed, or the whole tree the first time, with nothing
else left beside it \(em and a git bundle of the branch and the tags, or of every
branch with
.BR * ,
under
.BI _backups/ name / date _ time _ commit .bundle .
.B "git clone" " file" .bundle
makes the repository from one, its branch checked out. A run with nothing new
writes nothing. Every bundle of the last day is kept, and before it the newest
of each of the last 14 days, 8 weeks and 12 months; the rest are deleted.
Symbolic links and submodules are no files and are left out.
.IP
Which runtimes do the runs is
.BR \-\-sync .
With
.B never
\(em the default \(em the storage makes none, and the page says that a repository
added would not run. With
.B active
the runtime's first shard makes one
.B sync
runtime for the whole storage,
.IB storage \-sync ,
which pulls every user's repositories, each with that user's own credentials.
With
.B per\-user
it makes every one of its users a
.B sync
runtime of their own,
.IB storage \-sync\- user .
Either is owned by the storage, goes when it stops and comes back when it starts, and is one
.B "socketley ls"
and the dashboard list with its
.BR runs ,
.BR failures ,
.B running
and
.BR last_error .
It runs
.B socketley git\-sync
for that user's repositories alone, one at a time, with temporary credentials
of the user the storage hands it in
.IR dir /.ui/sync/,
0600, made again with three of their twelve hours left, through the runtime's
own S3 API on its port: what the user may not write it cannot write, quotas and
versioning apply, and a library that is not there is made as the user. A failed
run of one pulled by itself is tried again an hour later; a run asked for is
.IR dir /.ui/git/ id /asked,
which the user's sync runtime starts within a second. Each repository's bare clone, the state of its last run and its log are
under
.IR dir /.ui/git/ id /,
and the list is
.IR dir /.ui/git.json .
Git must be installed; a runtime on a unix socket has none of this.
.IP
A repository is fetched over https://, http:// or git://, never with
credentials in its URL. A private one is read with the GitHub token the user
keeps in the page, sent as git's http.extraHeader through its environment, so it
is on no command line; it is sealed with AES-256-GCM under a key only the user's
own secret derives, in
.IR dir /.ui/git\-tokens/,
and never shown again.
.IP
.B GET /~ui/git
lists the repositories the user keeps (an admin's: all), each with the state its
last run ended in, and whether the user has a token.
.B POST /~ui/git
takes
.B {"action":"add", "url", "branch", "bucket", "name", "private", "every"}
(branch
.BR main ,
library
.BI git\- user ,
the user's own, and every
.B never
by default, the name the URL's last part), which also starts the first run,
.B {"action":"every","id","every"}
.RB ( never ,
.BR day ,
.B week
or
.BR month ),
.BR {"action":"sync","id"} ,
.B {"action":"remove","id"}
(the files and bundles stay) and
.B {"action":"token","token"}
(empty removes it). A user adds into a library it may write.
.IP
A repository in the library
.B git
that every user once shared goes to its owner's own at the next start, and is
run there until its tree is whole and
.B "git\-sync \-\-move\-from git"
has taken its backups over and its files away; the old library goes once it is
empty.
.IR dir /.ui/git/ id /moved\-from
holds the move across restarts.
.
.SS Durability
.B \-\-fsync
decides when a write a client was answered for is on the disk, as a cache's
journal and Redis's appendfsync do. Every mode keeps what a crash of the process
would take, because the page cache outlives the process, and every mode syncs the
whole filesystem at a stop before it writes the clean marker.
.TP
.B always
The default. A put is answered once its file, its name and its directory are
synced, a delete once its directory is; versions, tags, uploads and parts alike,
and the entries of event notifications and replication beside them. An answered
write survives a crash of the machine. Small puts cost about 2 syncs each, which
is most of their time: about 1,500 4 KiB puts a second on one shard of the
measuring box, about 700 into a versioned bucket.
.TP
.B interval
Those syncs are skipped, and a thread syncs the filesystem (\fBsyncfs\fR) every
.B \-\-fsync\-interval
(default 1 second). A crash of the machine loses at most the writes of the last
interval; about 18,000 puts a second on the same box, about 9,600 versioned.
.TP
.B never
The syncs are skipped and only the kernel's own writeback (typically within
30 seconds) and a stop put the writes on the disk; for data that can be made again,
such as caches and build artefacts.
.PP
What a crash of the machine leaves behind under
.B interval
and
.B never
depends on the filesystem: ext4 in its default data=ordered mode leaves either
an object as it was or as it was written, never part of one, and a start that
finds a file whose header does not check out leaves it unserved; on other
filesystems a file written in the last interval may hold zeros where its data
was not yet written. Bucket creation and removal and the configuration documents
are synced in every mode. Backups and anything else that deletes its own copy
once a put is answered want
.BR always .
.PP
What
.B always
costs is the disk's: a drive with power\-loss protection (most enterprise NVMe and
SATA SSDs) answers a sync from its protected cache in microseconds, and there it
costs little, while a consumer or virtual disk answers it only once the data is on
the medium. XFS writes less per sync than ext4, and ext4's
.B fast_commit
feature (\fBtune2fs \-O fast_commit\fR) shortens a sync after a small change.
Concurrent puts already share the filesystem's journal commits, so
.B always
gains throughput with concurrency. A sync of the whole filesystem shared by every
put waiting, files written before they are needed and overwritten, and
.B O_DSYNC
writes were each measured against it and none did better under a sustained load
of small puts.
.B stats
counts the syncs that ran in
.BR fsyncs ,
those a mode skipped in
.BR fsyncs_skipped ,
and the filesystem syncs of
.B interval
in
.B syncfs_rounds
and
.BR syncfs_failed ;
a stop after a failed round is not a clean one.
.
.SS Sharding
.B \-\-shards
.I N
runs N worker processes on one port (\fBSO_REUSEPORT\fR) or, with
.BR \-\-listen ,
one node each (\fIname#i\fR.sock, as for a cache), all on the same
.BR \-\-dir .
Clients see one runtime. Every shard serves a GET or HEAD itself by opening the
object's file, whose header says what it is, so object bytes never move between
shards.
.PP
.B "One shard is the default, and on a machine of a few cores it is the right one."
One shard answered 95,000 signed GETs of 4 KiB a second on one core, about
3 Gbit/s of payload, so a runtime behind a 1 or 10 Gbit link runs out of network
before it runs out of its core. Shards pay only where small reads saturate that
core, and they cost wherever a request crosses shards. Measured on one box with
the runtime confined by
.B taskset
and a
.B wrk
client replaying signed GETs of 64 keys on cores of its own:
.PP
.RS
.TS
l l r.
CPUs	shards	GET 4 KiB
_
2	1	96,000 req/s
2	2	185,000 req/s
2	4	177,000 req/s
4	2	187,000 req/s
4	4	253,000 req/s
4	8	218,000 req/s
8	4	258,000 req/s
8	8	359,000 req/s
.TE
.RE
.PP
The last row is the client's six threads at their limit. More shards than CPUs is
slower, never faster. The price on the same box: a ListObjectsV2 page of 1000 keys
took 2.1x the CPU at two shards and 2.4 to 2.5x at four, since every shard walks up
to a page and one merges them; a 4 KiB PUT took 1.2 to 1.3x the CPU at two shards
and 1.4 to 1.6x at four, since at four shards three keys in four are committed by
another shard than the one the body landed on. Large objects move at the same rate
at any count: a 64 MiB GET was bound by the client and a 64 MiB PUT by the disk.
.PP
A key's
.I owner
is the shard that commits its changes: the CRC16/XMODEM of the string
\fIbucket\fB/\fIkey\fR masked to 16384 slots, exactly the function of a cache's
key slots, and the shard is \fIslot\fR * \fIN\fR / 16384, so a client can compute it
and send a request to the owner's node and cross nothing. A put streams into a
temporary file from whichever shard took the connection; that shard then sends the
owner the file's name and the object's metadata over the shard link, and the owner
renames it into place, syncs the directory and answers, appending to its own journal
(\fBindex.\fIi\fR, written by shard \fIi\fR alone) behind the answer. A delete, the
target of a copy and a create\-only put (\fBIf\-None\-Match: *\fR) go the same way,
which is what keeps the order of changes to a key one order and lets two racing
creators come out one refused. A listing is asked of every shard and the pages
are merged, so it is the listing one shard would give; a shard that cannot be
reached fails the listing with a 500 rather than shortening it.
.PP
Buckets are made and dropped by shard 0 and told to the others before it answers;
a bucket is dropped only when every shard agrees it holds nothing and is idle. A
bucket's CORS, lifecycle and versioning configurations and its policy are changed by shard 0, one change of a bucket at a time, whichever
shard took the request, and answered only once no shard can go on with the old document; a change a running shard
cannot be told of is refused with 503 and changes nothing (see
.BR "Bucket policy" ).
Each shard sweeps the keys it owns.
Temporary files are named for the shard that wrote them, so a shard restarted by
the supervisor removes only its own. A shard killed between a rename and its
journal record leaves the object in the directory, and its restart adopts it from
the header: after it every shard serves the object or none does, and a put that was
in flight through the killed shard is answered with an error.
.PP
A start with a different \fB\-\-shards\fR on a directory written by another split
is not refused, unlike a cache with a missing dump, because the object files are
the record: each shard sees that its journal was written for another shard count,
rebuilds its keys from the object headers filtered by owner, and shard 0 removes the
journals of shards that no longer exist. That start reads every header once per
shard, so it takes as long as the object count makes it. \fB\-\-max\-bytes\fR is an equal
share per shard, judged by the shard that took the put from its own bytes and again
by the owner, so it is approximate when keys are unevenly sized. \fBstats\fR gives a
section per shard with
.BR shard_commits_sent ,
.BR shard_commits_served ,
.BR shard_deletes_sent ,
.BR shard_deletes_served ,
.BR shard_list_fanouts ,
.BR shard_list_pages_served ,
.BR shard_bucket_ops ,
.BR shard_mpu_sent ,
.B shard_mpu_served
(multipart steps),
.B shard_hot_drops_sent
and
.B shard_hot_drops_served
(a sibling told to drop its copy of a key, once per sibling),
.B shard_hot_holds_sent
and
.B shard_hot_holds_served
(a sibling asking the owner to keep a copy) and
.BR shard_peer_failures ;
at one shard nothing is sent and they read zero.
.PP
A multipart upload lives on the shard that owns its key, so every step that names
it (create, part, complete, abort, ListParts) is that shard's, and ListMultipartUploads
asks every shard. A part's body is written where the connection landed and only its
name crosses to the shard that keeps the upload; CompleteMultipartUpload has that shard
check the list and seal the upload, the shard the request landed on assemble the object
from the parts on the shared disk and commit it through the owner of the key, and the
owner remove the upload, so nothing long is asked of a sibling over the link. An
upload whose assembler never reports is opened again after six hours, uploads are
found again by the shard that owns their key after a restart with another
.BR \-\-shards ,
and a bucket with an open upload on any shard is not dropped. TLS, credentials, the
credentials file, scripts and the metrics are each shard's own or their sum: every shard
terminates TLS and re\-reads the credentials file, a script's hooks run on the shard
that took the request, a script's
.B socketley.storage
call is answered by whichever shard the daemon relays it to and reaches the rest as any
request does, and the
.B socketley_storage_
series add the shards together (a bucket every shard holds is counted once).
.PP
.B stats
reports
.BR buckets ,
.BR objects ,
.BR bytes ,
.BR requests ,
.BR gets ,
.BR heads ,
.BR puts ,
.BR deletes ,
.BR copies ,
.BR lists ,
.BR client_errors ,
.BR server_errors ,
.BR zero_copy_gets ,
.BR copied_gets ,
.B hot_gets
(the copied GETs answered from the hot copy),
.B hot_bytes
(what the hot copies hold),
.BR zero_copy_bytes ,
.BR journal_records ,
.BR journal_compactions ,
.B fsyncs
(of files and directories alike, that ran),
.B fsyncs_skipped
(those
.B \-\-fsync interval
or
.B never
skipped),
.B syncfs_rounds
and
.B syncfs_failed
(the filesystem syncs of
.BR "\-\-fsync interval" ),
.B start_files_read
(object and version files whose headers the last start read: 0 when every journal was trusted),
.B pool_ready
(empty files the buckets' pools hold durable for small puts, 0 after a start until the first put),
.BR multipart_uploads ,
.BR multipart_parts ,
.BR multipart_bytes ,
.BR multipart_created ,
.BR multipart_completed ,
.BR multipart_aborted ,
.BR lifecycle_expired ,
.BR lifecycle_aborted ,
.BR timeouts ,
.B tls
(1 when the listener is TLS),
.BR tls_handshakes ,
.BR tls_failures ,
.B body_recvs
(receives that brought bytes of an upload's body) and
.B hash_handoffs
(pieces of digest work handed to the hashing threads),
.B sse
(1 when the runtime has a master key),
.B sse_puts
and
.B sse_gets
(bodies written encrypted, and GETs answered by opening an object's chunks),
.BR media_made ,
.B media_failed
and
.B media_served
(thumbnails and streams ffmpeg made and failed to make, and the answers served
from them),
.B media_direct
(ffmpeg jobs that read the object's file rather than through the listener),
.B media_hw
(rendition jobs encoded on the GPU),
and one line
.BI error_ Code
for every S3 error code the runtime has answered, such as
.BR error_NoSuchKey .
Bytes in and out and
.B total_messages
(requests) are the common fields.
A connection with a request head unfinished after ten seconds, or a body that
has not moved for a minute, is closed and its temporary file removed.
.SS Metrics
With the metrics endpoint on, each running storage runtime adds
.BR socketley_storage_buckets ,
.BR socketley_storage_objects ,
.BR socketley_storage_bytes_stored ,
.BR socketley_storage_bytes_received_total ,
.BR socketley_storage_bytes_sent_total ,
.BR socketley_storage_zero_copy_bytes_total ,
.B socketley_storage_tls_handshakes_total
and
.BR socketley_storage_tls_failures_total ,
all labelled
.BR name ,
and
.B socketley_storage_requests_total
(label
.B op
= get, head, put, delete, list, copy) and
.B socketley_storage_errors_total
(label
.B code
= the S3 error code). The subsystems have families of their own when a runtime
runs them: event notifications
.RB ( socketley_storage_notify_ *),
replication
.RB ( socketley_storage_replication_ *),
a cluster
.RB ( socketley_storage_cluster_ *),
the scrub
.RB ( socketley_storage_scrub_ *),
compression
.RB ( socketley_storage_compressed_ *),
tiers
.RB ( socketley_storage_tier_ *),
lifecycle, multipart, media and fsyncs. Every other figure of
.B stats
is
.B socketley_storage_stat
with its name as the label
.BR stat ,
so nothing a runtime counts is missing from a scrape. A mail runtime's figures
are
.BR socketley_mail_received_total ,
.BR _delivered_total ,
.BR _deferred_total ,
.BR _bounced_total ,
.BR _stored_total ,
.BR _quarantined_total ,
.B _auth_failed_total
and the gauges
.B socketley_mail_queued
and
.BR _queued_bytes ,
the rest
.B socketley_mail_stat
by name. A tunnel's are
.B socketley_tunnel_links
and
.B _agents
as gauges, and
.BR socketley_tunnel_streams_total ,
.BR _refused_total ,
.B _visitors_refused_total
(requests a protected service's auth refused),
.B _bytes_in_total
and
.BR _bytes_out_total .
.SS Lua
A script attached with
.B \-\-lua
may define three hooks on a storage runtime.
.TP
.B on_request(req) \(-> nil | true | false | string | table
Called for every request after it is authenticated and judged by its bucket's
policy, and before anything is done, so it sees only requests both let through and
can refuse any of them but let in none they refused; it runs
on a coroutine, so it may wait for another runtime (see
.B socketley.storage
below); the connection is not read from until it answers, so a refused upload is
answered before its body is read and its connection closed.
.I req
has
.BR method ,
.B operation
(the name S3 gives it: GetObject, PutObject, DeleteObjects, ListObjectsV2, CreateMultipartUpload,
UploadPart, UploadPartCopy, CompleteMultipartUpload, AbortMultipartUpload, ListParts,
ListMultipartUploads, PutBucketLifecycleConfiguration, .\|.\|.),
.BR bucket ,
.BR key ,
.B path
and
.B query
(as sent),
.BR host ,
.BR request_id ,
.B principal
(the access key, empty for a request with no credentials),
.B tls
(boolean),
.B client
(\fBip:port\fR, or \fBunix\fR),
.B content_length
(absent for a chunked body) and
.B headers
(lowercase names). Returning nothing or
.B true
lets the request go on;
.B false
refuses it as AccessDenied (403); a string refuses it as that S3 error, with the
status S3 gives that code (403 for a code the runtime does not know); a table
.B {deny="Code", message="...", status=403}
names all three. A raise, and a value of any other type, are refusals answered
500 InternalError and logged, because a hook whose failure lets requests through
is not a hook.
.TP
.B on_put(bucket, key, meta)
Called after an object is committed, for a put, for the destination of a copy,
for a completed multipart upload (once, when it is completed, and never for its parts)
and for an object a script put with
.BR socketley.storage.put ,
and never for one that was refused.
.I meta
has
.BR size ,
.BR etag ,
.BR content_type ,
.B mtime_ms
and
.B metadata
(the user metadata, without its prefix). It is called after the client's answer is
on its way.
.TP
.B on_delete(bucket, key)
Called after an object that was there is removed, from DeleteObject, DeleteObjects
and
.BR socketley.storage.delete ,
and not for a key that was not.
.PP
A script that writes in
.B on_put
is called again for what it wrote.
.SS socketley.storage
Any runtime's script may use a storage runtime by name, in this daemon or in
another process, without stopping the daemon while it waits:
.PP
.nf
socketley.storage.put(runtime, bucket, key, data [, {content_type=, metadata={}}] [, callback])
socketley.storage.get(runtime, bucket, key [, callback])
socketley.storage.head(runtime, bucket, key [, callback])
socketley.storage.delete(runtime, bucket, key [, callback])
socketley.storage.list(runtime, bucket [, prefix] [, {delimiter=, max_keys=, start_after=}] [, callback])
.fi
.PP
In a callback that can stop \(em
.BR on_message ,
.BR on_client_message ,
and the storage hooks above \(em a call without a callback waits and returns the
answer, and the connection a server handler is answering is kept open for it, as
it is for a linked cache. Anywhere else (an
.BR on_tick ,
a cache's
.BR on_write ,
a proxy's filter) the last argument is a function, the call returns
.B true
at once, and the function is called with the answer on a later turn. A call that
must wait, made where it cannot and with no function, returns
.B nil
and a message saying so.
.PP
The answers are:
.B put
and
.B head
a table with
.BR size ,
.BR etag ,
.BR content_type ,
.B mtime_ms
and
.BR metadata ;
.B get
the data and that table;
.B delete
.B true
(also for a key that was not there, as S3 answers);
.B list
a table with
.B objects
(each with
.BR key ,
.BR size ,
.BR etag ,
.BR content_type ,
.BR mtime_ms ),
.BR prefixes ,
.B truncated
and, when truncated,
.B next
\(em pass it as
.B start_after
for the following page. A failure is
.B nil
and the S3 error code and message
.RB ( NoSuchKey ,
.BR NoSuchBucket ,
.BR InvalidBucketName ,
.BR EntityTooLarge ),
or
.B nil
and a reason when the request did not reach the runtime
.RB ( "no such runtime" ,
the request channel is full). Metadata names are lowercased and stored as
.BR x\-amz\-meta\-* .
Buckets are made with the S3 API. An object moves whole in memory and the
request crosses a process boundary in a frame that waits in a 4 MiB queue, so a
script may put or get at most 1 MiB per call, and at most 1024 calls may be
waiting on one runtime; larger objects are for the S3 port.
.SH DNS FLAGS
A
.B dns
runtime is an authoritative name server: it answers, over UDP and TCP on one
port, for the zones in the files it is given, and for nothing else.
.PP
.nf
socketley create dns ns \-\-zone example.com \-s
dig @127.0.0.1 example.com SOA
.fi
.PP
It asks no other server and caches nothing: a name in none of its zones is
answered REFUSED, never looked up, so it cannot be used as an open resolver and
no answer it gives came from anybody else. It runs in a worker process of its
own like every other runtime, may run as
.B \-\-shards
processes that share the port, and binds IPv4 and, when no
.B \-\-bind
address is given and the host has it, IPv6 as well. Port 53 is the default;
below 1024 a port needs root or
.BR CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE ,
which the packaged service grants.
.BR \-p ,
.BR \-\-bind ,
.BR \-\-cpus ,
.B \-\-max\-connections
(TCP connections, 256 when not given) and the common flags apply as documented
under
.BR "COMMON FLAGS" ;
.B \-\-listen
and
.B \-\-tls
are refused.
.TP
.BI \-\-zone " name\fR|\fIfile"
The zone to serve, by its name or as a zone file, given once for each zone or
as several separated by commas. Required. A name, such as
.BR example.com ,
needs no file: the runtime writes one under
.IR state\-dir /zones/ name .zone
the first time, with its SOA, ns1 and ns2 as its name servers and the addresses
of
.B \-\-publish
(or this machine's public ones) as theirs and as the zone's own name's, the
entrance's, and serves it from then on; the file
is the user's to edit, and is never written again while it exists. Those two
names and addresses are what the registrar is told as the domain's name servers
and their glue. A path is made absolute against the caller's directory; a file
called
.IB zone .zone
that does not exist is written there the same way. A zone file is in the
master file format of RFC 1035. The zone's apex is the owner of its one SOA record; every
record has to lie at or below it, the apex has to name its servers with NS
records, a CNAME has to be the only record of its name, and below a delegation
only the addresses of its servers may stand. A file that is not such a zone is
refused at
.B create
and
.B start
with the line that is wrong.
.B $ORIGIN
and
.B $TTL
are read;
.B $INCLUDE
is refused, since it would let a zone file read any file the worker may.
Types A, AAAA, NS, CNAME, SOA, PTR, MX, TXT, SRV, CAA and HINFO are written in
their usual form, and any other type in RFC 3597's
.B TYPE\fIn\fB \e# \fIlength hex\fR.
A TXT string longer than 255 bytes is split into several, as a DKIM key needs.
.IP
The file is looked at once a second. Edited, it is read again; the new zone is
served once it has been read whole and checked, and a file that no longer parses
leaves the zone before it in place, with the reason in the log. A changed zone
whose serial did not change is served, and the log says that its secondaries
will not notice it until the serial is raised.
.TP
.BI \-\-transfer " list"
The addresses and blocks, comma\-separated, that may copy a zone whole by AXFR
over TCP (RFC 5936) \(em a secondary's, such as 192.0.2.53 or 2001:db8::/48.
Everyone else is REFUSED, so the names a zone holds are not handed to whoever
asks. An IXFR from them over UDP is answered with the SOA alone, which tells a
secondary the serial and to come over TCP; over TCP it is answered as an AXFR.
.TP
.BI \-\-notify " list"
The secondaries told by a NOTIFY (RFC 1996), as
.IR host [: port ],
comma\-separated, when the runtime starts and whenever a zone's serial changes.
A NOTIFY is sent again after 1, 3, 7, 15 and 31 seconds until the secondary
answers it. Only the first shard sends them.
.TP
.BI \-\-publish " list"
This machine's public addresses, IPv4 and IPv6, comma\-separated: every name a
runtime of the daemon is reached by
.RB ( \-\-domain ,
.BR \-\-acme ,
a storage runtime's
.B \-\-domain
and
.BR \-\-website\-domain )
that falls within a zone is answered with them. Without it, they are answered
with this machine's public addresses when the runtime has an entrance (below),
and not at all with
.BR "\-\-proxy none" .
.TP
.BI \-\-proxy " name\fR|\fBnone"
The entrance the names of runtimes are reached through: a proxy without
.BR \-\-backend ,
which routes each HTTP request by the name it asks for to the runtime that has
it (see
.BR "PROXY FLAGS" ).
.B create
makes it as this runtime's child, named
.IB dns \-proxy
when the flag is not given, listening on
.B \-\-proxy\-port
with
.BR \-\-tls ,
.B "\-\-backend\-timeout 5m"
(a site may take a while to begin answering, as behind any web server) and a
certificate it asks the CA for itself: each zone with its wildcard, proven
over DNS\-01 by this runtime's answers. Before that,
.B acme issue
asks the resolver of
.I /etc/resolv.conf
for a TXT record that exists only in this runtime's answers, after
.B SOCKETLEY_ACME_DNS_WAIT
seconds, and then asks every name server the zone is delegated to for it
directly, as a CA may ask any of them. A zone the world's DNS does not delegate
here yet, or one a secondary of which has not taken the record up in that time,
has each of its names proven by itself instead,
over TLS\-ALPN\-01 on 443, without the wildcard, which is asked for again every
six hours until the zone is delegated. On 443 it comes with
.IB dns \-proxy\-http
on port 80, which sends every request to HTTPS
.RB ( \-\-redirect\-https ).
Behind it, every response over TLS carries
.B "Strict\-Transport\-Security: max\-age=31536000"
unless the runtime answering set its own. Over TLS it speaks HTTP/2 to a client
that offers it by ALPN and HTTP/1.1 to one that does not: each stream is routed by
the name it asks for, as a request over HTTP/1.1 is, and one asking for a name
other than the one its connection's handshake gave is answered 421. A client may
have eight streams open at once;
.B SOCKETLEY_PROXY_HTTP2=0
turns HTTP/2 off. Over UDP on the same port it speaks HTTP/3 (RFC 9114), which every answer over TCP names in
.BR "Alt\-Svc: h3" ;
a stream of it is routed by its own
.B :authority
exactly as HTTP/2's is, and a browser that cannot reach the UDP port goes on over TCP.
.B SOCKETLEY_PROXY_HTTP3=0
opens no UDP socket. It stops, starts and is removed with
this runtime; removed while another dns runtime names it too, it becomes that
one's instead. A proxy of that name that exists already is taken as it is,
provided it is an entrance.
.B none
makes none. With an entrance and no
.BR \-\-publish ,
the names are answered with this machine's public addresses, read from its
interfaces and looked at again every minute.
.IP
.nf
socketley create dns ns \-\-zone example.com \-s     # and ns\-proxy on 443
socketley create server chat \-\-domain \-\-http /srv/chat \-s
curl https://chat.example.com/
.fi
.TP
.BI \-\-proxy\-port " port"
The port the entrance
.B create
makes listens on; 443 when not given.
.TP
.BI \-\-rrl " n"
Response rate limiting: at most
.I n
answers a second to one client network (an IPv4 /24, an IPv6 /56); past it,
every second answer is replaced by a truncated one (TC, no records, as large as
the query) and the others are dropped. A resolver behind that network retries
over TCP, which is not limited, and is served; a victim whose address a
flood forges receives nothing larger than what was sent. 100 by default, 0 for
no limit.
.TP
.B \-\-dnssec
Sign every zone as it is loaded (RFC 4033 to 4035): a key of ECDSA P\-256 with
SHA\-256 (algorithm 13) a zone, made at the first start and kept in
.IR state\-dir /dns/ name .dnssec/
for every shard, its DNSKEY at the apex, an NSEC chain through the names and
an RRSIG over every set the zone holds, for three weeks and made again a week
before they end. A query with the DO bit is answered with the signatures, and
a name or type that is not there with the NSEC records that prove it; a zone
transfer carries them. The parent zone is given the DS record
.B socketley dnssec
prints.
.SS Names runtimes are reached by
The daemon writes the names its runtimes are reached by into
.IR state\-dir /.dns/names
whenever they change, and
.B acme issue
writes a DNS\-01 answer there as
.BI acme\- token
while a CA checks it. A
.B dns
runtime looks at that directory once a second, as it looks at its zone files,
and adds to each zone the names within it, with the addresses of
.BR \-\-publish ,
TTL 300, and the answers, as TXT records of
.BI _acme\-challenge. name ,
TTL 10. What the zone file says of a name wins: a name with addresses of its
own, or an alias, keeps them, and nothing is added beneath a delegation.
.PP
.nf
socketley create dns ns \-\-zone /etc/zones/example.com.zone \\
    \-\-publish 203.0.113.7,2001:db8::7 \-s
socketley create storage files \-p 9301 \-\-domain files.example.com ...
dig files.example.com            # 203.0.113.7
dig photos.files.example.com     # 203.0.113.7
.fi
.PP
So that a secondary notices, the serial a zone is served with is its file's plus
the seconds from 2023\-11\-14 to when that directory last changed: it grows with
every change of the file's serial and of the published records, and is the same
in every shard and after every restart.
.SS What it answers
A name found is answered with its records, authoritatively. A CNAME is
followed, up to eight steps, while its target is in the same zone. A wildcard
.RB ( *.label )
stands for every name beneath its parent that does not exist. A name below a
delegation is referred: the NS records of the cut, and the addresses of those
servers where the zone holds them, without the authoritative flag. An absent
name is NXDOMAIN, and a name without the type asked for NODATA, both with the
zone's SOA, whose TTL there is the lesser of its own and its MINIMUM (RFC 2308).
The addresses of the hosts an NS, MX or SRV answer names follow in the
additional section when the zone has them and they fit.
.PP
ANY is answered with one HINFO record, as RFC 8482 allows, rather than with
everything, so that it amplifies nothing. Classes other than IN, such as
CHAOS's version.bind, are REFUSED. A query with EDNS (RFC 6891) is answered with
it; an answer over UDP is kept within the size the query offers and never past
1232 bytes, the size that needs no fragments, and one without EDNS within 512.
What does not fit is sent as the question alone with TC set, and the asker
comes back over TCP, where it is answered whole. A response is never answered,
a malformed query is FORMERR, and opcodes other than QUERY are NOTIMP.
.SS TCP
A connection may carry several queries one after another and is answered in
order (RFC 7766). One left quiet for 10 seconds, with nothing left to write, is
closed.
.SS Stats
.B stats
adds, for each shard:
.BR zones ,
.BR records ,
.BR udp_queries ,
.BR tcp_queries ,
the answers by rcode
.RB ( noerror ,
.BR nxdomain ,
.BR refused ,
.BR formerr ,
.BR notimp ,
.BR other_rcode ),
.BR truncated ,
.BR referrals ,
.B dropped
(packets that were no query),
.B rrl_dropped
and
.BR rrl_slipped ,
.B send_failed
(answers the socket would not take),
.BR tcp_conns ,
.BR tcp_refused ,
.BR transfers ,
.BR transfers_refused ,
.BR notify_sent ,
.BR notify_acked ,
.BR notify_given_up ,
.B zone_reloads
and
.BR zone_reload_failures .
.SH SYNC FLAGS
A
.B sync
runtime is one job that carries files between a directory and a storage. One
runtime is one job, so what goes wrong with it has its own line in
.BR "socketley ls" ,
its own figures, its own lines in the log and its own alert.
.PP
.nf
socketley create sync photos \-\-dir /srv/photos \-\-storage ui/photos \e
    \-\-credentials /etc/socketley/sync.creds \-\-mode out \-s
.fi
.PP
It listens on nothing and takes no
.BR \-p ,
and
.B \-\-shards
is refused: the shards would share no socket and would write one index from
several processes.
.TP
.BI "\-\-dir " path
The directory whose files are carried, absolute, and there when the runtime
starts. Required.
.TP
.BI "\-\-storage " target\fR[\fB,\fItarget\fR...]
Where they go, each one of three forms with the bucket last:
.RS
.TP
.B ui/photos
a storage runtime of this daemon, reached on its port at 127.0.0.1. A name
with no scheme and no port is always a runtime's, dots and all;
.TP
.B 10.0.0.5:9000/photos
a host and a port, over plain HTTP;
.TP
.B https://files.example/photos
a URL, http or https, with nothing between the host and the bucket.
.RE
.IP
A storage runtime that serves TLS itself is named by its URL, since its port
does not speak plain HTTP; naming it is refused, with the URL to use. A bucket name follows the S3 rules: 3 to 63 of
.BR a\-z ,
.BR 0\-9 ,
.B .
and
.BR \- .
More than one target is taken only with
.BR "\-\-mode out" ,
since carrying two of them into one directory cannot say which a file came from
when they differ. Required.
.TP
.BI "\-\-mode out" | in | inout
Who carries, in the same three words a server and a client take:
.B out
sends the directory to the storage,
.B in
brings the storage to the directory, and
.B inout
does both. The default is
.BR out .
.TP
.BI "\-\-credentials " file
The file the job signs with:
.B access_key
and
.B secret_key
as
.I key = value
lines, with
.B session_token
and
.B region
when they are wanted, and
.B #
beginning a comment. Required, since a sync signs every request it makes.
.TP
.BI "\-\-prefix " key
The key everything is written under, below the bucket; without the flag it is
the bucket's root.
.TP
.BI "\-\-every " duration
How often the whole directory is walked, and with
.B in
or
.B inout
the storage listed, as
.BR 30s ,
.BR 10m ,
.B 1h
or
.BR 1d ;
.B never
walks it once at a start. One hour by default; a job that brings the storage
here lists it once a minute when
.B \-\-every
is never. Between walks, inotify says
which files changed, and a file is carried once it is written and closed,
moved in or touched, and removed once it is deleted; a directory that comes or
goes, or more events than the kernel keeps, asks for a walk. When inotify is
not there or its watches run out
.RB ( fs.inotify.max_user_watches ),
it is said once in the log and the walk is what finds a change.
.TP
.BI "\-\-repositories " file
Makes the job a user's own: it pulls the git repositories that user keeps in a
storage runtime (see
.B "Git repositories"
under
.BR "STORAGE FLAGS" )
rather than carrying a directory, and takes no
.BR \-\-dir ,
.B \-\-storage
or
.BR \-\-credentials .
The storage runtime makes such a job for each of its users and writes the
file; it is not one to make by hand.
.TP
.BI "\-\-exclude " pattern\fR[\fB,\fIpattern\fR...]
Shell patterns, separated by commas, with spaces around them ignored:
.BR "\(aq*.tmp, *.md, node_modules\(aq" .
A pattern with no slash names a file or directory at any depth, as a
.I .gitignore
line does; one with a slash is matched against the whole path below
.BR \-\-dir ,
and its
.B *
crosses slashes. Anything matched is not carried, and a matched directory is
not walked.
.PP
.B "What it remembers."
A sync keeps an index of what it last carried, in
.IR state\-dir /sync/ name .index,
0600. Without it a deletion here cannot be told from a file never carried: the
first is taken off the far side and the second is sent. Every record carries a
checksum, and the first that fails it ends the file, so a crash costs at most
carrying again what the last moments carried \(em never a file believed
carried that was not. The index belongs to one directory and one prefix: when
either changes it is begun again, which carries everything once more and takes
nothing away.
.B "socketley remove"
deletes it, and a rename carries it along.
.PP
.B "How it carries."
Eight requests at a time per target, each on a connection kept between them,
and never two about one file. A file
above 512 MiB goes as a multipart upload in parts of 64 MiB, since one request
carries at most 5 GiB; a file that changes while its parts go is given up and
carried again once it is written. Without TLS a file's bytes go from the disk
to the socket by splice. A link to a file is carried as the file it names, and
taken away at the walk that finds it pointing at nothing, since no event names
a link whose target goes; a link to a directory is not followed. A
.B \-\-dir
that holds the directory of a storage runtime it names, or lies in it, is
refused, since every object carried would be carried again.
.PP
.B "What it takes away."
A file is removed from the storage only when it is provably gone here: deleted,
or a link to nothing. A file the walk could not read, one below a directory it
may not enter, and one an
.B \-\-exclude
names since it was carried are left where they are. A directory that is empty
when the index says files were carried from it removes nothing, and says why in
.B last_error
\(em a disk that is not mounted looks exactly like that, and a bucket emptied on
the strength of it cannot be filled again.
.PP
.B "Both ways."
With
.B in
or
.BR inout ,
the storage is listed first, at a start and then at every
.BR \-\-every ,
and each object is set against the index and the file here; with
.B inout
the walk follows each listing. A file the storage has and this side does not
is brought, in ranges of 8 MiB written below
.I .socketley\-sync
in
.B \-\-dir
and given its name once whole; one the storage changed since is brought again;
one the storage removed is removed here, unless it changed here since. A file
both sides have at the start with the same length is taken as the same.
A file both sides changed is kept twice: this side's at its name, carried out
over the storage's, and the storage's beside it as
.IR "name (conflict date time).ext" ,
on both sides. With
.B inout
every write and removal sent carries the etag this side last saw, so one the
storage made meanwhile refuses it and the next listing keeps both, rather than
the later write winning. With
.B in
nothing is carried out: a file changed here is put back as the storage has it,
and this side's version kept beside it. A key with an empty,
.B .
or
.B ..
segment is not brought, and a folder that is a link out of
.B \-\-dir
is not written through. A listing with nothing in it removes nothing here.
.PP
.B "What a failure means."
A file the storage refuses, and a target that does not answer, are counted,
logged once for as long as the reason stays the same, and tried again by a walk
thirty seconds later, whatever
.B \-\-every
says. A target that stops answering holds up its own queue and no other's. The
runtime keeps running: a sync that keeps failing for three readings in a row
raises an alert, with
.B last_error
as its detail, which clears once something is carried again. A runtime it
names that is not a storage, or not there, is a failure of the same kind.
.PP
.B "Its figures"
(\fBsocketley stats\fR) are
.BR mode ,
.BR dir ,
.B indexed
(files the index holds),
.BR carried ,
.BR removed ,
.BR bytes_carried ,
.B waiting
(what is queued),
.BR failures ,
.B failing
(failures since the last thing carried),
.B watched
(directories inotify watches),
.B brought
(files brought from the storage),
.B removed_here
(files removed here because the storage removed them),
.B conflicts
(files both sides changed),
.B last_run_secs
and
.BR last_error .
.SH IDP FLAGS
An
.B idp
runtime is an OpenID Connect provider that keeps its own accounts: a user
signs in once, and every application that asks the provider, every route a
proxy puts behind it, a storage's web interface and the dashboard know who
they are.
.PP
.nf
socketley create idp auth \-p 9400 \-\-domain auth.example.com \e
    \-\-clients /etc/socketley/idp.clients \-\-cookie\-domain example.com \-s
socketley idp\-user auth add smiley \-\-password \- \-\-groups admins
.fi
.PP
An account is a fixed random id and fields with no schema. The provider
reads
.BR username ,
.B password
(a scrypt hash),
.BR disabled ,
.B groups
(a comma list; members of
.B admins
are admins),
.BR name ,
.BR email ,
.B picture
and
.BR reset ;
every other field is kept for the applications as it is, so one they add needs
no change here and an older build keeps what it does not know. What one
application alone uses \(em a game's characters, a shop's orders \(em belongs
in that application, under the account's id. Every change is synced to a
journal under the state directory before it is answered, and folded into a
snapshot that names its format, which a build older than it refuses rather
than rewrites. See
.B idp\-user
under
.BR COMMANDS .
.PP
It serves the authorization code flow with PKCE, refresh tokens, discovery at
.BR /.well\-known/openid\-configuration ,
its key at
.BR /jwks ,
.BR /userinfo ,
which answers from the account as it is now, and
.BR /logout ;
at
.B /account
a user changes their name, address and password and signs out everywhere.
Turning a second step on or off and making an app password ask for the
password again, or a code where a second step is on, counted as a sign-in's
are, so a session alone changes neither; a second step on is replaced only by
turning it off. Signing out everywhere and a new password end the account's
app passwords with its sessions.
Tokens are signed ES256 with a P\-256 key made at the first start and kept in
the state directory, so a restart signs nobody out; a refresh token, which only
the provider reads back, is signed HS256 under a secret made from that key. A
refresh token is good once: a refresh gives the next of its sign-in, kept with
the account, and one used again ends that sign-in, its newest token too. A
password is hashed and checked off the event loop, on a thread for each core
the runtime may use, up to four shared out among its shards, each making up to
four sign-ins' scrypt at once with AVX2. It serves plain HTTP and is reached through the
entrance, which terminates TLS for its names as for every other runtime's;
.B \-\-tls
is refused.
.PP
.B Guessing.
Five failed sign-ins from one address within fifteen minutes refuse it until
they age out, an IPv6 address counted by its /64, and sign-ins still being
checked count beside them, so many sent at once are refused alike. Ten at one
account, from wherever, make its next try wait half a minute, doubling with each
one more to five minutes; a browser the account signed in on before keeps a
count of its own, by a cookie of the provider's that lasts ninety days, so
guesses from elsewhere never hold its user up. Three wrong codes end a
sign-in's second step, its password then asked again, and five at an account
hold its codes up as its passwords are. At most 64 password checks wait at a
shard, and a sign-in past them is told to try again in a moment (503).
.PP
.B On several cores.
With
.BI "\-\-shards " N
the provider is served by
.I N
worker processes on its one port
.RB ( SO_REUSEPORT ),
each signing in, giving tokens and answering
.B /userinfo
with the one key. Shard 0 writes the accounts, as a provider of one shard does,
and every other shard reads its journal from the same files before it answers,
so a change made with
.B idp\-user
or on the account page holds at every shard once it is answered; one made at
another shard is sent to shard 0. A code is redeemed at the shard that gave it,
which the code names, wherever the exchange arrives, and is good once there; a
failed sign-in is answered once every shard counts it against its address. The
number may change across a restart.
.PP
A sign-in is a cookie of the provider's, which a proxy with
.BR "\-\-auth idp:" ,
a storage with
.B \-\-idp
and the dashboard with
.B daemon.dashboard_idp
verify themselves against the key it publishes beside its state, each session
once until it expires. A session ended at the provider \(em signed out
everywhere, the account disabled \(em therefore ends there at once and at
those at the session's expiry, at most twelve hours, a month for one kept
signed in.
.TP
.BI "\-\-clients " file
The applications that sign in through it, one a line:
.IR "id secret redirect" " [" redirect ...],
with
.B \-
as the secret of a public client, which must then send a PKCE challenge. A
redirect is an https address compared whole, http only on localhost. In place
of a redirect,
.B device
lets the client sign a person in by the device grant, as
.B socketley login
does, and
.B ssh
lets its access tokens ask for SSH certificates. The file is read again when
it changes; one broken while running keeps the clients read before it.
.TP
.BI "\-\-ssh\-ca " hours
An SSH certificate authority: an Ed25519 key made at the first start and kept
in the state directory, its public key at
.BR /ssh/ca.pub ,
and at
.B /ssh/user\-cert
a user certificate holding
.I hours
(1 to 168) for the account whose access token is sent, given only to a client
marked
.B ssh
and never to a disabled account, nor on a sign-in more than twelve hours old,
refreshed tokens and all, which is told to sign in again. A user certificate permits a terminal, port
forwarding and agent forwarding, and neither X11 forwarding nor the user's
.IR ~/.ssh/rc .
Every certificate is said in the log with the
account, its principals and the key's fingerprint. See
.B ssh\-cert
and
.BR idp\-ssh .
.TP
.BI "\-\-issuer " url
The https address the provider is reached at, which every token names; when
not given,
.BI https:// name
for the first
.BR \-\-domain .
.TP
.BI "\-\-cookie\-domain " domain
The domain whose names share the sign-in cookie, so a user signed in here is
signed in for a proxy, a storage or the dashboard on any name below it;
without it the cookie is the provider's host alone.
.TP
.BI "\-\-shards " N
The worker processes the provider is served by, 1 to 64: shard 0 writes the
accounts and every one signs in (see
.B On several cores
above). It changes with the runtime stopped.
.PP
An address that failed to sign in five times in fifteen minutes is refused
until the oldest of those failures is fifteen minutes old, counted by the last
address
.B X\-Forwarded\-For
names when the request came through a proxy on this machine.
.SH DB FLAGS
A
.B db
runtime keeps JSON documents for applications \(em a game's characters, a
shop's orders and addresses, a user's settings \(em each owned by an account
of an
.B idp
runtime, which reaches its own and no other's. Large things, a picture or a
model, belong in a storage and their address here.
.PP
.nf
socketley create db data \-p 9500 \-\-domain data \-\-idp auth \e
    \-\-schema /etc/socketley/db.schema \-\-keys /etc/socketley/db.keys \-s
socketley create db copy \-p 9500 \-\-replicate https://data.example.com \e
    \-\-replicate\-key /etc/socketley/db.replica\-key \-\-keys /etc/socketley/db.keys \-s
.fi
.PP
A document lives at
.BI /c/ collection / id
and is read with
.BR GET ,
replaced with
.BR PUT ,
changed in place with
.B PATCH
and removed with
.BR DELETE ;
a
.B POST
to
.BI /c/ collection
makes one under a new id. It is answered with its fields and
.BR _id ,
.B _v
(its version, 1 when made and one more with each change, also its
.BR ETag )
and
.B _owner
(the account's id).
.B "If-Match: \(dq3\(dq"
writes only at that version and
.B "If-None-Match: *"
only where there is none: 412 otherwise. A patch is
.B {"set":{"a.b":1},"unset":["c"],"inc":{"n":1},"push":{"tags":"x"},"pull":{"tags":"y"}}
on dotted paths.
.BI POST " /c/" collection /_query
takes
.B {"where":{"level":{"gte":5},"name":"x"},"sort":"\-level","limit":50,"skip":0}
with the conditions eq, ne, gt, gte, lt, lte, in, prefix and exists, or
.B "count":true
for the number alone; it answers
.B {"docs":[...]}
and
.B next
when there are more. A range compares a field's values of one kind only, as an
index orders them: null, false, true, numbers, strings.
.B POST /tx
takes
.B {"ops":[{"op":"put","c":..,"id":..,"doc":{..}},{"op":"patch",..,"changes":{..},"if":3},{"op":"insert",..},{"op":"delete",..}]}
and makes every change or none.
.B GET /me
names who is asking.
.PP
Who asks is a key, sent as
.BI "Authorization: Bearer " secret\fR,
which reaches everything; an account of the
.B \-\-idp
runtime, by its sign-in cookie or by an access token the provider gave one of
its clients, sent as
.BR "Authorization: Bearer" ;
or nobody. A write by the cookie must be
.B application/json
from the database's own host or an origin of
.BR \-\-origins ,
which a form another site posts cannot be. A document over 1 MiB, or nested
deeper than 16, is refused.
.PP
The documents live on the disk, in segments under the state directory: each
write is one record, appended to the newest segment, which is closed at 64 MiB
and another begun. Memory holds where each document lies, its version, owner
and the values its indexes order it by, and a cache of the documents last read
.RB ( \-\-cache );
the rest are read from the disk, so a database holds far more than memory. Sealed
segments whose documents have mostly been written again or deleted are merged
off the event loop into one of their living documents, and the old ones go. A
record torn by a crash ends the newest segment where the last whole one does;
damage anywhere else refuses the start, for a restore.
.PP
No answer is given before what it rests on is on the disk: a write's own
record, or the change a read saw \(em so nothing is read that a crash could take
back. One sync runs at a time, off the event loop, and takes every record
written while the one before it ran, so a burst of writes costs a few syncs
rather than one each and reads go on being answered meanwhile. A read behind a
write on one connection is answered after it. A script of this daemon reaches
the documents as
.BR socketley.db .
It serves plain HTTP behind the entrance.
.PP
.B On several cores.
With
.BI "\-\-shards " N
the database is served by
.I N
worker processes on its one port
.RB ( SO_REUSEPORT ).
Shard 0 writes the one log, as a database of one shard does, and every other
shard reads it from the same files as shard 0 puts it on the disk: it answers
reads, queries and streams itself and sends shard 0 the writes, which are
answered once they are on its disk. A shard takes in what shard 0 has made
durable before it answers, so a read on any shard sees every write answered
before it began. Transactions, unique indexes, streams, replicas and backups
hold as they do on one shard, and the number may change across a restart, the
documents being one log whichever it is. Reads and queries are answered by every
shard; writes, which wait for one disk, are as many as one shard takes.
.PP
.B Changes, as they happen.
.BI GET " /c/" collection /_feed
is a stream of Server-Sent Events, which a browser reads with
.BR EventSource :
each change once it is on the disk, as an event
.B put
with
.B {"collection":..,"id":..,"owner":..,"version":..,"doc":{..}}
or
.B delete
with
.BR {"collection":..,"id":..,"deleted":true} ,
numbered by the change. The reader sees what it may read, as a query does: an
account its own documents of an owner collection.
.B GET /_feed
streams every collection's to a key. A stream that broke resumes with
.B Last\-Event\-ID
(or
.BR ?since= )
from the last change it read; one older than the database keeps, about 32 MiB
of changes, is told
.B reset
and should read again what it shows. A comment every fifteen seconds keeps a
quiet stream from looking dead.
.PP
.B Documents with a time to go.
A collection whose schema line names
.BI ttl: field
loses a document when that field's number, in seconds since 1970, has passed:
it is answered as absent from that moment, and its deletion is written and
streamed within the minute.
.PP
.B A copy on another machine.
A db runtime with
.B \-\-replicate
follows another's
.B /_replicate
stream with a key of that database: it takes every document once, then every
change, written to its own disk with the change's own number, so a restart of
either goes on where it was. When the one it copies no longer keeps the changes
it missed, it is sent everything again. A replica answers reads, queries and
streams of its own and refuses writes with 403, naming where to make them;
removing
.B \-\-replicate
.RB ( "import \-\-restart" )
makes it a database of its own.
.PP
.B Backups.
.BI "socketley db\-backup " "db file"
writes every document into one file, off the event loop, while the database
serves;
.BI "socketley db\-restore " "db file"
stops it, keeps its documents aside in
.IB dir .before\-restore
and starts it again on the backup's. With
.BR \-\-backup\-dir ,
backups are taken every
.B \-\-backup\-every
and the newest
.B \-\-backup\-keep
kept; a sync runtime carries that directory to a storage.
.TP
.BI "\-\-schema " file
The collections, a line each: a name, who reaches it, and the fields indexed,
.B unique:
before one whose values no two documents share.
.RS
.nf
# collection  access   indexes
characters    owner    level unique:name
posts         shared   created
scores        public   score
products      catalog  unique:sku price
sessions      owner    ttl:expires_at
.fi
.RE
.IP
.B private
(and any collection the file does not name): a key only.
.B owner
: an account reads and writes its own documents; another's is answered as not
there.
.B shared
: an account reads every document and writes its own.
.B public
: anybody reads; an account writes its own.
.B catalog
: anybody reads; a key writes. The file is read again when it changes; one that
does not read, or a unique index the documents already break, keeps the schema
before it and says why in
.BR stats .
.TP
.BI "\-\-keys " file
Service keys for backends, a line each:
.IR "name secret" ,
the secret at least 16 characters. Read again when it changes. A key may set a
document's
.BR _owner .
.TP
.BI "\-\-idp " runtime
The idp runtime whose accounts own documents.
.TP
.BI "\-\-idp\-audience " clients
The ids of the idp's clients whose access tokens it takes, comma-separated. A
token the provider gave any other application is refused, since it is that
application's and no key to this runtime; without the flag no access token is
taken, while cookies and passwords sign in as before.
.TP
.BI "\-\-origins " list
The web origins a browser may call it from, comma-separated, answered with
CORS and the browser's sign-in;
.B *
for any page, without it.
.TP
.BI "\-\-max\-bytes " size
The most the documents hold; a write past it is refused with 507.
.TP
.BI "\-\-cache " size
The memory the documents last read are kept in, 64M unless given; the rest
are read from the disk.
.TP
.BI "\-\-replicate " url
The http or https address of the database this one copies, as its entrance
answers it; https is verified against the system's roots.
.TP
.BI "\-\-replicate\-key " file
A file holding the secret of a key of that database.
.TP
.BI "\-\-backup\-dir " dir
Where scheduled backups are written, each
.IB name \- time .dbbackup .
.TP
.BI "\-\-backup\-every " duration
How often, 1d unless given.
.TP
.BI "\-\-backup\-keep " n
How many are kept, 7 unless given.
.TP
.BI "\-\-shards " N
The worker processes the database is served by, 1 to 64: shard 0 writes and
every one reads (see
.B On several cores
above). It changes with the runtime stopped.
.PP
In a script:
.nf
local c, err = socketley.db.get("data", "characters", id)
socketley.db.put("data", "characters", id, { name = "Merlin", level = 3 }, { ["if"] = c._v })
socketley.db.patch("data", "characters", id, { inc = { level = 1 } })
local r = socketley.db.query("data", "scores", { sort = "\-score", limit = 10 })
socketley.db.insert("data", "orders", { item = "sword" })
socketley.db.delete("data", "orders", id)
socketley.db.tx("data", { { op = "put", c = "a", id = "x", doc = {} }, { op = "delete", c = "b", id = "y" } })
.fi
.PP
Each answers the document (or the transaction's results), or nil, an error
code and a sentence. Inside a callback that can wait it waits; anywhere else
the last argument is a function called with the answer.
.SH MQTT FLAGS
An
.B mqtt
runtime is an MQTT broker, for 3.1, 3.1.1 and 5.0, on TCP, TLS and WebSocket:
devices, apps and browsers publish to topics and subscribe to them, and a
client that goes away keeps its session \(em its subscriptions and the messages
sent to them while it was gone \(em until it comes back or the session expires.
It is a runtime like the others: messages reach a cache, a db, a storage, a
server or another broker by rules of its own, and a storage's events and a
script's messages reach its subscribers.
.PP
.nf
socketley mqtt\-passwd /etc/socketley/mqtt.users sensor\-17
socketley create mqtt broker \-\-users /etc/socketley/mqtt.users \e
    \-\-acl /etc/socketley/mqtt.acl \-\-ws 8083 \-\-persistent on \-s
socketley create mqtt devices \-p 8883 \-\-tls \-\-cert dev.pem \-\-key dev.key \e
    \-\-ca devices\-ca.pem \-\-routes /etc/socketley/mqtt.routes \-s
.fi
.PP
It listens on 1883, or 8883 with
.BR \-\-tls ,
unless
.B \-p
says otherwise.
.B \-\-max\-connections
and
.B \-\-write\-buffer
hold as for a server: past the write buffer owed to a slow client its QoS 0
messages are dropped and counted, and QoS 1 and 2 wait in its session.
.PP
.B What it implements.
QoS 0, 1 and 2, the last exactly once by its packet identifier; retained
messages, cleared by an empty one; wills, sent when a connection is lost or a
5.0 client disconnects asking for it and not when it disconnects cleanly, after
a 5.0 will delay if one is given and not at all when the client comes back
within it; sessions kept by 3.1.1's clean session 0 and 5.0's session expiry,
one connection to a client id, a second taking the session over and the first
told so; keep alive, a client silent for one and a half times it disconnected;
a CONNECT within ten seconds or the connection closed; and of 5.0 the session
and message expiry, the receive maximum both ways, topic aliases, the maximum
packet size, subscription identifiers, no local, retain as published, retain
handling, shared subscriptions
.RB ( $share/\fIgroup\fB/\fIfilter\fR,
each message to one member, in turn), request and response (the response
topic and correlation data carried), user properties, the content type and the
payload format, and reason codes and strings on what it refuses. Wildcards
.B +
and
.B #
do not match a topic beginning with
.BR $ .
It publishes its own figures, retained, under
.B $SYS/broker/
every ten seconds: version, uptime, clients, messages and subscriptions. It
does not take the 5.0 AUTH exchange (a CONNECT naming an authentication method
is refused with 0x8C) and does not bridge to brokers outside the daemon.
.PP
.B Who may connect.
At least one of
.BR \-\-users ,
.BR \-\-idp ,
.B \-\-ca
or
.B \-\-anonymous
must be given. A client certificate the
.B \-\-ca
verified lets its client in under the certificate's common name. A password is
checked against the users file, off the event loop, or else, with
.BR \-\-idp ,
taken as an access token of that provider, its account's name the user (a user
name given with it must be that name). Without credentials a client is let in
only with
.BR \-\-anonymous .
A refusal is 0x86 (bad user name or password), 4 to a 3.1.1 client. A script's
.B on_auth
has the last word (see
.B Lua
below).
.PP
.B What each may do.
.B \-\-acl
names rules in Mosquitto's form, read again within two seconds of a change:
.RS
.PP
.nf
# before any user line: clients without one
topic read public/#
user alice
topic readwrite home/alice/#
topic write telemetry/#
topic deny telemetry/secret/#
pattern readwrite users/%u/#
pattern write devices/%c/state
.fi
.RE
.IP
.B topic
.RB [ read | write | readwrite | deny ]
.I filter
applies to the user named last;
.B pattern
to everyone, with
.B %u
the user and
.B %c
the client id, each standing for one level. A deny wins over any grant; what
no rule grants is refused. A publish refused is answered 0x87 to a 5.0 client,
acknowledged and dropped for a 3.1.1 one; a subscription is refused only when
no rule grants all of it, and a message a deny covers is held back from a
subscription it covers in part. Without
.B \-\-acl
every client may do everything.
.PP
.B Sessions across a restart.
With
.B \-\-persistent
the sessions, their queues and messages in flight, the retained messages and
the topic rules' queues are written to a snapshot every
.B \-\-persistent\-interval
when something changed, off the event loop, and at stop; a start reads them
back, so a stop, a restart of the daemon or a killed worker loses at most that
interval. A session with no expiry left, and the wills it was owed, are ended
and sent at the start that finds them. Without it the broker keeps nothing
across a restart.
.PP
.B Topic rules.
.B \-\-routes
names a file of rules, a filter and a target a line, that hand every message
the filter matches to another runtime of the daemon beside the broker's own
subscribers:
.RS
.PP
.nf
# filter          target
sensor/+/temp     cache://telemetry/stream/temps\-%1
alarm/#           cache://events/channel/alarms
device/+/state    db://fleet/devices/%1
log/#             storage://raw/mqtt/%Y/%m/%d/
chat/#            server://chat
share/#           mqtt://other/from\-edge/%t
door/#            https://hooks.example.com/door
.fi
.RE
.IP
.BI cache:// runtime /stream/ key
adds a stream entry of the fields
.BR topic ,
.BR payload ,
.B qos
and
.BR client ;
.BI cache:// runtime /channel/ name
publishes the payload on the channel;
.BI db:// runtime / collection\fR[\fB/\fIid\fR]
puts the payload as the document when it is a JSON object, and else
.B {"topic":..,"payload":..,"client":..,"qos":..}
\(em under
.I id
when given, as a new document otherwise;
.BI storage:// runtime / bucket\fR[\fB/\fIprefix\fR]
gathers the messages as JSON lines,
.BR {"at":..,"topic":..,"payload":..,"qos":..,"client":..} ,
into one
.B .ndjson
object a minute (or a megabyte, or at stop) under the prefix;
.BI server:// runtime
sends every client of the server
.B {"topic":..,"payload":..}
as a line; and
.BI mqtt:// runtime / topic
publishes on that broker, at the message's QoS and retained as it was; and
.BR http:// " or " https:// " URL"
POSTs
.B {"topic":..,"payload":..,"qos":..,"retain":..,"client":..}
to it, one at a time and in order on a connection it keeps, the host resolved
on the broker's loop (see What can stop the event loop): a 2xx is delivered, a
400 or another refusal is counted failed and not sent again, and no answer, a
408, a 429 or a 5xx is tried again as a stopped runtime is.
.B %1
to
.B %9
stand for the levels the filter's
.B +
matched,
.B %t
for the whole topic and
.B %c
for the client that published; a storage prefix also takes
.BR %Y ,
.BR %m ,
.BR %d ,
.B %H
and
.BR %M .
A line beginning with
.B #
is a comment, and so is a
.B #
after a target. The file is read at start; one that does not read stops it,
naming the line.
.IP
Each rule has a queue of its own, in order: a target that is stopped or does
not answer holds its messages, tried again after a pause that grows to five
seconds, without holding up the broker or the other rules, and up to 100000
messages, the oldest dropped past them and counted. What a rule holds goes into
the snapshot, those in flight in their place, so a restart sends them again: a
target gets each message at least once.
.B stats
names each rule with what it delivered, what its target refused, what was
dropped, what it holds and its last error.
.PP
.B From the other runtimes.
.B socketley mqtt\-pub
and a script's
.B socketley.mqtt.publish
publish as the broker's own, a storage's
.BI mqtt:// runtime / topic
notification target publishes its events (see
.BR \-\-notify\-targets ),
and
.B mqtt://
rules of another broker publish theirs:
.RS
.PP
.nf
local ok, err = socketley.mqtt.publish("broker", "game/" .. id .. "/move", move, { qos = 1 })
socketley.mqtt.publish("broker", "chat/lobby", text, { retain = false }, function(ok, err) end)
.fi
.RE
.IP
It answers true once the broker has the message, or nil and the reason; inside
a callback that can wait it waits, anywhere else the last argument is a function
called with the answer.
.PP
.B WebSocket.
.BI "\-\-ws " port
serves MQTT inside WebSocket on a second port for browsers (any path,
.B /mqtt
by convention), the subprotocol
.B mqtt
or
.B mqttv3.1
chosen when offered, a packet carried in any number of frames and frames
holding any number of packets. With
.B \-\-tls
it is wss.
.PP
.B On several cores.
With
.BI "\-\-shards " N
the broker is
.I N
worker processes sharing its ports
.RB ( SO_REUSEPORT ),
one broker to its clients: each shard tells the others what its clients
subscribe to and hands them the messages their subscribers want, a retained
message is kept by all, a shared subscription's message goes to one member of
the group over all the shards, and a client connecting to another shard than
before takes its session with it, the newest connection of a client id winning.
A shard that cannot take what another hands it as fast holds back that other
shard's publishers, which are not read until it can, rather than lose their
messages: at QoS 1 and 2 all of them, at QoS 0 those whose last message went to
it. One that has answered nothing for a second holds back only QoS 1 and 2
publishers, whose answer waits: a message for it is dropped at QoS 0 then, or
once 8 MiB wait for it, and at any QoS once what it was handed has failed for
three seconds, and counted in
.BR shard_dropped .
Each shard keeps a snapshot of its own,
.IB file . shard\fR,
and publishes its figures under
.BI $SYS/broker/shard/ n /\fR.
.PP
.B Lua.
A broker's
.B \-\-lua
script may decide who connects and what passes. Each hook that decides
answers
.B true
to allow,
.B false
to refuse, and nothing to keep what the broker decided; a hook that fails
refuses. A client is a table of
.BR id ,
.BR username ,
.B peer
and
.BR version .
.RS
.PP
.nf
function on_auth(c)          \-\- c.id c.username c.password c.peer
    if c.username == "robot" then return c.password == "beep" end
end                          \-\- c.certificate c.authenticated c.account c.version
function on_publish(client, msg)              \-\- msg.topic msg.payload msg.qos msg.retain
    if msg.topic == "shout" then return { payload = string.upper(msg.payload) } end
end
function on_subscribe(client, filter, qos) return filter ~= "secret/#" end
function on_connect(client) self.publish("presence/" .. client.id, "online", { retain = true }) end
function on_disconnect(client, why) self.publish("presence/" .. client.id, "offline", { retain = true }) end
.fi
.RE
.IP
.B on_auth
is given whether the credentials checked out
.RB ( authenticated )
and as whom
.RB ( account );
letting in a client they refused makes the user name it gave its user.
.B on_publish
may also answer a table, the message as it is to go on (any of
.BR topic ,
.BR payload ,
.BR qos ,
.BR retain );
a refused publish is answered 0x87.
.B self.publish(topic, payload [, {qos=, retain=}])
publishes as the broker,
.B self.retained(topic)
reads a retained payload,
.B self.sessions()
lists this shard's sessions and
.B self.kick(client_id [, end])
disconnects one. While a hook decides, that connection's next packets wait.
.TP
.BI "\-\-users " file
The accounts that sign in with a password,
.IB user : hash
a line as
.B socketley mqtt\-passwd
writes them; read again within two seconds of a change.
.TP
.BI "\-\-idp " runtime
The idp runtime whose access tokens, given as the password, sign clients in.
.TP
.BI "\-\-idp\-audience " clients
The ids of the idp's clients whose access tokens it takes, comma-separated. A
token the provider gave any other application is refused, since it is that
application's and no key to this runtime; without the flag no access token is
taken, while cookies and passwords sign in as before.
.TP
.BI "\-\-acl " file
Who may publish and subscribe to what (see
.B What each may do
above).
.TP
.B \-\-anonymous
Lets in clients without credentials, as the rules allow.
.TP
.BI "\-\-routes " file
Where messages go besides their subscribers (see
.B Topic rules
above).
.TP
.BI "\-\-ws " port
A second port, for MQTT over WebSocket.
.TP
.BI "\-\-persistent " file \fR|\fBon
The snapshot sessions and retained messages are kept in;
.B on
for
.IB state /mqtt/ name .snapshot
under the state directory.
.TP
.BI "\-\-persistent\-interval " duration
How often what changed reaches the snapshot, 1s unless given.
.TP
.BI "\-\-max\-packet " size
The largest packet a client may send, 64 bytes to 256M, 256K unless given; a
larger one is answered 0x95 and the connection closed. It is told to 5.0
clients, and no message larger than a client says it takes is sent to it.
.TP
.BI "\-\-session\-expiry " duration
The longest a session outlives its connection, whatever the client asks; 1d
unless given. A 3.1.1 session kept by clean session 0 lasts this long.
.TP
.BI "\-\-session\-queue " n
The messages kept for one session while its client is away or slow, 1000
unless given; past it the oldest go, counted.
.TP
.BI "\-\-retained\-max " size
The most bytes retained messages take, 64M unless given; a new one past it is
not kept, counted.
.TP
.BI "\-\-max\-sessions " n
The most sessions, connected or not, 100000 unless given; a new client past it
is refused with 0x97.
.TP
.BI "\-\-keepalive\-max " seconds
The longest keep alive granted, 1200 unless given; a 5.0 client asking more or
none is told this one.
.PP
.B stats
says the sessions and those connected, the subscriptions, the retained
messages and their bytes, what is queued and in flight, the messages received
and sent at each QoS, what was dropped and why, the connects and the refusals,
the snapshots and whether a change is in none yet, each topic rule, and over
shards what was handed between them.
.SH MAIL FLAGS
A
.B mail
runtime is a mail server: it takes the mail of its domains and the mail its
users send, keeps each message in a spool until it is delivered, hands mail for
elsewhere to the domain's exchanger or a smarthost, and keeps its users'
mailboxes in a storage runtime, read over IMAP. It is a runtime like the
others: a dns runtime gives its domains their records, an idp runtime its users,
a storage runtime its mailboxes, and the other runtimes, the CLI and the daemon's
alerts send mail through it. Its settings are changed with
.B import
(\-\-restart to take them at once): a running runtime takes its connection,
rate and idle limits as they come and the rest at its next start, and
.B \-\-users
and
.B \-\-aliases
are read again whenever their files change. A rename by
.B import
moves its spool, keys and mailboxes' state with it, and is refused while it
runs.
.B show
prints a
.B \-\-relay
password as ***, which an import of what it printed keeps as it was.
.PP
.nf
socketley create mail m \-p 25 \-\-dns ns \-\-no\-reply \-s
socketley send m \-\-to ops@example.com \-\-subject "Backup done" \-\-text ok

socketley create mail m \-p 25 \-\-dns ns \-\-idp auth \-\-storage files/mail \e
    \-\-submission 587 \-\-imap 143 \-\-tls \-s
.fi
.PP
It listens for SMTP on 25 unless
.B \-p
says otherwise; port 25 needs the daemon to be allowed to bind it. With
.B \-\-shards
the first shard alone takes mail, on SMTP, submission and LMTP, so one spool
commits it all with one sync, while every shard serves the mailboxes.
.B \-\-submission
adds a port its users send from, with STARTTLS and a password,
.B \-\-submissions
one that speaks TLS from the first byte, and
.B \-\-imap
and
.B \-\-imaps
the same two for reading the mailboxes;
.B \-\-pop3
and
.B \-\-pop3s
read INBOX over POP3 (RFC 1939, with STLS, SASL PLAIN, UIDL and TOP), and
.B \-\-lmtp
takes mail from this machine's own programs into the mailboxes over LMTP (RFC
2033), one answer for each recipient, and
.B \-\-jmap
serves JMAP (RFC 8620, RFC 8621) for mail clients and web apps, over HTTPS with
.BR \-\-tls . A message a signed\-in user sends
without a Date or a Message\-ID is given them, as Postfix's cleanup does; one
from another server is passed on as it came. With no
.B \-\-bind
a port takes IPv6 and IPv4 alike; an IPv4 client is seen, checked and logged by
its IPv4 address.
.PP
.B \-\-tls
with
.B \-\-dns
and no
.B \-\-cert
takes the certificate of the dns runtime's entrance, which holds the names it is
reached by and is renewed there, and the runtime takes the renewed one as it
does any. One the entrance has still to write \(em a runtime made before its
dns runtime, or on a machine where the entrance has not yet been given its
names \(em is waited for: mail is taken meanwhile on SMTP without STARTTLS, and
what takes a password or speaks TLS from the first byte opens within a second
of the certificate being there. A certificate given with
.B \-\-cert
is there when the runtime starts, or it does not start.
.PP
.B Signing in.
A user signs in with their password over TLS, or from this machine in the
clear, or with a token their idp gave, by XOAUTH2 or OAUTHBEARER on SMTP, IMAP
and POP3, as mail apps that sign in through an identity provider do; where the
account has its second step on, a mail app signs in with one of its app
passwords (see
.BR idp\-user ).
Ten wrong answers from one address in ten minutes refuse that address for ten
minutes; twenty at one account from three addresses or more refuse the
account, for ten minutes, to every address but those it signed in from within
thirty days, so guesses spread over many addresses lock none of its own devices
out. Both outlast a restart. Each sign-in is a line of the log: the user, the
protocol and the address.
.PP
.B Whose mail it takes.
Its domains are
.BR \-\-mail\-domain ,
or the zones of the dns runtime
.B \-\-dns
names, followed as they change. Mail for an address of them is taken when an
alias names it
.RB ( \-\-aliases ),
when it is a user's
.RB ( \-\-idp :
the local part a username, or the address the account's email), when it is
.B no\-reply@
and a bounce, or when the script takes it
.RB ( on_rcpt );
any other is refused at RCPT with 550. A local part with a tag,
.IB user + tag @\fR,
is the user's, the tag what Sieve's subaddress reads;
.B \-\-recipient\-delimiter
names other characters for it, or none. An address this runtime rewrote under
SRS (below) is the sender it stands for, taken while it is 21 days old and only
under the runtime's own key. Mail for elsewhere is taken from a user
signed in, and from 127.0.0.1, this machine's own programs; from anyone else it
is refused with 554, so the server is not a relay.
.PP
.B Taking mail.
ESMTP with PIPELINING, SIZE, 8BITMIME, SMTPUTF8, ENHANCEDSTATUSCODES, STARTTLS,
AUTH PLAIN and LOGIN, CHUNKING (BDAT), DSN, LIMITS (RCPTMAX, RFC 9422) and,
inside TLS, REQUIRETLS (RFC 8689); replies come in the order the commands
came. A message
past
.B \-\-max\-size
is refused, said at MAIL when its SIZE says so, after its end otherwise. Its end
is a CRLF, a dot and a CRLF and nothing else, or the chunk BDAT says is the
last, and a bare LF or a lone CR in it is written as CRLF, so a dot after either
ends no message here or on the next server; what
a client sent after STARTTLS before the handshake is dropped. A recipient given
twice is one recipient, and an EHLO name with a control byte or a space is
refused. AUTH is offered
over TLS, and in the clear to 127.0.0.1 alone; a wrong password is answered a
second later, and ten from one address within ten minutes lock that address out
for ten minutes, the right password too (454; IMAP's [UNAVAILABLE]). On the
submission ports nothing is taken from a client that has
not signed in, and a signed-in user sends only as their own address, in MAIL
FROM and in the From header alike: their username at one of its domains, the
email their idp account names, or the username where it is an address. A
connection quiet for five minutes is closed.
.PP
.B Limits.
One client address holds at most 50 connections to port 25 and 200 to each
other port, 127.0.0.1 none of these; a runtime with no
.B \-\-max\-connections
holds 8192. One past a limit is told 421 (BYE on IMAP and ManageSieve) and
closed. A client that sends more than its largest message and a mebibyte while a
command waits, or that reads none of 32 MiB of replies, is told 421 and closed,
reset if it still reads none five seconds later.
.B \-\-rate\-limit
.I n
takes
.I n
messages a second from one client address, or one signed-in user, and tells the
next MAIL 450;
.B \-\-global\-rate\-limit
does the same for the whole runtime.
.PP
.B What it checks.
With
.BR \-\-dnsbl ,
a connection on port 25 not from 127.0.0.1 has its address asked of each list
while it is greeted, and its MAIL waits for the answer if it has not come: one
a list names (an address in 127/8) sends nothing, 554, unless it signs in. A
list's error answer (127.255.255.x) names nobody, and a list that does not
answer lets the mail by; an answer is kept five minutes. With
.BR \-\-greylist ,
a stranger's first try of a sender and a recipient from a network (a /24, or a
/64) is told 451 and taken once it tries again after the delay and within a
day, and from then on for 36 days; what a shard learns every shard knows. With
.BR \-\-rspamd ,
each message from elsewhere is scanned by that Rspamd's
.B /checkv2
beside the checks: reject refuses it with 554, soft reject and greylist with
451, add header and rewrite subject file it in Junk, and its score goes into
Authentication\-Results as x\-spam, which Sieve's spamtest reads. A scanner that
does not answer in ten seconds lets the mail by.
Mail from elsewhere (port 25, no user signed in, not 127.0.0.1) is checked
before it is taken: SPF (RFC 7208) for the sender's domain, or HELO's for a
bounce; every DKIM signature, rsa\-sha256 and ed25519\-sha256, up to ten, those
of the From's organization first, one whose l= leaves part of the body out
neutral and an RSA key under 1024 bits refused; the ARC chain; and DMARC (RFC
7489) for the domain of From, its labels past ASCII as A\-labels, with SPF or
DKIM aligned as its record says and pct= sampling what its policy is for. A
message DMARC fails under
.B p=reject
is refused with 550 and not kept, as is one whose author cannot be told
\(em two From headers, one that does not read, or authors of two domains; under
.B p=quarantine
it goes to the recipient's Junk folder; a passing ARC chain sealed last by one
of
.B \-\-arc\-trust
takes the policy off. What each said is written into the
mailbox's
.B Authentication\-Results
line, and one a sender put there under this host's name is taken out, as is
the sender's Return-Path. The
lookups run on sixteen threads of their own and never on the loop, a message's
together within twenty seconds, past which they are a temporary error; an
answer is kept a minute. Past 512 messages waiting to be checked, the next is
told 451. The organizational
domain DMARC compares is a name's registrable domain by the Public Suffix List
of the publicsuffix package
.RB ( /usr/share/publicsuffix ),
so mail.example.co.uk is example.co.uk, its rules past ASCII compared as
A\-labels; without the package a name is its own, so relaxed alignment is
strict, and the log says so once.
.PP
.B The spool.
A message is synced before the client is told 250, kept under
.IB state /mail/ name /spool
until every recipient is done. One up to a mebibyte is a record of a segment, a
file of 32 MiB written out before it is used, so one fdatasync commits every
message that ended while the last one ran; a larger one is a file of its own.
Each record carries its segment's generation and a CRC, so a record a crash cut
is dropped at the next start, and the log says so. What became of each
recipient is kept in a log beside them, so a restart tries again what was
waiting and never brings back what was dropped. A segment a few deferred
messages keep has them moved after a minute and is used again. A message under
a mebibyte is kept in memory as well, and its delivery reads nothing back.
.PP
.B Sending it on.
Recipients of one domain go in one transaction, and a connection to a domain
carries one message after another while there are more for it, at most four to
a domain and 128 in all; MAIL, RCPT and DATA go in one write where the other
side offers PIPELINING. The exchangers are the domain's MX records, from the
resolver on the loop, or the domain itself when it has none; a domain that does
not exist or says it takes no mail (RFC 7505) fails at once. With
.B \-\-relay
everything for elsewhere goes through that smarthost. TLS is spoken wherever
it is offered, and an exchanger's certificate is checked where its domain asks:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
DANE (RFC 7672): where DNSSEC signed a domain's MX records, the TLSA records of
each exchanger are asked, and with any there the mail goes over TLS only, its
certificate one they name (DANE\-TA or DANE\-EE). A record is believed only
when the resolver set the AD bit and every nameserver
.B /etc/resolv.conf
names is on this machine, a validating resolver such as unbound; elsewhere
nothing is believed and nothing is checked.
.IP \(bu 2
MTA\-STS (RFC 8461): where a domain has an
.B _mta\-sts
TXT record, its policy is fetched from
.BI https://mta\-sts. domain /.well\-known/mta\-sts.txt
on a thread, again when its id changes, and kept under the runtime's state for
the next start. Under
.B mode: enforce
only the exchangers it names are tried, over TLS, each with a certificate for
its name from the system's CAs; under
.B testing
what would have failed is logged and counted, and the mail goes as before.
.RE
.IP
Mail that cannot go so waits and is tried again (4.7.5). Elsewhere the
certificate is not checked, as no mail server checks one, and a smarthost's
always is, and its password is never sent in the clear. A recipient refused for now is tried again after
1m, 5m, 15m, 30m, 1h, 2h, 4h and every 8h after, until
.BR \-\-queue\-lifetime ;
one refused for good, or out of time, is bounced to the sender as a delivery
status report from the null sender. A server that offers CHUNKING is sent the
message in BDAT chunks, nothing stuffed, and one whose LIMITS say how many
recipients a transaction takes is sent them that many at a time. A server that
answers 421 is given a rest: no new connection to its domain for thirty
seconds, doubled by each 421 until it takes a message, to fifteen minutes.
REQUIRETLS mail goes on only over TLS whose certificate DANE or an enforced
MTA\-STS policy vouched for (a smarthost's always is), to a server that offers
REQUIRETLS, and is returned 550 5.7.10 otherwise, its notice carrying no more
than its header; a Sieve redirect keeps the requirement on. Mail from
elsewhere that it sends on goes from an SRS address of its first domain
(SRS0=hash=time=domain=local@), so SPF at the next hop judges this host and a
bounce finds its way back. What a sender asked of reports (RFC
3461) is kept with each recipient: NOTIFY, ORCPT, RET and ENVID go on to a
server that offers DSN, which reports from there; elsewhere this runtime reports
a SUCCESS asked for as delivered or relayed, a DELAY once a message has waited
four hours, and no failure where NOTIFY said NEVER. RET=FULL has the report
carry the message whole, up to 4 MiB; otherwise it carries the header. Mail from one of its domains is signed with
DKIM twice, rsa\-sha256 under the selector and ed25519\-sha256 (RFC 8463) under
.IB selector \-ed ,
relaxed, with keys made at its first start and kept in
.IB state /mail/ name .dkim. selector .key
for every shard. A key is rotated by naming the new selector with
.B \-\-dkim\-next
first, which makes its pair and publishes it beside the old one, and with
.B \-\-dkim\-selector
once DNS has it. Mail from elsewhere that it sends on, as an alias does, is
sealed with ARC (RFC 8617): what its checks found, signed and sealed for its
first domain, so the next hop can know what the message was as it came.
.PP
.B Reports.
With
.BR \-\-reports ,
it sends the reports other domains ask for: DMARC aggregate reports (RFC 7489)
to the rua of each domain whose mail it checked, and TLS reports (RFC 8460) to
the rua of each domain's
.B _smtp._tls
record it sent mail to under DANE or MTA\-STS, once a day, after midnight UTC,
gzip'd, each shard what it saw.
.B \-\-dmarc\-rua
and
.B \-\-tls\-rpt
publish where others send theirs about its own domains.
.PP
.B Its records.
With
.B \-\-dns
it gives each of its domains, through the dns runtime:
.RS
.PP
.nf
example.com.                 MX   10 mail.example.com.
example.com.                 TXT  "v=spf1 mx \-all"
skl._domainkey.example.com.  TXT  "v=DKIM1; k=rsa; p=..."
skl\-ed._domainkey.example.com. TXT "v=DKIM1; k=ed25519; p=..."
_dmarc.example.com.          TXT  "v=DMARC1; p=quarantine; adkim=r; aspf=r"
_imaps._tcp.example.com.     SRV  0 1 993 mail.example.com.
_25._tcp.mail.example.com.   TLSA 3 1 1 ...
.fi
.RE
.IP
with an SRV record (RFC 6186, RFC 8314) for each of
.BR \-\-submission ,
.BR \-\-submissions ,
.BR \-\-imap ,
.BR \-\-imaps ,
.BR \-\-pop3 ,
.BR \-\-pop3s ,
.B \-\-jmap
and
.B \-\-sieve
it opens, so a mail client finds them from the address alone; with
.B \-\-tls
the TLSA record of its certificate's key, for senders that check DANE where the
zone is signed; with
.B \-\-mta\-sts
an
.B _mta\-sts
record whose id follows the policy, and with
.B \-\-mta\-sts\-port
.BI mta\-sts. domain
as a CNAME of its host, which serves the policy there over TLS; with
.B \-\-tls\-rpt
an
.B _smtp._tls
record, and with
.B \-\-dmarc\-rua
a rua in its DMARC record,
.IP
and its host,
.BI mail. domain
unless
.B \-\-hostname
says another, the addresses the dns runtime's
.B \-\-publish
gives its names, and an SPF record of its own,
.BR "v=spf1 a \-all" ,
for a receiver that checks the name it greets with (RFC 7208 2.3). A record the
zone file has at the name wins. With
.B \-\-relay
SPF ends in ~all, since mail leaves from the smarthost's addresses. Without a
dns runtime,
.B socketley mail
.I m
.B dns
prints the lines for a zone kept elsewhere.
.PP
.B No\-reply.
With
.BR \-\-no\-reply ,
a message
.B socketley send
or a script writes without a sender goes from
.BI no\-reply@ domain\fR.
Mail to that address is refused, but a bounce is taken and read. Such a message
goes out under
.BI no\-reply+ tag @ domain\fR,
the tag a keyed hash of the recipient's domain that only this runtime can make,
so a bounce that comes back under the tag of an address's domain suppresses that
address: it is not mailed again by any shard until
.B socketley mail
.I m
.B forgive
.I address
lets it go, or ninety days pass. A bounce under no tag, or another domain's, is
read and acts on nothing, so no stranger can stop mail to an address. The
runtime's own bounce of such a message is its own word, so an address is learned
dead from its first try. A recipient whose Status is 4.x.x, or 5.2.2 (its mailbox
full), is no address that is dead and is not suppressed.
.IP
A provider's feedback report (ARF, RFC 5965) that one of its users called a
message spam \(em as Microsoft's JMRP and the other feedback loops send them, to
whichever address of its domains was given to them \(em unsubscribes that user
from its advertisements, as their own click would, where the report's sender is
who it says by SPF or DKIM; the report is still delivered.
.B socketley mail trace
names it.
.PP
.B Mailboxes.
With
.B \-\-idp
and
.BR \-\-storage ,
a user's mail is an object of the storage runtime's bucket (mail unless
.B \-\-storage
names another), made when first needed:
.IB user /INBOX/ id .eml
under a Return\-Path, a Delivered\-To and the Authentication\-Results line,
written whole under a mebibyte and in pieces of one above. A message in a
mailbox is told to the targets of
.B \-\-notify\-targets
as
.BR {"event":"mail.received","mailbox":..,"folder":..,"key":..,"from":..,"subject":..,"size":..} ,
under
.BI mail/ user
for a broker. A bucket that encrypts or compresses what it keeps does so to
mail too, and a message is read back a range at a time either way.
.PP
.B IMAP.
IMAP4rev1, and IMAP4rev2 (RFC 9051) for a client that enables it, with LITERAL+,
SEARCHRES, SASL\-IR, IDLE, NAMESPACE, UIDPLUS, MOVE, UNSELECT,
CHILDREN, SPECIAL\-USE, ID, ENABLE, CONDSTORE and QRESYNC, ESEARCH,
LIST\-EXTENDED and LIST\-STATUS, STATUS=SIZE, QUOTA, SORT (with DISPLAYFROM and
DISPLAYTO), THREAD (ORDEREDSUBJECT, REFERENCES), WITHIN (OLDER and YOUNGER),
SAVEDATE, PREVIEW and COMPRESS=DEFLATE, what a
loop turn says compressed as one flushed block, on the mailboxes of
.BR \-\-storage ;
a password goes over STARTTLS or
.B \-\-imaps
only (LOGINDISABLED before), except from 127.0.0.1. Every mailbox has INBOX,
Sent, Drafts, Junk and Trash, and CREATE makes more, with the folders above a
name such as Work/2026; a client sets keywords of its own beside the system
flags. A folder's UIDs, flags and keywords are kept per user in
.IB state /mail/ name .imap/ user ,
a snapshot and a journal beside it: every shard serves IMAP, a change is a line
appended under the user's lock once the journal is read to its end, so a UID is
given once whichever shard gives it, and each shard reads the lines it has not
before each command. The journal is folded into the snapshot once it passes a
quarter of a mebibyte. A delivery, an APPEND and a COPY write their line as
the message is stored, so IDLE is told of new mail within a second and the
quota counts it at once. The bucket stays what counts: listed when a folder is
selected, and every minute under NOOP and IDLE, what went from it is expunged,
and a message no line names is given the next UID once it is a minute old. Every change of a message has the folder's next
mod\-sequence, so a client that reconnects asks for what changed and vanished
since it last looked. SEARCH, SORT and THREAD, like JMAP's text, body and
header filters, read a message's header as written and with its encoded words
decoded and its text parts as written and with their transfer encoding and
charset undone, not its attachments. A filter of each message's trigrams, made
as a delivery or an APPEND stores it (or, for one over a mebibyte delivered,
the first time a search reads it), as large as the different trigrams it holds
and kept in
.IB state /mail/ name .imap/ user .fts
for every shard, rules out what cannot hold the string asked for, so a search
reads only the messages that may. A user's
.B mail_quota
field in the idp runtime
.RB ( "socketley idp\-user" " ... " "\-\-set mail_quota=500M" )
bounds what their mailboxes hold: IMAP tells it as one quota root over every
folder, an APPEND or a COPY past it is refused OVERQUOTA, and a delivery past it
is refused 552 5.2.2, as Dovecot refuses one; a user with no
.B mail_quota
has
.BR \-\-default\-quota .
A user whose mailboxes pass
.B \-\-quota\-warn
percent of their quota (90 unless it says) is mailed a warning once, and again
only after they fell below it. All of a runtime's mailboxes together, every
domain's, are bounded by the quota of their bucket (PutBucketQuota on the
storage runtime): a delivery past it waits, 452 4.2.2, until room is made, and a
bound for each domain is a mail runtime for each, on a bucket of its own. IMAP also offers MULTIAPPEND, which puts every
message it is given in or none, UTF8=ACCEPT, under which folder names come and
go as UTF\-8 and a message may be a literal8, CREATE\-SPECIAL\-USE, which takes
a special use only of the folder whose name has it, BINARY, which gives a part
with its transfer encoding undone, OBJECTID, whose MAILBOXID a RENAME keeps,
METADATA (RFC 5464), entries of the server and of each folder kept in the
index, at most 1000 a user of 64 KiB each, NOTIFY (RFC 5465), which tells a
session of new mail, expunges and flags in the folders it watches as STATUS,
of folders made and gone as LIST, and of the selected folder between commands,
within a second, ACL (RFC 4314), by which a user gives others rights on a
folder, seen by them under
.BI "Other Users/" owner /
with what those rights allow (l r s w i k x t e a), and APPENDLIMIT; RENAME
takes the folders under a name along, and RENAME INBOX moves its messages and
leaves it empty. A connection quiet for thirty minutes is closed.
.PP
.B JMAP.
.B /.well\-known/jmap
is the session; the API at
.B /jmap/api
takes Core/echo, Mailbox/get, query, set and changes, Email/get, query, set,
import and changes, Thread/get with every Email of a conversation in any
mailbox, Quota/get, query and changes (RFC 9425) with the octets of the
user's quota, Identity/get and set, and EmailSubmission/set with its
onSuccessUpdateEmail and onSuccessDestroyEmail, back\-references among them;
.B /jmap/upload
and
.B /jmap/download
move blobs, and
.B /jmap/eventsource
tells of state changes. A user signs in with HTTP Basic, their idp password,
over TLS or from 127.0.0.1, or with an idp bearer token. Their account is the
mailboxes IMAP reads: a Mailbox id is its MAILBOXID and an Email id its EMAILID,
which a move keeps, a Thread the conversation the Message-ID its References
begin with names, which collapseThreads folds a query by, a keyword an IMAP flag, so a
client of either sees what the other did at once. A state is a digest of the
index, the same on every shard, and a */changes since another answers
cannotCalculateChanges, after which a client reads again. An upload is kept a
day; an EmailSubmission sends as the user's own address only. Email/parse reads
a blob, an upload or an attached message, as an Email. VacationResponse, kept
in the user's METADATA, answers what their mailbox takes, once a week a
sender, never a list, a bounce, spam or what answers itself (RFC 3834), whether
or not a Sieve script runs. A PushSubscription (at most 50 a user, a week each
until renewed) is verified by the code pushed to its URL, then sent a
StateChange whenever the types it asked for change, over HTTPS to a public
address, encrypted where it gave keys (RFC 8291).
.PP
.B Calendars and contacts.
On the same ports, CalDAV (RFC 4791) and CardDAV (RFC 6352) for every user:
.B /.well\-known/caldav
and
.B /.well\-known/carddav
lead to
.BR /dav/ ,
whose principal names the user's calendars under
.IB /dav/ user /calendars/
and address books under
.IB /dav/ user /contacts/\fR,
.B personal
and
.B default
there for every user and more made with MKCALENDAR or MKCOL. A client puts,
reads and deletes each event, task or card whole, under If-Match and
If-None-Match; PROPPATCH keeps a collection's name, colour and description;
the multiget and query REPORTs, a query's component filter among them, and
sync-collection (RFC 6578) answer. The entries are objects of the
.B \-\-storage
bucket under
.IB .dav/ user /\fR,
apart from the mail folders, and what changed in a collection is a line of a
journal under
.IB state /mail/ name .dav/\fR,
whose length is its sync token. Through the entrance a user signs in as for
JMAP; Thunderbird's autoconfig, the Apple profile and
.B _caldavs._tcp
and
.B _carddavs._tcp
in the zone point a client there.
.PP
.B Webmail.
With
.B \-\-web
and
.B \-\-domain
the entrance serves a mail client in the browser under the name, in the look
of the storage interface: the mailboxes with their unread counts and a starred
view, the conversations a page at a time, each read as its messages from every
mailbox with what they quote folded away, a search over all of them, reading,
writing, replying and forwarding with attachments, drafts saved as they are
written, moving, archiving, flags and deletion, a whole folder selected or
emptied, a message's headers and its print, a send undone within seconds or
put off until a time, plain text or formatted, a sender of the user's
addresses, the one a message was sent to for its reply, a name, a signature,
an out-of-office answer between two dates, filters for arriving mail as a
Sieve script, and new mail as it comes, told in the background where the
browser lets it. It speaks German where the browser asks for it, English
otherwise. It signs in through the idp runtime, whose session cookie under its
.B \-\-cookie\-domain
signs the user in to every runtime of it at once, and sends a page not signed
in to the idp's
.BR /login .
A change the cookie alone signs carries the page's
.B X\-Socketley\-UI
header, which another site cannot make a browser send, and an answer to the
cookie is readable by no other origin. A message's HTML is cleaned of what
runs, submits or reaches out, and shown in a frame sandboxed without scripts;
its remote images wait until the reader asks for them, for that message or
for its sender from then on. The page's one script runs by its hash alone. On
the same port a JMAP client signs in as on
.BR \-\-jmap ,
with a password only where the entrance forwarded HTTPS, and a client that
fails ten times is locked out by the address the entrance forwards for it.
.RS
.PP
.nf
socketley create mail m \-p 25 \-\-dns ns \-\-idp auth \-\-storage files/mail \e
    \-\-imaps 993 \-\-submissions 465 \-\-tls \-\-cert mail.pem \-\-key mail.key \e
    \-\-web 9480 \-\-domain mail \-s
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B From the other runtimes.
.B socketley send
and a script's
.B socketley.mail.send
write and queue a message; a storage's
.BI mail:// runtime / address
notification target mails its events; and
.B daemon.alert_mail
mails the daemon's alerts.
.RS
.PP
.nf
local id, err = socketley.mail.send("m", { to = "ops@example.com",
    subject = "order " .. order.id, text = "paid",
    attachments = { { name = "invoice.pdf", type = "application/pdf", data = pdf } } })
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B Sieve.
A user files their own mail with Sieve (RFC 5228) and its extensions fileinto,
reject, ereject, envelope, encoded\-character, imap4flags, copy, vacation,
vacation\-seconds, subaddress, body, relational, comparator\-i;ascii\-numeric,
variables, date, index, mailbox, regex, editheader, duplicate, include,
environment, spamtest, spamtestplus, virustest, enotify (mailto) and ihave.
Their scripts are put over ManageSieve (RFC 5804)
on
.B \-\-sieve
(STARTTLS, and SASL PLAIN with the password IMAP takes) or with
.BR "socketley mail" " ... " sieve ,
compiled as they are put, at most 32 of 64 KiB and 1 MiB in all, and kept
under the runtime's state for every shard; one is made active only where it
compiles with what it includes. The active one is run on a thread for each
message that reaches the user's mailbox, the whole message what it reads: keep
and fileinto file it, with the imap4flags' flags IMAP shows, fileinto into a
folder not there only with :create (else into INBOX); discard keeps nothing;
reject bounces it to its sender; redirect sends it on from the user's own
address, never again if it comes back; vacation answers a sender once in its
days or seconds, an hour at least, from the null sender; notify mails a mailto
URI, auto\-notified, from the null sender, at most three times a message.
What editheader adds or deletes is in what is kept and sent on, the Received
and Auto\-Submitted headers excepted. duplicate keeps a message's ID twelve
hours unless :seconds says, a week at most; it and vacation remember at most
10,000 per user. include :global reads the scripts put for the user
.B .global
.RB ( "socketley mail" " m " "sieve .global put" " name file ),
include :personal the user's own. environment tells host, domain,
remote\-ip, location MDA, phase during, name and version; spamtest and
virustest are 0 until a scanner says more. A script that fails keeps the
message in INBOX.
.IP
imapsieve (RFC 6785): a folder whose
.B /shared/imapsieve/script
entry names one of the user's scripts, set by SETMETADATA, has it run for each
message a client APPENDs or COPYs or MOVEs into it, and for each whose flags a
STORE changes there; environment's imap.cause, imap.mailbox, imap.user,
imap.email and imap.changedflags say what was done, and imap4flags starts from
the message's flags. keep leaves it with the flags the script left, fileinto
files a copy, redirect sends one on, and where nothing keeps it the message
leaves the folder once its copies are in; reject is an error and vacation does
nothing. What its own fileinto puts elsewhere runs no script there.
.PP
.B Lua.
A script of the runtime decides each recipient and is told each message:
.RS
.PP
.nf
function on_rcpt(r, default)          -- r.to, r.from, r.peer, r.user
    if r.to == "orders@example.com" then return true end   -- the script's alone
    if r.from:match("@spam%.test$") then return false end  -- refused 550
end                                   -- nil keeps the default
function on_message(m)                -- m.id, m.from, m.to, m.size, m.subject, m.message, m.peer, m.user
    socketley.mqtt.publish("bus", "mail/in", m.subject or "", { qos = 1 })
end
.fi
.RE
.IP
A recipient
.B on_rcpt
takes that no mailbox or alias has is the script's: its message reaches
.B on_message
and nowhere else.
.B m.message
is the whole message where it is under a mebibyte.
.TP
.BI "\-\-mail\-domain " domain\fR[,\fIdomain\fR...]
The domains whose mail it takes.
.TP
.BI "\-\-dns " runtime
The dns runtime whose zones are its domains, given their records.
.TP
.BI "\-\-hostname " name
The name it greets with and its MX records name, mail.\fIfirst domain\fR unless given.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-reply
Send from no\-reply@ where no sender is said, take no mail there but bounces,
and learn from those.
.TP
.BI "\-\-idp " runtime
The idp runtime whose users have mailboxes here and sign in to send and read.
.TP
.BI "\-\-idp\-audience " clients
The ids of the idp's clients whose access tokens it takes, comma-separated. A
token the provider gave any other application is refused, since it is that
application's and no key to this runtime; without the flag no access token is
taken, while cookies and passwords sign in as before.
.TP
.BI "\-\-storage " runtime\fR[/\fIbucket\fR]
The storage runtime, and bucket, the mailboxes are kept in.
.TP
.BI "\-\-users " file
Accounts that sign in to send, a name and a scrypt hash a line as
.B socketley mqtt\-passwd
writes them, read again when it changes.
.TP
.BI "\-\-aliases " file
Addresses taken besides the users',
.IR "address target" [, target ...]
a line, a target a mailbox (a name) or an address mail is sent on to, and
.BI *@ domain
any local part of a domain that names none of its own; read again when it
changes.
.TP
.BI "\-\-relay " smtp\fR[\fBs\fR]\fB://\fR[\fIuser\fB:\fIpassword\fB@\fR]\fIhost\fR[\fB:\fIport\fR]
A smarthost every message for elsewhere goes through; smtps:// speaks TLS from
the first byte (465), smtp:// STARTTLS (587).
.TP
.BI "\-\-submission " port
A port users send from with STARTTLS and a password; it needs
.B \-\-tls
and
.B \-\-users
or
.BR \-\-idp .
.TP
.BI "\-\-submissions " port
A port users send from over TLS from the first byte; it needs
.BR \-\-tls .
.TP
.BI "\-\-imap " port
.TQ
.BI "\-\-imaps " port
A port mail clients read the mailboxes on, with STARTTLS or over TLS from the
first byte; they need
.BR \-\-storage .
.TP
.BI "\-\-notify\-targets " file
Where each message stored in a mailbox is told, in the form a storage runtime's
file takes.
.TP
.BI "\-\-max\-size " size
The largest message taken, 1K to 1G, 25M unless given.
.TP
.BI "\-\-max\-recipients " n
The most recipients of one message, 100 unless given; past them a RCPT is
answered 452.
.TP
.BI "\-\-queue\-lifetime " duration
How long a message is tried before it is bounced, 1m to 30d, 5d unless given.
.TP
.BI "\-\-autoexpunge " duration
How long a message stays in a user's Junk or Trash, from when it was put there,
before it is expunged, 1h to 3650d; never unless given. The first shard sweeps
every user it has met hourly.
.TP
.BI "\-\-dkim\-selector " name
The selector its DKIM key is published and signed under, skl unless given; its
Ed25519 key is
.IR name \-ed .
.TP
.BI "\-\-dkim\-next " name
A selector whose keys are made and published, not yet signed with, for a key's
rotation.
.TP
.BI "\-\-arc\-trust " domain\fR[,\fIdomain\fR...]
Forwarders, such as a mailing list's domain, whose ARC seal of a chain that
passes lets mail through that fails DMARC because they forwarded it.
.TP
.BI "\-\-sieve " port
ManageSieve (RFC 5804), for the users' Sieve scripts; needs
.B \-\-idp
and
.BR \-\-storage .
.TP
.BI "\-\-pop3 " port
POP3, STLS on it; needs
.B \-\-idp
and
.BR \-\-storage .
.TP
.BI "\-\-pop3s " port
POP3 over TLS from the first byte.
.TP
.BI "\-\-jmap " port
JMAP for the mailboxes, HTTPS with
.BR \-\-tls ;
needs
.B \-\-idp
and
.BR \-\-storage .
.TP
.BI "\-\-web " port
The webmail, and JMAP beside it, in plain HTTP on 127.0.0.1 for the entrance in
front of it, which takes the TLS (see
.BR Webmail );
needs
.B \-\-idp
and
.BR \-\-storage .
.TP
.BI "\-\-domain " name\fR[,\fIname\fR...]
The names the entrance of a dns runtime with
.B \-\-proxy
reaches the webmail under, a name without a dot under each of its zones, as
.B webmail
or
.BR mail ;
needs
.BR \-\-web .
Under each of its zones the webmail also answers
.B autoconfig
and
.BR autodiscover ,
where mail clients ask how a mailbox is reached: Thunderbird's
.B /mail/config\-v1.1.xml
(and
.BR /.well\-known/autoconfig/ ),
Outlook's
.B /autodiscover/autodiscover.xml
and the JSON lookup that points at it, with
.B _autodiscover._tcp
in the zone; each names its host, the ports it opens with TLS, and the address
as the login, and an address of no domain it keeps is answered 404. A user
signed in to the webmail finds the same under Settings, and a profile that sets
up an iPhone, an iPad or a Mac at once
.RB ( /~mail/mobileconfig ).
.TP
.BI "\-\-lmtp " port
LMTP for this machine's own programs, into the mailboxes only; a connection
from elsewhere is closed.
.TP
.BI "\-\-mta\-sts " enforce\fR|\fBtesting
Publish an MTA\-STS policy (RFC 8461) of that mode naming its host.
.TP
.BI "\-\-mta\-sts\-port " port
Serve the policy over HTTPS on this port, with the certificate of
.BR \-\-tls ,
which the daemon's ACME asks for
.BI mta\-sts. domain
too; senders fetch it on 443.
.TP
.BI "\-\-tls\-rpt " address
Publish where TLS reports (RFC 8460) about its domains go.
.TP
.BI "\-\-dmarc\-rua " address
Publish where DMARC aggregate reports about its domains go.
.TP
.BI "\-\-dmarc\-policy " none\fR|\fBquarantine\fR|\fBreject
What its DMARC record asks of mail that claims its domains and fails SPF and
DKIM; quarantine unless given, reject once the reports show only its own mail
passes.
.TP
.BI "\-\-bimi " https://logo.svg\fR[,\fIhttps://certificate.pem\fR]
Publish a BIMI record, its SVG logo, and the mark certificate the providers
that show a logo beside a message (Gmail among them) ask for; a domain DMARC
quarantines or rejects is shown.
.TP
.B \-\-reports
Send the DMARC and TLS reports other domains ask for.
.TP
.BI "\-\-greylist " duration
Greylist strangers (RFC 6647) for this long, such as 5m; 0 for none.
.TP
.BI "\-\-refuse\-attachments " ext\fR[,\fIext\fR...]|\fBdefault\fR|\fBnone
Mail from elsewhere with an attachment of one of these extensions is refused,
554, its name in the answer: unless given, programs, scripts, shortcuts, disk
images and Office documents with macros (exe, scr, js, vbs, ps1, lnk, iso,
docm, xlsm and the rest of Gmail's list).
.TP
.BI "\-\-clamav " host:port\fR|\fIsocket
The clamd every message from elsewhere is streamed to (INSTREAM) before it is
taken; one it names a virus in is refused with that name, and one it does not
answer for is taken and logged, as Rspamd's silence is.
.TP
.BI "\-\-rspamd " url
The Rspamd that scans mail from elsewhere, such as http://127.0.0.1:11333.
Without it, or where it did not answer, mail is scored by the runtime's own
filter, which learns from the users: a message moved into Junk is spam, and one
moved out of it, or filed from the INBOX into a folder of their own, is not,
over IMAP, JMAP and the webmail's Report junk and Not junk alike. Its words are
those of the subject, the text and the HTML's text, the From's domain and the
links' hosts, weighed as SpamBayes does (Robinson's chi\-square of Fisher); a
message is learned once, and one said otherwise is learned again. Once it has
learned 20 of each, every message is given its score as x\-spam\-bayes, and one
of 0.9 or more is put in Junk; nothing is refused for it.
.B "socketley mail learn"
.IB user / folder
.B \-\-as spam\fR|\fBham
teaches it a folder at once, and
.B "socketley stats"
says how much it learned
.RB ( mail_bayes_spam ", " mail_bayes_ham )
and how much it put in Junk
.RB ( mail_bayes_junked ).
.TP
.BI "\-\-recipient\-delimiter " chars\fR|\fBnone
What divides a user from a tag in an address, + unless it says.
.TP
.BI "\-\-default\-quota " size
The quota of a user whose idp account names none; 0 for no bound.
.TP
.BI "\-\-quota\-warn " percent
How full a user's mailboxes get before they are told, 90 unless it says; 0 for
never.
.TP
.BI "\-\-dnsbl " zone\fR[,\fIzone\fR...]
DNS blocklists (RFC 5782), such as zen.spamhaus.org: a stranger's address is
asked under each as it connects, and MAIL from one a list names is refused with
554.
.PP
.B stats
says what was received, delivered, deferred, failed and bounced, the other
delivery reports, what is queued and its bytes, the spool's commits, segments
and moves, the recipients and relays refused, what was too big, the spool's
failures, what was checked and what DMARC refused or quarantined, the bounces
taken and the addresses suppressed, what was stored in mailboxes, the sign\-ins
and their failures, STARTTLS and TLS failures, the connections out, their TLS
and transactions, the certificates DANE and MTA\-STS checked and the
connections refused for want of TLS, the policies fetched and what testing ones
would have failed, what Sieve rejected, sent on and answered on vacation, the
MX lookups, what the spam filter refused and filed in Junk, what was
greylisted, the reports sent, and the IMAP connections and reads.
.SH TUNNEL FLAGS
A
.B tunnel
runtime makes a machine that has no address of its own reachable \(em one at
home behind a router, behind carrier-grade NAT, on a network that lets only
port 443 out. The machine runs an
.B agent
that dials out to an
.BR edge ,
a tunnel runtime on a machine the internet reaches, and holds that one
connection open; the edge serves the agent's services under its own names and
carries each visitor through the connection the agent made. Nothing is opened
on the router.
.PP
.nf
# on the machine with the address
socketley create tunnel edge \-\-mode edge \-p 9450 \-\-domain edge \-\-idp auth \-s
# on the machine at home, signed in as an admin of auth
socketley tunnel join https://edge.example.com \-\-http home=127.0.0.1:8123 \-\-tcp mc:25565=127.0.0.1:25565
.fi
.PP
An HTTP service is named beside the edge, here
.BR https://home.example.com :
the edge serves it on a socket the entrance routes that name to, with the
entrance's certificate, and the dns runtime answers the name, once the agent
has joined. A name a runtime has stays that runtime's, another runtime is not
given a name a service has, and of two edges with a service of one name the
first by name keeps it. An edge named as a whole domain has no name beside
it, and its services none. A TCP service is a port on the edge, here 25565. The edge says
nothing about a stream it carries: what reaches the agent is what the visitor
sent, and the service answers as if they were on the same machine.
.PP
.B Who may visit.
An HTTP service invited as
.IB name = auth
lets through only the requests
.I auth
admits, with what a proxy's
.B \-\-auth
takes:
.BI idp: runtime
sends a browser to sign in and back,
.BI bearer: file
and
.BI basic: file
ask for a token or a key, and several joined by
.B +
let in a visitor any one of them admits:
.B idp:auth+bearer:/etc/socketley/hub.token
takes a person signed in and a program with the token alike. Every request is asked, not only a connection's
first, since the entrance hands one connection to the edge from visitor to
visitor; nothing of a refused request reaches the agent, a body is carried as
a body and never read as the next request, and a connection whose upgrade the
service refused ends with that answer. A TCP service is the agent's to guard.
.PP
Each connection is a multiplexed link (one TLS connection, many streams, each
with its own credit so one slow reader holds back only its own stream), opened
by an HTTP upgrade at
.B /.tunnel
that passes the entrance like any other. An agent pings every 15 seconds; a
link silent for 45 is given up and the agent dials again, after a pause that
doubles to half a minute.
.PP
.B Who may connect.
.B socketley tunnel join
is an admin of the edge's
.B \-\-idp
adding an agent; or, without an idp,
.B socketley tunnel
.I edge
.B invite
on the edge's machine prints an invitation once, for the agent's
.BR \-\-join ; the agent joins with it at its first connection and
is given a key of its own in its place, kept in its state directory (0600),
and the invitation is spent. The edge keeps only hashes.
.B revoke
cuts an agent off within a second;
.B set
changes what it offers and keeps its key.
.PP
.B People.
An edge with
.B \-\-forward
carries a person signed in at its
.B \-\-idp
to the targets it names, and to nothing else, as
.B socketley tunnel connect
or
.B tunnel ssh
asks; this is how ssh reaches a server through port 443:
.RS
.PP
.nf
Host server
    ProxyCommand socketley tunnel ssh edge.example.com
.fi
.RE
.TP
.BR "\-\-mode edge" | agent
Which end this is; an edge unless told.
.TP
.BI "\-p " port
An edge's port for agents and people, on 127.0.0.1 unless
.B \-\-bind
says otherwise, since the entrance in front of it terminates TLS.
.TP
.BI "\-\-domain " name
An edge's name, beside which its agents' HTTP services are named:
.B home.example.com
for an edge
.BR edge.example.com .
.TP
.BI "\-\-to " url
The edge an agent dials,
.BI https:// host
.RI [: port ];
.B http://
only for one on the same machine.
.TP
.BI "\-\-join " invitation
What
.B invite
printed, used once.
.TP
.BI "\-\-offer " service = host : port ,...
What an agent carries the edge's streams to, by the service names the edge
invited it with; a name it does not offer is refused at the agent.
.TP
.BI "\-\-forward " host : port ,...
Where an edge carries a signed-in person's stream.
.TP
.BI "\-\-idp " idp
The idp runtime whose people may use
.BR \-\-forward ,
and whose admins add agents with
.BR "tunnel join" .
.TP
.BI "\-\-idp\-audience " client ,...
The clients whose access tokens are taken;
.B socketley
(the client
.B "socketley login"
signs in as) when none is named.
.PP
A tunnel takes no
.BR \-\-shards :
each agent's link is held by one process.
.SH REGISTERED TYPES
A runtime type beyond the built-in seven is delivered as a manifest plus a
worker binary. The daemon reads the manifest \(em a declarative file, never
code \(em to learn the type's name, its worker executable, and the schema of
its configuration keys; the behaviour lives in the binary, which the daemon
forks and execs exactly as it does its own workers. No third-party code ever
runs in the daemon's process.
.PP
Manifests are read at daemon startup from
.B /etc/socketley/types/*.toml
for a system daemon and
.B ~/.config/socketley/types/*.toml
for a development daemon:
.PP
.RS
.nf
name = "greeter"
exec = "/usr/local/lib/socketley/greeter-worker"
manifest_version = 1

[config.greeting]
kind = "string"        # string | int | bool | enum
required = true

[config.repeat]
kind = "int"
default = "1"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Each
.BI [config. key ]
section becomes a
.BI \-\- key
flag on
.B create
and
.BR edit ,
is validated against its kind, and is persisted under an
.B extra
object in the state file, which
.B show
prints and
.B import
accepts. A key the manifest does not declare is refused by name, exactly as a
misspelled built-in flag is. A required key must be given at
.BR create ;
a declared default is filled in when the key is omitted. An edit takes effect
at the next start, because the worker reads its configuration once, when it is
spawned.
.PP
The worker binary is built with the SDK's
.B <socketley/worker.h>
\(em
.B register_type
with a factory for the type, then
.B run(argc, argv)
\(em and speaks the same versioned control-socket protocol as the daemon's own
workers, so it survives daemon upgrades that do not change frame meanings. A
worked example is
.BR sdk/examples/custom\-type/ .
.PP
A registered type cannot be created from a runtime's Lua script, and a state
file whose type has no manifest on the next boot is left on disk and not
restored, with the reason in the daemon log.
.SH LUA CONFIGURATION
.B "Two different files are both called \(lqa Lua script\(rq, and they do not mix."
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
A
.I daemon configuration
\(em
.BR "socketley \-\-lua config.lua" .
This runs in the CLI process, is read for its
.B daemon
and
.B runtimes
tables, brings the daemon to what they say and then exits; the daemon reads the
same tables from its own configuration file when it starts. It never becomes
part of any runtime. A callback defined in such a file is dead code: nothing loads it,
nothing calls it, and nothing says so.
.IP \(bu 2
A
.I runtime handler
\(em
.BR "socketley create server api \-p 9000 \-\-lua handler.lua" ,
or the same path given as a runtime's
.B config
key inside a configuration. This one is loaded by the daemon into that runtime's
own interpreter, and it is the only file whose
.BI on_ *
callbacks,
.B filters
and
.B routes
mean anything.
.RE
.PP
A configuration attaches a handler by naming its path, never by defining the
callbacks itself:
.nf

runtimes = {
    { type = "server", name = "api", port = 9000,
      config = "handler.lua",     \-\- the file whose callbacks run
      start = true },
}
.fi
.PP
.B config
and
.B lua
are accepted as spellings of the same key. Everything below this point in this
chapter, except
.B "Configuration File Format"
itself, describes a
.IR "runtime handler" .
.PP
.B Module loading:
The Lua
.B require()
function resolves modules relative to the script's directory.
For a script at
.IR /home/user/handler.lua ,
.B require("utils")
loads
.IR /home/user/utils.lua .
The default Lua package path is appended, so standard modules still work.
.PP
.B Standard library:
.BR base ", " string ", " table ", " math ", " os ", " io " and " package
are open. Six
.B os
functions are removed, because a daemon serving every runtime from one process
cannot let one script reach outside its own runtime:
.BR os.execute ,
.BR os.remove ,
.BR os.rename ,
.BR os.tmpname ,
.B os.exit
and
.BR os.setlocale .
The last of those is not a filesystem or process call and is removed for a
reason of its own:
.B setlocale
is process-wide, and
.B socketley.json_encode
formats numbers with
.B %.14g
while
.B socketley.json_decode
parses them with
.BR strtod ,
both of which read
.BR LC_NUMERIC .
One script switching to a comma-decimal locale therefore makes
.I every
runtime in the daemon emit
.B 1,5
and truncate
.B 1.5
to
.BR 1 ,
for as long as the daemon lives.
.B os.time,
.BR os.clock ,
.B os.date
and
.B os.difftime
stay \(em those are reads.
.SS Configuration File Format
.nf
\-\- Optional: configure the daemon itself
daemon = {
    name = "node1",
    cluster = "/shared/cluster"
}

runtimes = {
    { type = "server", name = "api", port = 9000, group = "api",
      config = "handler.lua", start = true },
    { type = "client", name = "cli", port = 9000, target = "127.0.0.1:9000" },
    { type = "proxy",  name = "gw", port = 8080,
      backends = {"@api", "127.0.0.1:9000"}, strategy = "round-robin" },
    { type = "cache",  name = "store", port = 9001,
      persistent = "/data/store.bin" }
}
.fi
.PP
The
.B daemon
table is processed first (name, then cluster). If omitted, the daemon
runs without cluster mode.
.PP
Its keys \(em
.BR name ", " cluster ", " log_level ", " metrics_port " and " cpus
\(em are read by the daemon itself from the file
.B SOCKETLEY_CONFIG
names, at startup. The last three of those are settled before the ring exists
and cannot be told to a daemon that is already serving, so
.B "socketley \-\-lua"
refuses them by name rather than dropping them \(em except in the daemon's own
file, where they are its next start's. A flag typed on
.B "socketley daemon"
outranks the same setting in the file.
.B config
is the table's older spelling and still works; naming both in one file is
refused.
.PP
Recognised
.B runtimes
keys, and the flag each becomes. Every flag a
.B create
accepts is reachable from here, which is what makes a configuration file and a
command line two spellings of one thing rather than two capabilities:
.nf
\(em\(em every type \(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em
type, name                 (required)
port                       \-p
listen                     \-\-listen
shards                     \-\-shards
log                        \-\-log
write                      \-w
group                      \-g
cpus                       \-\-cpus
max_connections            \-\-max\-connections
rate_limit                 \-\-rate\-limit
global_rate_limit          \-\-global\-rate\-limit
idle_timeout               \-\-idle\-timeout  300, or 5m / 2h / 1d
write_buffer               \-\-write\-buffer   8M, 256K, 65536
read_buffer                \-\-read\-buffer    1M, 8M, 65536
drain (bool)               \-\-drain
test (bool)                \-\-test
tls (bool)                 \-\-tls
cert, key, ca              \-\-cert / \-\-key / \-\-ca
reconnect (bool or n)      \-\-reconnect     true keeps trying
config / lua               \-\-lua           the runtime's handler script
bash                       \-b / \-bp / \-bt / \-bpt
start (bool)               \-s
\(em\(em server \(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em
mode                       \-\-mode
udp (bool)                 \-\-udp
http                       \-\-http
http_cache (bool)          \-\-http\-cache
master_pw                  \-\-master\-pw
send_to (string)           \-\-send\-to
cache                      \-\-cache
upstreams (array)          \-\-upstream a;b;c
\(em\(em client \(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em
target                     \-t
mode                       \-\-mode
udp (bool)                 \-\-udp
\(em\(em proxy \(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em
backends (array)           \-\-backend a,b,c
protocol                   \-\-protocol
strategy                   \-\-strategy
health_check               \-\-health\-check
health_interval            \-\-health\-interval  30, or 5m / 2h
health_path                \-\-health\-path
health_threshold           \-\-health\-threshold
circuit_threshold          \-\-circuit\-threshold
circuit_timeout            \-\-circuit\-timeout  30, or 5m / 2h
backend_timeout            \-\-backend\-timeout  30, or 5m / 2h
retry                      \-\-retry
retry_all (bool)           \-\-retry\-all
client_ca                  \-\-client\-ca
client_cert                \-\-client\-cert
client_key                 \-\-client\-key
sidecar (bool)             \-\-sidecar
\(em\(em cache \(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em
persistent                 \-\-persistent
persistent_interval        \-\-persistent\-interval     5m, 30s, 1h
maxmemory                  \-\-maxmemory     256M, or a byte count
eviction                   \-\-eviction
resp (bool)                \-\-resp
replicate                  \-\-replicate
cluster (bool)             \-\-cluster
.fi
.PP
Every other flag of a type is the key of its own name, its dashes gone and a
hyphen an underscore:
.B \-\-mail\-domain
is
.BR mail_domain ,
.B \-\-submission
is
.BR submission ,
.B \-\-queue\-lifetime
is
.BR queue_lifetime .
A list key takes a table, or one string for the list of one. A key no flag of
the type has is refused, naming it, rather than dropped in silence: a misspelled
.B http_cache
used to configure nothing while the runtime came up looking correct. A value of
the wrong shape is refused the same way, and so is a value containing a tab
\(em the daemon's command line splits on it, so a password with one in it
would arrive as two arguments. A space is carried inside its value, as it is
inside a quoted argument on the command line.
.PP
A key the daemon then refuses on that type \(em
.B \-\-client\-ca
on a cleartext proxy, say \(em is refused by the daemon, in the daemon's own
words, under the name of the runtime it belongs to. The remaining entries are
still applied, and the exit code is 1.
.PP
.B daemon_process
was in this list and is gone with the flag it emitted. A configuration file
still carrying it is refused, naming the line, rather than accepted with the key
quietly dropped \(em which would leave an operator believing a runtime is in the
daemon while it is not.
.PP
Nothing replaces it. The whole cross\-runtime API works in a worker:
.BR socketley.send ,
.BR .list ,
.B .get
and
.B socketley.cluster
are answered from the replica map the daemon pushes;
.BR socketley.create ,
.BR .start ,
.BR .stop
and
.B .remove
are asked of the daemon and wait for its answer; and
.B socketley.pipe
and
.B self.route
reach a runtime in another process. See
.B "Process Isolation"
above.
.PP
The health\-check, circuit\-breaker, retry and TLS families were unreachable
from a configuration until the table above named every flag; a file written
against that build still reads the same way.
.PP
The interpreter that reads this file is not the daemon's, and it is much
smaller. It opens
.BR base ,
.BR package ,
.BR table ,
.B string
and
.B math
\(em there is no
.B os
and no
.BR io .
.RB ` "os.execute(\(dqmkdir -p /tmp/logs\(dq)" `
at the top of a configuration is not a sandbox refusal but
.BR "attempt to index global 'os' (a nil value)" :
the whole file is refused, exit 1, and no runtime is created. Create the
directories a configuration needs from the shell that invokes it.
.B string
and
.B math
are open because a configuration computes names and port numbers, and the
daemon's own configuration file opens the same set, so one file can be read
by either.
.SS A machine's setup
A setup is the runtimes of a machine as Lua files, so that another machine
given the files is given the same runtimes; their data is not in them.
.B include
reads a file and is what it returns, or reads each
.B .lua
of a directory in the order of their names and is the list of what they return;
a list among the
.B runtimes
is its entries. A relative path, whether an include's or a value's \(em a
credentials file, a certificate, a handler script, a zone file \(em is one
beside the first file, wherever the command was typed:
.RS
.PP
.nf
\-\- /etc/socketley/config.lua
daemon = { metrics_port = 9190, dashboard_idp = "auth" }
runtimes = {
    include "runtimes/ns.lua",
    include "runtimes/auth.lua",
    include "runtimes/sites",            \-\- every .lua in it
}

\-\- /etc/socketley/runtimes/ns.lua
return { type = "dns", name = "ns", zone = { "zones/example.com.zone" },
         publish = { "203.0.113.7" }, start = true }
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B "socketley \-\-lua"
brings the daemon to what the files say. A runtime it does not have is made; one
it has is planned as
.B create
would make it from the file and compared with what
.B show
says of it, and where they differ it is told what differs \(em a setting the
file no longer says is cleared \(em across a restart where it runs, and asked
again, so a runtime that does not take a setting is named, exit 1. One that is
as the file says is not touched.
.B start
starts or stops it; a runtime without
.B start
is left running or stopped as it is. A runtime of the daemon's that the files do
not name is left alone.
.PP
The daemon reads the
.B runtimes
of its own configuration file when it starts, after the runtimes it kept, and
makes each it has not, started where
.B start
says so; one it has is left as it is, for
.B "socketley \-\-lua"
to bring to the file. A new machine therefore needs the files in
.B /etc/socketley
and nothing else run.
.B "socketley export"
writes a running machine's setup this way.
.SS Handler Privileges
.B "A Lua handler runs with the daemon's full privileges."
It is not sandboxed and there is no supported way to sandbox it. Read a
handler the way you would read a program you are about to run as the user the
daemon runs as, because that is exactly what it is.
.PP
Which makes the control socket's permission the whole of the boundary. A
handler is attached with
.BR "create \-\-lua" ,
which is an ordinary command on that socket, so whoever may open it may run
code as the daemon's user. The socket is
.B 0600
for a daemon you start yourself, and
.B "0660 socketley:socketley"
for the packaged one \(em membership of that group is therefore equivalent to
being the daemon's user, and should be granted on that understanding. This is
the same arrangement Docker has, and it is worth stating rather than inferring:
nothing else in this daemon stands between a local account and the handler.
.PP
Concretely, a handler can:
.TP
.B run any command
.B io.popen("...")
and
.B os.execute("...")
are both present.
.TP
.B read or write any file that user can
.B io.open()
is present in every mode, including
.BR w ,
which truncates.
.TP
.B load native code
.B package.loadlib()
is present, as are
.BR load (),
.BR loadstring (),
.B dofile()
and
.BR loadfile ().
.TP
.B read the daemon's environment
.B os.getenv()
is present.
.PP
This is deliberate. Handlers legitimately read and write files \(em a logging
pipeline appends to one, a configuration\-driven handler reads its table off
disk \(em so closing
.B io
would break supported use. What it means in practice is that
.B "the right to hand this daemon a \-\-lua script is the right to run code as its user,"
and the thing that controls that right is the permission on the IPC socket, not
anything in the interpreter. See
.B THE IPC SOCKET
under
.BR FILES .
.PP
A handler's interpreter opens
.BR base ,
.BR string ,
.BR table ,
.BR math ,
.BR os ,
.B io
and
.BR package .
Two names are removed from
.BR os ,
and calling one is
.B "attempt to call field '...' (a nil value)"
rather than a refusal with a reason:
.PP
.nf
os.exit    os.setlocale
.fi
.PP
Neither removal is a security boundary \(em nothing here is. The test both
pass is narrower: one call damages the process that every
.I other
runtime in this daemon shares, and does so by accident rather than by intent.
.B os.exit()
ends the daemon from inside a callback, and a handler is very often a
standalone script that grew into one, ending the way standalone scripts end.
.B os.setlocale()
changes the process's
.BR LC_NUMERIC ,
which is what
.B socketley.json_encode
and
.B socketley.json_decode
format and parse numbers with \(em one line in one handler and every other
handler's JSON spells 1.5 as 1,5 and decodes it as 1, silently, for as long as
the daemon lives.
.PP
.BR os.time (),
.BR os.clock (),
.BR os.date ()
and
.BR os.difftime ()
remain: they are reads, and reading the clock or the locale harms nothing.
.PP
Earlier releases also removed
.BR os.execute (),
.BR os.remove (),
.BR os.rename ()
and
.BR os.tmpname ().
Those four are back, because removing them denied no capability \(em
.B io.popen
is a shell and
.B io.open(path, \(dqw\(dq)
truncates \(em while suggesting to anyone reading a handler that the daemon
guarded its files and its shell. It did not. A fence with an open gate beside
it is worse than no fence, because people plan around it.
.PP
Note that
.B io.open
and every write through it block the event loop for as long as the write takes.
.PP
.B require()
resolves against the script's directory and then the default
.BR package.path ,
but
.B package.cpath
is left at its default and no binary module ships with socketley. A
.B require
of a C module \(em
.BR cjson ,
.BR lsqlite3 ,
a
.B luasql
driver \(em therefore fails unless that module is installed where the system Lua
would find it. For JSON, use the built\-in
.B socketley.json_encode
and
.B socketley.json_decode
instead; they need nothing installed.
.SS Available Callbacks
.TP
.B on_start()
Called when runtime starts.
.TP
.B on_stop()
Called when runtime stops.
.TP
.B on_message(msg)
Called when message received (server/client).
When defined on a server, this callback claims message handling: the default broadcast
(in \fBinout\fR and \fBmaster\fR modes) is suppressed. Use \fBself.broadcast(msg)\fR
to relay messages manually.
.TP
.B on_client_message(client_id, msg)
Called when message received with sender identification (server only).
Fires alongside on_message for backward compatibility.
.TP
.B on_http_body(client_id, chunk, remaining)
A piece of a request body, with how many bytes are still owed after it \(em so
.B remaining
reaches 0 exactly once per body. Defining this callback IS the opt\(hyin: a
server whose script has it streams request bodies to it instead of gathering
them, and a server whose script does not gets the behaviour it always had,
including the 413 past
.BR \-\-read\-buffer .
.IP
That is what makes a body larger than memory possible. The bytes are handed
over and erased as they arrive, so
.B req.body
in the
.B on_http_request
that follows is deliberately EMPTY \(em carrying them a second time is the
memory this exists to avoid. Measured on one worker, one connection at a time:
.IP
.nf
start             19,796 kB
after 50 MB #1    37,344 kB
after 50 MB #2    37,344 kB     unchanged
after 50 MB #3    37,344 kB     unchanged
after 200 MB      37,428 kB     +84 kB
after 500 MB      37,444 kB     +16 kB
.fi
.IP
The first upload costs 17.5 MB once, which is the provided\(hybuffer ring being
faulted in, and every one after it costs nothing: half a gigabyte received
inside 37 MB resident.
.IP
Keep per\(hyupload state keyed by
.BR client_id ,
and clear it in
.BR on_disconnect :
a body that is abandoned never reaches
.BR on_http_request ,
so nothing there can be the reset, and
.B on_disconnect
is the only signal it produces. It does fire, with whatever arrived, which is
what a script writing to a temporary file needs in order to remove it.
.IP
.nf
local st = {}
function on_http_body(id, chunk, remaining)
    st[id] = (st[id] or 0) + #chunk
    if remaining == 0 then ... end
end
function on_disconnect(id) st[id] = nil end
.fi
.IP
.B "A body of unknown length."
A client that cannot say how long its body is sends
.B "Transfer-Encoding: chunked"
\(em fetch() with a ReadableStream body, anything piping a process's output,
anything that cannot stat what it is sending \(em and that used to be 501. With
.B on_http_body
defined it is decoded and delivered like any other body, unbounded, because
there is no length to bound it by.
.IP
.B remaining
is
.B nil
for every chunk of such a body, since how much is still owed is genuinely not
knowable, and the end of the framing produces ONE final call with an empty
chunk and
.BR "remaining = 0" .
So "remaining reaches 0 exactly once per body" holds for both forms and a
script written against
.B Content-Length
needs no change. Measured, 62 MB in 1000 chunks: 1708 calls, 1707 of them nil
and one zero; 250 MB after that costs 148 kB more resident memory.
.IP
Chunked and nothing else.
.B "Transfer-Encoding: gzip, chunked"
is framable \(em the chunk sizes do delimit it \(em and its contents are still
compressed, so taking the framing off and handing the bytes on as the body would
hand on gzip and call it the body. That stays 501. A coding that is not chunked
at all leaves the message with no way to say where the body ends and is a 400.
.IP
Trailers are consumed, bounded at 8 KB, and discarded: turning them into
headers after the request has been dispatched is how a trailer becomes request
smuggling. Framing that contradicts itself \(em a chunk not followed by CRLF, a
size that is not hex, a size line past 256 bytes \(em is a 400, because nothing
after it can be trusted to be payload.
.IP
Header names keep the case the client sent them in, so
.B req.headers["Host"]
and not
.BR req.headers["host"] .
.TP
.B on_upstream_writable(conn_id)
An upstream's write queue has come back under
.BR self.watermark() ,
so a producer that stopped because
.B self.upstream_pending(conn_id)
was high can carry on. Its own callback and not
.BR on_writable ,
because an upstream id and a client id are different spaces and one function
taking both would have to be told which it had been given.
.TP
.B on_writable(client_id)
The write queue for this connection has come back down under
.BR self.watermark() ,
so a producer that stopped because
.B self.pending(client_id)
was high can carry on. Edge\(hytriggered: it fires on the crossing, once per
crossing, not once per completed write.
.IP
A client runtime has the same callback without the argument \(em
.B on_writable()
\(em because it has one connection and a number naming it would be a fiction.
Its refusal is named too: a connected
.B \-\-mode out
client whose server has stopped reading used to be told it "is not connected to
its server, or was created \-\-mode in and does not send", three statements
that are all false of it. Measured, 400 chunks of 64 KB into a peer reading
nothing: 129 taken, 271 refused, 8,396,928 bytes standing.
.IP
This is what makes a body larger than the queue bound sendable at all. Without
it a script had the hard bound and nothing leading up to it \(em at 8 MiB the
connection is closed \(em so the only safe strategy was to know the bound in
advance and never approach it. Measured with a client reading in 256 KB bites
with a 5 ms pause, sending 200 chunks of 64 KB (13,107,400 bytes):
.IP
.nf
unpaced   8,454,273 bytes delivered, then the connection closed
paced    13,107,400 bytes delivered, on_writable fired 3 times
.fi
.IP
Pace against
.B self.watermark()
and not a number of your own. It is a call and not a field because
.B \-\-write\-buffer
is editable, and a number copied into the script's table at load would go
stale the moment it moved. The callback fires on the crossing, so a producer
that stops below the watermark never crosses it and is never called back \(em it
would wait for an event its own caution prevented:
.IP
.nf
local function pump(id)
    while remaining > 0 do
        if (self.pending(id) or 0) >= self.watermark() then return end
        if not self.send(id, next_chunk()) then return end
        remaining = remaining \- 1
    end
end

function on_writable(id) pump(id) end
.fi
.IP
The call is made a loop turn after the write that emptied the queue, not on that
write's completion, so a connection that closes in between is simply not called.
.TP
.B on_connect(client_id)
Called when a client connects (server/client). Also called on a sub\-server when
a connection is routed to it. The client_id is opaque: for this runtime's own
clients it is the file descriptor, and for a connection routed here from another
runtime it is a handle above 16777216 that names the connection wherever it
lives. Pass it back to
.BR self.send() ,
.BR self.owner_send() ,
or anything else taking a client_id; do not compare it against a descriptor or
assume anything about its value.
.TP
.B on_disconnect(client_id)
Called when a client disconnects (server/client).
.TP
.B on_rcpt(rcpt, default) \(-> bool
A mail runtime's, deciding each recipient as it is said, and told each message
taken by
.BR on_message(msg) .
See
.B Lua
under
.BR "MAIL FLAGS" .
.TP
.B on_publish(client, msg) \(-> bool | table
.TQ
.B on_subscribe(client, filter, qos) \(-> bool
An mqtt runtime's, deciding what passes; there
.BR on_auth ,
.B on_connect
and
.B on_disconnect
are given a client table. See
.B Lua
under
.BR "MQTT FLAGS" .
.TP
.B on_send(msg)
Called when message sent.
.TP
.B on_route(method, path, backend_path)
Proxy only, and
.B "\-\-strategy lua"
only. Custom backend selection. Returns backend index (0-based).
Answers only "where does this go"; to accept or refuse a request before any
backend is chosen, see
.BR "Proxy Filter Chain" .
.IP
The strategy is what consults it. A proxy created without
.B "\-\-strategy lua"
keeps round\-robin (or
.BR random ,
if that was asked for) and never looks the callback up, so a script defining
.B on_route
on such a proxy is loaded, is reported by nothing, and routes exactly as if the
function were not there. That is the usual reason a
.B on_route
that was tested and works appears to do nothing.
.IP
.I path
is the path
.I as the client sent it,
this proxy's own
.BI / name /
prefix included, and
.I backend_path
is the same path with that prefix removed \(em what the backend will actually
receive. A proxy named
.B gw
handed
.B /gw/api/users
sees both:
.nf

function on_route(method, path, backend_path)
    -- path         == "/gw/api/users"
    -- backend_path == "/api/users"
    if backend_path:match("^/api/") then return 0 end
    return 1
end

.fi
Both are offered because both are the truth about a different thing: the filter
chain judges the request the client made, so it is handed
.IR path ,
and a routing decision is usually about the endpoint being addressed, which is
.IR backend_path .
.IP
.I backend_path
exists so that scripts stop computing it. Doing it by hand means stripping
.B "\(dq/\(dq .. self.name .. \(dq/\(dq"
and getting the bare case right, and the bare case is the one nobody gets
right: the gateway's own root arrives as
.B /gw
\(em three characters against a five-character prefix \(em and must become
.BR / ,
where a
.B sub()
past the end yields the empty string and matches no pattern at all.
.IP
The arguments are passed on
.B "\-\-protocol http"
only. A TCP proxy has no request line to read them from and calls
.B on_route
with none, so a body written for HTTP \(em
.RB ` "return hash(path) % n" `
\(em raises on the first request when it is moved to a TCP proxy.
.IP
A handler declaring two parameters is unaffected: Lua discards arguments a
function does not name, so every
.B on_route
written before this existed behaves exactly as it did.
.IP
A call that raises, or that returns anything other than an index inside the
backend list, selects nothing: the proxy reports the error on stderr and falls
back to the selection it would have made without the callback. The request is
forwarded either way. Refusing a request is the filter chain's job, not this
one's.
.TP
.B filters
Proxy only. Not a callback but an array of them: see
.BR "Proxy Filter Chain" .
.TP
.B routes
Not a callback either, but a table this runtime declares about what it answers,
which the daemon publishes to the proxies in front of it: see
.BR "Declared Routes" .
.TP
.B on_proxy_request(client_id, data) \(-> string | nil
Proxy only. Called for every chunk forwarded from a client to a backend.
Return a modified string to replace the data, or
.B nil
to drop the chunk entirely (the backend never receives it).
.IP
Returning nothing at all forwards the chunk unchanged, and that is not the same
as returning
.BR nil .
A hook written to watch traffic
.RB ( "function on_proxy_request(id, data) log(data) end" )
ends without a return, and reading that as
.B nil
made the most natural three lines in this API discard every byte through the
proxy. The filter chain draws the same distinction \(em nothing returned means
the filter has no opinion and the chain continues.
.IP
Fail\-closed, like the filter chain and for the same reason. A hook that throws
a Lua error, or that returns anything other than a string or
.BR nil ,
refuses the request: the connection to the client and the one to the backend are
both closed, and the reason is logged. The hook is where a script strips a
header or rejects a request, so forwarding the unmodified bytes when it could
not run would admit exactly what it was there to refuse \(em and
.B "return false"
is a rejection, not a value to guess at.
.IP
The refusal closes rather than answering a status: this callback is on the byte
path, is called with body chunks as well as with the request head, and carries
TCP as readily as HTTP. To refuse a request with a status line, use the filter
chain \(em see
.BR "Proxy Filter Chain" .
.TP
.B on_proxy_response(client_id, data) \(-> string | nil
Proxy only. Called for every chunk forwarded from a backend to a client.
Same semantics as
.BR on_proxy_request :
returning nothing forwards the chunk unchanged, a replacement string replaces
it, nil drops it, and an error or a return of any other type refuses \(em here by closing the client connection, since the
response this hook was to redact must not be delivered unredacted.
.TP
.B on_master_auth(client_id, password) \(-> bool
Server only (master mode). Custom authentication. Return
.B true
to grant the master role. If defined, overrides
.BR \-\-master\-pw ,
so this callback is the entire gate on that server.
Read strictly, and fail\-closed: only the boolean
.B true
grants. Anything else denies \(em
.BR false ,
.BR nil ,
no return at all, and every truthy non\-boolean such as a string, the number 0
or a table. If the callback throws a Lua error the attempt is denied and the
error is logged.
A denial from this callback is counted against the source address exactly as a
wrong
.B \-\-master\-pw
is, so the per-address lockout described there applies here too.
.TP
.B on_auth(client_id) \(-> bool
Server only. Called immediately after a client is accepted, before
.B on_connect
fires. Return
.B true
to admit the client or
.B false
to reject and close the connection. If the callback throws a Lua error, the client is also rejected (fail-closed). When no
.B on_auth
callback is defined, all clients are admitted.
.TP
.B on_websocket(client_id, headers)
Server only. Called immediately after a WebSocket upgrade handshake completes,
after
.B on_connect
has already fired. The
.I headers
argument is a Lua table containing any subset of the following keys from the
HTTP upgrade request:
.BR cookie ,
.BR origin ,
.BR protocol
(Sec-WebSocket-Protocol), and
.BR authorization .
Only keys present in the request are set; absent headers are nil.
This callback never fires for plain TCP clients.
.TP
.B on_http_request(req) \(-> table
Server only. Called when an HTTP request is received (auto-detected from the
first bytes; see
.BR "Multi-Protocol Auto-Detection" ).
The
.I req
argument is a table with the following fields:
.nf
method      string   HTTP method ("GET", "POST", etc.)
path        string   Request path (e.g. "/api/users")
query       string   Query string without leading ? (empty if none)
body        string   Request body (empty for GET)
client_id   number   File descriptor of the client
version     string   HTTP version (e.g. "1.1")
headers     table    Request headers {["Host"]="...", ...}
.fi
.IP
Header names arrive as the client spelled them. Values arrive with
optional whitespace \(em spaces and tabs \(em removed from both ends, so
.B "X\-Token:\ \ abc\ "
reaches the callback as
.BR abc .
The callback is not reached at all by a request whose framing this server
refuses; see
.B "Request Framing"
above for what those are and what they are answered.
.IP
Return a table with the HTTP response:
.nf
{
    status  = 200,                              -- HTTP status code
    headers = {["Content-Type"] = "text/html"}, -- response headers
    body    = "<h1>Hello</h1>"                  -- response body
}
.fi
.IP
If no
.B on_http_request
callback is defined, the server falls back to static file serving
.RB ( \-\-http )
or returns 404. Returning nothing, or anything that is not a table, means the
same thing: the request falls through to the static file or the 404. Only
.B nil
and no return at all are silent about it \(em those are how a handler declines.
Anything else is a script that meant to answer, so the fall-through is named in
the daemon's log as
.BR "[lua <runtime>] on_http_request returned a string; expected a table or nil ..." .
.IP
In the returned table,
.B status
must be a number,
.B body
a string, and
.B headers
a table of string to string. A field the script never set takes its default
(200, an empty body, no headers), but a
.B status
or
.B body
that is present and of the wrong type is a response this server cannot build:
the request is answered
.BR "500 Internal Server Error" ,
and the field and the type it arrived as are written to the daemon's log. A
header whose name or value is not a string costs the client that one header
rather than the whole answer, and is logged the same way. None of these were
reported before, and a
.B body
that was not a string was served as a
.B 200
with nothing in it.
.IP
Supports HTTP/1.1 keep-alive \(em the connection is reused
for subsequent requests unless the client's
.B Connection
header asked to close, which is honoured for a Lua response exactly as it is
for a static file.
.IP
A callback that
.B raises
is not a callback that declined. The request is answered
.BR "500 Internal Server Error" ,
and the error is written to the daemon's log as
.BR "[lua <runtime>] on_http_request error: ..." .
Falling through to the 404 would make a handler that broke indistinguishable,
at the client, from one that had no answer.
.IP
Example:
.nf
function on_http_request(req)
    if req.method == "GET" and req.path == "/health" then
        return {status = 200, body = "ok"}
    end
    return {
        status  = 200,
        headers = {["Content-Type"] = "application/json"},
        body    = '{"method": "' .. req.method .. '", "path": "' .. req.path .. '"}'
    }
end
.fi
.TP
.B on_tick(dt)
Called at a configurable interval while the runtime is running.
.I dt
is the actual elapsed time in milliseconds since the last tick (double).
Requires a top-level
.B tick_ms
global to be set in the Lua script: a whole number of milliseconds, 10 or more,
defaulting to 100 when the global is omitted. A value below 10, or one that is
not a whole number, refuses the script at load with a reason rather than being
rounded into something else \(em 10 is the shortest interval
.B on_tick
can be armed at, and a script that asks for less is asking for something the
daemon will not do.
Example:
.nf
tick_ms = 50
function on_tick(dt)
    self.broadcast("ping")
end
.fi
.TP
.B on_miss(key) \(-> value [, ttl_seconds]
Cache only. Called when
.B GET
finds nothing, so the script can fetch the value from somewhere else; what it
returns is stored and answered as a hit. See
.B "Database Backend"
for the four cache callbacks in full, with their ordering and their failure
behaviour.
.TP
.B on_write(key, value, ttl)
Cache only. Called after a successful
.BR SET / SETEX / SETNX / MSET .
.TP
.B on_delete(key)
Cache only. Called after a
.B DEL
that removed something.
.TP
.B on_expire(key)
Cache only. Called after the TTL sweep removes a key.
.TP
.B on_upstream(conn_id, data)
Server only. Called for each newline-delimited message received from an
upstream connection. Use \-u to configure upstreams.
.TP
.B on_upstream_connect(conn_id)
Server only. Called when an upstream connects (including auto-reconnects).
.TP
.B on_upstream_disconnect(conn_id)
Server only. Called when an upstream disconnects.
.TP
.B on_cluster_join(daemon)
Cluster only. Called when a new remote daemon is discovered. See
.BR "Cluster Event Callbacks" .
.TP
.B on_cluster_leave(daemon)
Cluster only. Called when a remote daemon becomes stale or disappears.
.TP
.B on_group_change(group, members)
Cluster only. Called when the member count of a group changes.
.SS Callback Deadline
Every Lua callback invocation is given a wall\-clock budget. A callback that has
not returned when its budget runs out is stopped with an error, exactly as if
the script had called
.BR error() :
the daemon logs the reason, the callback's usual failure path runs, and the
runtime carries on serving. The default budget is
.B 10000
milliseconds.
.PP
The bound exists because the event loop is a single thread serving every runtime
in the daemon. Before it,
.B "while true do end"
in any callback froze all of them \(em traffic, the metrics endpoint and the IPC
socket \(em with no recovery short of
.BR "kill \-9" .
.PP
What is bounded:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
each invocation of any
.BI on_ *
callback, separately \(em a periodic
.B on_tick
gets a fresh budget on every tick
.IP \(bu 2
each invocation of a
.B socketley.set_timeout
or
.B socketley.set_interval
callback, and of a
.B socketley.subscribe
handler
.IP \(bu 2
the whole
.B filters
chain for one request, as a single budget rather than one per filter
.IP \(bu 2
the script's top level, which runs inside
.BR create .
A top level that never returns refuses the create with a reason instead of
blocking the terminal on a command that never answers
.RE
.PP
This budget is time on the loop and not time on the clock, which the two
callbacks that may wait make visible.
.B on_message
and
.B on_client_message
run on a coroutine, so a handler stopped in
.B self.cache
is not holding the thread and is not spending this: each stretch it actually
runs gets the budget, and how long it may stay stopped is a separate bound of
ten seconds with its own answer. See
.B self.cache
under
.BR "Runtime Methods" .
.PP
A stopped callback is a callback that raised, so wherever a raise already has a
defined answer, the deadline gets that answer.
.B on_auth
denies and says why;
.B on_route
falls back to the usual backend pick; a
.B filters
entry refuses the request with a 500. In
.B on_proxy_request
and
.B on_proxy_response
it is the refusal case and not the "returned nothing" case \(em the connection
closes, and the bytes of a request whose hook never finished deciding are not
forwarded as though the hook had waved them through.
.PP
.B socketley.http
lives inside the same budget: its
.B timeout_ms
is clamped to what is left of the callback's, so a callback that spends its
budget waiting on a host gets an ordinary
.B "ok = false"
result rather than being stopped. A script that needs a long fetch raises the
callback budget to suit. Nothing can interrupt a request mid\-socket \(em the
mechanism is a VM instruction hook and a blocking call executes no instructions.
.PP
.B Setting it.
A script sets its own with a top\-level
.B callback_timeout_ms
global, next to
.BR tick_ms :
.nf

callback_timeout_ms = 30000   \-\- this script does slow work on purpose
callback_timeout_ms = 0       \-\- no bound at all for this script

.fi
An operator moves the default for every script that says nothing with
.B SOCKETLEY_LUA_TIMEOUT_MS
in the daemon's environment; see
.BR ENVIRONMENT .
.PP
.B What it costs.
Not the JIT, which is what this said before it was measured. On this build
LuaJIT compiles traces with a count hook installed; what the hook costs is the
hooked dispatch path, which checks on every VM instruction instead of every ten
thousandth. Measured at 64 bytes on one connection: a callback that merely
answers a message showed no difference above the run\-to\-run noise \(em 42581
and 43130 messages a second with the hook against 42571 and 42644 without \(em
while one doing about 1300 arithmetic operations per message ran at 85% of its
unhooked rate.
.PP
.B What 0 buys.
Setting the bound to
.B 0
installs no hook and also opens LuaJIT's
.B jit
library, which is the only thing that turns the trace compiler on at all:
without it every Lua callback this daemon runs is interpreted. On that same
arithmetic callback the difference is 11722 messages a second bounded against
28085 unbounded, at 85.2 against 30.3 microseconds of worker CPU each.
.PP
A callback that only answers a message gains very little, because there is
almost no loop to compile. On the echo fixture the two arms do not separate in
throughput at all \(em 42850 and 43929 messages a second bounded against 43577
and 44350 unbounded, an arm-to-arm gap smaller than the spread inside either
arm. What does separate is Lua's own share of the worker's CPU, 5.2 and 5.1%
against 3.9 and 4.0%, which is the compiler doing a little work on two
bytecodes. Round it to about 1% end to end, against 2.4x on a callback that
computes.
.PP
A streaming producer sits between those two and had never been measured, which
matters because it is the shape that runs per byte rather than per message. A
handler generating 40,000 CSV rows into
.B self.send_chunk
gains 25 to 48 per cent, not 2.4x \(em and the profile says why: with the hook
installed the interpreter is 92.7 per cent of the worker's core, but what is
inside that is
.BR lj_str_new ,
.BR lj_str_resize ,
.B gc_sweepstr
and the allocator, about 35 per cent between them. A compiled trace allocates
and interns every string it builds exactly as the interpreter does, so the
compiler removes the dispatch and leaves the string machinery standing.
.PP
Which makes how the script builds its strings the larger lever, and the two
multiply rather than add. The same handler with
.B string.format
in place of a chain of
.B ..
concatenations, at 8 concurrent requests and then 64:
.IP
.nf
                        bounded      unbounded
\&.. concatenation        97.8/s      130.6/s   (+34%)
string.format           93.8/s      160.0/s   (+71%)
\&.. at 64 concurrent      68.0/s      100.8/s   (+48%)
string.format at 64     71.5/s      142.7/s  (+100%)
.fi
.IP
Both arms put byte\-identical responses on the wire. Note the second column is
what changes:
.B string.format
is worth nothing in the interpreter \(em 4 per cent behind the concatenation,
inside the spread \(em because there it is one C call per row either way. It
pays only once the compiler is on, and then it pays twice: one string per row
instead of a chain of intermediates, and an integer formatter instead of the
double one, which was 15.6 per cent of the core on its own. End to end, from
the default with concatenation to the compiler with
.BR string.format ,
that is 64 per cent more throughput at 8 streams and 110 per cent at 64, for
about half the CPU per request.
.PP
A C server building the same rows with
.BR snprintf (3)
still serves 2.4x what the best of those does, so this is a decision about how
much of a stream's work belongs in Lua at all \(em not a setting that closes
the gap.
.PP
.B What decides the size of the gain
is how much of the work is at the Lua level, not how many bytes pass through.
Measured on a proxy, which is a second runtime type with a per\(hybyte hook, both
handlers forwarding the bytes untouched so only the work differs:
.IP
.nf
                              bounded   unbounded   CPU per request
one C library call per chunk    120.9      121.9     8223 -> 8155 us
a Lua loop over every row       417.1     1452.0     2378 ->  669 us
.fi
.IP
The first is a
.B gsub
over each chunk and gains one per cent: the call is C either way and there is
almost no Lua around it to compile. The second walks the rows in a Lua
.B while
loop and gains 248 per cent at 72 per cent less CPU. Every runtime type reads the
same setting from the same place, so the five shapes measured so far order
themselves by that one question \(em about one per cent for a C call per byte or
a callback that only answers, 34 to 48 per cent for building strings (where the
allocator and the intern table are the cost and the compiler cannot remove them),
2.4x for arithmetic, and 248 per cent for Lua control flow over many items.
.PP
.B How the reply is assembled decides both,
and the number of pieces decides whether it matters at all. Four idioms building
one reply out of N parts, on a server, one binary and one C client, interleaved,
each pair of rows below the same script with the compiler closed and open:
.IP
.nf
  N = 24, compiler closed       exchanges/s   spread
    s = s .. piece                 160,495      22%
    s = s .. string.format(..)     153,658      24%
    t[i] = piece, concat once      159,721      23%
    t[i] = format, concat once     166,023      15%
.fi
.IP
Nothing there is a finding: every spread is larger than every difference. A short
reply is assembled the way that reads best, and reaching for
.B string.format
because it is faster is reaching for something that is not there.
.IP
.nf
  N = 1000                      exchanges/s   us cpu/req   spread
    s = s .. piece                   1,251         801      6%
                  compiler open      1,426         703      6%
    s = s .. string.format(..)         923       1,087      8%
                  compiler open        843       1,192      5%
    t[i] = piece, concat once        7,618         131      6%
                  compiler open     13,361          75      2%
    t[i] = format, concat once       8,656         115      7%
                  compiler open     17,675          57      5%
.fi
.IP
At a thousand pieces the best shape is 14.1 times the worst at a fourteenth of
the CPU per request. Joining once is most of it \(em 6.1x on its own \(em and the
formatter is worth a further 14 per cent behind it, against being 26 per cent
.I slower
when it is used to accumulate instead. The same call, opposite signs, decided by
the shape around it.
.IP
What it does to the compiler is the part worth keeping. Opening it is worth 14
per cent on the accumulating shape, nothing outside the spread on the
accumulating one that formats, and 75 and 104 per cent on the two that join
once. That is the rule above seen from the other side: accumulating moves the
work out of Lua and into the allocator, where there is nothing left for the
compiler to reach. A script that is slow because of its idiom does not become
fast because of the setting.
.PP
.B What 0 gives up.
A compiled trace does not call an instruction hook back, so with the compiler
on a runaway callback cannot be interrupted at all. Measured:
.B "while true do end"
against a 300 millisecond deadline stopped in 300.2 milliseconds with the
compiler off and never with it on, and a hard ceiling inside the hook did not
help either, because the hook is not reached from inside a trace. That is why
the two are one setting rather than two knobs that can disagree.
.PP
The blast radius is one runtime rather than the daemon. Measured with a server
whose callback never returns: its own worker process sat at 83% of a core while
a second server on the same daemon went on echoing and the daemon went on
answering
.BR ps .
A runaway in the script's top level is still refused outright whatever
.B callback_timeout_ms
says, because the top level runs under the default before the script's own
declaration has been read.
.PP
.B What it does not bound.
A callback stopped at its deadline has still held the loop for that long, so a
CLI command issued during the stall can hit its own five second receive timeout
and report the daemon unreachable; it succeeds on the retry. A runaway
.B on_tick
is stopped on every tick rather than once. And
.BR getaddrinfo (3),
inside
.BR socketley.http ,
executes no VM instructions and is not interrupted.
.SS Proxy Filter Chain
Proxy only, HTTP mode only.
.B filters
is a global array of functions run in order on each request \fIbefore\fR a
backend is selected. Each filter either says nothing \(em carry on to the next
\(em or returns the answer, which ends the request there.
.PP
The ordering is the point. A request a filter refuses never reaches backend
selection, so no endpoint is resolved, no circuit breaker's half-open probe is
claimed, no pooled socket is spent and no connection is opened. This is where
authentication, rate limiting and header rewriting belong;
.B on_route
runs after a backend has already been chosen and answers a different question.
.PP
The chain is independent of
.BR \-\-strategy .
It runs whatever picks the backend afterwards, and on a proxy with no
.B on_route
at all. A proxy whose script declares no
.B filters
array pays nothing: the chain is guarded by the same callback mask as every
other hook.
.PP
Each filter is called with one argument:
.nf
method      string   HTTP method ("GET", "POST", ...)
path        string   Request path as the client sent it, this proxy's
                     prefix included \(em the same path on_route sees
version     string   HTTP version (e.g. "HTTP/1.1")
client_id   number   File descriptor of the client; pass it to
                     self.peer_ip() to identify the peer
headers     table    Request headers. Names are lowercased, values
                     are trimmed, and a field sent more than once is
                     joined with ", ".
.fi
.PP
And returns one of three things:
.TP
.B nil
(or nothing) \(em this filter has no opinion; the next one runs.
.TP
.BI "{ status = " N ", body = " string ", headers = " table " }"
The answer. The chain stops, the request is never forwarded, and this response
goes to the client.
.I status
must be 200\-599.
.I body
and
.I headers
are optional. The connection is closed afterwards, as it is for every other
response the proxy writes itself.
.TP
.BI "{ set_headers = " table " }"
Carry on, with these headers set on the request that goes to the backend. A
header set here replaces one the client sent under the same name rather than
joining it, and is emitted with a lowercased name.
.TP
.BI "{ remove_headers = { " name ", " name ", ... } }"
Carry on, with these headers taken off the request before it goes to the
backend \(em every occurrence of each name, so a field the client sent twice is
removed entirely. For a header the backend must never see whatever the client
sent: an internal trust header a caller forged, a cookie with no business past
the edge.
.I set_headers
and
.I remove_headers
may appear in the same table. A name that is both set and removed takes the
later of the two statements, exactly as two
.I set_headers
for one name resolve.
.PP
A filter that returns anything else, or that raises an error, refuses the
request with 500. A filter that did not say "carry on" is never treated as
though it had. An entry in
.B filters
that is not a function refuses the whole script at load time, for the same
reason: a silently dropped filter looks exactly like one that admits everybody.
.PP
Names a filter may not set \fIor remove\fR are dropped, with a line on stderr:
.B Content\-Length
and
.B Transfer\-Encoding
on either side, because the proxy forwards exactly the body the parsed request
declared and a filter that could change the length would make the proxy and the
backend disagree about where the request ends;
.B Host
on the request, because the proxy rewrites it to the endpoint it selected; and
the hop-by-hop set
.RB ( Connection ", " Keep\-Alive ", " TE ", " Trailer ", " Upgrade ", " Proxy\-* ).
So is any name that is not a bare token and any value carrying CR or LF \(em a
filter is frequently echoing something the client supplied.
.PP
Filters run on the event loop thread. Whatever a filter does, every request on
the proxy waits for: no blocking, no synchronous I/O, no long loops.
.PP
Example:
.nf
filters = {
    function(req)
        if not req.headers["authorization"] then
            return {
                status  = 401,
                body    = "authentication required\\n",
                headers = {["WWW-Authenticate"] = "Bearer"},
            }
        end
        return {set_headers = {["x-authenticated-user"] = "alice"}}
    end,
}
.fi
.IP
See
.B examples/proxy/filter\-chain.lua
for authentication, per-IP rate limiting and header rewriting as three filters
in one chain.
.SS Declared Routes
.B routes
is a global array in which a runtime states what it will answer: a path, and
the methods allowed on it. It is declared in the \fIhandler's\fR script and
published to the proxies that forward to that runtime, which then refuse those
methods at the edge \(em before any endpoint is resolved, any socket is taken
from the pool and any connection is opened.
.PP
The declaration lives with the handler on purpose. Configured on the proxy, a
method list is a second copy of something the handler already states in its own
code, in another runtime and soon in another process, and two copies drift: the
handler grows
.BR PATCH ,
the gateway goes on refusing it, and the 405 is explicable from neither side
alone. Nothing is configured on the proxy; editing the handler's script and
running
.B socketley reload\-lua
changes what the gateway enforces.
.PP
.nf
routes = {
    { path = "/endpoint/login", methods = {"POST", "GET"} },
    { path = "/static/*",       methods = {"GET"} },
}
.fi
.PP
.B path
is the path as the \fIhandler\fR sees it \(em the proxy's own
.BI / name /
prefix already stripped \(em and a query string is not part of it. A trailing
.B *
makes it a literal prefix; that is the whole of the pattern language. Exact
matches win over prefixes, and the longest prefix wins among prefixes.
.B methods
is a list of method names; a bare string is accepted for a single one. The
declaration is uppercased when the script loads, so
.B "methods = {\(dqpost\(dq}"
declares POST. The method a client sends is compared as sent \(em RFC 9110 9.1
makes the method token case-sensitive, and a request for
.B post
is one the handler would refuse too.
.PP
What the proxy does with it:
.TP
A declared method
is forwarded, as always.
.TP
An undeclared method on a declared path
is answered
.B 405 Method Not Allowed
with an
.B Allow
header listing what would have worked. No backend is involved.
.TP
.B HEAD
is allowed wherever
.B GET
is (RFC 9110 9.3.2) \(em a handler that serves GET serves HEAD whether or not it
listed it.
.TP
.B OPTIONS
is never refused. A plain OPTIONS \(em "what may I do here", RFC 9110 9.3.7 \(em
is answered
.B 204
with
.B Allow
out of the declaration. A CORS \fIpreflight\fR (an OPTIONS carrying
.B Origin
and
.BR Access\-Control\-Request\-Method )
is always forwarded to the handler instead: the declaration says nothing about
origins, and an answer without
.B Access\-Control\-Allow\-Origin
breaks the preflight exactly as a 405 would \(em the real request is never sent
and the browser reports a CORS error. A handler that declares
.B OPTIONS
itself always gets it.
.TP
A path nobody declared
is forwarded, whatever the method. A declaration speaks about the paths it names
and says nothing about the others; it is not a whitelist, and declaring one
route does not hide the rest.
.TP
A backend given as \fIhost:port\fR
has no declaration and never will \(em the published snapshot is keyed by
runtime name and by group. Such a backend is forwarded to as before.
.PP
For a proxy with several backends, or an
.BI @ group
backend, one rule covers both halves of the fold: nothing may be refused that
\fIeither\fR possible target might have served. A path only some of them declare
is dropped \(em a runtime that never mentioned it may well serve it. A path all
of them declare keeps the \fIunion\fR of their methods, since the proxy does not
know which target a request will reach. One backend or group member that
declares nothing therefore leaves that proxy enforcing nothing at all, silently
and on purpose: it has said nothing about what it answers, so nothing can be
refused on its account.
.PP
.B "Edge rejection is not input validation."
The proxy can confirm the method is one the handler declared. It cannot confirm
the body is a well-formed login, and it is not the only way in: a socketley
server listens on its own port, and a request that arrives there passed no edge
at all. A handler that stops checking its input because "the gateway checks" is
safe only for as long as every request comes through the gateway, which is a
property of the deployment and not of the code. Declare routes to save the
round trip and to say what you mean; go on validating.
.PP
.B socketley stats \fIproxy\fR
reports
.B declared_routes
\(em how many routes that proxy is actually enforcing. It is the first thing to
look at when a method that should be refused is not: a backend that declares
nothing, a backend named by address, a script that failed to load and a proxy
whose handler had not started yet all look identical from the client side, and
all of them read 0 here.
.PP
A malformed entry refuses the script at load time, with the reason on stderr,
rather than being dropped: a declaration that is not the one in the file is
invisible from both sides. A
.B routes
table with no entry at index 1 is refused too \(em it is an array, and
.B routes = {["/a"] = {"GET"}}
would otherwise load and declare nothing.
.PP
Neither end of this has to be in the daemon. A proxy in a worker process of its
own enforces declarations the same way: the table reaches it in the endpoint
frame that already carries the addresses. And a
.I handler
in a worker declares them the same way, which is less obvious \(em the
declaration is produced by running the script, and only the worker runs it, so
it travels back to the daemon in a
.B routes
frame on the control socket and the placeholder publishes the worker's copy.
Where either runtime runs makes no difference to which methods are refused.
.IP
See
.B examples/proxy/declared\-routes.lua
for a handler that declares an AJAX endpoint and the gateway in front of it.
.SS Runtime Methods (in Lua)
.nf
-- Every type
self.name      -- Runtime name (read-only)
self.port      -- Port number (read-only)
self.state     -- Current state (read-only)
self.type      -- Runtime type (read-only)
self.connections()         -- Current connection count (all four types).
                              With --shards, the whole runtime's count summed
                              across its worker processes, not the shard's own

self.protocol  -- server/client: "tcp" or "udp"; proxy: "tcp" or "http"
               -- (not set on a cache)
self.send(msg)             -- Send message (client)
self.send(client_id, msg)  -- Send to specific client (server)
self.broadcast(msg)        -- Broadcast to all clients (server).  Answers two
                           -- numbers: how many took it, and how many were
                           -- dropped because their queue was full.  See below.
self.pending(client_id)    -- Queued for that client and not yet on the wire,
                           -- in bytes (server); nil if the id is not one of
                           -- this runtime's connections.  See below.
self.respond_stream(id, status, headers)
                           -- Begin a response this script will write itself,
                           -- chunk by chunk (server).  See below.
self.send_chunk(id, data)  -- One chunk of it; false means it was not taken
self.end_response(id)      -- The last chunk has gone; finish the response
self.watermark()           -- The byte count on_writable fires on the way down
                           -- through (server and client).  Pace against this,
                           -- not a literal.  A call and not a field, because
                           -- --write-buffer is editable.  See on_writable.
on_http_body(id, chunk, n) -- A request body streamed, rather than gathered
                           -- whole (server).  Defining it is the opt-in.
self.pending()             -- The client's own form, without an id: a client
                           -- has one connection.  Bytes queued for its server
                           -- and not yet on the wire.

-- ─── Sending faster than the socket ───
-- A write is queued, not performed, and the queue has a bound.  Reach it and
-- the runtime closes the connection -- which used to be the only way a script
-- found out: the message that filled the queue was dropped and answered true,
-- and the one after it was refused with "there is no open connection", the
-- sentence a peer hanging up gets.
--
-- self.send() now answers false for a message that was not taken, and names
-- the backlog when that is the reason, so a producer can tell "slow down" from
-- "it is gone".  self.pending() is the number to watch BEFORE sending, which
-- is the difference between pacing and a post-mortem:
--
--     if (self.pending(id) or 0) >= self.watermark() then return end
--
-- and on_writable(id) is how a producer that stopped there is started again,
-- which is what makes a body past the bound sendable rather than merely
-- survivable.  See on_writable in CALLBACKS.
--
-- self.broadcast() has the same bound at its own door, and used to report
-- nothing at all: a broadcast to 3000 clients that dropped a third of them was
-- indistinguishable from one that reached every single one.  It answers two
-- numbers now --
--
--     local taken, behind = self.broadcast(msg)
--
-- counting THIS shard's connections, which is the scope a script can act on; a
-- held message counts as taken, because an unclassified connection's hold is a
-- deferred take and not a drop.  And a client dropped by the broadcast gets
-- the backlog sentence from a later self.send() rather than the peer's, which
-- it did not before -- one flag, both entrances.
--
-- Measured with a client that connects and never reads, against 400 chunks of
-- 64 KB: 129 taken, 271 refused, and the queue standing at its bound.

-- ─── A response written rather than returned ───
-- on_http_request has to RETURN its body, so a generated answer had to fit in
-- memory.  A FILE of any size was already served by the splice path and a Range
-- of it by the 206 path; a generated one of any size was not expressible at all
-- -- transcoding, an export assembled from a cache, a CSV of ten million rows.
--
--     self.respond_stream(id, 200, {["Content-Type"] = "text/csv"})
--     for row in rows() do
--         if (self.pending(id) or 0) >= self.watermark() then return end   -- wait
--         self.send_chunk(id, row)
--     end
--     self.end_response(id)
--
-- Paced through self.pending()/on_writable like any other producer, which is
-- what makes it a stream rather than a slower way to run out of memory.
-- Measured: 200 MB of generated rows through an 8 MiB queue, delivered in
-- 51,200 chunks with on_writable resuming it 67 times, for 6 MB of resident
-- memory.
--
-- The handler returns nothing after starting one: the response is already on
-- its way, and the static-file and 404 fallbacks are skipped for this request.
-- A handler that only tried to send a chunk has NOT answered, so its ordinary
-- return still applies.
--
-- Content-Length, Transfer-Encoding and Connection are dropped from the header
-- table if given: a Content-Length beside a chunked body is a message carrying
-- two answers to its own length, which this server refuses with a 400 on the
-- way in.  Measured with it passed through: the client read 119 bytes where 5
-- were sent, having believed the wrong header.
--
-- send_chunk on a HEAD request answers false and names it, rather than dropping
-- the bytes: a HEAD asked for the headers and nothing else, and a script that
-- thinks it is sending a body to one has a bug this refusal is the only report
-- of.  An empty chunk is accepted and not sent, because a zero-length chunk is
-- how the terminator is spelled on the wire -- passing one through as payload
-- would end the response while the script was still writing it.

.fi
.PP
.nf
-- A question whose answer is somewhere else (server with --cache)
self.cache(command)        -- Run one command against the LINKED cache and
                           -- answer with what it said.  The handler STOPS here
                           -- and goes on when the reply arrives; the daemon
                           -- keeps serving meanwhile.  nil plus a reason when
                           -- the answer is not coming.

-- ─── A handler that waits ───
-- Every callback used to run to the end in one go, so a script could answer
-- only out of what it already held.  A server linked to a cache could not read
-- that cache at all, and the one call that reaches outside the process --
-- socketley.http -- does it by blocking the event loop for every runtime in the
-- daemon, which is why its own entry says to use it in on_start and nowhere
-- else.
--
--     function on_client_message(id, msg)
--         local v, err = self.cache("get " .. msg)
--         self.send(id, v or ("miss: " .. err))
--     end
--
-- The handler runs on a coroutine.  At the call it suspends, the request goes
-- to the cache, and the event loop carries on with every other connection and
-- every other runtime; when the reply lands the handler picks up at the same
-- line with the answer as that call's return value.  Nothing else in the script
-- changes, and a handler that never calls it is not affected: a callback that
-- runs straight through costs the same as it did, measured at 0.158 us against
-- 0.161 for the plain call it replaced, because the coroutines are pooled.
--
-- Where it may be called from: on_message and on_client_message.  Anywhere
-- else -- a timer, on_http_request, a filter -- answers nil and says so,
-- because those call sites still expect an answer on the spot.
--
-- Three things are guaranteed, and each exists because the shape without it
-- was reachable:
--
--   * It always returns.  A cache that accepts the request and never answers
--     leaves the handler stopped for ever otherwise -- measured against a
--     SIGSTOPped cache worker, whose request is parked against a ticket nothing
--     completes.  After ten seconds the call answers nil, "timeout".
--
--   * The connection is still there.  While the handler waits, the connection
--     it is answering is held open exactly as a client's own `cache get` is:
--     a promised reply, which the close path defers on.  Without it every
--     `echo | nc` fails, because the peer half-closes the moment it has sent
--     its line and the answer does not exist yet.
--
--   * It cannot pile up.  4096 stopped callbacks per runtime, and a server that
--     is already owed as many answers as one connection may have refuses the
--     call with a reason rather than stopping the handler.
--
-- The command is the cache's own text protocol, so the answer is that
-- protocol's: `get` on a key that is not there answers the string "nil" rather
-- than a Lua nil, which is the same answer a client reading the port gets.
.fi
.PP
.nf
-- Upstream actions (server with -u)
self.upstream_send(conn_id, msg) -- Send to a specific upstream; false means
                                 -- the bytes are not going.  See below.
self.upstream_broadcast(msg)     -- Send to all connected upstreams.  Answers how
                                 -- many took it and how many were dropped
self.upstream_pending(conn_id)   -- Bytes queued for that upstream and not yet on
                                 -- the wire; nil if the id is not one of this
                                 -- runtime's upstreams
self.upstreams()                 -- Table of connected upstream conn_ids

-- ─── The same bound, pointed outwards ───
-- An upstream's write queue fills exactly as a client's does, and these used to
-- report none of it.  upstream_send() tested only whether the upstream was
-- connected; a queue that reached the bound sets `closing` and leaves
-- `connected` alone, so every later send answered TRUE for ever while nothing
-- left the process.  Measured, 400 sends of 64 KB at an upstream that accepted
-- the connection and then read nothing:
--
--     before   sent=400  failed=0    with 8,396,928 bytes standing in the queue
--     after    sent=129  failed=271  the same 129/271 the client-facing side
--                                    reports, because it is the same bound
--
-- The refusal names the backlog apart from a missing upstream, because "is not
-- connected" is false of a connected upstream whose queue this runtime filled --
-- and the two want opposite reactions.  Pace with self.upstream_pending() and
-- not self.pending(): that one resolves a CLIENT id, and an upstream is in
-- another map entirely.
--
-- on_upstream_writable(conn_id) is the other half, and without it the number
-- above is only a post-mortem: a producer could ask how deep the queue was and
-- never be told when there was room.  The same 400 sends of 64 KB, at an
-- upstream that reads:
--
--     unpaced   sent=129  failed=271   and the upstream closed
--     paced     sent=400  refused=0    resumed 6 times
--
-- Same messages, same bound, same upstream.  129 of 400 is what a producer that
-- does not look gets, and it is not a limit on how much can be sent.

-- Cache: mode
-- A call and not a field, because --mode is editable and a field written when
-- this table was built would go stale.  "readonly", "readwrite" or "admin" --
-- the same three names --mode, `show` and the wire protocol use.
self.cache_mode()   -- Which mode this cache is in

-- Cache: which keys are this process's
-- Under --shards the script runs in all N workers and the calls below act only
-- on the slice this one owns, answering a foreign key the way they answer a
-- refusal.  True for every key when the cache is one process.
self.owns(key)      -- Does this shard own the key?

-- Cache: strings
self.get(key)       -- Get string value
self.set(key, val)  -- Set string value; false in readonly mode
self.del(key)       -- Delete key (any type); false in readonly mode
self.exists(key)    -- Is the key there?  See below.

-- Cache: lists
self.lpush(key, val)        -- Push to front; false in readonly mode
self.rpush(key, val)        -- Push to back; false in readonly mode
self.lpop(key)              -- Pop from front; "" in readonly mode
self.rpop(key)              -- Pop from back; "" in readonly mode
self.llen(key)              -- List length

-- Cache: sets
self.sadd(key, member)      -- 1 added, 0 already a member,
                            -- nil refused (readonly mode)
self.srem(key, member)      -- Remove member; false in readonly mode
self.sismember(key, member) -- Check membership
self.scard(key)             -- Set cardinality

-- Cache: hashes
self.hset(key, field, val)  -- Set hash field; false in readonly mode
self.hget(key, field)       -- Get hash field
self.hdel(key, field)       -- Delete hash field; false in readonly mode
self.hexists(key, field)    -- Is the field there?  See below.
self.hlen(key)              -- Hash field count

-- Cache: TTL
self.expire(key, seconds)   -- Set expiry; false in readonly mode
self.ttl(key)               -- Get remaining TTL
self.persist(key)           -- Remove expiry; false in readonly mode

-- Cache: pub/sub
-- Under --shards the message reaches every shard's subscribers, but the count
-- is this shard's own: the siblings answer on a later turn and this call
-- answers on the turn it was made.
self.publish(channel, msg)  -- Publish to channel; returns this shard's count

-- Server: connection control
-- Every mutator below answers `true`, or `false, "reason"`.  They returned
-- nothing at all before, so an id that named no connection was a silent no-op.
self.disconnect(client_id)         -- Terminate a connection from Lua
self.peer_ip(client_id)            -- Get client IP (IPv4 or IPv6 string)
                                   -- also on a proxy, same meaning
self.ws_headers(client_id)         -- Get WS upgrade headers table (nil for TCP)

-- Server: client enumeration and multicast
self.clients()                     -- Return table of all connected client_ids
                                   -- also on a proxy, same meaning
self.multicast(ids, msg)           -- Send msg to a subset of clients (ids = array of
                                   -- client_ids).  Returns how many were reached.
                                   -- An entry that is not a whole number refuses the
                                   -- whole call rather than being dropped from it.

-- Server: named groups
self.join_group(id, group)         -- Add client to a named group
self.leave_group(id, group)        -- Remove client from a group
self.group_send(group, msg)        -- Send msg to all clients in a group.  Returns how
                                   -- many were reached; 0 is a group with nobody in it.

-- Server: zero-copy pipe pairing (bypasses Lua callbacks)
self.pipe(id_a, id_b)             -- Pair two clients: data forwarded directly between them
self.unpipe(id)                    -- Remove pipe pairing for a client
socketley.pipe(src, fd_a, src, fd_b)   -- Pair two connections of one server
socketley.pipe(src, fd, dst)           -- Route a connection to another server.
                                   -- Despite the name it hands no descriptor over:
                                   -- src keeps the socket and relays the bytes

-- Server: per-connection metadata
self.set_data(id, key, val)        -- Set string metadata on a connection (nil val deletes key)
self.get_data(id, key)             -- Get metadata string or nil

-- Server: client routing
self.route(client_id, target)      -- Forward client to sub-server, in this or
                                   -- any other process. This server keeps the
                                   -- socket and relays; the target answers.
self.unroute(client_id)            -- Stop forwarding
self.get_route(client_id)          -- Get current route (string or nil)

-- Server: owner-targeted sending (sub-server only)
self.owner_send(client_id, msg)    -- Send to a connection routed here, through
                                   -- the runtime that owns it. Returns false if
                                   -- that connection has gone.
self.owner_broadcast(msg)          -- Broadcast to all owner's clients

-- Proxy
self.clients()                     -- Table of connected client_ids
self.peer_ip(client_id)            -- Client IP (IPv4 or IPv6 string)
self.close_client(client_id)       -- Close one client connection
self.backends()                    -- Array of backend addresses, in the
                                   -- order on_route indexes them (1-based
                                   -- in Lua; on_route returns 0-based)
self.backend_health()              -- Circuit-breaker state per backend,
                                   -- same 1-based order:
                                   -- "closed" | "open" | "half_open"
self.stats()                       -- {connections, bytes_in, bytes_out,
                                   --  total_connections, total_messages,
                                   --  backend_count}
.fi
.PP
.B "Why exists and hexists are there."
.B self.get
answers with the value, and an absent key and a key holding an empty string
both come back as the empty string.
A script cannot tell those apart, and this page's own example leans on it \(em
.I examples/cache/cache\-integration\-handler.lua
reads
.B "self.get(\(dqconfig:timeout\(dq) == \(dq\(dq"
to mean "not set yet".
.PP
Making
.B get
answer
.B nil
for an absent key would settle it and would break that reading wherever it has
been written, and an empty string is a value a cache is entitled to hold. So the
missing question was added instead, under the name the wire protocol has always
used for it.
.nf
.fi
.PP
.B "The proxy table is smaller than the server's on purpose."
A proxy relays between a client and a backend and speaks for neither, so there
is no
.BR broadcast() ,
no
.B send()
and no per\-connection metadata: it has no message of its own to put on the
wire. To change what crosses it, use
.B on_proxy_request
and
.BR on_proxy_response ,
and to refuse a request, the filter chain.
.PP
.B self.backends()
is indexed from 1, like every Lua array, while
.B on_route
returns an index from 0 \(em so the address
.B on_route
selects with
.B n
is
.BR "self.backends()[n + 1]" .
.PP
.B self.owner_send()
takes only a client_id this runtime was actually given \(em one that arrived
through
.BR self.route() ,
reported by
.BR on_connect() .
It answers
.B false
for anything else, including a client_id whose connection has since closed. It
used to take any number and write it into the owner's connection table as a
descriptor index, so a script holding an id past its connection's life wrote
into whichever client had inherited that descriptor.
.SS Global Functions
.TP
.B socketley.log(msg)
Write one line to the daemon's log at
.B INFO
level, prefixed with the name of the runtime that wrote it:
.BR "[12:04:31] [INFO] [lua my\-server] ..." .
It goes through the daemon's logger, so
.B log_level
in the configuration file turns it down like anything else; at the default
level nothing that used to appear stops appearing. Every Lua diagnostic carries
the same
.B [lua <runtime>]
prefix, which is what separates two scripts sharing one stderr \(em and two
runtimes commonly share one script file, so the file name in an error is not an
identity.
.TP
.B socketley.set_timeout(ms, fn) \(-> timer_id
Fire
.I fn
once after
.I ms
milliseconds using an io_uring timeout SQE.
Returns an integer timer ID that can be passed to
.BR socketley.clear_timeout() .
Shares the event loop with the runtime \(em no extra threads.
.TP
.B socketley.set_interval(ms, fn) \(-> timer_id
Fire
.I fn
every
.I ms
milliseconds. Returns an integer timer ID that can be passed to
.BR socketley.clear_interval() .
.IP
Both kinds are cancelled when the runtime stops, and are
.B not
restored if it is started again. A stopped runtime executes no script, so a
timer armed by one does not survive it \(em including a timer armed inside
.BR on_stop ,
which is severed with the rest. The registrations are made at the script's top
level, which runs once, when the runtime's worker is forked \(em so a
.B start
after a
.B stop
forks a new worker, runs the top level again, and arms them again. What is not
replayed is anything the previous worker held: a timer is not restored, it is
re-armed by a script that is being run afresh.
.IP
Both return
.B 0
and arm nothing when
.I ms
is not greater than zero, when the runtime has no event loop, or when the
runtime has been stopped \(em the last of those covers the script that calls
.B socketley.stop
on its own runtime and carries on, since
.B stop
returns to it and everything after that line runs on a runtime that is already
down. A
.B start
lifts it, before
.BR on_start ,
so a restarted runtime arms exactly as it did the first time.
.B socketley.subscribe
answers 0 in the same situation and for the same reason. Real ids
start at 1, so
.B 0
is unambiguous; the reason is written to the daemon's log, because a timer that
will never fire is otherwise silent.
.TP
.B socketley.clear_timeout(timer_id)
Cancel a pending timeout. The timer will not fire. Safe to call with
an already-expired or invalid ID (no-op).
.TP
.B socketley.clear_interval(timer_id)
Cancel a repeating interval. The timer stops after the current cycle
completes. Safe to call with an already-expired or invalid ID (no-op).
.IP
Both withdraw the timer rather than merely muting it: the io_uring timeout is
removed, the timer's storage is released, and the reference to the callback
goes with it \(em so the closure and everything it captured become collectable
at the call rather than when the timer would have expired. Cancelling a
one-hour interval used to hold all of that for the hour.
.IP
Both are safe to call from inside the timer's own callback, which is how a
one-shot repeating timer is written.
.TP
.B socketley.json_encode(value) \(-> string | false, why
Encode a Lua value (table, string, number, boolean, nil) as a JSON string.
Tables with consecutive integer keys starting at 1 are encoded as JSON
arrays; all others as objects.
.IP
Object keys are written in sorted order, so encoding equal data twice gives the
same string. A Lua table has no order of its own \(em one filled
.IR alpha ", " beta ", " gamma ", " delta
iterates
.IR beta ", " gamma ", " delta ", " alpha
\(em so without sorting the output was not a function of the value, and two
tables holding the same data could encode differently. Anything that compares
encoded documents depends on this: an ETag, a cache key, a signature, a
deduplication hash, or a diff of two logged payloads.
.IP
Refused, with
.BR false " and a reason, when the value cannot be written as a document:"
a table that contains itself (directly or through any chain of fields), or one
nested deeper than 128 levels. A table reachable twice by different paths is
not a cycle and encodes normally.
.IP
A string is truthy and
.B false
is not, so
.B "local s = socketley.json_encode(t)"
reads unchanged and
.B "if not s then"
is how the refusal is tested.
.TP
.B socketley.json_decode(str) \(-> value | false, why
Decode a JSON string into a Lua value. Objects become tables with string
keys, arrays become tables with integer keys (1-based). Null becomes nil.
.IP
Refused, with
.BR false " and a reason, for a document nested deeper than 128 levels, for"
bytes that are not a complete JSON document, and for trailing data after the
document ends.
.IP
The refusal on malformed input is what makes the return value trustworthy. The
decoder used to answer with whatever it had understood so far, so
.B [1,2
\(em a request body cut short by a dropped connection \(em decoded to an
ordinary two-element table,
.B {"a":}
to an empty one, and
.BR nul ", " garbage
and an empty string all to nil, which was also the answer for a document that
really was
.BR null .
A handler could not tell a truncated body from a whole one and carried on with
the fields that happened to arrive.
.IP
This limit is not a formatting preference. This call is written to be pointed
at data from the network \(em a request body, a message payload \(em so the
nesting depth is chosen by whoever sent it, and the decoder recurses to follow
it. Without the limit a few hundred kilobytes of
.B [[[[
took the whole daemon down, and no callback deadline covers it: those measure
Lua execution, and this recursion is native.
.TP
.B socketley.subscribe(cache_name, channel, fn) \(-> subscription_id
Register
.I fn(channel, message)
to be called whenever the named cache runtime publishes to
.IR channel .
The function is called on the subscribing runtime's event loop turn (not from the cache itself).
Multiple subscriptions to the same channel are all fired in registration order.
.IP
Returns an integer id for
.BR socketley.unsubscribe() .
Ids start at 1 and are never reused, so
.B 0
is a refusal: it means the runtime has been stopped and this registration would
have outlived the stop that ended the others. See
.B socketley.set_interval
for the whole of it \(em the two refuse together and a
.B start
lifts both.
.IP
Only a
.B running
runtime is delivered to. Every registration this one holds is ended when it
stops, and one that has never been started receives nothing either \(em its
top level has run, so the registration exists, but a runtime that is not
running executes no script, which is the same rule the timers are severed
under. They
.B do
come back if it is started again, which is where they differ from
.B set_interval
and
.BR set_timeout :
a subscription is registered from
.BR on_start ,
and
.B start
runs
.BR on_start ,
where a timer is armed at the script's top level and only a load runs that.
.TP
.B socketley.unsubscribe(subscription_id) \(-> bool
End one registration. Returns
.B true
when it removed a live one and
.B false
when the id names none \(em never issued, or already unsubscribed.
.IP
The id names one registration, not a channel, so two handlers on the same
channel can be ended independently. Safe to call from inside a subscriber's own
callback, which is how a one-shot subscriber is written; the removal takes
effect for the next message rather than in the middle of delivering this one.
.IP
Ending one explicitly matters while the runtime is up:
.B stop
ends them all, and
.B remove
and
.B reload\-lua
destroy the context they live in, but nothing does either of those on a
runtime that is simply serving.
Without ending them, a script that subscribes from a callback that runs more
than once \(em
.B on_tick
is the ordinary one \(em registers the same handler again on every call, and
each publish is delivered to every copy.
.TP
.B socketley.mqtt.publish(runtime, topic, payload [, {qos=, retain=}] [, fn]) \(-> true | nil, why
A message published on an mqtt runtime of this daemon, as the broker's own;
true once the broker has it. See
.B From the other runtimes
under
.BR "MQTT FLAGS" .
.TP
.B socketley.mail.send(runtime, {to=, cc=, bcc=, from=, reply_to=, subject=, text=, html=, headers=, attachments=, raw=} [, fn]) \(-> id | nil, why
A message written and queued by a mail runtime of this daemon; its queue id once
it is in the spool.
.BR to ,
.B cc
and
.B bcc
are an address or a list of them, an attachment
.BR {name=,type=,data=} .
See
.BR "MAIL FLAGS" .
.TP
.B socketley.http(opts) \(-> table
Synchronous HTTP/HTTPS client.
.B WARNING:
Blocks the event loop thread \(em the wait is bounded, but it is not
asynchronous: the whole exchange completes before any other runtime in
the daemon is served again. Use only in
.BR on_start / on_stop
or low-frequency
.B on_tick
callbacks.
.PP
.I opts
table keys:
.nf
url         string   Request URL (http:// or https://)
method      string   HTTP method (default "GET")
body        string   Request body (optional)
headers     table    Extra headers e.g. {["Content-Type"]="application/json"}
timeout_ms  number   Timeout in milliseconds, 1..3600000 (default 5000)
.fi
.PP
A key that is present and of the wrong type or out of range comes back as
.B "ok = false"
with the reason in
.BR error ,
rather than being replaced by the default: `timeout_ms = "soon"` used to become
5000 and make the request anyway, on a number nobody chose. A key that is
absent keeps its default, which is not a mistake and is not reported.
.PP
Returns a table:
.nf
ok      bool    true if HTTP status 2xx
status  number  HTTP status code (e.g. 200, 404)
body    string  Response body
error   string  Error message on failure
.fi
.PP
.I timeout_ms
is one deadline for the whole exchange \(em connect, TLS handshake,
request and response are all measured against it \(em not a separate
timeout per operation. Name resolution is the exception: it runs before
the deadline is armed and has no bound of its own. A response is refused
once it exceeds 8 MB rather than buffered without limit.
.PP
A response that declares a
.B Content-Length
and then ends before reaching it is refused too, with the shortfall named in
.BR error ,
because nothing else in the answer would say so: the close that cut it short is
the same close that frames a response carrying no length at all, and over
HTTPS the
.B close_notify
that would tell those two apart is one many servers omit and this client
accepts the absence of. Only a declared length can be fallen short of \(em a
chunked response, or one with no length, is read until the close either way and
comes back as before.
.PP
HTTPS uses OpenSSL and validates the peer. The certificate chain is
verified against the system CA store, and the certificate must have been
issued to the host in the URL \(em a host name is matched against the
certificate's DNS names with partial wildcards refused, an address
literal against its IP SAN. TLS 1.2 is the minimum accepted protocol
version. Failure of either check aborts the request with the reason in
.IR error ,
and there is no option to disable it, so a service presenting a
self-signed or private-CA certificate must have that CA in the trust
store OpenSSL reads (overridable with
.B SSL_CERT_FILE
or
.BR SSL_CERT_DIR ).
If no CA store is available at all the call fails immediately with
.BR "no system CA store available" .
.SS Runtime Management API
Create, start, stop, and remove runtimes from Lua scripts:
.nf
socketley.create(type, name, config)  -- Create runtime
socketley.start(name)                 -- Start runtime
socketley.stop(name)                  -- Stop runtime
socketley.remove(name)                -- Stop + remove runtime
socketley.send(name, msg)             -- Send/broadcast to runtime
socketley.list()                      -- List all runtime names (table)
socketley.get(name)                   -- Get info table or nil
socketley.pipe(src, fd_a, src, fd_b)  -- Pair two connections of one server
socketley.pipe(src, fd, dst)          -- Route a connection to another server
.fi
.P
.B "Every call above answers " true " when it happened, or " false ", \(dqreason\(dq" " when it did not."
.RS
.PP
The reason is a sentence naming the runtime and what was wrong with the
request, so a script can tell the four situations apart that all used to answer
a bare
.BR false :
.PP
.nf
local ok, why = socketley.start("web")
--  false  there is no runtime called "web"
--  false  "web" is already running
--  false  "web" is failed, and a failed runtime cannot be started again ...
--  false  "web" could not be started; it is failed now, and the reason is
--         in the daemon's log        <- a held port, a TLS pair that is not one
.fi
.PP
This is the shape Lua itself uses \(em
.BR io.open ,
.BR os.remove ,
.B pcall
\(em so
.B "assert(socketley.start(n))"
prints the reason with no other change. Success returns ONE value, not
.BR "true, nil" .
.PP
.B What a second return value changes.
Lua truncates a multiple-return call to one value everywhere except three
places. Unaffected: assignment, \fBif not f() then\fR, \fBand\fR/\fBor\fR,
concatenation,
.BR tostring ,
comparison, and any argument position that is not the last. Affected:
.B print(f())
now prints two values,
.B "{ f() }"
builds a two-element table, and
.B g(f())
passes
.B g
two arguments where it passed one.
.RE
.PP
.B socketley.pipe
is two calls and the argument count says which. With four arguments it pairs
two connections of ONE server, both descriptors named and both used. With three
it hands the connection to another
.IR runtime ,
which decides what answers it \(em there is no destination descriptor in that,
so a fourth argument across two runtimes is refused rather than accepted and
discarded.
.P
.B Config table keys:
.BR port ,
.B config
(or
.BR lua ,
a script path),
.BR target ,
.B mode
(server only),
.B http
and
.B http_cache
(server only),
.BR group ,
.B on_parent_stop
("remove" or "stop"), and
.B start
(bool).
.B The table is validated and a refusal is a refusal:
a port outside 0..65535, a mode or policy that is not one of the listed values,
a key that is not on this list, a script that will not load, or a
.B start
that cannot bind, each returns
.B false
with the reason in the log and leaves no runtime behind. Lua-created runtimes
have parent-child ownership; port=0 creates internal-only servers that receive
forwarded messages only.
.PP
.B "Every call above works in a worker, which is where every runtime runs."
.RS
.PP
These calls act on the runtime map, and the runtime map lives in the daemon \(em
so a script written against them is running somewhere the map is not. All of
them work there anyway, and how they work differs:
.RS
.PP
.BR socketley.send ,
.BR .list ,
.B .get
and the four
.B socketley.cluster
calls are answered from a
.I replica.
The daemon pushes the worker a copy of the map and a snapshot of the cluster
view, and refreshes both when they change.
.PP
.BR socketley.create ,
.BR .start ,
.BR .stop
and
.B .remove
.I change
the map, and a copy is not the thing to change. They are asked of the daemon
instead, over a socket the worker holds for exactly this, and they wait for the
answer \(em so
.B "if socketley.create(...) then"
tests what happened. The wait is what makes them honest and it is bounded: 9.95,
10.20 and 10.52 microseconds over three runs of 20000 for a call that does not
fork anything, and at most five seconds for one that does. They are made in
.B on_start
or on a rare control message, never per request, which is what makes a wait
affordable at all \(em see
.B socketley.http()
in the same table, which blocks the same loop for a network round trip.
.PP
The answers are the daemon's own, word for word what the same call says in the
daemon:
.B "there is no runtime called \(dqx\(dq" ,
.B "\(dqx\(dq is already running" ,
.BR "a runtime called \(dqx\(dq already exists" .
A script cannot tell from them which process it is running in, which is the
point.
.RE
.PP
.B "Two calls used to be refused there, and are not."
.B socketley.pipe
across two runtimes, and
.BR self.route() .
Their refusal said a pipe hands a file descriptor between runtimes and that no
channel existed to pass one over. Across two runtimes it hands no descriptor
over at all: the origin keeps the socket and relays, which needed a way to name
another runtime's connection rather than a way to move one. Both cross a process
boundary now, with no change to how either is called.
.PP
One refusal is left, and it is the one to read carefully if you meet it.
.BR socketley.create ,
.BR .start ,
.BR .stop
and
.B .remove
raise in a worker that was forked
.I without
the socket they travel on \(em an older daemon, or one that could not create it.
That message says so and tells you to restart the daemon. It is not the
permanent refusal these four used to carry.
.PP
.B socketley.pipe
and
.B self.route()
were the last two refused in a worker, and are not any more. Their message said
a pipe hands a file descriptor between runtimes, that a descriptor cannot travel
in a snapshot of the map, and that it would need
.B SCM_RIGHTS
over a channel nothing had built. The mechanism it named
.RB ( shared/net/conn_transfer.h )
was real and the diagnosis was wrong: across two runtimes
.B socketley.pipe
does not hand a descriptor over at all. It is
.BR route_client () ,
which writes into the target's connection maps, runs the target's
.B on_connect
and then keeps the socket where it is and relays the bytes through it. What both
needed was a way to name another runtime's connection and reach it, which is
.BR shared/ipc/route_relay.h .
.PP
So they cross a process boundary now, and what changes for a script is nothing:
the same call, the same return value, the same target name. The origin stays in
the data path either way \(em that is what routing is \(em and a message from a
routed client reaches the target's
.B on_client_message
while the target's reply reaches the client through the origin.
.PP
The four\(hyargument form,
.BR "socketley.pipe(src, fd_a, src, fd_b)" ,
pairs two connections of one server and crosses nothing. It has to be called
from that server's own script, because only that process holds its connections.
.PP
When a runtime holding routed connections goes away \(em stopped, removed,
crashed or killed \(em every runtime on the other end of those routes is told to
let go, and the target's
.B on_disconnect
runs. That is done by the daemon rather than by the departing runtime, because a
killed worker runs no teardown at all.
.PP
.B unroute() ,
.B get_route()
and
.B owner_send()
read the runtime's own tables and always did.
.PP
Where the raise lands is the thing to plan for. At a script's top level it fails
the load, and a worker whose script did not load does not serve: the
.B create
is refused with a reason and the runtime is left
.BR failed .
Inside a callback it is caught and logged like any other callback error, and the
runtime carries on. In
.B on_start
that means a runtime that is up and serving with none of what
.B on_start
was for having happened \(em
.B ls
green, connections accepted \(em so the command that started it exits 1 and
names the callback and the reason. The runtime is left running either way; the
exit code and the message are how the two are told apart.
.PP
Everything that does not reach for the map works in a worker exactly as it does
in the daemon:
.BR socketley.log ,
.BR .http ,
.BR .json_encode / .json_decode ,
the timers,
.BR .subscribe ,
the whole
.B self
table, and every connection and request callback. See
.B "Out-of-process runtimes"
under
.B LIMITATIONS
for the full account.
.RE
.SS Cluster Introspection API
When the daemon runs in cluster mode (\fB\-\-cluster\fR), the
.B socketley.cluster
table provides read-only introspection into the full cluster topology.
All functions return empty tables when not in cluster mode, so scripts work in
both standalone and cluster setups without modification.
.PP
That last sentence holds for cluster mode being off, and it now holds in a
worker process too: the daemon pushes each worker a snapshot of the cluster view
before the frame that loads its script, and these four are answered from it.
.PP
One case is left. A daemon that is in no cluster has no view to push, and an
empty table is what a
.I successful
call returns for a daemon alone in one \(em so with no view installed the four
raise instead of answering, naming themselves. A daemon that
.I joins
a cluster after a worker's script has loaded leaves that script with the refusal
until it is reloaded.
.TP
.B socketley.cluster.daemons() \(-> table
Returns an array of daemon info tables. Each entry has:
.nf
name      string   Daemon name (from \-\-name)
host      string   Advertised address, from gethostname(2), falling
                   back to the daemon name if that call fails
runtimes  number   Count of runtimes on this daemon
.fi
.IP
The local daemon is always the first entry. Remote daemons follow in discovery order.
.TP
.B socketley.cluster.runtimes() \(-> table
Returns a flat array of all runtimes across every daemon in the cluster:
.nf
daemon       string   Name of the daemon hosting this runtime
name         string   Runtime name
type         string   "server", "client", "proxy", or "cache"
port         number   Listen port (0 = internal-only)
group        string   Group tag (empty string if unset)
state        string   "created", "running", "stopped", or "failed"
connections  number   Current connection count
.fi
.TP
.B socketley.cluster.group(name) \(-> table
Returns running members of the named group that have a valid port.
Each entry has:
.nf
daemon       string   Daemon name
host         string   Advertised address
port         number   Listen port
connections  number   Current connection count
.fi
.IP
Useful for building custom routing, health checks, or failover logic from Lua.
.TP
.B socketley.cluster.stats() \(-> table
Aggregate cluster statistics:
.nf
daemons   number   Total daemon count (local + remote)
runtimes  number   Total runtime count across all daemons
running   number   Count of runtimes in "running" state
groups    table    Maps group name (string) \(-> member count (number)
.fi
.SS Cluster Event Callbacks
These optional callbacks fire when the cluster topology changes.
Events are detected during the 2-second scan/publish cycle, so callbacks
may fire up to 2 seconds after the actual change.
They fire only on a
.B running
runtime: a cluster callback is a callback, and a runtime that has been stopped
or has never been started executes no script. A batch of events that reaches a
runtime and then stops it ends there \(em the remaining events in that batch are
not delivered to it.
A daemon is considered stale and triggers
.B on_cluster_leave
after 10 seconds without a heartbeat update.
.TP
.B on_cluster_join(daemon)
Fired when a new remote daemon is discovered in the cluster directory.
.I daemon
is a table with
.B name
(string) and
.B host
(string) fields.
.TP
.B on_cluster_leave(daemon)
Fired when a remote daemon's heartbeat file becomes stale (>10s old) or is
removed from the cluster directory.
.I daemon
is a table with a
.B name
(string) field.
.TP
.B on_group_change(group, members)
Fired when the total member count of a group changes (a runtime joins or
leaves the group, or a daemon with group members appears/disappears).
Only fires on actual count changes, not on every scan cycle.
.I group
is the group name (string).
.I members
is an array of tables, each with
.BR daemon ", " host ", and " port
fields representing the current group membership.
.PP
Example \(em log topology changes and maintain a local routing table:
.nf
local backends = {}

function on_cluster_join(daemon)
    socketley.log("daemon joined: " .. daemon.name .. " at " .. daemon.host)
end

function on_cluster_leave(daemon)
    socketley.log("daemon left: " .. daemon.name)
end

function on_group_change(group, members)
    if group == "api" then
        backends = members   \-\- update routing table
        socketley.log("api group now has " .. #members .. " members")
    end
end

function on_start()
    \-\- Initial snapshot
    local stats = socketley.cluster.stats()
    socketley.log("cluster: " .. stats.daemons .. " daemons, "
                  .. stats.running .. " running runtimes")
end
.fi
.SH DASHBOARD
When
.B daemon.metrics_port
is set in the daemon configuration file, the metrics endpoint serves
multiple routes:
.TP
.B GET /
The dashboard, in the storage interface's look, whose stylesheet and helpers it
takes from the same binary. Its overview shows the daemon's runtimes,
connections, messages, bytes in and out, CPU and resident memory, each with its
last three minutes as a line; its runtimes, each with its state and the same
figures, filtered by type and name; and each runtime with its configuration and
its
.B stats .
Signed in, it starts, stops, restarts and removes a runtime, reloads its Lua,
edits it in a form of its type filled with what
.B show
says (or as the JSON
.B import
takes), and makes one: the type, then a form of every flag
.B create
takes for it, by section, with the command it sends shown as it is filled in.
.IP
An account the
.B idp
runtime that
.B daemon.dashboard_idp
names signed in, of the group
.BR admins ,
is signed in here too, and the page offers that sign-in; signing out signs out
at the provider. Otherwise signing in takes the access key and secret of an
admin of the credentials file
.B daemon.dashboard_credentials
names, a storage runtime's format, as a proxy's
.B "\-\-auth basic:"
checks them: an address that fails five times in fifteen minutes is refused
even the right secret, 429, behind a proxy on this machine the address in its
X\-Forwarded\-For. The session is a cookie sealed with a key the daemon draws at
start, HttpOnly and SameSite=Strict (Secure behind HTTPS), good for twelve
hours; a command also carries the page's own header, which no other site's
request can, goes over the daemon's control socket as the CLI's does, and is
logged with who sent it. The page sends only
.BR create ,
.BR edit ,
.BR import ,
.BR start ,
.BR stop ,
.BR remove ,
.BR reload ,
.BR reload\-lua ,
.BR show ,
.BR stats ,
.B ls
and
.BR ps ,
none with a word holding a space. With credentials named, every route but the
page and the sign\-in needs the session, and
.B /metrics
needs it from anywhere but a scraper on this machine without a proxy between;
without them the page shows the figures, as it always did, and changes
nothing.
.IP
Its figures are drawn over the last fifteen minutes, hour, six hours or day, as
chosen, the value and time under the pointer shown: the endpoint's thread takes
a point every ten seconds for each runtime and for the daemon as a whole
\(em connections and memory as they are, messages, bytes and CPU per second
\(em keeps a day of them, and writes them to
.B .history
in the state directory every five minutes and when the daemon stops, readable
by its user alone, so a restart keeps them; a runtime that is gone keeps its
points until they are a day old.
.BR "GET /api/history?runtime=" name "&range=" seconds "&points=" n
gives one runtime's, or without a name the daemon's, as the mean of each of
.I n
buckets, null for one without a point: an object of
.B from
(the first bucket's start, in seconds since 1970),
.B step
(a bucket's seconds) and the arrays
.BR conn ", " msg ", " in ", " out ", " cpu
(percent of one CPU) and
.BR rss ;
the range is held between a minute and a day, the buckets between 10 and 1440.
.IP
Its Alerts say what is wrong, looked at every ten seconds whether a page is
open or not: a runtime that failed, with the last line it wrote that says why,
or was started again three times within the hour; a certificate a runtime
serves that ends within two weeks (critical within three days, or once it has
ended); the disk under a storage runtime or the state directory, or the host's
memory, at 90% (critical at 95%); a storage runtime at 90% of its
.B \-\-max\-bytes
or a cache that refuses writes at 90% of its
.BR \-\-maxmemory ;
a git repository whose last pull failed; a mail runtime with a hundred messages
waiting for five readings (critical at a thousand), or whose own address a
blocklist of its
.B \-\-dnsbl
names. A level that comes and goes is raised
after three readings and every alert is cleared after three without it; both
are logged, listed on the page with the last hundred, shown on the overview,
and \(em with
.B daemon.alert_webhook
set to an http or https URL \(em posted to it as JSON whose
.B content
and
.B text
carry the message, which Discord, Slack and Telegram's sendMessage read, and
whose
.B state
.RB ( firing ,
.BR resolved )
and
.B alert
carry the rest. A message the hook does not take with a 2xx is posted again,
after a pause that doubles up to eight seconds, and one it has not taken is
kept in
.B .notify
in the state directory and posted after a restart. With
.B daemon.alert_mail
set to
.BI mail:// runtime / address\fR[,\fIaddress\fR...]
each is mailed as well, or instead, by that mail runtime, its subject the
alert's first line, held the same way until the runtime runs. The alerts told of are kept
in
.B .alerts
in the state directory, readable by the daemon's user alone, so a daemon
started again does not tell of them a second time.
.BR "GET /api/alerts" ,
and
.B POST /api/alerts/test
with the page's header posts a test through the hook.
.IP
With
.B daemon.events
naming an mqtt runtime, the daemon publishes on it at QoS 1, retained, how
each runtime stands, on
.BI socketley/runtimes/ name /state
as JSON with its
.BR name ", " type ", " state " and " port ,
at every change and cleared when the runtime is removed; and each alert while
it holds, on
.BI socketley/alerts/ id
as the JSON
.B alert
carries above, cleared when it clears. A subscriber to
.B socketley/#
reads how things stand the moment it subscribes and is told of every change
after. The broker may be made after the daemon starts: until it runs, what is
owed waits in
.B .notify
in the state directory, as the webhook's does.
.IP
Its Logs follow the daemon's recent log live, filtered by runtime, by level and
by text, and each runtime's page shows its own; a runtime that failed says the
last warning or error it wrote, where it is listed and on its page, beside how
often it was started again. Each worker writes its stderr into a pipe of its
own, which the daemon reads, passes on to its own stderr unchanged \(em the
journal gets every line as before \(em and keeps, named by the runtime, with
the daemon's own lines, the last 10,000 in memory:
.BR "GET /api/logs?since=" seq "&max=" n "&runtime=" name "&level=" warn .
.IP
Its Domains show every certificate a runtime serves \(em its names, its issuer,
how long it has, whether it is issued by itself and whether a wildcard waits
for its zone's delegation, and the last line of a failed issuance \(em each
entrance's routes, a name to the runtime it goes to, and the zones of the dns
runtimes, whose records it edits: the text is checked as the runtime reads a
zone file before it is written, and a serial left as it was is raised, so
secondaries take the change.
.BR "GET /api/edge" ,
and
.B GET
and
.B PUT /api/zone
for a file a dns runtime serves and no other. The endpoint listens on 127.0.0.1 only; on a server, reach it through an
SSH tunnel, \fBssh \-L 9100:127.0.0.1:9100\fR \fIhost\fR, and open
\fBhttp://localhost:9100/\fR. On an installed system the port is set in
\fB/etc/socketley/config.lua\fR, as \fBdaemon = { metrics_port = 9100 }\fR,
and read when the service starts.
.TP
.B GET /metrics
Prometheus exposition format (existing). Suitable for scraping by
Prometheus, Grafana Agent, or similar monitoring stacks.
.TP
.B GET /api/overview
JSON object with high-level daemon statistics:
.nf
{
    "version": "1.1.0",
    "uptime_seconds": 86400,
    "runtimes_total": 5,
    "runtimes_running": 4,
    "connections_total": 18230,
    "connections_active": 142,
    "messages_total": 9120044,
    "bytes_in": 12345678,
    "bytes_out": 9876543,
    "cpu_seconds": 812.40,
    "rss_bytes": 163577856,
    "memory_bytes": 94371840,
    "processes": 5,
    "cpus": 4
}
.fi
.IP
.B cpu_seconds
is the user and system CPU time of the daemon and every process its runtimes
run in, read from
.BR /proc ;
the change between two readings over the time between them is the CPU used.
.B rss_bytes
is their resident memory added up, which counts a page shared between shards once per shard, and
.B memory_bytes
what they cost the machine, each shared page counted by the share the process holds of it, so that the figures of the
runtimes add up to the memory they hold between them (the kernel's Pss).
.B processes
is how many there are, and
.B cpus
the CPUs online.
.TP
.B GET /api/runtimes
JSON array of all runtimes with name, type, port, state, connections,
message/byte counters, and
.BR cpu_seconds ", " rss_bytes ", " memory_bytes " and " processes
for the runtime's own processes, one per shard (none while it is stopped).
.TP
.BI "GET /api/runtime/" name
JSON object for a single runtime identified by
.IR name .
Returns 404 if the runtime does not exist.
.PP
The
.B dashboard
CLI command prints the dashboard URL:
.nf
$ socketley dashboard
Dashboard: http://localhost:9100/
.fi
.SH SERVICE MESH
The proxy runtime includes service-mesh primitives that can be composed
to build sidecar proxies, API gateways, or resilient service-to-service
communication layers.
.SS Health Checks
Active health checking probes backends at a regular interval
.RB ( \-\-health\-interval )
using either a TCP connect or an HTTP GET
.RB ( \-\-health\-check ).
A backend is marked unhealthy after
.B \-\-health\-threshold
consecutive failures and is excluded from the load balancer pool.
A single successful check restores the backend.
.SS Circuit Breaker
The circuit breaker prevents cascading failures by tracking consecutive
errors per backend. It has three states:
.TP
.B closed
Normal operation. Requests are forwarded to the backend. Errors are
counted.
.TP
.B open
Triggered when errors reach
.BR \-\-circuit\-threshold .
All requests fail immediately without attempting a connection. After
.B \-\-circuit\-timeout
seconds, the circuit moves to half-open.
.TP
.B half-open
A single probe request is allowed through \(em one, not one per
selection: while that probe is outstanding the backend is unavailable to
everyone else. If it succeeds, the circuit returns to
.BR closed .
If it fails, the circuit re-opens.
.SS Response Deadline
A backend that accepts connections and then stops answering fails no
check: a TCP health probe connects to it successfully, and a request
that never completes is counted as neither an error nor a success, so
the circuit breaker has nothing to open on.
.B \-\-backend\-timeout
(default 2 seconds) bounds the wait for the first byte of a response and
records the expiry as a backend error, which is what puts such a backend
through the breaker like any other failure. The request is retried
elsewhere if
.B \-\-retry
allows it and answered
.B 504 Gateway Timeout
otherwise.
.PP
The deadline covers the handshake as well as the answer: a backend that
never completes its connect inside it is answered
.B 504 Gateway Timeout
too, rather than the client being closed without a response. A request
routed through an
.B @group
backend is charged to the member it actually reached, so a group member
that stalls is taken out of rotation by the same breaker as a listed
backend.
.PP
The deadline is on by default and only covers the wait for the first
byte, so a large or streaming response body is unaffected. An endpoint
that legitimately needs more than 2 seconds to start answering has to be
given a longer
.B \-\-backend\-timeout
or
.BR "\-\-backend\-timeout 0" ;
otherwise the proxy treats it as hung. It applies to HTTP mode only \(em
in TCP mode there is no request boundary to measure from.
.SS Request Framing
In HTTP mode the proxy is strict about how a request's header block is
framed, and refuses anything it and a backend could read differently.
These are the same rules a socketley server applies to a request it
receives directly, from one implementation \(em see
.B "Request Framing"
under
.BR "SERVER FLAGS" .
The proxy forwards bytes to a backend that parses them independently; if
the two disagree about where a request ends, the next request on the
same connection starts in the wrong place, which is request smuggling.
Normalising the request instead would mean guessing which way the
backend will read it, so the proxy declines to guess.
.PP
A request is answered
.B 400 Bad Request
and its connection is closed, without contacting any backend, when the
header block contains any of:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
a bare LF or a bare CR \(em every line must end with CRLF
.IP \(bu 2
whitespace between a field name and its colon
.RB ( "Content\-Length : 6" )
.IP \(bu 2
a continuation (obs\-fold) line, i.e. one starting with a space or tab
.IP \(bu 2
a field line with no colon
.IP \(bu 2
both
.B Content\-Length
and
.BR Transfer\-Encoding ,
or more than one
.B Content\-Length
field
.IP \(bu 2
a
.B Content\-Length
that is not a whole decimal number
.RB ( "Content\-Length: 6x" )
.IP \(bu 2
a
.B Transfer\-Encoding
whose final coding is not
.B chunked
.RB ( "Transfer\-Encoding: gzip" ,
.BR "Transfer\-Encoding: chunked, gzip" ,
or
.B chunked
listed twice) \(em such a request has no determinable body length at all
.RE
.PP
Every request on a connection is framed and checked on its own, including
one pipelined behind another. Only the body a request declares travels
with it: bytes past its
.B Content\-Length
are not body, and are held back and parsed as the next request rather
than forwarded. Pipelining still works \(em a second request sent before
the first is answered is served \(em but it is answered in turn, after
the same checks, and a smuggling attempt hidden behind a clean request is
refused exactly as it would be if it had been sent first. A request held
this way is subject to the same 10 second deadline as a half\-arrived one:
if the exchange in front of it never reaches an end this proxy can point
at \(em a backend whose response is framed in a way it cannot read \(em the
held request is answered
.B 408 Request Timeout
rather than left waiting. A
.B chunked
request is the one body this proxy cannot measure, because it never
de\-chunks; its body is forwarded as an opaque stream, so a request
pipelined behind a chunked one is framed by the backend alone.
.PP
Compliant clients emit none of these. A request whose header block is
still arriving is held until the terminating blank line, subject to the
16 KiB header limit
.RB ( "431 Request Header Fields Too Large" )
and a 10 second header deadline
.RB ( "408 Request Timeout" ).
.PP
There is a second, larger limit behind the header one, and it counts the
whole of what is buffered for an exchange rather than one header block: at
most 1 MiB is held for a connection whose request has already been
forwarded \(em the body it sends past its declared
.BR Content\-Length ,
and the requests pipelined behind it. Nothing drains that until the
response in front of it is finished, so a client that writes faster than
the backend answers reaches it on a well\-formed connection. It is
answered
.B 413 Request Entity Too Large
and the connection is closed. The one exception is the same one the
gateway statuses make: if part of the backend's response is already on
that client's wire, no status line is written behind it \(em it would land
at an offset that is not a message boundary \(em and the connection is
closed with the response left visibly truncated.
.PP
The header deadline starts at the first byte of a request and ends when
its header block does. It is fixed, is not configurable, and is
deliberately independent of
.BR \-\-idle\-timeout ,
which defaults to 0 (never) and so cannot be relied on to end a
half\-sent request: a client that sends a request line and then stops
sends no further bytes for the 16 KiB limit to count, and would
otherwise hold its connection, its descriptor and its 64 KB buffer for
as long as it liked. Neither an idle keep\-alive connection between
requests nor a slow request body is measured against it \(em only the
gap between a request's first byte and its terminating blank line.
.PP
The same strictness is applied to backend responses, but only to decide
whether the backend connection may be reused: a response whose length
cannot be established with certainty \(em because its header block was
split across reads, or was not strictly CRLF-framed, or because more
bytes arrived than the length it declared \(em is forwarded to
the client unchanged, and its connection is closed instead of returned
to the pool. A backend that answers
.B Connection: close
is not pooled either: it has said the socket is going away.
.PP
A
.B Connection: close
sent by the
.I client
does not do this. It is a hop-by-hop field about the client's own
connection to the proxy, so it is not forwarded upstream and it does not
end the backend connection: the proxy answers the request, returns the
backend socket to the pool, and closes the client connection as asked.
The field is a comma-separated list and is parsed token\-wise and
case\-insensitively, as it is on the server, so
.B "Connection: keep\-alive, close"
counts as close while a value that merely begins with those letters \(em
.B "Connection: clientinfo"
\(em does not.
A client that opens a connection per request \(em curl, a health checker,
any HTTP library used without a session \(em therefore costs one accept
and no backend connect, the same as a keep-alive client.
.PP
A connection already in the pool is checked before it is handed to the
next request, and one with unread data on it is closed rather than
reused. Nothing was asked of an idle pooled socket, so anything waiting
on it is something the backend sent unbidden \(em the tail of an answer
whose length was mis\-declared, or a second answer volunteered behind the
first. Reusing it would make those bytes the head of the next client's
response, so the connection is dropped and a fresh one opened. The proxy
logs this once per instance.
.PP
Reuse covers an
.B @group
backend as well as a listed one. A pooled connection is filed under the
endpoint it was opened to, never under the group, so it is only ever
handed back to a request routed to that same member; a member that leaves
the group has its pooled connections closed at once rather than left to
the idle sweep.
.PP
Reuse covers a
.B TLS
backend too, and the session is pooled with the descriptor rather than the
descriptor alone \(em a reused connection carries on the record layer it
already had, at the sequence number it had reached. A TLS connection carries
more than an address: a negotiated session, a peer identity verified against a
name, a client certificate that may have been presented, and a record layer
part way through a stream. The endpoint the socket is filed under answers three
of those, because it is what the name was pinned to before the handshake and
what the certificate was checked against; the client certificate and the trust
store belong to the runtime and are rebuilt on every start, and the pool is
emptied on every stop, so a pooled session can never outlive the configuration
that verified it.
.PP
What a pooled TLS socket cannot do is inherit an answer nobody asked for. A
cleartext socket with bytes waiting on it is destroyed rather than reused,
because a socket in the pool has been asked nothing since its last answer
completed. That test cannot be carried across a record layer unchanged: a
TLS peer that has said nothing still sends records \(em a session ticket, a key
update, a
.BR close_notify .
So the socket is drained into the session and the question is put to the record
layer instead of to the kernel: records that produce no application data are
the peer's own housekeeping and the connection is kept, and application data is
a response that belongs to a previous exchange and the connection is dropped.
The second case is logged once per runtime.
.SS Retries
When
.B \-\-retry
is set, failed requests are automatically retried on the next backend
in the load balancer pool. By default only idempotent methods are
retried; use
.B \-\-retry\-all
to include POST and PATCH.
.SS mTLS
Both legs can be encrypted.
.BR "\-\-tls \-\-cert \-\-key"
terminates TLS on the proxy's own port and
.B \-\-client\-ca
makes it demand a certificate from its callers;
.B https://
on a backend address, or any of
.BR \-\-ca ,
.B \-\-client\-cert
and
.BR \-\-client\-key ,
takes the backend leg to TLS, with the chain and the endpoint's name verified
and a client certificate presented when one is configured. A full sidecar pair
looks like this:
.nf
socketley create proxy edge -p 8443 \\
    \-\-tls \-\-cert edge.pem \-\-key edge\-key.pem \\
    \-\-client\-ca mesh\-ca.pem \\
    \-\-backend https://10.0.0.7:8443 \\
    \-\-ca mesh\-ca.pem \\
    \-\-client\-cert edge.pem \-\-client\-key edge\-key.pem \\
    \-\-sidecar \-s
.fi
.PP
A TLS backend connection is returned to the connection pool and reused, so a
busy sidecar pays for a handshake per idle period rather than per request. The
pooled socket keeps its session, goes back only to the endpoint it was opened
to, and is dropped rather than reused if the backend has left application data
on it.
.B \-\-health\-check http
works against a TLS backend and probes it over TLS, verified to the same
standard as the data path \(em see
.B LIMITATIONS
for what the probe does and does not establish.
See
.B "mTLS Flags"
under
.B "PROXY FLAGS"
for what is verified and what cannot be switched off.
.SS Sidecar Deployment
The
.B \-\-sidecar
flag enables a production-ready default set: TCP health checks, circuit
breaker (threshold 5, timeout 30s), 2 retries, and graceful drain. Deploy
alongside your application:
.nf
socketley create proxy myapp \-\-sidecar \\
    \-\-backend 127.0.0.1:3000 -p 8080 -s
.fi
.PP
The proxy listens on port 8080 and forwards to the local application on
port 3000. Unhealthy backends are automatically removed, and the circuit
breaker prevents thundering-herd retries during outages.
.PP
.B \-\-sidecar
does not change
.BR \-\-backend\-timeout ,
which is 2 seconds by default and charges its expiry to the circuit
breaker that
.B \-\-sidecar
enables. If the application can take longer than that to send its first
response byte, say so explicitly:
.nf
socketley create proxy myapp \-\-sidecar \-\-backend\-timeout 30 \\
    \-\-backend 127.0.0.1:3000 -p 8080 -s
.fi
.SH RUNTIME STATES
.TP
.B created
Initial state after creation.
.TP
.B running
Active and processing I/O.
.TP
.B stopped
Stopped but can be restarted.
.TP
.B failed
Setup failed, cannot recover.
.SH EXAMPLES
Create a simple server (daemon is managed by systemd when installed):
.PP
.nf
socketley create server myserver -p 9000 -s
.fi
.PP
Create client connecting to server:
.PP
.nf
socketley create client myclient -t 127.0.0.1:9000 -s
.fi
.PP
Create HTTP proxy with load balancing:
.PP
.nf
socketley create proxy gateway -p 8080 \\
    --backend 127.0.0.1:9001,127.0.0.1:9002 \\
    --strategy round-robin -s
.fi
.PP
Server with bash output (messages to stdout):
.PP
.nf
socketley create server echo -p 9000 -b -s
# With timestamp and prefix:
socketley create server echo -p 9000 -bpt -s
.fi
.PP
Send message to runtime:
.PP
.nf
socketley send myserver "Hello from CLI"
socketley send myclient "Message to server"
.fi
.PP
Create persistent cache with maxmemory:
.PP
.nf
socketley create cache store -p 9000 \\
    --persistent /data/cache.bin \\
    --maxmemory 256M --eviction allkeys-lru -s
.fi
.PP
Create cache with RESP mode (Redis-compatible):
.PP
.nf
socketley create cache redis-compat -p 6379 --resp -s
redis-cli -p 6379 SET mykey myval
redis-cli -p 6379 GET mykey
.fi
.PP
Server with connection limits and rate limiting:
.PP
.nf
socketley create server api -p 9000 \\
    --max-connections 100 --rate-limit 1000 -s
.fi
.PP
View runtime statistics:
.PP
.nf
socketley stats myserver
# Output: name, type, port, connections, total_messages, bytes_in/out, ...
.fi
.PP
Restart a running runtime:
.PP
.nf
socketley reload myserver
# Stops and restarts the runtime
socketley reload *
# Restarts all running runtimes
.fi
.PP
Hot-reload Lua script:
.PP
.nf
socketley reload-lua myserver
# Re-loads the Lua script without stopping the runtime
socketley reload-lua *
# Hot-reloads Lua on all runtimes with scripts
.fi
.PP
Start/stop all runtimes:
.PP
.nf
socketley start *
# Starts all non-running runtimes
socketley stop *
# Stops all running runtimes
socketley remove *
# Stops all and removes everything
.fi
.PP
Multiple names and glob patterns:
.PP
.nf
socketley stop myserver myclient
# Stops both runtimes
socketley stop my*
# Stops all runtimes starting with "my"
socketley stats cache?
# Shows stats for cache1, cache2, etc.
socketley start myserver cache*
# Starts myserver and all cache-prefixed runtimes
socketley remove api-* test-*
# Removes all api- and test- prefixed runtimes
.fi
.PP
Interactive mode (live terminal session):
.PP
.nf
# Start server and enter interactive session
socketley create server echo -p 9000
socketley start echo -i
# Type messages to broadcast, see received messages
# Press Ctrl+C to detach (server keeps running)

# Attach to already-running cache
socketley start mycache -i
# Type cache commands: set foo bar, get foo, etc.
.fi
.PP
Interactive edit (opens JSON config in $EDITOR):
.PP
.nf
socketley edit myserver           # Opens config in editor
socketley edit myserver -r        # Opens editor, auto-reloads Lua after save
socketley edit myserver -p 9001   # Flag-based edit (direct)
.fi
.PP
Cache replication (leader-follower):
.PP
.nf
socketley create cache leader -p 9000 -s
socketley create cache follower -p 9001 --replicate 127.0.0.1:9000 -s
.fi
.PP
Encrypted server. The listener terminates TLS itself; everything above it \(em
line protocol, HTTP, WebSocket, RESP \(em runs on the decrypted stream, so
this same runtime serves
.B https://
and
.B wss://
on the one port:
.PP
.nf
socketley create server secure -p 9443 \\
    --tls --cert server.pem --key server-key.pem -s
curl --cacert server.pem https://localhost:9443/
.fi
.PP
Mutual TLS \(em a client must present a certificate signed by the given CA:
.PP
.nf
socketley create server mtls -p 9443 \\
    --tls --cert server.pem --key server-key.pem \\
    --ca trusted-clients.pem -s
.fi
.PP
Renewing a certificate: the files are read at start, so
.B reload
picks up a new one.
.PP
.nf
socketley reload secure
.fi
.PP
An incomplete configuration is refused rather than served in the clear:
.PP
.nf
socketley create server oops -p 9443 --tls --cert server.pem
# --tls: a TLS listener needs --key <file> to go with --cert
# exit status 1
.fi
.PP
Encrypted client. The dial is the mirror of the listener above: one flag turns
the transport on, and the peer is verified on both counts \(em the chain
against the trust store, and the certificate against the host in
.BR \-t .
.PP
.nf
# verify against the system trust store
socketley create client feed -t events.example.com:9443 --tls -s

# verify against a private CA instead
socketley create client feed -t 10.0.0.7:9443 \\
    --tls --ca internal-ca.pem -s

# and present a certificate of our own, for a peer that demands one
socketley create client feed -t 10.0.0.7:9443 \\
    --tls --ca internal-ca.pem \\
    --cert client.pem --key client-key.pem -s
.fi
.PP
A peer whose certificate does not verify costs the socket \(em the client
re-dials on its backoff and never falls back to cleartext. There is no
skip-verify flag: encryption without verification is protection against
somebody recording the wire and none at all against somebody sitting on it,
and the two look identical from the outside.
.PP
The same rules apply as on the listener. Half a certificate pair is refused,
a trust store without
.B \-\-tls
is refused, and
.B reload
is how a renewed client certificate is installed:
.PP
.nf
socketley create client bad -t host:9443 --tls --cert client.pem
# --cert: needs --key <file> as well -- a certificate with no key
# presents nothing
# exit status 1
.fi
.PP
Graceful shutdown (drain pending writes):
.PP
.nf
socketley create server api -p 9000 --drain -s
socketley stop api   # Flushes write queues before closing
.fi
.PP
UDP server (fire-and-forget messaging):
.PP
.nf
socketley create server metrics -p 9000 --udp -b -s
echo "cpu.load 0.42" | socat - UDP:localhost:9000
.fi
.PP
UDP client:
.PP
.nf
socketley create client udpcli -t 127.0.0.1:9000 --udp -s
.fi
.PP
Create read-only cache (for shared lookups):
.PP
.nf
socketley create cache lookup -p 9000 --mode readonly -s
.fi
.PP
Master mode server (one controller, many viewers):
.PP
.nf
socketley create server broadcast -p 9000 \\
    --mode master --master-pw secret -s
# Client A: sends "master secret" \(-> "master: ok"
# Client A: sends "hello" \(-> broadcast to all others
# Client B: sends "hello" \(-> silently dropped
.fi
.PP
Master mode with forwarding:
.PP
.nf
socketley create server ctrl -p 9000 \\
    --mode master --master-pw admin123 --send-to master -s
# Non-master messages forwarded to master as "[<fd>] msg"
.fi
.PP
WebSocket server (auto-detected, no flags needed):
.PP
.nf
socketley create server ws -p 9000 -s
# Browser: new WebSocket("ws://localhost:9000")
# Raw TCP clients also work on the same port
.fi
.PP
Cache access through server:
.PP
.nf
socketley create cache db -p 9001 -s
socketley create server api -p 9000 --cache db -s
# Client sends: "cache set user:1 alice" \(-> "ok"
# Client sends: "cache get user:1" \(-> "alice"
# Client sends: "hello" \(-> broadcast as usual
.fi
.PP
Dynamic proxy with group-based backend discovery:
.PP
.nf
socketley create server api1 -p 9001 -g api -s
socketley create server api2 -p 9002 -g api -s
socketley create proxy gw -p 8080 --backend @api -s
# Proxy routes to all running members of group "api"

# Add a new member dynamically (no proxy restart needed)
socketley create server api3 -p 9003 -g api -s

# Mix group and direct backends
socketley create proxy gw2 -p 8081 \\
    --backend @api,10.0.0.5:9000 -s
.fi
.PP
Lua HTTP worker (auto-detected, no flags needed):
.PP
.nf
socketley create server api -p 9000 --lua handler.lua -s
# handler.lua:
# function on_http_request(req)
#     return {status=200, headers={["Content-Type"]="application/json"},
#             body='{"hello":"world"}'}
# end
# curl http://localhost:9000/api
.fi
.PP
Sidecar proxy with health checks and circuit breaker:
.PP
.nf
socketley create proxy sidecar -p 8080 \-\-sidecar \\
    \-\-backend 127.0.0.1:3000 -s
.fi
.PP
Proxy with HTTP health checks and retries:
.PP
.nf
socketley create proxy gateway -p 8080 \\
    \-\-backend 10.0.0.1:9000,10.0.0.2:9000 \\
    \-\-health\-check http \-\-health\-path /ready \\
    \-\-health\-interval 10 \-\-health\-threshold 5 \\
    \-\-retry 3 \-\-circuit\-threshold 10 -s
.fi
.PP
Proxy with mTLS on both legs. The listener terminates TLS and demands a
certificate from its callers; the backend leg dials TLS, verifies the chain
and the endpoint's name, and presents a certificate of its own:
.PP
.nf
socketley create proxy secure \-p 8443 \\
    \-\-tls \-\-cert proxy.pem \-\-key proxy\-key.pem \\
    \-\-client\-ca trusted\-clients.pem \\
    \-\-client\-cert backend\-client.pem \-\-client\-key backend\-client\-key.pem \\
    \-\-ca mesh\-ca.pem \\
    \-\-backend https://10.0.0.1:9000 -s
# Backend connections are pooled with their sessions: a handshake per
# idle period, not per request.
# --health-check http probes over TLS and verifies the backend too.
.fi
.PP
TLS to the backend only \(em the proxy listens in cleartext on loopback and
encrypts the hop that leaves the machine:
.PP
.nf
socketley create proxy egress \-p 8080 \\
    \-\-backend https://api.example.com \-s
# Verified against the system CA store, name checked against the certificate.
# A private CA goes in --ca; there is no way to skip verification.
.fi
.PP
Open the live dashboard:
.PP
.nf
socketley dashboard
# Dashboard: http://localhost:9100/
# Open in browser for live runtime view
.fi
.PP
Query dashboard API:
.PP
.nf
curl http://localhost:9100/api/overview
curl http://localhost:9100/api/runtimes
curl http://localhost:9100/api/runtime/myserver
.fi
.PP
Load complex configuration:
.PP
.nf
socketley --lua /etc/socketley/setup.lua
.fi
.SH CLUSTER MODE
.B "The cluster directory is as privileged as the daemons that read it."
A proxy with an
.BI @ group
backend picks its targets out of the files in that directory, so whoever can
write there decides where that traffic goes. Treat write access to it exactly
as you would treat the control socket: it belongs to the daemons and to nobody
else. A shared volume between containers you control is the intended
arrangement; a directory any other workload can write to is not.
.PP
Multi-daemon cluster discovery allows proxies to route to runtimes on remote
daemons. Each daemon publishes its state to a shared directory (typically a
Docker volume) every 2 seconds. Stale entries (heartbeat older than 10s) are
automatically excluded.
.PP
.B How it works:
.RS
.IP 1. 3
Each daemon writes its state to
.IR dir / name .json
.IP 2. 3
Every 2 seconds, each daemon scans the directory for peer files
.IP 3. 3
Proxy
.B @group
resolution includes both local and remote group members
.IP 4. 3
On graceful shutdown, the daemon deletes its file
.RE
.PP
.B Restarting a node, and name conflicts:
.PP
A name is one node's.  Two daemons publishing one file would give every peer an
endpoint set belonging to neither, and split traffic for
.I api1
between two machines that each believe they are api1 \(em so a daemon that finds
its name held by a live entry is refused
.BR --cluster ,
and told so.
.PP
A node that was killed and restarted is not that case.  Every restart policy
this project documents \(em a container restart, a redeploy, systemd's
.BR "Restart=on-failure" ,
a crash loop \(em brings the node back in a second or two, well inside the 10s
threshold, and it meets its own entry still fresh.  It reclaims it.
.PP
The entry says who wrote it: the advertised host, the daemon's control socket
path, the kernel boot id, and the pid.  A fresh entry may be taken over only
when the first three are this daemon's own, and that is a proof rather than a
guess \(em a unix socket path admits one process at a time, and everything else
that reaches a daemon connects to that path instead of binding it.  So on one
machine, under one hostname, whoever holds the path
.I is
the daemon; an entry carrying our own three was written by a process that is
gone.  A different machine has a different boot id, and a fresh entry cannot
predate a reboot.  The pid is published for the operator and never used to
decide: a restarted container's pid namespace is new.
.PP
What this cannot tell apart is two containers on
.I one
host given the
.I same
hostname and both on the default socket path.  Their entries are identical, and
so is the address every peer would dial for either of them.
.PP
A daemon that could not join keeps serving \(em it is a working server that is
not in a cluster \(em and says so in three places: the exit status of the
command, an
.B ERROR
line in its log naming the holder, and every
.B socketley cluster
subcommand, which reports
.B "cluster mode is off"
with the reason instead of the same "not in cluster mode" a daemon that was
never given
.B --cluster
reports.  Clustering is never accepted and silently not applied.
.PP
.B Docker Compose example:
.PP
A complete, working one is
.IR examples/cluster/docker-compose.yml ,
and the same shape with assertions on it is
.IR test/docker/ .
Three things about it are not optional and were wrong in the example this
paragraph used to print:
.RS
.IP \(bu 3
.B seccomp.
Docker's default profile does not permit
.BR io_uring_setup (2),
.BR io_uring_enter (2)
or
.BR io_uring_register (2),
and socketley's whole I/O layer is a ring, so every node dies at startup with
.B "failed to init event loop"
under it.  Run the containers with a profile that allows those three syscalls
\(em during development,
.BR "security_opt: [seccomp:unconfined]" .
.IP \(bu 3
.B entrypoint.
The image's ENTRYPOINT is
.BR socketley ,
so a compose
.B command:
is passed to it as arguments.  A node that needs a shell has to say
.BR "entrypoint: [\(dq/bin/sh\(dq, \(dq-c\(dq]" ,
and give its script as a one\-element list \(em compose word\-splits a bare
string into an argv and everything past the first
.B &
is lost.
.IP \(bu 3
.B Readiness.
.B daemon --name
and
.B daemon --cluster
are configuration commands against a daemon that is already running, and they
auto\-start a detached one when the socket does not answer.  Sleeping for a
second before them is a race whose loss forks a second daemon and takes the
container down without a message.  Wait for the daemon's own
.B "daemon started"
line instead.
.RE
.PP
Alternatively, use a Lua config file:
.PP
.nf
\-\- /etc/socketley/setup.lua
daemon = { name = "api1", cluster = "/cluster" }
runtimes = {
    { type = "server", name = "web", port = 9001, group = "api", start = true }
}
.fi
.PP
.nf
socketley daemon &
sleep 1 && socketley --lua /etc/socketley/setup.lua
.fi
.PP
.B Inspecting the cluster:
.PP
.nf
socketley cluster ls
socketley cluster ps
socketley cluster group api
socketley cluster stats
socketley cluster watch
.fi
.PP
.B Stale detection:
.TS
l l l.
Scenario	Behavior	Recovery
_
Graceful shutdown	File deleted	Immediate
Container killed	Stale heartbeat	~10 seconds
Container restart	File overwritten	Immediate
.TE
.SS Raft mode (experimental, opt-in)
.B Experimental.
A daemon started with
.B \-\-raft\-listen
runs one Raft node on its own loop, and nothing else changes: the cluster
directory above, and every runtime, proxy and cache that is not declared as a
cluster runtime, behave exactly as without it. The cluster elects a leader,
replicates a log to disk and keeps membership, and the state machine behind the
log holds the cluster's runtimes (see
.B Cluster runtimes
below): a runtime is declared once, placed on distinct nodes by the leader, and
started on a survivor when its node dies; a cache is replicated, and when the
node that holds its primary dies the leader promotes the follower that holds
the most (see
.B Cluster caches
below).
.PP
.B Flags
(on the command line that starts the daemon, like
.BR \-\-cpus ;
refused beside
.B \-\-name
and
.BR \-\-cluster ):
.TP
.BI \-\-raft\-listen " ip:port"
Enable Raft mode and accept peer connections here. Numeric only: a peer's
address travels through the replicated log and is dialled without a DNS
lookup.
.TP
.BI \-\-raft\-advertise " ip:port"
The address peers dial, when it is not the listening one. Required when
.B \-\-raft\-listen
is a wildcard.
.TP
.BI \-\-raft\-name " name"
This node's name in the membership, and the name its certificate must carry
(as a DNS subjectAltName or common name). Defaults to the advertised address.
A name is one node's: a node that joins under the name of a member, or under one
that reads the same once
.B {node}
has made it safe for a command line (see
.BR "Cluster runtimes" ),
is refused by the leader with the reason, and stops with that reason in its log
and in
.BR "cluster raft\-status" ,
because two nodes named alike would give every cluster runtime the same socket
path or file.
.TP
.B \-\-raft\-init
Found a new single-voter cluster, on first start only.
.TP
.BI \-\-raft\-join " ip:port"
Join the cluster a member at this address belongs to, on first start only. The
node is added as a learner, receives the log without voting, and is promoted
to voter by a single-server change once its log has caught up to the leader's
commit index. A member that is not the leader passes the request on.
.TP
.BI "\-\-raft\-cert " file " \-\-raft\-key " file " \-\-raft\-ca " file
Mutual TLS between members, which is required. Every member's certificate is
signed by the CA; a connection whose certificate does not name the member it
introduces itself as \(em by the name membership records for its id, or for a
newcomer the name it claims \(em is closed. The join target, whose id is not
known yet, is held to the address that was dialled.
.TP
.B \-\-raft\-insecure
Run Raft in cleartext. Refused beside the certificate flags; without it and
without them the daemon refuses to start.
.TP
.BI \-\-raft\-down\-after " duration"
How long the leader waits for a node's lease request before it declares the
node down and moves its replicas: seconds, or with an
.B s
or
.B ms
suffix (\c
.BR 4 ,
.BR 4s ,
.BR 3500ms ).
Default four election timeouts (4 s). Refused below three election timeouts,
because a leader change would then look like a node loss. Every member of one
cluster should use the same value; see
.B Cluster runtimes
for what depends on it.
.PP
A daemon whose state directory already holds Raft state ignores
.B \-\-raft\-init
and
.B \-\-raft\-join
and comes back as the member it was. One with no state and neither flag
refuses to start. A daemon that cannot bind the port or read its state exits 2
rather than serve without the cluster it was asked for.
.PP
.B On disk:
.IR state-dir /raft/
holds
.B node\-id
(a random 64-bit id, generated once),
.BI wal\- seq .log
(CRC32C-checked records: hard state, entries, truncations) and
.BI snap\- index \- term
files, all 0600. Every write is followed by an fsync linked behind it in
io_uring, and nothing a node says to a peer leaves before the fsync reports.
Recovery cuts a torn tail at the first bad record. The log is compacted every
1000 applied entries.
.PP
.B Timing:
100 ms ticks measured from elapsed monotonic time, an election timeout of
1\(en2 s, heartbeats every tick, pre-vote and check-quorum. A leader stopped by
SIGTERM hands leadership to its most up-to-date voter (TimeoutNow) before it
exits, so the cluster does not wait out an election timeout.
.PP
.B "socketley cluster raft\-status"
prints this node's id and name, role, term, leader, commit and applied
indexes, the log's range, voters, learners, whether a quorum of voters was
heard from within an election timeout, and the transport and WAL counters. It
exits 0 with a leader and a reachable quorum, 2 without (saying so on its last
line), and 1 on a daemon not in Raft mode.
.PP
.B Cluster runtimes.
A cluster runtime is declared once, for the cluster, and every node applies the
same log:
.PP
.nf
socketley cluster create server api \-p 8080 \-\-replicas 2
socketley cluster create server api \-\-listen unix:/run/api\-{node}.sock
socketley cluster scale api 3
socketley cluster remove api
socketley cluster placement
.fi
.PP
.B cluster create
takes
.IR type ,
.I name
and the flags
.B create
takes, plus
.B \-\-replicas
(default 1). It is a server, a proxy, a client or a cache (see
.BR "Cluster caches" ).
The flags are stored as a list of arguments, so a value with a space in it
stays one argument, and run as they are on every node that holds a replica, so a file they
name must exist on each node (a relative path is made absolute against the
caller's directory, as for
.BR create ),
and
.B {node}
in them is replaced by the name of the node running the replica
.RB ( \-\-raft\-name ,
with anything but letters, digits,
.BR \- ,
.B _
and
.B .
made
.BR _ ).
That is what lets several nodes of a test share one host: two replicas that
both ask for
.B \-p 8080
would collide, and
.B \-\-listen unix:/run/api\-{node}.sock
does not. On separate hosts each node binds its own
.B \-p
and needs no placeholder. The flags are also run through
.B create \-\-test
on the node that takes the command, so a typo is refused there, with exit 1,
and not found on every node at once; \c
.B \-\-test
itself is refused in a spec.
.PP
A command may be given to any node: a follower hands it to the leader. Success
is silent, and the command has taken effect on the node that took it when it
returns, or the CLI says it did not within five seconds and exits 2. Exit 1 is a
refusal (a duplicate, an unknown name, a replica count outside 1 to 64, a
runtime of that name already on this node, bad flags), exit 2 a cluster with no
leader that can take it. On a daemon that is not in Raft mode every verb says so
and exits 1.
.PP
.B cluster scale
changes the replica count, taking a replica from the most loaded node when it
shrinks. A runtime has no edit: remove it and declare it again.
.PP
.B Placement.
The leader alone decides, and writes each assignment to the log, so every node
agrees on it. The replicas of one runtime go to distinct nodes, one to the node
that holds the fewest replicas and the lowest node id on a tie. When a node is
declared down its replicas are moved to live nodes, and a node that comes back,
or joins, is not given work back: nothing is rebalanced, so a running replica
is never moved for tidiness. Asking for more replicas than there are live nodes
runs what fits;
.B cluster placement
says how many replicas are unplaced. It prints the runtimes with their
replicas, where each is placed and how many are unplaced, each cache's primary
and the copies it has (see
.BR "Cluster caches" ),
the nodes with the
number of replicas each holds and, on the leader, whether the leader counts each
up and when it last heard from it, and this node's replicas with what each is
doing (running, waiting for a lease, failed with the reason, or blocked by a
local runtime of the same name).
.PP
.B On each node,
a reconciliation runs at the end of every Raft tick and compares what the log
says the node owns with what it runs. It creates a replica with
.B create \-s
and stops, starts and removes it with
.BR stop ,
.B start
and
.BR remove :
the same code as the CLI, and nothing else spawns a replica. It never waits
for a worker to come up and runs at most four commands a tick, so a replica
whose startup hangs, or fifty arriving at once, cannot stall the Raft loop; a
failure shows as the replica's state on a later tick. A replica that
fails to start is tried again every three seconds and its reason is logged once
and shown by
.BR "cluster placement" .
A local runtime with the same name is never touched. A replica is marked
.B [cluster]
in
.B ls
and
.B ps
after its state,
.B \(dqcluster\(dq:true
in
.BR "\-\-json" ,
and
.B cluster:replica
in
.BR stats ;
it has no state file, so a node that restarts does not bring back what has moved
away, and
.BR stop ,
.BR start ,
.B remove
and
.B edit
refuse it by name, since the next tick would undo them.
.PP
.B Liveness and the lease.
A follower asks the leader, every quarter election timeout, to count it alive,
and the leader answers when it holds a quorum. The leader declares a node down
when it has not heard a request for
.B \-\-raft\-down\-after
(a new leader starts every node's clock at its own election). A node may run its
replicas only while it holds a lease, which lasts three quarters of that time
counted from when the request was sent, so a late answer shortens it and a
stalled loop cannot extend it. The leader's own lease is its last look at a
quorum. The margin between the lease and the failure timeout, a quarter of it (1 s
by default), is what a node has to stop its replicas in before the leader gives
them to another node. A lease that had lapsed is used again only once the node has
applied the end of the leader's log as it stood when it was granted, so a node that
comes back does not run a replica that moved while it was away.
.PP
.B Partitions and duplicates.
A node that cannot reach a quorum stops serving its replicas within its lease
(measured 2.9\(en3.8 s after the last answer at the defaults) and keeps them
defined, so they start again the moment a lease is granted; the majority moves
them after the failure timeout, at least a quarter of it later. So a minority never serves what it
cannot confirm it owns, a minority holding the old leader included, and a healed
node runs only what the log still gives it. The same holds for a leader that loses its quorum,
and it means that a cluster that loses its quorum stops serving every cluster
replica, on every node, until it has one again: the cluster chooses one copy or
none over two. A leader change that takes longer than the lease
(a split vote; about one in ten kills of a leader in simulation) stops a
survivor's replicas until the new leader has granted a lease, a few hundred
milliseconds after it is elected. The lease is enforced by the daemon's loop,
so a daemon that is hung and not dead (SIGSTOP, a blocked disk) cannot stop
the workers of a server, a proxy or a client, which are separate processes (a
cache's worker fences itself: see
.BR "Cluster caches" );
and whatever a replica does on
its way down (a
.B \-\-drain
that flushes its connections) spends part of the margin.
.PP
.B Failover time,
kill \-9 of a node to its replica running on a survivor, at the defaults on one
host with a real daemon, and 60 seeds of a simulation of the same logic:
about 3.9\(en4.3 s when the node was a follower (the failure timeout and a
commit), about 5.1\(en6.0 s when it was the leader (an election first), and up
to 8.7 s in the simulation when a split vote stretched the election.
.PP
.B Cluster caches.
A cache is a cluster runtime that holds data, so keeping it running is not
enough:
.PP
.nf
socketley cluster create cache db \-p 6379 \-\-replicas 3
socketley cluster create proxy gw \-p 7000 \-\-backend @db \-\-replicas 3
.fi
.PP
The leader places the replicas as for any runtime, and the log names one of
them the
.B primary
of an
.BR epoch :
a number that only grows, so two leaders that race to promote agree on which was
first. The others are its followers, and follow it with the cache's own
replication (\c
.BR \-\-replicate :
a full dump, then every write), dialling the primary's
.B \-p
on the host its node advertises to Raft. That is why a cluster cache needs
.BR \-p ;
it is refused with
.BR \-\-listen ,
.BR \-\-shards ,
.BR \-\-tls ,
.B \-\-replicate
(the cluster sets it) and without a port. On one host each replica needs its own
port, which is what
.B {node}
is for: named 1, 2 and 3, the nodes run
.B \-p 630{node}
as ports 6301, 6302 and 6303. On separate hosts a follower is a peer on another
machine, which a cache serves only with
.BR \-\-public :
put it in the spec (the tests here ran on one host and did not try it). A cache
with one replica has a primary and nothing to promote. The first primary is the replica on the lowest node id,
chosen once every replica has said it exists (or after an election timeout). A
replica is a process the log can name (its
.BR incarnation ),
and a restarted one is another, holding nothing the old one did.
.PP
.B Who may write.
A replica takes a client's command only under its worker's last order from its
daemon, sent every 100 ms and standing for the lease the daemon held when it
sent it. A replica that is
.B fenced
answers every command, reads included, with an error, and refuses
.BR replicate ;
a
.B follower
answers reads and refuses writes; the
.B primary
takes writes. A replica starts fenced, and leads only if this node holds a lease
AND the log names the very process it runs as the primary; anything else follows
whoever the log names, or waits when nobody is named. A fence
.B keeps the data:
the process stays up, so a node that lost its quorum for a minute and got it back
is the primary again with everything it had, and a follower keeps its link and
its copy through a spell without a lease. When the orders stop, for any reason
(the node lost its quorum, or the daemon hung or died), the worker fences itself
when the last one runs out, without the daemon's help. Measured in nine runs
with real daemons: a worker whose daemon was stopped with SIGSTOP refused writes
after 2.7\(en3.1 s, and a primary that lost its quorum stopped taking writes after
2.8\(en3.2 s (the lease is three quarters of the failure timeout, 3 s at the
defaults).
.PP
.B Promotion.
The leader replaces a primary when nobody leads, when its node has been declared
down (in the same round that takes its replica away), or when its node is alive
but has not, for the failure timeout, named the process the log calls the
primary in a lease request: a primary whose daemon restarted comes back empty
and does not lead, and a replica that has the data is promoted after that
timeout. Every lease request carries, for each cache replica the node runs, its
incarnation, the epoch of the primary whose stream the copy came from, its
offset in that stream, and whether it leads. The offset is a count of records:
the primary counts every record it produces, whether or not a follower is
connected, the end-of-dump marker carries the count at which the dump was taken,
and a follower is at that number plus the records it has applied since; a copy is
empty from the first line of a dump to its last. The leader promotes the replica
whose copy is the most up to date, comparing epochs first (offsets of two
primaries are not on one scale), then offsets, then the lowest node id, and
writes it with the next epoch to the log. The offsets the leader has are up to
about half a second old (a stats push and a request interval), but the primary
has been gone for the failure timeout by the time it looks, so the followers' last
reports are their final positions. A primary that comes back, or a node that is
given a replica again, is a new process: it follows the primary of the epoch it
finds and takes a full dump that replaces whatever it held, so nothing it wrote
that the cluster did not have survives, and it does not lead a cache whose log
names another. A cache scaled down never loses its primary.
.PP
.B How long,
kill \-9 of the node that holds the primary to a survivor taking a write, on one
host with real daemons (the default timing): 3.9\(en4.1 s when the node was not
the Raft leader (the failure timeout and a commit; eleven kills) and 5.2\(en5.7 s when
it was (an election first; four kills). In a simulation of the same logic over
60 kills it was 4.1\(en4.2 s and 5.6\(en7.2 s, the longer ones being a split vote. A
follower dials the new primary within about a fifth of a second of being told,
and a proxy follows through the endpoint group the primary is published as: the
first write through a proxy on a survivor succeeded 20\(en95 ms after the first
write made directly to the new primary (five runs).
.PP
.B What a failover can lose.
Replication is asynchronous: the primary answers a write and streams it
afterwards, so when its node dies the promoted follower lacks the acknowledged
writes it had not received. What survives is always a prefix of the primary's
history (replication is ordered, and the tests read every acknowledged key back
to check that no write is lost that a later one outlived). How long the tail is
depends on how far behind the best follower was. Measured with a client writing
continuously and kill \-9 of the primary's node, three kills each: one
connection writing one key at a time, about a thousand a second (4,500 to 4,900
acknowledged), none missing; 200 writes pipelined at a time as fast as one
connection goes (630,000 to 720,000 acknowledged, some 230,000 a second), none missing; and
the same with both followers' workers stopped (SIGSTOP) for the four seconds before
the kill, so that neither was receiving, about 1.6 million acknowledged and
876,000 to 922,000 of them missing, which is every write the primary took while
no follower had it.
So the window is how far behind the best follower was when the primary died: a
few writes when the followers keep up (the first two conditions lost none), and
everything since a follower stopped reading when they do not. Only what a
follower had already received survives (in a separate check, a stopped follower
held about 20,000 of 925,000 acknowledged keys after its leader was killed), and a
primary drops a follower that is 8 MiB behind, after which it keeps acknowledging
writes that no follower is receiving until that follower dials again. A machine
that loses power, or a network that is cut, could not be tried without root.
There is no synchronous mode: a write that must survive its primary is written to
two caches.
.PP
.B Finding the primary.
.B "cluster placement"
prints, under
.BR "CACHE PRIMARIES" ,
each cache's primary and epoch and, for each replica, the epoch and offset of its
copy and how old that news is: the leader hears every node's, the others their
own. A proxy with
.BI \-\-backend " @name"
follows the primary: the cache's name is published as a group of one, the
primary's host and port, on every node that holds a lease, and republished when
it moves. The
.B stats
of a replica carry
.BR cluster_role ,
.BR cluster_epoch ,
.B cluster_offset
and
.BR cluster_incarnation .
.PP
.B Limits.
A cache whose replicas all die at once starts again empty: the data lived in
memory, and a
.B \-\-persistent
file is loaded by a restarted process but does not take part in choosing the
primary, and is replaced when the replica syncs from the primary that was
chosen. A cache with one replica loses its data with its node. A worker that
answers its daemon and takes no orders is not replaced.
.PP
.B Not yet:
removing a member, snapshots sent in chunks (a snapshot goes in one frame of
at most 16 MiB), a cluster id that keeps two clusters from mixing, editing a
cluster runtime in place, moving a replica off a node before it is stopped for
maintenance (a node stopped with SIGTERM keeps its assignments until the failure
timeout), a worker that fences itself when its daemon is hung for a server, a
proxy or a client (a cache does), synchronous replication of a cache, and a
replica that fails to start on one node being tried on another.
.SH INSTALLATION
.SS Quick Install
.nf
sudo bash packaging/install.sh
sudo systemctl start socketley
.fi
.SS Debian Package
.nf
bash packaging/build-deb.sh 1.1.0
sudo dpkg -i socketley_1.1.0_amd64.deb
sudo systemctl start socketley
.fi
.SS Uninstall
.nf
sudo bash packaging/uninstall.sh
.fi
.SH STATE PERSISTENCE
Runtime configurations are persisted as JSON files. When a runtime is created,
its configuration is saved. When the daemon starts, all persisted runtimes are
restored. Runtimes that were running when the daemon stopped are auto-started.
.PP
This means runtimes survive daemon restarts and system reboots (like Docker
with restart-always policy).
.PP
State is saved after create, start, stop, remove, and edit commands.
The
.B was_running
field tracks whether a runtime should be auto-started on daemon startup.
.SS A State File Is A Secret
A runtime's record is not a note about the runtime, it
.I is
the runtime. It carries, in plaintext:
.TP
.B master_pw
the password given to
.BR "\-\-master\-pw" ,
verbatim.
.TP
.BR key_path ", " mesh_client_key
the paths of the TLS and mTLS private keys this runtime uses.
.TP
.B lua_script
the path of the handler the daemon loads, which runs with the daemon's
privileges \(em see
.B Handler Privileges
under
.BR "LUA CONFIGURATION" .
.PP
And the daemon restores every runtime from these files when it starts. So
whoever can
.I write
one chooses what the daemon runs at its next start, which is the same thing as
being able to send it commands.
.PP
Accordingly the daemon writes them
.BR 0600 ,
stated at creation rather than inherited from its umask, and the packaged state
directory is
.B 0750
owned by
.BR socketley:socketley .
Neither is configurable, and there is no supported reason to widen them: an
operator reaches this data through the CLI, over a socket with the same
group boundary, not by reading the directory.
.PP
.B "Earlier versions wrote these files 0666 & ~umask"
\(em 0644 under systemd's default, 0666 for a daemon started from a shell with
.BR "umask 0" .
Any local user could read a master password, and in the last case rewrite the
record. On its first start after an upgrade the daemon narrows every file it
finds in its state directory to 0600 and logs how many:
.PP
.nf
state: tightened 3 file(s) in /var/lib/socketley/runtimes to 0600 ...
.fi
.PP
It only ever narrows, it is idempotent, and it does not touch the directory
\(em that belongs to the installer. Seeing the line on every start means
something outside socketley is putting the modes back.
.PP
.B "A password that was exposed should be treated as exposed."
The repair closes the hole; it cannot un-read what was already readable. Rotate
any
.B \-\-master\-pw
that lived on a multi-user machine under an affected version, with
.B "socketley edit"
or by recreating the runtime.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Read at daemon startup; all are optional. The first selects which daemon this
is. The rest are tuning knobs whose defaults suit a general-purpose deployment
and only need changing for an unusual workload. Each knob can be set in the
.B settings
table of the daemon's configuration file instead, by its name in lower case
without
.B SOCKETLEY_
(see
.BR "Daemon Configuration" ),
and
.B socketley settings
lists every one with its value and where that came from. A number outside a
knob's range, or that is not a whole number, is said once in the log and the
default is used.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_SOCKET
Which daemon you are, or which one you are talking to. Overrides the default
lookup
.RB ( /run/socketley/socketley.sock
when installed and privileged, otherwise
.BR /tmp/socketley.sock ).
.IP
No workload calls for setting it. A deployed node \(em a container or a host \(em has
a filesystem namespace to itself, so one daemon on the default path is the whole
arrangement, and that is what the systemd unit ships. Clustering does not use
this variable either: members publish themselves into a shared directory and
reach each other over network addresses, never over socket paths. See
.BR "CLUSTER MODE" .
.IP
What it is for is running several daemons inside
.I one
namespace, which is the development and test situation and nothing else. Two
checkouts on one workstation, or two test runs at once, are otherwise the same
daemon: whichever process binds the default path first owns it, and every later
one silently talks to that process instead of starting its own. Anything that
starts a daemon on the default path is claiming the identity the real daemon
runs under, so the test suite gives every test a socket of its own.
.IP
It selects the state directory as well as the socket, and it has to. Several
daemons in one namespace reading one state directory are not several daemons:
each restores everything in it at startup, including the others' runtimes, onto
ports the others are already serving, and each
.B remove
deletes a state file a daemon that is still running it depends on. See
.BR "WHERE STATE LIVES" .
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_CPUS=\fImax\fR|\fI0\-3\fR|\fI0,1,2,3\fR
The set of CPUs the daemon's I/O thread may run on. Default
.BR max ,
which applies no pinning at all rather than a mask of the CPUs that happen to be
online now.
.IP
A CPU this machine does not have, or a set that comes out empty, is refused and
the daemon runs unpinned with a warning; it is never rounded up to
.BR max .
.IP
.BI "socketley daemon \-\-cpus " set
says the same thing on the command line and wins when both are given. There the
same impossible set is refused outright and the daemon does not start, because a
flag typed for this one run is a requirement rather than an inherited default.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_CACHE_BUF_SIZE
Size in bytes of each buffer in a cache runtime's io_uring provided-buffer ring.
Default
.BR 65536 .
Larger buffers let one read completion carry more commands, and what the ring
costs is its footprint, count times size, against the core's cache \(em see
the section on the receive ring for what was measured.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_CACHE_BUF_COUNT
Number of buffers in that ring. Default
.BR 64 .
Together with the size this bounds how much concurrent traffic the runtime can
absorb before the pool runs dry and reads fall back to a slower per-read path;
the pool costs count times size of memory per cache runtime.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_KILL_AFTER_RENAME
A test hook: a sharded storage runtime's commit of a key that begins with this
string kills the worker with
.B SIGKILL
after renaming the body into place and before the journal record, so a test can
put a crash at the one step that leaves a file the journal does not name. Unset
by default.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_KILL_AFTER_EVENT
A test hook: a change of a key that begins with this string, in a bucket that sends
events for it, kills the worker with
.B SIGKILL
once the change's event record is durable and before the change itself is made,
so a test can show that the restarted worker sends no event for it. Unset by
default.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_HOLD_CONFIRM
A test hook: a change of a key that begins with this string is made and answered
but the record saying it was made is never written and its events never go out,
as a crash right after the answer would leave them, so a test can kill the worker
and show that the restarted one sends them. Unset by default.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_PART_LIST_MAX
A test hook: the bytes a multipart object's part sizes, the numbers of an
encrypted one's parts, and its parts' checksums may each take in its header before
the list is kept after its body instead. Default
.BR 4096 ,
which takes some five hundred parts of unequal sizes to pass; a small value lets a
test reach the list after the body with a few parts.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_DOC_FAULT
A test hook: a directory in which a file named
.IB shard . phase
(\fBprepare\fR, \fBcommit\fR or \fBabort\fR) makes that shard of a sharded storage
runtime answer the message of that phase of a bucket document change as a sibling
that could not be reached would, without acting on it, for as long as the file
exists, so a test can lose a message at each step of the change. Unset by default.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_PROXY_HTTP3
.B 0
opens no UDP socket beside a
.B \-\-tls
entrance, which then offers HTTP/3 to nobody and names none in
.BR Alt\-Svc ;
HTTP/2 over TCP is unaffected.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_PROXY_HTTP2
.B 0
turns HTTP/2 off at an entrance, which then speaks HTTP/1.1 to every client over
.BR \-\-tls ;
see the entrance under the dns runtime's
.BR \-\-proxy .
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_HTTP2
.B 0
turns HTTP/2 off, so a storage runtime speaks HTTP/1.1 to every client; see
.B HTTP/2
under the storage runtime.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_HTTP3
.B 0
opens no UDP socket beside a
.B \-\-tls
storage runtime, which then offers HTTP/3 to nobody; see
.B HTTP/3
under the storage runtime.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_HTTP3_ADVERTISE
The port the
.B Alt\-Svc
header names for HTTP/3, when a proxy in front forwards UDP from another than the
runtime's own; 0 advertises none. Default: the runtime's port.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_HTTP3_CC
The congestion controller HTTP/3 sends with:
.BR bbr ,
.B cubic
or
.BR reno .
Default
.BR bbr .
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_HTTP3_RETRY_AFTER
The QUIC handshakes under way past which a new client is sent a Retry and must
prove its address first; 0 asks it of every client. Default 64.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_SPLICE_MIN
The size in bytes from which a storage GET is sent by splice from the file to
the socket instead of being read into memory and written. Default
.BR 65536 ;
.B 0
splices every body. Below the default a read and a write cost less than the
pipe a splice needs, so this changes what
.B zero_copy_bytes
counts and how much CPU a GET takes, never what it returns.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_HOT_BYTES
How many bytes of small objects (up to 60000 bytes each) a storage shard keeps in
memory to answer repeated GETs without opening their files, of any key, whichever
shard owns it.
Default
.B 33554432
(32 MiB);
.B 0
keeps nothing. This changes CPU use and GET throughput and the memory a worker
holds, never what a GET returns.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_COMPACT_FLOOR
A test aid: the fewest records a bucket's journal holds before a storage shard
rewrites it without its superseded records. Default
.BR 4096 .
A test lowers it to see compactions without thousands of writes. This changes how
often a journal is rewritten, never what it holds.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_HASH_THREADS
How many helper threads a storage worker takes the digests of large uploads on
(the ETag's MD5, any \fBx\-amz\-checksum\-\fR the request asked for or the default CRC64NVME, and the SHA\-256
that verifies a signed body: a hex payload hash or aws\-chunked signatures), so
that the worker's loop is not the one core every upload's hashing has to fit in. Default
.BR 3 ,
or the machine's cores divided by the runtime's shards when that is fewer, at
least one, so that the shards' threads together do not outnumber the cores;
.B 0
hashes on the loop. The threads are started by the first upload of 256 KiB or
more (or of unknown length) and end with the runtime; smaller bodies are hashed
in line, since the hop costs more than their digest, and a body that arrives a few
segments at a time is handed over 64 KiB at a time for the same reason. This changes CPU use and
the throughput of large puts, never what is stored.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_LIFECYCLE_DAY_SECS
A test aid: the number of seconds a bucket lifecycle rule counts as one day, 1 to
86400, read by the lifecycle sweep alone, which then also runs as often as every
second. Unset, a day is a day. It changes when objects expire, so it is never for a
runtime holding data anyone wants (see Bucket lifecycle).
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_SHARE_NOTIFY_ALLOW
The URL prefixes a share link's webhook may begin with, comma\-separated; unset,
the webhooks of Discord, Slack and ntfy.sh (see Drive, links, media and the web
interface).
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_SHARE_BURN_GRACE_SECS
A test aid: how many seconds the files of a share link that burns outlive its last
allowed download, 600 when unset (see Drive, links, media and the web
interface).
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_RESOLV_CONF
A test aid: the file the resolver on a runtime's loop reads its nameservers,
search list and options from, in
.BR resolv.conf (5)'s
form, so a test can point it at a nameserver of its own; /etc/resolv.conf when
unset (see What can stop the event loop). A mail runtime's SPF, DKIM and DMARC
lookups ask its nameservers too.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_MAIL_MX_PORT
A test aid: the port a mail runtime hands mail to a domain's exchangers on, 25
when unset, so a test's own server can be one.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_MAIL_TLS_CA
A test aid: the CA file a mail runtime checks an exchanger's certificate
against where DANE or MTA\-STS asks it to, the system's roots when unset, so a
test's own exchanger can be trusted.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_MAIL_STS_CA
A test aid: the CA file a mail runtime checks the server of an MTA\-STS policy
against, the system's roots when unset.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_MAIL_STS_CONNECT
A test aid: the
.IR host : port
every MTA\-STS policy is fetched from, the name in its URL still the one
checked, so a test can serve the policies of domains it does not own; each
policy's own host when unset.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_MAIL_DELAY_NOTICE_MS
A test aid: the milliseconds a mail runtime tries a message before a sender who
asked (NOTIFY=DELAY) is told it is late, four hours when unset.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_MAIL_SPOOL_MOVE_MS
A test aid: the milliseconds a sealed spool segment waits before the few
messages still in it are moved and it is used again, a minute when unset.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_MAIL_DEAD_DOMAIN_MS
A test aid: the milliseconds a mail runtime holds a domain it found not to exist,
or to take no mail, before it looks it up again, ten minutes when unset.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_MAIL_PUSH_CONNECT
A test aid: the
.IR host : port
every JMAP push is posted to, the name in its URL still the one its
certificate is checked for, so a test can receive the pushes of a URL it does
not own; the URL's own host when unset.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_MAIL_PUSH_CA
A test aid: a file of CA certificates a JMAP push URL's certificate is checked
against; the system's when unset.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_MAIL_IDLE_MS
A test aid: the milliseconds any connection to a mail runtime may be quiet, or
its TLS handshake take, before it is told so and closed; unset, five minutes
for SMTP, POP3 and ManageSieve, thirty for IMAP, and thirty seconds for a
handshake.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_MAIL_BAYES_FOLD_MS
A test aid: the milliseconds what a mail runtime's spam filter learned stays in
its journal before the first shard folds it into the filter's file; unset, ten
minutes.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_MAIL_QUEUE_SCALE
A test aid: how many times faster a mail runtime's retries and
.B \-\-queue\-lifetime
run, so a test sees a message tried again and given up within seconds; 1 when
unset.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_MAIL_BUSY_HOLD_MS
A test aid: the milliseconds a mail runtime opens no new connection to a domain
after one of its exchangers answered 421, doubled by each 421 until a message is
taken, to fifteen minutes; thirty seconds when unset.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_MAIL_SPOOL_KEEP_BYTES
A test aid: the bytes of message bodies a mail spool keeps in memory beside its
segments, 64 MiB when unset, so a test can have them read back from disk.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_NOTIFY_HOSTS
A test aid: a file of
.I name address
lines that the resolver of a storage runtime's notification webhooks reads before
the system resolver, on every resolve, so a test can point a webhook's host name at
another address. Unset, a name is asked of the resolver on the loop and then of
the system's (see Event notifications).
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_NOTIFY_QUEUE
The most events a storage shard holds undelivered, beyond which a new one is
dropped and counted in
.BR notify_dropped .
Default: 10000. A test lowers it to reach the bound.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_CLUSTER_QUEUE
The most changes a storage shard queues for another node of its cluster before it
lets them go and sends that node everything instead once it answers (see Cluster).
Default: 100000. A test lowers it to reach the bound with a few writes.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_REPLICATION_HOSTS
A test aid: a file of
.I name address
lines that the resolver of a storage runtime's replication targets reads before
the system resolver, on every resolve, so a test can name a target by a host.
Unset, a name is asked of the resolver on the loop and then of the system's (see
Replication).
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_STOP_HOLD_MS
A test aid: milliseconds, 0 to 3000, that a storage shard's stop waits after its
in\-flight work has finished and before it lets go of its directory, multiplied by
the shard's number plus one, so a test can start a runtime beside shards that are
still stopping. Unset, a stop waits for nothing but its own work. It slows every
stop and restart, so it is never for a runtime in use.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_START_SPIN_MS
A test aid: milliseconds, 0 to 30000, that a storage shard's start works through
before it opens its store, using CPU as one reading a large store's files does.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_START_SLEEP_MS
A test aid: milliseconds, 0 to 60000, that a storage shard's start waits doing
nothing before it opens its store, as a wedged start would.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_LOG_FLUSH_MS
A test aid: milliseconds, 100 to 3600000, that a storage shard holds a server
access log's lines before it delivers them, instead of a minute.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_RECOVERY_THREADS
How many threads a storage worker reads its object headers on at a start after a
stop that was not clean, 1 to 64. Default: the CPUs the worker may run on, at most
16. The threads end before the listener opens. This changes how long such a start
takes, never what it finds.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_RECOVERY_DEPTH
How many opens and header reads each of those threads keeps in flight on an io_uring
of its own, 0 to 256; both this and the thread count are lowered to fit half of what
.B RLIMIT_NOFILE
leaves. Default
.BR 32 ;
.B 0
reads one file at a time without a ring. This changes how long a start from a cold
cache takes, never what it finds.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_RECV_BUFS
How many 16 KiB buffers the receive ring of a storage worker holds, a power of two.
Default
.BR 64 ;
.B 0
takes the ring away, and every connection reads into a buffer of its own. A connection
is lent a buffer only for the length of one completion, so an idle keep\-alive
connection holds none, and a receive that finds the ring empty is taken by a buffer
of its own for that one read, counted as
.B recv_fallbacks
in
.BR stats .
This changes the memory a worker holds and never what it returns.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_TLS_FAILURE_REPORT_SECS
How often a runtime serving TLS reports the inbound handshakes that failed
since its last report, in one line: every 60 seconds by default, 1 to 3600. A
handshake that fails is a port scanner far more often than a client, so each
one is counted rather than logged.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_ACME_DIRECTORY
The directory URL of the ACME CA that
.B \-\-acme
and
.B acme issue
use. Default: Let's Encrypt,
.BR https://acme\-v02.api.letsencrypt.org/directory ;
its staging CA is
.BR https://acme\-staging\-v02.api.letsencrypt.org/directory .
Each CA has an account of its own.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_ACME_CHALLENGE
The challenge
.B acme issue
and
.B \-\-acme
prove a name with when none is asked for:
.BR http\-01 ,
the default,
.B tls\-alpn\-01
or
.BR dns\-01 .
A wildcard is proven over DNS\-01 whatever this says.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_ACME_DNS_WAIT
Seconds between putting a DNS\-01 answer where the
.B dns
runtimes serve it and telling the CA to look: the time a secondary of the zone
has to take the new serial, and the time the delegation probe gives it too.
Default 10, at most 3600. A secondary that is told by NOTIFY and transfers on it
takes seconds; give one that waits for its refresh the minutes it takes.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_ACME_DNS_PROBE
The resolver
.B acme issue
asks, as
.IR host [: port ],
whether a zone of
.B \-\-dns\-zones
is delegated to this daemon's
.B dns
runtimes, before it proves anything there over DNS\-01; it then asks each name
server of the zone, by its IPv4 addresses, the way a CA would.
.B off
asks nothing and takes every zone as delegated. Default: the first
.B nameserver
of
.IR /etc/resolv.conf .
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_ACME_CA
A PEM file of the roots the ACME CA's own HTTPS is checked against, for a CA
whose certificate no system store holds, as Pebble's. Default: the system's roots.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_GIT_ACCESS_KEY
The access key
.B git\-sync
writes into a library with; a storage runtime that starts it gives it temporary
credentials of the user the repository is kept for.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_GIT_SECRET
That key's secret.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_GIT_SESSION_TOKEN
The session token of temporary credentials, sent as x-amz-security-token.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_GIT_TOKEN
A GitHub token
.B git\-sync
reads a private repository with, given to git as an http.extraHeader in its
environment, never on a command line.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_FFMPEG
The ffmpeg a storage runtime makes thumbnails and streams with, under
.BR /~media/ ,
and the
.B ffprobe
beside it, or on
.BR PATH ,
that reads what a film is. Default: the first
.B ffmpeg
on
.BR PATH ;
with neither, a media request is answered
.BR "501" .
Read at a runtime's first media request.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_MEDIA_HWACCEL
Where a storage runtime's renditions are encoded:
.B off
for x264 on the CPU always, a render node such as
.BR /dev/dri/renderD129 ,
or, by default,
.BR /dev/dri/renderD128 .
At its first medium a worker runs one second of test picture through the GPU's
whole chain (upload, scale, H.264 encode), with the full encoder and then the
low-power one, and uses the GPU for renditions only when one of them works;
until it knows, renditions use x264. A rendition keeps the encoder its first job
chose, and one whose first GPU job fails before anything is made goes on with
x264. HLG is tone-mapped on the CPU, HDR10 on the GPU.
.B vaapi\-unchecked:\fInode\fR
skips the test run, for a test of that fallback.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_MEDIA_CACHE_MB
The size in MiB past which a storage runtime removes the media it made, least
recently asked for first, from
.IR dir /.media/.
Default: 10240. The medium made last is kept even when it alone is larger,
and none is removed while it is being made.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_CACHE_TTL_SWEEP_MS
Interval between periodic sweeps for expired cache keys, in milliseconds.
Default
.BR 500 .
This never affects correctness: an expired key is not served no matter when the
sweep runs, because every read path checks the expiry itself. It controls only
how quickly keys that are never accessed again release their memory.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_LUA_TIMEOUT_MS
How long one Lua callback invocation may hold the event\-loop thread, in
milliseconds. Default
.BR 10000 ;
.B 0
switches the bound off entirely and turns LuaJIT's trace compiler on with it,
which is worth about 2.4x on a callback that computes and about 1% on one that
does not. See
.BR "Callback Deadline" ,
which prices both halves of that trade.
.IP
This is the default for every script that does not declare its own
.B callback_timeout_ms
global; a script that does declare one overrides it. A value that is not a
whole number of milliseconds in 0\(en3600000 is refused with a warning and the
default is used.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_HTTP_REVALIDATE
Set to
.B 0
to switch off the response cache that plain
.B \-\-http
keeps, putting it back on an open, a read and a close per request. Any other
value, or the variable being unset, leaves it on. Read when the runtime starts,
so a
.B restart
picks up a change. What the server serves is the same either way \(em the cache
is revalidated on every request \(em so this exists for measurement and for
ruling the cache out when diagnosing something else.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_PROXY_SPLICE
Forward
.B \-\-protocol tcp
proxy traffic with
.BR splice (2)
through io_uring instead of copying it through userspace. Unset (off) by
default; any value other than
.B 0
turns it on. Read once per process and inherited by worker processes.
.IP
Off because it is slower here, and not by a little.
.B IORING_OP_SPLICE
is prepped
.B REQ_F_FORCE_ASYNC
in the kernel, so every splice is handed to an io\-wq worker thread \(em a cost
per transfer that no way of submitting it avoids. Measured on loopback, one
connection, serial round trips through a proxy in front of an echoing backend,
round\-trip p50: 47.9\ \(mcs spliced against 26.3\ \(mcs copied at 128 B, 57.3
against 38.4 at 16 KB, and 394.5 against 350.5 at 256 KB. The copy path wins at
every size measured. It also costs a third less CPU \(em 0.53 cores against
0.86 at 128 B, which is 2.9 times the CPU per request for splice.
.IP
It is kept because what was measured is one machine and one shape of traffic.
Zero copy is a claim about memory bandwidth; the case it could still win is
bulk streaming across a real NIC on a box with less of it, and nobody has
measured that. A TLS listener or TLS backend never splices whatever this is set
to, nor does a pair with a 127.0.0.1 leg while the daemon runs with
.BR \-\-fast\-lo ,
and neither does a proxy with an
.B on_proxy_request
or
.B on_proxy_response
hook \(em the kernel moves those bytes without the script ever seeing them.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_FAST_LO
Set by a daemon started with
.B \-\-fast\-lo
once the shortcut is attached, to
.BR own ,
.B all
or
.BR trust ,
so its workers keep a proxied pair with a 127.0.0.1 leg off splice. Not a setting: the daemon clears any inherited value at startup.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_NAPI_BUSY_POLL_US
Enable io_uring NAPI busy polling with this timeout in microseconds. Unset by
default. Trades CPU for lower receive latency on busy links.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_HUGEPAGE
Set to
.B 0
to stop cache workers asking glibc for transparent huge pages. On by default and
only a cache asks: its hot path walks a keyed structure larger than the TLB
covers, which is what a huge page is for, and a server measured no gain for the
extra resident memory. The tunable is read by glibc at process startup, so the
daemon puts it in the worker's environment at exec \(em a running process cannot
ask for itself.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_RECV_MULTISHOT
Set to
.B 0
to arm one read at a time instead of a multishot receive. On wherever the kernel
supports it. A multishot arm is one submission for a stream of messages instead
of one per message; the switch exists so the two can be measured against each
other on one binary.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_RECV_BUNDLE
Set to
.B 0
to stop packing several provided buffers into one receive
.RB ( IORING_RECVSEND_BUNDLE ).
On where the kernel supports it, which is probed once rather than assumed, and
it falls back to the plain multishot arm wherever the flag is refused.
.IP
Both of these read
.B only
the exact string
.BR 0 .
.B SOCKETLEY_RECV_MULTISHOT=off
leaves multishot on, which is the opposite of what it looks like. The
.BI SOCKETLEY_NO_ *
switches below take the other rule: any value except
.B 0
turns the thing off.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_NO_SHARD_MESH
Any value but
.B 0
stops shards linking to each other directly; they relay through the daemon
instead. The daemon logs that it is off at startup. It is the measurement
control for the mesh, so the two paths differ in the route alone and not in the
build.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_NO_PEER_LINKS
Any value but
.B 0
stops runtimes opening direct links to their peers, for the same reason and read
the same way.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_NO_COMPANION
Any value but
.B 0
stops a unix\-socket listener being placed beside a port.
.TP
.B RES_OPTIONS
Not read by socketley \(em passed to glibc's resolver, and set for worker
processes when you have not set one yourself. The default is
.BR "timeout:1 attempts:2" .
.IP
It is set because
.BR getaddrinfo (3)
blocks the thread that calls it, and in a worker that thread is the event loop
with the daemon's control socket armed on the same ring: while a lookup waits,
.B stop
and
.B stats
go unanswered. glibc's own default is five seconds twice, which against a
nameserver that drops packets measured 10.0\ s for one lookup \(em and a client
re\-resolves on every reconnect once its cached address stops accepting. One
second twice has the same ceiling as two seconds once and still retries a lost
packet.
.IP
Set it yourself and yours is used unchanged, for the daemon and every worker.
Raise it if a slow but working nameserver is being given up on; the cost of
giving up is one more reconnect round, not a failed runtime.
.TP
.B EDITOR ", " VISUAL
Which editor
.B socketley edit
opens when given no flags.
.B VISUAL
is tried first.
.TP
.B TMPDIR
Where that editor's temporary file is written. It holds the runtime's entire
configuration while it is open \(em for a cache that includes
.BR master_pw ,
so point this at a directory of your own if
.I /tmp
is shared. The file is created 0600 and removed when the editor exits.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_DNS_CACHE_MS
How long
.B socketley.http()
reuses a resolved address before asking the resolver again, in milliseconds.
Default
.BR 60000 .
.B 0
disables reuse and makes every call resolve, which is what it did before.
.IP
It is a ceiling and not a promise. A connect to the cached address that is
refused, or that times out, drops the entry at once \(em so an endpoint that
moved is followed on the next call, and this only decides how long one that
moved
.I without
refusing stays wrong, which is the case where something else answers on the old
address.
.IP
Worth knowing why it exists:
.BR getaddrinfo (3)
blocks the thread that calls it, glibc caches nothing of its own, and in a
worker that thread is the event loop. Every other resolver in this daemon is
bounded by something \(em a client and a server re-resolve at most once per
second of reconnect backoff, a proxy once per thirty-second sweep, the rest run
once at start.
.B socketley.http()
was the exception: it resolved on every call, and neither the call's own
deadline nor the callback budget covers a resolver.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_RAFT_TICK_MS
The Raft tick of a daemon started with
.BR \-\-raft\-listen ,
in milliseconds. Default
.BR 100 ;
the election timeout is ten ticks to twenty, and a heartbeat is sent every
tick, so every member of one cluster should use the same value.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_RAFT_COMPACT_EVERY
How many applied Raft entries go by between snapshots. Default
.BR 1000 .
A small value makes a joining node catch up through a snapshot, which is what
the test suite sets it for.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_CONFIG
Path to the daemon configuration file.
.SH FILES
.SS System Mode (installed via package)
.TP
.I /usr/bin/socketley
Binary.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/socketley.service
systemd unit file.
.TP
.I /run/socketley/socketley.sock
Unix domain socket for daemon IPC.
.BR 0660 ,
owned by
.BR socketley:socketley .
.TP
.I /var/lib/socketley/runtimes/
Per-runtime JSON configuration files. Directory
.BR 0750 ,
files
.BR 0600 ,
owned by
.BR socketley:socketley .
They carry master passwords and private-key paths; see
.B A State File Is A Secret
under
.BR "STATE PERSISTENCE" .
.TP
.I /etc/socketley/config.lua
Daemon configuration (name, cluster, log_level, metrics_port, cpus).
.TP
.I /etc/socketley/types/*.toml
Registered-type manifests, read at daemon startup; see
.BR "REGISTERED TYPES" .
.SS Development Mode (no system install)
.TP
.I /tmp/socketley.sock
Unix domain socket for daemon IPC.
.BR 0600 ,
owned by the user who started the daemon.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/socketley/runtimes/
Per-runtime JSON configuration files,
.BR 0600 .
.TP
.I ~/.config/socketley/config.lua
Daemon configuration (or set SOCKETLEY_CONFIG env var).
.TP
.I ~/.config/socketley/types/*.toml
Registered-type manifests, read at daemon startup; see
.BR "REGISTERED TYPES" .
.SS THE IPC SOCKET
Every
.B socketley
command travels over the socket above, and everything the daemon can do is
reachable through it: stopping a runtime, reading
.BR stats ,
rewriting a runtime's configuration with
.BR edit ,
and \(em since a
.B \-\-lua
handler runs with the daemon's privileges, see
.B Handler Privileges
under
.B LUA CONFIGURATION
\(em running arbitrary code as the user the daemon runs as.
.PP
So opening the socket is an administrative right, and the mode says so:
.PP
.nf
/run/socketley/socketley.sock   0660  socketley:socketley
any other path                  0600  the user who started the daemon
.fi
.PP
The packaged daemon runs as
.B socketley
and the operator does not, so the packaged socket is shared with one group and
membership of that group is how the right is granted:
.PP
.nf
sudo usermod \-aG socketley "$USER"     # then log in again
.fi
.PP
A daemon on any other path \(em a development daemon, or one started with
.B SOCKETLEY_SOCKET
\(em keeps its socket to itself. That is owner\-only rather than
owner\-and\-group because a shared primary group is not unusual \(em
.B users
on several distributions \(em and there the daemon's group is every human on
the machine.
.PP
.B "This socket used to be 0666,"
world\-writable, on both paths. Any local uid could drive any daemon on the
machine, whoever it belonged to. After upgrading past that, a user who has
been running
.B socketley
against a system daemon without being in the
.B socketley
group will get
.PP
.nf
not permitted to open the daemon socket at /run/socketley/socketley.sock
.fi
.PP
and needs to be added to it. A user who is not added is not locked out of the
program: with the system socket unreachable the CLI falls back to development
mode and starts a daemon of that user's own, with that user's privileges and
that user's runtimes.
.PP
On a machine where two people use development mode at once, the second gets the
same message about
.I /tmp/socketley.sock
\(em one path, one owner, and it is no longer shared. That was never a
supported arrangement, it was a shared daemon nobody had asked for. Give each
their own:
.PP
.nf
export SOCKETLEY_SOCKET="/tmp/socketley-$USER.sock"
.fi
.PP
which also gives each a state directory of its own; see
.B WHERE STATE LIVES
above.
.SS WHERE STATE LIVES
The two paths above are where a daemon's runtimes live when it is serving the
default socket, which is every deployed daemon and every daemon that has never
heard of
.BR SOCKETLEY_SOCKET .
Nothing about that has changed or will.
.PP
A daemon on any
.I other
socket path gets a directory of its own, a sibling of the one above named after
that path: the leading slash is dropped and every character outside
.BR A-Za-z0-9._-
becomes an underscore.
.PP
.nf
SOCKETLEY_SOCKET unset          ~/.local/share/socketley/runtimes
SOCKETLEY_SOCKET=/tmp/a.sock    ~/.local/share/socketley/runtimes.tmp_a.sock
SOCKETLEY_SOCKET=/tmp/socketley.sock
                                ~/.local/share/socketley/runtimes
.fi
.PP
Setting the variable to the path that would have been used anyway is the
default, not a second daemon, so a script that spells the default out keeps the
default directory. The name is readable rather than hashed on purpose: the
answer to "which of these is mine" should come out of an
.BR ls .
.PP
The daemon logs the directory it is using at startup
.RB ( "state directory: ..." ),
and that line is the authoritative answer. It is worth knowing where to find it
before you need it.
.PP
.B "Upgrading."
The first time a daemon uses one of these keyed directories, everything in the
shared directory it used to read is copied into it, once, and an INFO line says
how many runtimes were adopted and from where. The originals are left exactly
where they are, so an older binary rolled back onto the machine still finds what
it has always found. A
.I .adopted
file in the new directory records what happened and is what makes it a one-time
event \(em without it, an operator who later removed every runtime would find
them all back after the next restart.
.PP
.B "...but never out from under a running daemon."
Adoption is skipped entirely while a daemon is serving the default socket path,
because those runtimes are that daemon's and adopting them would mean binding
ports it is already serving. The check is a probe of the default path, and
anything answering there \(em daemon or not \(em is enough to decline.
.PP
This is not a rare corner. An operator debugging beside a running daemon reaches
it with one command, and the second bind
.I succeeds
under
.BR SO_REUSEPORT ,
so nothing but the port count would ever have reported it.
.PP
The decline is logged at
.B WARN
with both directory paths and what to do about it, because from the outside it
looks exactly like the runtimes having vanished. It writes no
.I .adopted
marker: "somebody is using these right now" is a fact about this minute rather
than a decision, so stopping the daemon on the default path and restarting this
one adopts them as normal.
.PP
.B "The failure this trades against."
A daemon started with a
.B SOCKETLEY_SOCKET
it did not have last time finds no runtimes. On a development machine that is
correct: it is a different daemon and it owns nothing. It cannot happen to a
deployed node, because a deployed node runs on the default path with the
variable unset, and the default path is exempt from the rule for exactly that
reason. If it happens anyway, nothing has been destroyed \(em the other
directory is still on disk with everything in it, and starting the daemon with
the variable it had before brings it all back.
.PP
The alternative, one state directory for every daemon in the namespace, is not
the safe direction. Two daemons then bind each other's ports on boot, each
reports the other's runtimes as its own, and a
.B remove
sent to either deletes a state file the other is still serving \(em and there is
no undo for that.
.SS Daemon Configuration
The daemon config file is a Lua script that sets a
.B daemon
table:
.nf
daemon = {
    name = "node1",          -- this daemon's name in a cluster
    cluster = "/shared/dir", -- cluster directory; needs name
    log_level = "info",      -- debug, info, warn, error
    metrics_port = 9100,     -- Prometheus metrics HTTP endpoint
    dashboard_credentials = "/etc/socketley/admin.creds",
                             -- the dashboard's admins sign in from here
    dashboard_idp = "auth",  -- or as admins of this idp runtime
    alert_webhook = "https://discord.com/api/webhooks/...",
                             -- where alerts are posted
    alert_mail = "mail://m/ops@example.com",
                             -- and mailed, by this mail runtime
    events = "bus",          -- an mqtt runtime the runtimes' states and
                             -- the alerts are published on
    cpus = "0-3",            -- where this daemon's I/O runs
    settings = {             -- any SOCKETLEY_* knob, by its short name
        storage_hash_threads = 4,
        lua_timeout_ms = 2000,
        proxy_splice = true,
    }
}
.fi
.PP
.B config
is the same table under its older name and still works; a file naming both is
refused rather than merged.
.B name
and
.B cluster
are the two keys
.B "socketley \-\-lua"
can also set on a daemon that is already running \(em the other three are
settled before the ring exists, so they are read only from here, and a
.B \-\-cpus
typed on
.B "socketley daemon"
outranks the file.
.PP
.B settings
sets any variable under
.B ENVIRONMENT
that an operator may turn, by its name without
.B SOCKETLEY_
and in lower case:
.B storage_hash_threads
is
.BR SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_HASH_THREADS .
A number is written as one, an on-or-off as
.B true
or
.BR false ,
anything else as a string. The daemon puts each in the environment it starts
its workers with, so a runtime reads the file's value as it would the
variable's. The file comes first: a variable in the daemon's own environment
counts only for a setting the file does not name, and the default only for one
neither sets. A
name that is no setting, a value outside the setting's range, and a variable a
command reads from its own environment (the
.B SOCKETLEY_GIT_*
ones, and
.B SOCKETLEY_SOCKET
and
.BR SOCKETLEY_CONFIG ,
which choose this file) are each said on standard output, and the daemon does
not start.
.B socketley settings
shows what was taken.
.PP
When
.B metrics_port
is set, the daemon starts an HTTP endpoint that serves a live web dashboard
at
.BR "GET /" ,
Prometheus exposition format metrics at
.BR "GET /metrics" ,
and JSON API endpoints at
.BR "/api/overview" ", " "/api/runtimes" ", and " "/api/runtime/<name>"
(see
.BR DASHBOARD ).
Metrics include: runtimes total/running, connections total/active,
messages total, bytes in/out, and per-runtime breakdowns, among them
.BR socketley_runtime_cpu_seconds_total ,
.B socketley_runtime_resident_memory_bytes
(resident, a shared page counted once per shard) and
.B socketley_runtime_memory_bytes
(what the runtime costs the machine, a shared page counted by the share it holds).
.SH C++ SDK
Socketley ships a three-tier modular C++ SDK in
.BR include/linux/socketley/ .
Tiers 1 and 3 are fully self-contained header-only files \(em copy them anywhere,
compile with
.BR "g++ -std=c++17" ,
no library needed. Tier 2 requires building from source.
.PP
.TS
l l l l .
Tier	Header	Use Case	Dependencies
_
1	socketley/control.h	Control a running daemon	None (POSIX)
2	socketley/{server,cache,proxy,client}.h	Embed the full engine	libsocketley_sdk.a
3	socketley/attach.h	Register with a daemon	None (Tier 1)
.TE
.SS Tier 1: Daemon Control (header-only)
.B socketley/control.h
provides full programmatic control over a running daemon through the same Unix
socket IPC that the CLI uses. Zero dependencies beyond POSIX.
.PP
.B "Result type:"
.nf
struct socketley::result {
    int         exit_code;  // 0=success, 1=bad input, 2=fatal, -1=connect fail
    std::string data;       // response body
};
.fi
.PP
.B "Runtime management functions"
(all in namespace
.BR socketley::ctl ):
.nf
command(cmd)               // raw command (glob patterns work)
command(socket_path, cmd)  // custom socket path
create(type, name, flags)  // "server", "client", "proxy", "cache"
start(name)                stop(name)
remove(name)               send(name, message)
ls()   ps()   stats(name)  show(name)
reload(name)               reload_lua(name)
edit(name, flags)
.fi
.PP
.B "Cache string operations"
(direct IPC, no TCP connection needed):
.nf
cache_get(cache, key)           cache_set(cache, key, value)
cache_del(cache, key)           cache_exists(cache, key)
cache_incr(cache, key)          cache_decr(cache, key)
cache_incrby(cache, key, n)     cache_decrby(cache, key, n)
cache_append(cache, key, val)
.fi
.PP
.B "Cache list operations:"
.nf
cache_lpush(cache, key, val)    cache_rpush(cache, key, val)
cache_lpop(cache, key)          cache_rpop(cache, key)
cache_llen(cache, key)          cache_lrange(cache, key, start, stop)
cache_lindex(cache, key, idx)
.fi
.PP
.B "Cache set operations:"
.nf
cache_sadd(cache, key, member)  cache_srem(cache, key, member)
cache_sismember(cache, key, m)  cache_scard(cache, key)
cache_smembers(cache, key)
.fi
.PP
.B "Cache hash operations:"
.nf
cache_hset(cache, key, f, v)    cache_hget(cache, key, field)
cache_hdel(cache, key, field)   cache_hlen(cache, key)
cache_hgetall(cache, key)
.fi
.PP
.B "Cache TTL:"
.nf
cache_expire(cache, key, sec)   cache_ttl(cache, key)
cache_persist(cache, key)
.fi
.PP
.B "Cache pub/sub:"
.nf
cache_publish(cache, ch, msg)
.fi
.PP
.B "Cache admin:"
.nf
cache_size(cache)               cache_memory(cache)
cache_keys(cache, pattern)
cache_flush(cache [, path])     cache_load(cache [, path])
.fi
.PP
Socket path resolution: checks
.B SOCKETLEY_SOCKET
env, then
.BR /run/socketley/socketley.sock ,
then
.BR /tmp/socketley.sock .
Set the variable only to reach a daemon that is not the one for this namespace,
which in practice means a second daemon on a development machine; a deployed
node runs one daemon on the default path.
.PP
.B "Example: lifecycle management"
.nf
#include <socketley/control.h>
#include <cstdio>

int main() {
    auto r = socketley::ctl::create("server", "myapp", "-p 9000 -s");
    if (r.exit_code != 0) return 1;

    socketley::ctl::send("myapp", "Hello from C++!");
    auto stats = socketley::ctl::stats("myapp");
    printf("%s\\n", stats.data.c_str());

    socketley::ctl::stop("myapp");
    socketley::ctl::remove("myapp");
}
// g++ -std=c++17 myapp.cpp -Iinclude/linux -o myapp
.fi
.PP
.B "Example: fleet management with globs"
.nf
for (int i = 0; i < 5; ++i) {
    std::string name  = "api_" + std::to_string(i);
    std::string flags = "-p " + std::to_string(9000+i) + " -s";
    socketley::ctl::create("server", name, flags);
}
socketley::ctl::command("start api_*");    // start all
socketley::ctl::command("stats api_*");    // query all
socketley::ctl::command("stop api_*");     // stop all
socketley::ctl::command("remove api_*");   // remove all
.fi
.PP
.B "Example: cache operations"
.nf
socketley::ctl::create("cache", "store", "-p 6379 --mode admin -s");
socketley::ctl::cache_set("store", "user:1", "alice");
auto r = socketley::ctl::cache_get("store", "user:1");
printf("user:1 = %s\\n", r.data.c_str());  // "alice"
socketley::ctl::cache_del("store", "user:1");
socketley::ctl::cache_flush("store", "/tmp/store.bin");
.fi
.SS Tier 2: Embed Engine (build from source)
Embed the full io_uring event loop and all four runtime types in your own binary.
Maximum performance with no IPC overhead. Requires
.B libsocketley_sdk.a
and link flags
.BR "-luring -lssl -lcrypto [-lluajit]" .
.PP
Headers:
.B socketley/core.h
(event_loop, runtime_manager, enums),
.B socketley/server.h ,
.B socketley/client.h ,
.B socketley/proxy.h ,
.B socketley/cache.h .
.PP
Define
.B SOCKETLEY_NO_LUA
to build without LuaJIT (omit
.B -lluajit
from link flags).
.PP
.B "High-Level Wrapper Classes (recommended)"
.PP
The
.B socketley::server\fR, \fBsocketley::client\fR, \fBsocketley::proxy\fR, and \fBsocketley::cache
classes wrap the raw engine API into a fluent, chainable interface.
All boilerplate (event loop, runtime manager, signal handling) is internal.
.PP
.B "socketley::server"
.nf
server(uint16_t port)

// Config (all return server& for chaining)
tls(cert, key)         // terminates TLS on this listener
client_ca(ca)          // mutual TLS on it: clients must present a certificate
                       // (tls_ca(ca) is the older spelling; it also sets tls)
max_connections(n)     rate_limit(r)
global_rate_limit(r)   idle_timeout(s)       lua(path)
mode(m)                udp()                 http_dir(path)
http_cache()           upstream(addr)        master_pw(pw)
drain()                group(g)              tick_interval(ms)

// Callbacks (return server& for chaining)
on_start(void())                             on_stop(void())
on_connect(void(int fd))                     on_disconnect(void(int fd))
on_message(void(int fd, string_view msg))    on_tick(void(double dt_ms))

// Actions
send(fd, msg)       broadcast(msg)      disconnect(fd)
peer_ip(fd)         clients()           multicast(fds, msg)
set_data(fd,k,v)    get_data(fd,k)

// Lifecycle
start()   stop()

// Escape hatches (raw engine access)
instance() -> server_instance*
manager()  -> runtime_manager&
loop()     -> event_loop&
.fi
.PP
.B "socketley::client"
.nf
client(string_view host, uint16_t port)

// Config
tls()              // dial over TLS; the host from the constructor is pinned
                   // and the chain verified against the system trust store
tls_ca(ca)         // verify against that CA instead of the system store
client_cert(c, k)  // the certificate this client presents when the peer asks
                   // for one - the client half of mutual TLS; needs tls()
reconnect(max)     mode(m)            udp()
lua(path)          tick_interval(ms)

// Callbacks
on_start    on_stop    on_connect    on_disconnect
on_message(void(string_view msg))   on_tick

// Actions
send(msg)

// Lifecycle: start()  stop()
// Escape hatches: instance()  manager()  loop()
.fi
.PP
.B "socketley::proxy"
.nf
proxy(uint16_t port)

// Config
backend(addr)      strategy(s)        protocol(p)
tls(cert, key)     // terminates TLS on this proxy's listener
client_ca(ca)      // mutual TLS on it: clients must present a certificate
backend_ca(ca)     // trust store for the backend leg; turns that leg to TLS
client_cert(c, k)  // the certificate presented to a backend that asks
max_connections(n) idle_timeout(s)
lua(path)          group(g)

// Callbacks: on_start  on_stop
// Lifecycle: start()  stop()
// Escape hatches: instance()  manager()  loop()
.fi
.PP
.B "socketley::cache"
.nf
cache(uint16_t port)

// Config
persistent(path)   max_memory(bytes)   eviction(policy)
resp()             mode(m)
tls(cert, key)
max_connections(n) idle_timeout(s)     replicate(target)
lua(path)          group(g)

// Callbacks: on_start  on_stop
// Lifecycle: start()  stop()
// Escape hatches: instance()  manager()  loop()
.fi
.PP
.B "Limitations:"
One
.B start()
per process (the signal handler points to one event_loop).
.B start()
blocks until SIGINT/SIGTERM. For multi-runtime or async patterns, use the raw API
via the escape hatches or directly.
.PP
.B "TLS: on a server and a proxy only."
.B server().tls()
and
.B proxy().tls()
terminate TLS on that runtime's listener, and
.BR proxy().backend_ca() ,
.B proxy().client_ca()
and
.B proxy().client_cert()
configure the proxy's two legs, exactly as the CLI flags do \(em
.B start()
goes through
.B runtime_manager::start
and reaches the same gate. On a
.B client
or
.B cache
builder the same entry points still store their arguments and are read by
nothing, and there
.B start()
refuses rather than listening in cleartext. See
.B TLS
under
.BR LIMITATIONS .
.PP
.B "Example: echo server (3 lines)"
.nf
#include <socketley/server.h>
#include <cstdio>

int main() {
    socketley::server srv(9000);

    srv.on_connect([](int fd) {
        printf("client %d connected\\n", fd);
    });
    srv.on_message([&](int fd, std::string_view msg) {
        srv.send(fd, "echo: " + std::string(msg));
    });
    srv.on_disconnect([](int fd) {
        printf("client %d disconnected\\n", fd);
    });

    srv.start();
}
// g++ -std=c++23 echo.cpp $(sdk/socketley-config --cflags --libs)
.fi
.PP
.B "Example: server with Lua scripting"
.nf
int main(int argc, char* argv[]) {
    socketley::server srv(9000);
    srv.lua(argv[1]);
    srv.start();
}
.fi
.PP
.B "Example: TCP proxy"
.nf
socketley::proxy px(8080);
px.backend("127.0.0.1:9001")
  .backend("127.0.0.1:9002")
  .protocol(protocol_tcp)
  .strategy(strategy_round_robin);
px.start();
.fi
.PP
.B "Example: Redis-compatible cache"
.nf
socketley::cache c(6379);
c.persistent("/var/data/store.dat")
 .resp()
 .max_memory(256 * 1024 * 1024)
 .eviction(evict_allkeys_lru);
c.start();
.fi
.PP
.B "Example: client with auto-reconnect"
.nf
socketley::client cli("127.0.0.1", 9000);
cli.reconnect(0);  // 0 = infinite
cli.on_connect([&](int) { cli.send("hello"); });
cli.on_message([](std::string_view msg) {
    printf("%.*s\\n", (int)msg.size(), msg.data());
});
cli.start();
.fi
.PP
.B "Raw Engine API (advanced)"
.PP
For multi-runtime processes or custom event loop control, use the raw types directly.
The wrapper classes use this API internally.
.PP
.B "Core pattern:"
create event_loop, create runtime_manager, create runtimes, configure, run, loop.
.PP
.B "C++ callbacks"
(alternative to Lua scripts, set on any runtime):
.nf
set_on_start(function<void()>)
set_on_stop(function<void()>)
set_on_connect(function<void(int fd)>)
set_on_disconnect(function<void(int fd)>)
set_on_client_message(function<void(int fd, string_view)>)
set_on_message(function<void(string_view)>)
set_on_tick(function<void(double dt_ms)>)
.fi
.PP
.B "Common runtime setters:"
.nf
set_port(uint16_t)            set_event_loop(event_loop*)
set_runtime_manager(mgr*)     set_cache_name(string_view)
// set_tls / set_cert_path / set_key_path / set_ca_path are honoured
// on a server runtime and refused on every other type (see LIMITATIONS).
set_max_connections(uint32_t)
set_rate_limit(double)        set_idle_timeout(uint32_t)
set_log_file(string_view)     set_group(string_view)
set_drain(bool)               load_lua_script(string_view)
.fi
.PP
.B "Server-specific:"
.nf
set_mode(mode_inout|mode_in|mode_out|mode_master)
set_udp(bool)          set_http_dir(string_view)
broadcast(msg)         send_to(fd, msg)
disconnect(fd)         peer_ip(fd)
clients()              multicast(fds, msg)
.fi
.PP
.B "Cache-specific:"
.nf
set_mode(cache_mode_readonly|readwrite|admin)
set_persistent(path)   set_max_memory(bytes)
set_resp_forced(bool)  set_replicate_target(host:port)
.fi
.PP
.B "Proxy-specific:"
.nf
set_protocol(protocol_http|protocol_tcp)
set_strategy(strategy_round_robin|random|lua)
add_backend(addr)      clear_backends()
.fi
.PP
.B "Example: multi-runtime with raw API (server + cache)"
.nf
#include <socketley/server.h>
#include <socketley/cache.h>

// ... (event_loop + runtime_manager setup as above)

mgr.create(runtime_cache, "store");
auto* cache = static_cast<cache_instance*>(mgr.get("store"));
cache->set_port(6379);
cache->set_mode(cache_mode_admin);
cache->set_runtime_manager(&mgr);
cache->set_event_loop(&loop);

mgr.create(runtime_server, "api");
auto* srv = static_cast<server_instance*>(mgr.get("api"));
srv->set_port(9000);
srv->set_cache_name("store");  // clients can "cache set k v"
srv->set_runtime_manager(&mgr);
srv->set_event_loop(&loop);

mgr.start("store", loop);
mgr.start("api", loop);
loop.run();
.fi
.SS Cross-Platform SDK (Windows / macOS)
Header-only TCP clients for connecting to Socketley runtimes from non-Linux platforms.
One copy, in
.BR include/portable/socketley/ ,
reached as
.B <socketley/ws_client.h>
from any of
.IR include/linux ", " include/darwin " or " include/windows .
.PP
.B socketley/ws_client.h
\(em WebSocket (RFC 6455) + raw TCP client for server runtimes.
Embedded SHA-1 and Base64 (no OpenSSL). Modes:
.BR ws_mode::websocket " and " ws_mode::raw_tcp .
.PP
.B socketley/cache_client.h
\(em text protocol client for cache runtimes. Supports all cache commands
(get, set, del, lpush, sadd, hset, expire, publish, subscribe, etc.).
Returns
.B cache_result
with
.IR ok ", " value ", " values ", " integer
fields.
.PP
Build with C++17, no external dependencies:
.RS
.nf
# MSVC
cl /std:c++17 /I include\\windows app.cpp

# clang++ (macOS)
clang++ -std=c++17 -I include/darwin app.cpp -o app

# g++ (Linux)
g++ -std=c++17 -I include/linux app.cpp -o app
.fi
.RE
.SS Tier 3: Daemon Attach (header-only)
.B socketley/attach.h
registers your own process as a runtime with a running daemon.
After attaching, your process shows up in
.BR "socketley ls" ,
.BR "socketley stats" ,
and
.BR "socketley ps" .
Fully self-contained (builds on control.h). Auto-deregisters on
.BR exit() .
.PP
.B "API:"
.nf
bool daemon_attach(name, type, port)  // register, auto-detach on exit
void daemon_detach(name)              // explicit deregister
void daemon_detach()                  // deregister attach'd name
.fi
.PP
.B "Example:"
.nf
#include <socketley/attach.h>
#include <cstdio>

int main() {
    // Start your own TCP server (any framework)
    // ...bind, listen on port 8080...

    if (socketley::daemon_attach("myservice", "server", 8080))
        printf("registered with daemon\\n");
    else
        printf("daemon not running (standalone mode)\\n");

    // Your accept loop here...
    // atexit() automatically calls daemon_detach()
}
// g++ -std=c++17 myapp.cpp -Iinclude/linux -o myapp
.fi
.PP
.B daemon_attach()
returns false when the process may not open the daemon's IPC socket, which is
the same permission every
.B socketley
command needs \(em see
.B THE IPC SOCKET
under
.BR FILES .
A program that runs as a different user from the daemon must be in the
.B socketley
group to register with it. False is not an error to abort on: the example above
is the intended shape, the program runs standalone and is simply not in the
daemon's inventory.
.SS Mixed: Engine + Daemon Attach (Tier 2 + 3)
Embed the full engine for maximum performance, and register with the daemon
for fleet monitoring. Best of both worlds.
.PP
.nf
#include <socketley/server.h>
#include <socketley/attach.h>

socketley::server srv(9000);
srv.on_message([&](int fd, std::string_view msg) {
    srv.send(fd, msg);
});

// Register with daemon so socketley ls/stats can see it
socketley::daemon_attach("app_srv", "server", 9000);

srv.start();  // daemon sees the runtime
.fi
.PP
.B "When to use each tier:"
.PP
.TP
.B "Tier 1"
Orchestration scripts, deployment tools, monitoring, CI/CD.
.TP
.B "Tier 2"
Custom daemons, game servers, IoT gateways, performance-critical services.
.TP
.B "Tier 3"
Legacy services, third-party processes, fleet visibility for external apps.
.TP
.B "Mixed"
Embedded engine with daemon monitoring \(em maximum performance with fleet management.
.SH LIMITATIONS
.SS WebSocket and other protocol upgrades
A proxy in
.B http
mode relays a connection that has switched protocols, which is what makes a
.B ws://
client work through it. What that consists of, and what it deliberately is
not:
.PP
The upgrade is negotiated by the two endpoints and by nothing in between. The
proxy forwards the request's
.B Upgrade
and
.B Connection
headers unchanged instead of stripping them as hop-by-hop, relays the
.B 101
verbatim, and computes no part of it \(em there is no
.B Sec\-WebSocket\-Accept
calculation here, no check that the key matches, and no opinion about the
protocol named. It keys off the 101 and not off the token, so any upgrade two
endpoints agree on is relayed the same way.
.PP
.B "Frames are never parsed."
After the 101 the connection carries opaque bytes. The proxy does not know
where one frame ends and the next begins, so there is no fragmentation
handling, no ping or pong answered on a backend's behalf, no
.B permessage\-deflate
negotiation, and no per-message limit \(em
.B \-\-max\-connections
still counts connections, and nothing counts frames. The
.B on_proxy_request
and
.B on_proxy_response
callbacks still see these bytes, but they see whatever a read delivered rather
than whole frames, and a callback that rewrites them corrupts the stream: a
frame carries its own length and a client-to-server frame is masked.
.PP
.B "A tunnelled backend connection is never pooled."
There is no message boundary to return it at, so one WebSocket costs one
backend TCP connection for its whole life, however long that is. A proxy in
front of many long-lived WebSocket clients holds that many backend sockets.
.PP
The deadlines behave as follows.
.B \-\-backend\-timeout
bounds the wait for the backend's first response byte, and the 101 is that
byte \(em so it applies to the handshake and stops applying the moment the
switch happens. A tunnel is not measured against it and cannot be answered
.B 504
for being quiet, which is the point: nothing may arrive for minutes.
.B \-\-idle\-timeout
still applies, and on such a connection it measures bytes in
.I either
direction rather than only what the client sends: a stream the backend is
feeding is not idle. A tunnel that is genuinely silent both ways for longer
than the timeout is closed by it, as any other connection would be.
.PP
For the circuit breaker a tunnel is one successful response \(em the 101 \(em
and nothing more; a session ending is a connection closing and is not charged
to the backend. In
.B stats
the bytes relayed afterwards are counted in
.B bytes_in
and
.BR bytes_out ,
and
.B total_messages
counts the reads that carried them rather than frames or requests, so a proxy
carrying WebSocket traffic reports figures that do not correspond to messages
in either protocol's sense.
.PP
A proxy in
.B \-\-protocol tcp
mode is unaffected by any of this: it was already a byte relay and never looked
at a status line.
.SS What can stop the event loop
One thread serves every runtime in a process, so a call that waits stops all of
them. Six places do, and they are named here rather than left to be found: the
first of them already carried a warning at its own entry and the other five
carried none.
.PP
Measured where measuring was possible, on a Lua echo server and a static file
server under load. A share of a worker's CPU is what a cost looks like on a
healthy machine; what none of these figures say is what the same call costs when
the disk is busy or the resolver is down, which is the case they matter for.
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.B socketley.http()
\(em the whole exchange, per call from a script: name resolution, the connect
and the transfer, bounded only by the callback's remaining budget. Its own entry
says to use it in
.B on_start
and nowhere else, which is the honest way of saying the capability is not usable
where it is wanted.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR open (2)
and
.BR fstat (2)
in the static file path, per request that is not answered from
.BR \-\-http\-cache .
Measured at about 4.8 per cent of the worker \(em 1.7 in the kernel's open path
and 3.1 in
.BR statx ,
which is what glibc turns an
.B fstat
into.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR getaddrinfo (3)
on a client's first dial and a proxy's setup, both before the runtime serves
anything, so a start against a resolver that is not answering takes as long as
the resolver does. A client's reconnect, a server's upstream, a cache's
.B \-\-replicate,
a proxy's later lookups and every webhook, replication target and tier named by
a host ask the nameservers of
.I /etc/resolv.conf
from the loop itself, after
.IR /etc/hosts ,
over UDP on a socket that never waits, and take the answer when they next look;
no call there takes longer than a tenth of a millisecond, a nameserver that does
not answer included, which is passed over for the next at its timeout. A name
those cannot answer \(em one only an NSS module knows, an answer cut short \(em
is asked of the system's resolver on a thread, as every name was before.
.IP \(bu 2
The mutation journal's flush, once per
.B \-\-persistent\-interval
while anything is buffered. A write to the page cache and nothing more on a
healthy disk; a write to a full or slow one is the whole interval's worth of
clients waiting.
.IP \(bu 2
The cache's snapshot and its
.BR fsync ,
at teardown rather than periodically \(em which is why it is last of the disk
ones and not first. A stop takes as long as the dump takes.
.RE
.PP
Not among them: a runtime's state file, written and synced with its directory
on a thread of its own whenever the runtime's state changes, so no loop waits
for that disk.
.PP
.B "A storage runtime blocks twice, and never while it serves."
On start, before its listener exists, it reads the directory, removes temporary
files and, after any stop that was not clean, reads every object's header to
reconcile the journals \(em a start that takes as long as the object count
makes it, on threads and many reads at once, which have all ended before it serves. On stop it syncs a marker file saying the stop was clean. Between the
two every read and write of a file is an io_uring operation, so a slow disk is
latency on the requests waiting for it and not a runtime that stopped answering
its other connections.
.PP
.B "What is NOT on this list, having been suspected and measured."
A static file server spends 7.3 per cent of its worker in the kernel clearing
pages, which reads like an allocation churning. It is not: the same server
answering half a million requests took fourteen minor page faults in total, so
the pages are the kernel's own, for the 1.7 GB a second it is putting on the
wire. The malloc and free around each file buffer are served from the heap and
never reach it.
.SS Out\-of\-process runtimes
A runtime of any type runs in its own supervised worker process. There is no
longer an alternative and no flag to ask for one. What that costs is worth
stating plainly.
.PP
.B "The script runs in the worker and nowhere else."
An out\-of\-process runtime exists twice: the worker that owns the listening
socket and serves every byte, and a placeholder in the daemon that carries its
name, port, group and state so
.BR ls ,
.B stats
and the endpoint table have something to answer with. The script belongs to the
worker. The placeholder compiles it \(em so a file that does not parse is still
refused by
.B create
on the spot, rather than becoming "the worker exited and the runtime is failed"
\(em and then discards it without executing a line.
.IP
It used to load it as well, so every top\-level line ran twice, and the two
copies did not do the same thing: the daemon's placeholder holds the runtime
manager and the worker does not, so a top\-level
.B socketley.create()
created a runtime from inside the daemon while the identical line in the worker
was refused.
.IP
A script's
.B routes
declaration is not given up with it, though it was until the worker learned to
send it. The declaration is produced by running the script, and only the worker
runs it; the placeholder had none, so
.B publish_endpoints
published none and a gateway in front of such a runtime forwarded every method
to it instead of refusing the undeclared ones at the edge with a 405 \(em
enforcement that quietly stopped existing, which is the worst way for a gate to
fail. The worker now encodes the declaration into a
.B routes
frame on the control socket it already holds, the supervisor decodes it onto the
placeholder, and the daemon publishes the worker's copy in preference to a local
one. A
.B reload\-lua
on such a runtime waits for the new declaration before republishing, so the
gateway is never enforcing the old script's methods against the new script's
handler. The declaration is a handler's feature rather than a proxy's, which is
what made this matter the moment every type got its own process by default.
.PP
.B "create waits for the worker's verdict."
Compiling the script in the placeholder catches a file that does not parse, and
nothing else: the rest of the configuration is judged in the worker, where the
script actually runs, the port is actually bound and the TLS pair is actually
read. So
.B create
does not answer when it has forked a process \(em it waits until that process
says whether it can serve what it was handed.
.IP
Three things end the wait. The worker reports that it is serving, and
.B create
exits 0. The worker refuses, or starts and cannot bind, resolve its backend or
load its certificates, and
.B create
exits 1 with the reason in the daemon's log \(em the runtime is left
.B failed
where
.B ls
shows it, rather than being removed, so there is still something to ask about.
Or neither answer arrives within two seconds, and
.B create
exits 2 saying so; the runtime stays under supervision and
.B ls
reports whatever it settles on.
.IP
The bound is a backstop, not a budget. A worker that has to compile a script,
build its ring and bind a listener reports back in about 20 ms, and a refusal
comes back sooner because the child stops at the script; two seconds is only
reached by a worker that has wedged.
.B create
never blocks indefinitely on a child.
.PP
.B "A runtime in a worker can still ask another runtime by name."
.B \-\-cache
works across the boundary in both directions. A request for a name the asking
process does not hold goes out on the control socket; the daemon holds the
whole map and is the only process that can route, so it looks the name up,
posts the request to whatever answers for it \(em in the daemon or in another
worker \(em and writes the answer back. Neither the asking runtime nor the
client on the other end of the socket can tell which arrangement served it.
A name nobody answers to is refused, with the same
.B "error: no such runtime"
an in\-process runtime gives, rather than being dropped in silence.
.PP
.B "The cross\-runtime management API is refused inside a worker."
A worker is a separate process holding one runtime and no runtime manager, so
.BR socketley.create ,
.BR .start ,
.BR .stop ,
.BR .remove ,
.BR .send ,
.BR .list ,
.BR .get ,
.B .pipe
and the whole
.B socketley.cluster
table have no inventory to act on. They are not offered, and they do not quietly
answer nothing: each raises a Lua error naming the call, the reason and the way
out. Every one of them used to return a false, a nil or an empty table \(em
which is exactly what a
.I successful
call returns on a daemon with nothing in it, so neither the script nor the
operator could tell the two apart.
.IP
Called from a callback, the refusal is reported by whatever ran it: a filter
that raised has not said "carry on", so the request is answered
.B 500
and the message goes to the log. Called at the script's top level, the script
does not load \(em and a worker whose script did not load does not serve. It
exits saying this is a configuration it will never accept, and the supervisor
leaves the runtime
.B failed
rather than forking five more copies of the same refusal. It used to start
anyway and relay with none of the filters, routes or callbacks that were
written, behind a single warning.
.IP
Everything that does not reach for the runtime map works normally:
.BR socketley.log ,
.BR .http ,
.BR .json_encode / .json_decode ,
the timers,
.BR .subscribe ,
the
.B self
table, and the connection and request callbacks.
.IP
.B "self.route() crosses the boundary, and this section used to say it could not."
It names another runtime, so it was grouped with the calls that reach for the
map, and its refusal told a reader that "a connection cannot be routed out of a
worker
.I today"
and that naming a runtime and handing it an open connection were separate
mechanisms of which only the first existed.
.IP
That was measured and correct at the time \(em with two servers at the
out\-of\-process default it answered
.BR false ,
the target counted no connection and its
.B on_connect
never fired \(em and the conclusion drawn from it was wrong.
.BR route_client ()
never needed a descriptor to travel. It needed the target's connection tables,
which it reaches by
.B conn_ref
now, through
.BR shared/ipc/route_relay.h .
The origin keeps the socket and relays the bytes, exactly as it does when both
runtimes are in one process.
.IP
.BR unroute() ,
.B get_route()
and
.B owner_send()
answer from this runtime's own tables, and always did.
.PP
.B "Cross\-runtime pub/sub reaches a worker."
A cache's
.B PUBLISH
is delivered to a worker's
.B subscribe
callbacks over its control socket, and the daemon no longer runs them against
the placeholder it keeps for the runtime. It used to: the placeholder loads the
same script, so its copy of the callback fired \(em inside the daemon, attached
to an instance with no connections \(em while the process actually relaying the
traffic heard nothing. Delivery is one\-way and best\-effort, like every other
frame on that socket. A publish is an event and not a state, so a frame dropped
because the child is not reading is not re\-sent, and one that arrives before
the worker has been configured is discarded rather than replayed at a subscriber
that did not exist when it happened.
.PP
.B "Interactive mode (start \-i) is not offered for a proxy, on either path."
Interactive mode is a stream: what is typed reaches a server's clients, a
client's server, or a cache's command parser. A proxy relays between a client
and a backend and speaks for neither, so there is nothing for typed input to
mean, and offering it would mean inventing a meaning rather than exposing one.
The command refuses and exits 1
.I without starting anything
\(em it used to start the proxy, persist
.BR was_running ,
and then report the failure, leaving a listener up and a state file that would
bring it back on the next daemon restart.
.PP
.B "A worker's figures are read when they are printed."
.BR connections ,
.BR total_connections ,
.BR total_messages ,
.B bytes_in
and
.B bytes_out
for an out\-of\-process runtime are summed out of a small shared\-memory region
at the instant the command prints them. Each worker stores its own line into
that region once per turn of its event loop; the daemon adds the lines up. There
is no round trip, nothing waits on a child that may be wedged, and there is no
interval between the reading and the printing for a figure to go stale in.
.PP
Every figure is therefore identical on both paths, which is the property the
split exists to have. Earlier releases printed
.B figures:pushed
and
.BI figures_age_ms: n
here, because a worker pushed its counters on a quarter\-second timer and the
difference was real. Those lines no longer exist and nothing replaces them:
there is no age left to report.
.PP
The part of
.B stats
that is not a summable counter \(em a cache's key count, a proxy's healthy
backends, the ring's granted polling mode \(em is still computed inside the
worker, because it is a walk of tables that exist only there. It crosses the
control socket when it changes, and for a runtime with no traffic that is never:
an idle worker sends nothing and wakes for nothing but a TLS listener's look at
its certificate files, once every two seconds.
.PP
Until the first such report has arrived,
.B stats
prints
.B worker_report:none
in place of those figures. The window is ordinarily the first fraction of a
second after a start; a runtime that shows it while serving traffic has a
worker that is alive but not publishing \(em wedged, or its frames being lost
\(em and that line is the difference between "no data" and "no traffic". Earlier
releases printed the placeholder's own untouched figures instead:
.B keys:0
from a store no command had ever reached, indistinguishable from a running
cache that nobody had used.
.PP
.B "And which process it is."
.B stats
prints
.BI pid: n
for a runtime that has one \(em an out\-of\-process proxy's worker, or a managed
external runtime's child. Nothing else in the output says the runtime is
elsewhere, so this is the line that names the process to strace, to read
.B /proc
for, or to account a machine's memory to. An in\-process runtime prints no such
line, and neither does one whose worker has been stopped or reaped \(em a pid
that has been returned to the system is worse than none, because it is a number
somebody will go and look at.
.PP
A sharded runtime prints
.BI shards: n
and
.B shard_pids:
instead, because there are
.I n
processes and naming one of them arbitrarily is the wrong answer. Its counters
are still one set of figures: the shared region has a line per shard and the sum
over them is what the runtime is serving.
.PP
Every type can be split, and every type is split unless it asks not to be. The
proxy was first, and the rest followed once they too routed from pushed data
rather than from a pointer into another runtime. What is left is written down in
this section: the cross\-runtime management API,
.BR self.route() ,
and figures that are pushed rather than read.
.SS TLS
.B "Every runtime type speaks TLS: a server's and a cache's own listener, a proxy \(em on both of its legs \(em and a client runtime's dial."
Created with
.BR "\-\-tls \-\-cert " file " \-\-key " file ,
a
.B server
accepts, handshakes and moves records itself. OpenSSL never touches the
socket: each connection gets a memory BIO pair, the ciphertext is carried in
and out by the same io_uring submissions as every other byte in the daemon,
and the handshake advances one completion at a time so nothing blocks the
event loop. Protocol detection, WebSocket, HTTP and RESP all run on the
decrypted stream, so
.B wss://
and
.B https://
work on a listener configured this way.
.BI \-\-ca " file"
adds mutual TLS \(em a client must present a certificate signed by that CA.
.PP
A peer that opens a connection and never finishes the handshake is dropped
after 30 seconds. Fixed, not configurable, and deliberately not
.BR \-\-idle\-timeout :
a connection that did handshake and then went quiet is legitimate on a
daemon built for long\-living ones, while a peer that has sent nothing the
record layer accepted has not become a client at all \(em and would
otherwise hold a slot against
.B \-\-max\-connections
for as long as it liked. The clock starts when the handshake does and later
bytes do not move it, so a peer that dribbles inside an unfinished record is
dropped on the same schedule as one that sends nothing at all. A
.B client
runtime applies the same 30 seconds to a target that accepts its connection
and then never handshakes, after which
.B \-\-reconnect
applies to it like any other lost connection.
.PP
A
.B proxy
runs the same record layer on both of its legs. The same three flags terminate
TLS on its listening port and
.BI \-\-client\-ca " file"
makes it demand a certificate from its callers; an
.B https://
backend address \(em or
.BR \-\-ca ,
.B \-\-client\-cert
or
.BR \-\-client\-key ,
which apply to every backend \(em takes the backend leg to TLS, with
.B \-\-client\-cert
and
.B \-\-client\-key
presenting a certificate to a backend that asks for one. A TLS backend
connection is always verified, against
.B \-\-ca
or the system store, with the endpoint's name checked and TLS 1.2 as the
minimum version; there is no option to turn that off. Such connections are
pooled and reused, with their sessions, so a handshake is paid per idle period
rather than per request; a pooled socket is only ever returned to the endpoint
it was opened to, which is the name it was verified against.
.PP
.B "\-\-health\-check http"
works against a TLS backend and probes it over TLS. The probe runs the same
record layer the data path does \(em a memory BIO pair, records carried by
ordinary io_uring submissions, the handshake advanced one completion at a time
\(em and is held to the same standard: the chain against
.B \-\-ca
or the system store, the certificate against the endpoint's own name, TLS 1.2
minimum. A backend whose certificate does not verify is marked
.B unhealthy
rather than reported healthy, which is the only answer that agrees with what
the data path would do with it. Everything above the record layer is unchanged:
the same
.BI \-\-health\-path " path"
is fetched, and only a 2xx counts.
.PP
A
.B client
runtime runs the dialling half of that same record layer on its outbound
socket. Created with
.BR \-\-tls ,
it puts a ClientHello on the wire as soon as it has a socket and advances the
handshake one completion at a time, exactly as the other two do; the line
framing and the Lua callbacks above it see the same plaintext they always did.
The peer is verified against
.BI \-\-ca " file"
or the system store, and the certificate must be valid for the host in
.B \-t
\(em matched against its DNS SAN, or its IP SAN for an address literal.
.BI \-\-cert " file"
and
.BI \-\-key " file"
present a certificate to a peer that asks for one. There is no skip-verify
option, by design. A peer that fails either check costs the socket: the client
drops it and re-dials on its usual backoff, and never retries in cleartext.
.PP
A
.B cache
terminates TLS on its own listener with the same three flags a server uses, and
.BI \-\-ca " file"
demands a client certificate there in the same way. RESP is auto-detected from
the first byte of the decrypted stream, so an existing Redis client speaks the
protocol it always did, one layer up, and the line protocol works on the same
port.
.PP
.B "What a cache still cannot do over TLS is replicate."
Replication is not a listener and is not encrypted by anything: a follower
dials its leader itself, in cleartext, and on a leader a promoted follower is
written to by a second writer that goes around the record layer by design. So
the combination is refused rather than half-honoured \(em
.B \-\-tls
together with
.B \-\-replicate
is rejected at
.BR create ,
by
.BR edit " and " import ,
and again at every
.BR start ,
so a state file carrying both leaves the runtime
.B failed
rather than coming up quietly wrong; and a TLS cache asked to become a leader
by a
.B REPLICATE
command answers with an error instead of promoting itself. A cache that is
encrypted for its clients and cleartext for its replicas would be worse than
one that is honestly cleartext, because only the first of those looks safe.
.PP
Two further combinations are refused for the same reason:
.B \-\-udp
on a client or a server (DTLS is not implemented) and, on a server,
.B \-\-upstream
(the upstream connection would still be made in cleartext). A proxy in
.B \-\-protocol tcp
mode terminates TLS on its listener like any other, and forwards the decrypted
stream to its backend; the zero-copy splice path is bypassed on any connection
with a session, because a splice would move bytes the record layer never saw.
.PP
One outbound exception exists and is not transport:
.B socketley.http()
in a Lua callback speaks HTTPS, with the certificate chain and the host
name verified and TLS 1.2 as the minimum version (see
.BR "Global Functions" ).
That is a client call a script makes; it encrypts nothing that socketley
itself serves or forwards.
.PP
Earlier releases accepted the TLS flags on every runtime type, stored them,
persisted them, showed them in
.BR "socketley show" ,
and then encrypted nothing. A proxy created with
.B "\-\-tls \-\-cert \-\-key"
answered a plaintext HTTP request with 200 on the port it claimed to encrypt,
and a proxy aimed at an HTTPS backend sent a plaintext request line the
backend logged as a failed handshake. An operator asking for encryption was
told they had it. Both of those are now transport rather than decoration; the
roles that still have no handshake go on refusing the flags.
.PP
Everything that is not implemented therefore fails closed, and so does every
configuration that is incomplete:
.IP \(bu 2
.BR \-\-tls ", " \-\-cert ", " \-\-key " and " \-\-ca
on a
.B cache
runtime, and
.BR \-\-client\-ca ", " \-\-client\-cert " and " \-\-client\-key
on anything but a proxy, are rejected by
.B create
with exit status 1 and a message naming the flag.
.IP \(bu 2
on a
.BR server ", " client " or " proxy
runtime the same flags are accepted, but only as a complete set.
.B \-\-tls
without a certificate and key on a
.BR server " or " proxy ,
or a certificate or trust store without
.B \-\-tls
on any of the three, is refused and no runtime is created \(em either would
describe a listener that could only serve cleartext, or a dial that would be
made in it. On a
.B client
.B \-\-tls
alone is complete, but half a
.BR \-\-cert / \-\-key
pair is refused: it would present nothing while the flags said a certificate
was configured. On a proxy,
.B \-\-client\-ca
without
.B \-\-tls
and half a
.BR \-\-client\-cert / \-\-client\-key
pair are refused on the same reasoning.
.IP \(bu 2
flag-based
.B edit
applies the same rule, and restores the previous TLS settings if the edit
would leave them incomplete. It is a subset of
.BR create ,
so most flags it does not accept come back as
.BR "unknown flag" ;
these do not, because changing TLS on a live runtime is exactly what an
operator would be attempting.
.IP \(bu 2
.B import
applies the same rule to the JSON document's fields.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR start ", " reload
and the daemon's own boot-time restore refuse a runtime carrying a
configuration that cannot be honoured \(em which is how one restored from a
state file written by an older release is caught. Nothing is brought up; the
runtime goes to
.BR failed .
.IP \(bu 2
a certificate or key that cannot be read, or a key that does not match its
certificate, fails the runtime at start. The port is never bound.
.PP
To clear the settings from a runtime whose configuration is refused, dump it,
delete the TLS fields, and import it back (see
.BR import ).
.PP
The embedded C++ SDK reaches the same gate: its
.B start()
goes through
.BR runtime_manager::start ,
so
.B server().tls(cert, key)
and
.B proxy().tls(cert, key)
terminate TLS exactly as the daemon-managed runtime does, and the same call on
a client or cache fails the runtime instead of running in cleartext.
.PP
For encrypted transport on a
.BR client " or " cache
\(em or on a server's upstream connections \(em terminate TLS in a front-end
proxy (nginx, HAProxy, stunnel) and have it forward over loopback or an
otherwise trusted network path. A socketley
.B proxy
is now one of the things that can play that part.
